1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/burgkvien.htm#AlbertIVTourPindied1269<br/>
2 CONT a)         ALBERT [IV] de la Tour-du-Pin (-before Feb 1269).  "Hugo de Turre, abbas Claromontensis, et Guido frater eiusdem, canonicus Claromontensis" noted a donation to Cluny by charter dated 13 Oct 1238, which names “dominum Al
2 CONC bertum de Turre patrem nostrum, et Albertum filium eius fratrem nostrum…”[2128].  “Albert seigneur de la Tour et son fils Albert” donated revenue to Notre-Dame d’Ambronay, for the soul of “leur épouse et mère Béatrix“, by charter 
2 CONC dated Jan 1241 (O.S.)[2129].  “Hugo...Claromontanus episcopus et Humbertus dominus Bellijoci, et Arbertus dominus de Turre, et Guido archidiaconus Lugdunensis, et Arbertus de Turre, et Hugo senescalcus Lugdunensis fratres” agreed 
2 CONC an alliance under a charter dated Sep 1246[2130].  Seigneur de Coligny: “Albertus de Turre dominus Coloniaci, filius Alberti” renounced rights “super manso de Faye...et...de la Barandonire” in favour of “domus hospitalis Jerosolim
2 CONC itani de les Foillies” by charter dated Feb 1246 (O.S.?)[2131].  The testament of “G. de Turre archidiaconus Lugdunensis”, dated 24 Feb 1249 (O.S.), bequeathed property to “Hugonem senescalcum Lugdunensem...Humberti nepoti meo...f
2 CONC rater suus...nepotem meum Albertum...”[2132].  "Albers Segnor de la Tor et Albers son filz" swore homage to "Monsegnor Perron de Savoye" by charter dated Sep 1250[2133].  Seigneur de la Tour-du-Pin.  “Albert le jeune seigneur de l
2 CONC a Tour et de Coligny”, on the advice of “son père Albert de la Tour, de ses frères Hugues de la Tour sénéchal de Lyon, et Humbert de la Tour” granted freedoms to his town of Treffort en Bresse by charter dated Apr 1259[2134].  "Al
2 CONC bertus dominus de Turre filius quondam domini Alberti de Turre" confirmed the grant of sheep grazing rights to Grenoble Saint-Robert made by "Albertus quondam dominus de Turre avus noster et dictus dominus Albertus pater noster" b
2 CONC y charter dated 2 Jan 1265[2135].  The Feb 1269 charter of his wife quoted below implies that Albert was deceased at the time.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ([Sep 1250/Mar 1251]) ADALASIA Fiesco, daughter of [TEODORO/OPIZZONE] Fiesco [Conte di Lavagna] & his wife Simona --- (-after May 1273).  Matthew Paris records that Pope Innocent IV arranged the marriage of his niece to "d
2 CONC omino de Tur de Pin" in 1251 and that he accepted his bride "non ratione personæ muliebris, sed pecuniæ eam concomitantis"[2136].  Her mother’s name is confirmed by the reference to her mother in the May 1273 testament quoted belo
2 CONC w.  On the basis of the primary sources currently identified, her father could have been either Teodoro Fieschi or his brother Opizzone Fieschi, whose wives were both named Simona.  The marriage presumably formed part of arrangeme
2 CONC nts when Albert [III] swore homage to Pierre de Savoie in Sep 1250 for la Tour (see above).  “Adalasia dame de la Tour” placed under her protection “des hommes de la chartreuse” at the request of the prior of Portes by charter dat
2 CONC ed Mar 1251[2137].  “Adalasia domina de Turre” took under her protection “tres homines...ad Chalona...” by charter dated Dec 1251 and others by charter dated Mar 1252[2138].  "Humbertus de Turre Delphinus Viennæ" confirmed the rig
2 CONC hts of "fratrum nostrum Dom. Albertum quondam dominum de Turre et de Coliniaco…dom. Alisiæ quondam uxori suæ" in "castrum sancti Saturnini…villam de Brango…villæ et castri Crimiaci…" as well as in all property (unspecified) which 
2 CONC Alix had acquired from "Guefredo de Vireu domicello quondam filio Dom. Alberti de Vireu militis defuncti et Berengariæ eiusdem G. matris, quæ acquisita sunt in mandamento castri de Turre" and from "Giroudo Vimont quondam clerico",
2 CONC  by charter dated Feb 1269, which also provides if "Alisiæ ad secundas nuptias convolaret"[2139].  The family connections, if any, between Adalasia and Guifred de Virieu and Géraud Vimont are not specified in the document.  In the
2 CONC  case of the former, it seems unlikely that he was Adalasia’s first husband as the document refers to the possibility of her "secundas nuptias" not "tertias".  In any case, Guifred de Virieu is named in a document dated 1267 (alth
2 CONC ough it appears that he died before Feb 1269)[2140], when it is likely that Alix was already married to Albert [IV] de la Tour-du-Pin.  Maybe they were related to Adalasia through her mother.  The testament of "Adalasiæ relictæ Al
2 CONC berti junioris domini de Turre Pini et de Coloniaco", dated May 1273, bequeathed property to "Humberto de Turre fratri domini mei et mariti mei…dominæ Simondæ…matri meæ…domine Comitisse del Borget consanguinee mee…Thome et Amedeo 
2 CONC de Sabaudia filiis eiusdem…Hugoni de Turre senescallo Lugdun."[2141].  Could "…domine Comitisse del Borget consanguinee mee… " in this document be Simone [Sibylle] Dame de Bâgé, wife of Amédée de Savoie whose mother was Adalasia’s
2 CONC  sister? 
