1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/PROVENCE.htm#MargueriteMLouisIXFrancedied1270
2 CONT * https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CAPET.htm#LouisIXdied1270B
2 CONT <nowiki>—————————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT Marguerite of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite of Provence (Forcalquier, c. 1221 – December 21, 1295, Paris) was the eldest daughter of Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence and Beatrice of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her maternal grandparents were Thomas I of Savoy and Marguerite of Geneva, daughter of William I of Geneva.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her younger sisters were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Provence became the Queen consort of Henry III of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sanchia of Provence who became the Queen consort of Richard, Earl of Cornwall and rival King of the Germans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice of Provence who was the Queen consort of Charles I of Sicily
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite herself became the Queen consort of Louis IX of France and mother to eleven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (1240–April 29, 1243) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (March 2, 1241–January 28, 1271), married Theobald II of Navarre 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (February 25, 1244–January 1260) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe III (May 1, 1245–October 5, 1285) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean (born and died in 1248) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Tristan (1250–August 3, 1270) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre (1251–1284) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (1253–1323), married Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castile 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite (1254–1271), married John I, Duke of Brabant 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert, Count of Clermont (1256–February 7, 1317). He was the ancestor of King Henry IV of France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes of France (c. 1260–December 19, 1327), married Robert II, Duke of Burgundy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite, like her sisters, was noted for her beauty, she was said to be "pretty with dark hair and fine eyes", and in the early years of their marriage she and Louis enjoyed a warm relationship. Her Franciscan confessor, Willia
2 CONC m de St. Pathus, related that on cold nights Marguerite would place a robe around Louis' shoulders, when her deeply religious husband rose to pray. Another anecdote recorded by St. Pathus related that Marguerite felt that Louis' p
2 CONC lain clothing was unbecoming to his royal dignity, to which Louis replied that he would dress as she wished, if she dressed as he wished. However, the chronicler Joinville noted with disapproval that Louis rarely asked after his w
2 CONC ife and children, and in later years Louis became vexed with Marguerite's ambition.
2 CONT
2 CONT She accompanied Louis on his first crusade and was responsible for negotiations and ransom when he was captured. She was thus for a brief time the only woman ever to lead a crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Louis on his second crusade, during which she remained in France, she returned to Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was devoted to her sister Queen Eleanor of England, and they stayed in contact until Eleanor's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Nascimento: ou c. 1221.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
