2 CONT
2 CONT LEOPOLD (4 Aug 1290-Strasbourg 28 Feb 1326, bur Königsfelden).  The Dytheri de Helmestat Notæ Historicæ names "dominus Lupoldus dux Swevie, dominus Heinricus, dominus Albertus et dominus Otto duces Austrie et Stirie" as the four b
2 CONC rothers of "dominum Fridericum ducem Austrie"[418].  He succeeded in 1306 as LEOPOLD I Duke of Austria and Steiermark.  He led the Austrian troops which were routed by the Swiss confederates at the Pass of Morgarten 15 Nov 1315, w
2 CONC hich preceded the declaration of renewal of the Eidgenossen 9 Dec 1315[419].  The necrology of Königsfelden records the death "II Kal Mar 1326" of "Lupoldus dux Austrie et Styrie filius Alberti regis Romanorum hic sepultus cum mat
2 CONC re sua…regine Elizabeth nostre fundatricis"[420].  The Necrologium Austriacum records the death "1326 II Kal Mar" of "Leupoldus dux Austrie et Styrie" and his burial in Königsfelden[421].  m (Contract 20 Apr 1310, Basel 26 May 131
2 CONC 5) CATHERINE de Savoie, daughter of AMEDEE V Comte de Savoie & his second wife Marie de Brabant ([1300/03][422]-Rheinfelden 30 Sep 1336, bur Königsfelden).  The contract of marriage between "Leopoldo Duca d'Austria" and "Cattarina
2 CONC  figlia del Conte Amedeo di Savoia" is dated 20 Apr 1310[423].  The Continuatio Claustroneoburgensis records the death "pridie Kal Oct 1336" of "Katherina, relicta ducis Austrie Leupoldi, filia comitis Sabaudie", specifying that s
2 CONC he was buried "in Chunigsveld"[424].  The necrology of Wettingen records the death "II Kal Oct 1337" of "Katharina duceyssa Austrie uxor ducis Lupoldi"[425].  Duke Leopold I & his wife had two children:  
2 CONT
2 CONT a)             KATHARINA (9 Feb 1320-28 Sep 1349, bur Königsfelden).  The Necrologium Austriacum records the death "1336 II Kal Oct" of "Katherina filia coma de Saphoy relicta Leopoldi ducis Austrie" and her burial at Königsfelden
2 CONC [426].  The date so recorded is inconsistent with her two marriages shown below, although the way in which her mother is referred to in the entry does suggest that the latter was alive when Katharina died.  It is also surprising t
2 CONC hat there is no reference to Katharina's second husband in the source, which is the practice in other entries.  As all other events are apparently recorded accurately in this source, one possibility is that the wife of the two hus
2 CONC bands was another, as yet unidentified, daughter of Duke Leopold I who is otherwise unnamed in the sources.  She died of plague.  m firstly ([Vincennes 25 Nov 1338]) ENGUERRAND [VI] Sire de Coucy [Guines] (-killed in battle 25 Aug
2 CONC  1346, bur Ourecamp).  m secondly (1348) KONRAD Burggraf von Magdeburg  (-25 Sep 1349). 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.thepeerage.com/p3917.htm#i39169
2 CONT
2 CONT  Katharina von Habsburg was born in 1320. She was the daughter of Leopold I Herzog von Österreich and Catherine de Savoie. She married, firstly, Enguerrand VI de Coucy, Sire de Coucy before 12 January 1337. She married, secondly, 
2 CONC Graf Conrad von Hardegg in 1348. She died in 1349.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Katharina von Habsburg was also known as Catherine of Habsburg.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Katharina von Habsburg and Enguerrand VI de Coucy, Sire de Coucy
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Enguerrand VII de Coucy, Sire de Coucy+1 b. c 1339, d. 18 Feb 1397
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 69. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------
