1 NOTE {geni:about_me} OTHON de Grandson (-[1375]).  “Pierre de Grandson chevalier seigneur de Belmont” notified his sale of property to la Grande Chartreuse, with the consent of “Blanche de Savoie son épouse et de Othenin son fils”, by 
2 CONC charter dated 8 Oct 1317[797].  A charter dated 5 Feb 1336 records the settlement of disputes between “Rodulphum comitem et dominum Novi Castri...dominum Ludovicum eius filium, Girardum de Arber dominum de Vaulengins” and “Petrum 
2 CONC dominum Grandissoni...dominum Otthonem eius filium”[798].  The 29 Mar 1340 testament of Louis [II] de Savoie Comte de Vaud names his nephew Otto de Grandson, Guillaume his brother and his sisters[799].  Seigneur de Grandson.  Afte
2 CONC r the death of Aimon Count of Savoy in Aug 1343, the guardians of his son appointed “Louis comte de Neuchâtel, Gérard de Montfaucon seigneur d’Orbe et d’Echallens, Othe sire de Grandson, et Hugues de Joinville seigneur de Gex chev
2 CONC aliers” as administrators of “[le] pays roman”[800].  “Othes sires de Gransson” issued a charter dated 28 Jun 1347[801].  He was named executor in his first wife’s 7 Sep 1349 testament.  The testament of “Loys, cons et sires de Nu
2 CONC efchastel en la dyocese de Losene”, dated 14 Apr 1354, appointed “mon frere monsegniour Othe segniour de Gransson” as guardian of his infant children[802].  “...Jacoz de Grançon signour de Pesmes...Mess. Othes sires de Grançon” ar
2 CONC e named among fiefholders of the duke of Burgundy in the county of Burgundy in a charter dated 25 Dec 1356[803].  The testament of Othon de Grandson is dated 1375[804].  Othon presumably died before the [1375] charter quoted below
2 CONC  in which his sons partitioned their territories.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (contract 1323) JEANNE de Pesmes, daughter of GUILLAUME [V] Seigneur de Pesmes & his wife Gille de Courcelles (-after 7 Sep 1349, bur Pesmes).  The history of the family of the seigneurs de Pesmes was studied in the late
2 CONC  19th century by Perchet[805].  Perchet states that “la promesse de mariage” between Othon de Grandson and Jeanne de Pesmes was dated 1323[806].  The testament of Guillaume Seigneur de Pesmes, dated “le dimanche avant la fête de l
2 CONC ’Ascension de l’an 1327”, appointed “Jeannette de Pesmes sa fille mariée à Othe de Grandson” as his heir and named “Gilles de Courcelles son épouse...” among his executors[807].  The testament of “Johanna domina de Grandisono”, da
2 CONC ted 7 Sep 1349, requested burial “in ecclesia beati Ylarii de Pesmis”, appointed as her universal heir “filium meum Jacobum de Grandisono filiumque eciam domini Otthonis domni de Grandisono militis, domicellum”, and appointed “mar
2 CONC itum meum...Otthonem dominum de Grandisono” as executor, witnessed by “Johanne Humberto de Grandisono canonico Bisuntino et Lausanensi...Johanne dicto Le Bastard filio Theobaldo de Grandisono...”[808].  
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly BLANCHE de Châtillon Dame de Poix, daughter of --- (-after 9 Jul 1371).  The primary source which confirms her parentage has not been identified.  Othon Seigneur de Grandson ratified the duke of Burgundy’s consent to “m
2 CONC adame Blanche de Châtillon dame de Grandson” granting property to a member of her household on her marriage, by charter dated 9 Jul 1371[809].  Othon de Grandson submitted to the duke of Burgundy the properties he held “au nom de 
2 CONC sa femme Blanche de Châtillon : la forteresse de Puits...les fiefs d’Isabeau de Recey fille de Jean de Recey chevalier et d’Etienne de Chanteloup sire de Velle-forgeul, à cause de Marie de Nesle sa femme” by charter dated 1372[810
2 CONC ].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Othon & his first wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         JACQUES de Grandson (-[Jun/19 Dec] 1381, bur Pesmes Saint-Hilaire).  The testament of “Johanna domina de Grandisono”, dated 7 Sep 1349, appointed as her universal heir “filium meum Jacobum de Grandisono filiumque eciam 
2 CONC domini Otthonis domni de Grandisono militis, domicellum”[811].  He succeeded his mother as Seigneur de Pesmes. -        SEIGNEURS de PESMES. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Othon & his second wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT b)         HUGUES de Grandson (-1397).  An undated charter, dated to “vers 1375”, records the division of territories agreed between “messire Jacques de Grantson seigneur de Pesmes et messire Hugues de Grantson, frères, enffans de
2 CONC  messire Othe seigneur dudit Granson”, under which Hugues received “la terre et seignorie de Belmont et tout ce que lesdits frères pouvoient avoir ou pays de Vaulx”[812].  Seigneur de Grandson et de Lompnes en Bugey.  He was conde
2 CONC mned to death in 1389 and his properties confiscated, but escaped from prison and fled to England where his cousin Othon de Grandson was accused of involvement in his death in 1397[813]. 
2 CONT
