1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Charles II''', known as "the Lame" (French le Boiteux, Italian lo Zoppo) (1254 – 5 May 1309) was;  King of Naples (7 Jan 1285 - 5 May 1309); King of Albania (1285 - 1294);  Prince of Salerno,  Prince of Achaea C
2 CONC ount of Anjou (1254 - 1290)
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles II surrendered his rights to Albania to his son Philip II in 1294. Philip II reigned as "Lord of the Kingdom of Albania".
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor: Charles I Successor: Robert Spouse: Maria of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue: Charles Martel of Anjou Saint Louis of Toulouse Robert of Naples Philip I of Taranto Raymond Berengar John of Anjou Tristan of Anjou Peter Tempesta John of Gravina Marguerite of Anjou Blanche of Anjou Eleanor of Anjou Maria
2 CONC  of Anjou Beatrice of Anjou
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Charles I of Naples Mother 	Beatrice of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles II, known as "the Lame" (French le Boiteux, Italian lo Zoppo; 1254 – 5 May 1309), was King of Naples and Sicily, titular King of Jerusalem, and Prince of Salerno.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Charles I of Anjou, who had conquered the Kingdom of Naples and Sicily in the 1260s. His mother was Beatrice of Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Sicilian Vespers, he had been captured by Roger of Lauria in the naval battle at Naples in 1284. When his father died in 1285, he was still a prisoner of Peter III of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1288, King Edward I of England mediated to make peace, and Charles was liberated on the condition that he was to retain Naples alone. Sicily was left to the Aragonese. Charles was also to induce his cousin Charles of Valois to 
2 CONC renounce, for twenty thousand pounds of silver, the kingdom of Aragon, which had been given to him by Pope Martin IV to punish Peter for having invaded Sicily, but which the Valois had never effectively occupied.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was then released, leaving three of his sons and sixty Provençal nobles as hostages, promising to pay 30,000 marks and to return a prisoner if the conditions were not fulfilled within three years. He went to Rieti, where t
2 CONC he new Pope Nicholas IV immediately absolved him from all the conditions he had sworn to observe, crowned him King of Sicily in 1289, and excommunicated King Alfonso III of Aragon. Charles of Valois, in alliance with Castile, prep
2 CONC ared to take possession of Aragon, reopening the Aragonese Crusade. Alfonso, being hard pressed, agreed to the conditions of the Treaty of Tarascon: he had to promise to withdraw the troops he had sent to help his brother James in
2 CONC  Sicily, to renounce all rights over the island, and pay a tribute to the Holy See.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso died childless in 1291 before the treaty could be carried out, and James took possession of Aragon, leaving the government of Sicily to the third brother Frederick.
2 CONT
2 CONT The new Pope Boniface VIII, elected in 1294 at Naples under the auspices of King Charles, mediated between the latter and James, and the dishonourable Treaty of Anagni was signed: James was to marry Charles’s daughter Bianca and w
2 CONC as promised the investiture by the pope of Sardinia and Corsica, while he was to leave the Angevin a free hand in Sicily and even to assist him if the Sicilians resisted.
2 CONT
2 CONT An attempt was made to bribe Frederick into consenting to this arrangement, but being backed up by his people he refused, and was afterwards crowned King of Sicily. The ensuing war was fought on land and sea but Charles, though ai
2 CONC ded by the pope, his cousin Charles of Valois and James, was unable to conquer the island, and his son the prince of Taranto was taken prisoner at the battle of La Falconara in 1299. Peace was at last made in 1302 at Caltabellotta
2 CONC . Charles gave up all rights to Sicily and agreed to the marriage of his daughter Leonora and King Frederick; the treaty was ratified by the pope in 1303. Charles spent his last years quietly in Naples, which city he improved and 
2 CONC embellished.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Naples in August 1309, and was succeeded by his son Robert the Wise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1270, he married Maria of Hungary (c. 1257 – 25 March 1323), the daughter of Stephen V of Hungary and Elizabeth the Cuman. They had fourteen children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Charles Martel d'Anjou, titular King of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Saint Louis of Toulouse (9 February 1275, Nocera Inferiore – 19 August 1298, Chateau de Brignoles), Bishop of Toulouse
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Robert the Wise, King of Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Philip I of Taranto, Prince of Achaea and Taranto, Despot of Romania, Lord of Durazzo, titular Emperor of Constantinople
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Raymond Berengar (1281–1307), Count of Provence, Prince of Piedmont and Andria
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. John (1283 – aft. 16 March 1308), a priest
2 CONT
2 CONT    7. Tristan (1284–bef. 1288)
2 CONT
2 CONT    8. Peter (1291 – 29 August 1315, Battle of Montecatini), Count of Gravina
2 CONT
2 CONT    9. John of Gravina (1294 – 5 April 1336, Naples), Duke of Durazzo, Prince of Achaea, and Count of Gravina, married March 1318 (div 1321) Matilda of Hainaut (29 November 1293–1336), married 14 November 1321 Agnes of Périgord (d.
2 CONC  1345)
2 CONT
2 CONT   10. Marguerite (1273– 31 December 1299), Countess of Anjou and Maine, married at Corbeil 16 August 1290 to Charles of Valois
2 CONT
2 CONT   11. Blanche of Anjou (1280 – 14 October 1310, Barcelona), married at Villebertran 1 November 1295 James II of Aragon
2 CONT
2 CONT   12. Eleanor of Anjou, (August 1289 – 9 August 1341, Monastery of St. Nicholas, Arene, Elis), married at Messina 17 May 1302 Frederick III of Sicily
2 CONT
2 CONT   13. Maria (1290 – c. 1346), married at Palma de Majorca 20 September 1304 Sancho I of Majorca, married 1326 Jaime de Ejerica (1298 – April 1335)
2 CONT
2 CONT   14. Beatrice (1295 – c. 1321), married April 1305 Azzo VIII d'Este, marchese of Ferrara etc. (d. 1308); she married secondly 1309 Bertrand III of Baux, Count of Andria (d. 1351)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_II_of_Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Charles I of Anjou, who had conquered the Kingdom of Naples and Sicily in the 1260s. His mother was Beatrice of Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Sicilian Vespers, he had been captured by Roger of Lauria in the naval battle at Naples in 1284. When his father died in 1285, he was still a prisoner of Peter III of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1288, King Edward I of England mediated to make peace, and Charles was liberated on the condition that he was to retain Naples alone. Sicily was left to the Aragonese. Charles was also to induce his cousin Charles of Valois to 
2 CONC renounce, for twenty thousand pounds of silver, the kingdom of Aragon, which had been given to him by Pope Martin IV to punish Peter for having invaded Sicily, but which the Valois had never effectively occupied.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was then released, leaving three of his sons and sixty Provençal nobles as hostages, promising to pay 30,000 marks and to return a prisoner if the conditions were not fulfilled within three years. He went to Rieti, where t
2 CONC he new Pope Nicholas IV immediately absolved him from all the conditions he had sworn to observe, crowned him King of Sicily in 1289, and excommunicated King Alfonso III of Aragon. Charles of Valois, in alliance with Castile, prep
2 CONC ared to take possession of Aragon, reopening the Aragonese Crusade. Alfonso, being hard pressed, agreed to the conditions of the Treaty of Tarascon: he had to promise to withdraw the troops he had sent to help his brother James in
2 CONC  Sicily, to renounce all rights over the island, and pay a tribute to the Holy See.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso died childless in 1291 before the treaty could be carried out, and James took possession of Aragon, leaving the government of Sicily to the third brother Frederick.
2 CONT
2 CONT The new Pope Boniface VIII, elected in 1294 at Naples under the auspices of King Charles, mediated between the latter and James, and the dishonourable Treaty of Anagni was signed: James was to marry Charles’s daughter Bianca and w
2 CONC as promised the investiture by the pope of Sardinia and Corsica, while he was to leave the Angevin a free hand in Sicily and even to assist him if the Sicilians resisted.
2 CONT
2 CONT An attempt was made to bribe Frederick into consenting to this arrangement, but being backed up by his people he refused, and was afterwards crowned King of Sicily. The ensuing war was fought on land and sea but Charles, though ai
2 CONC ded by the pope, his cousin Charles of Valois and James, was unable to conquer the island, and his son the prince of Taranto was taken prisoner at the battle of La Falconara in 1299. Peace was at last made in 1302 at Caltabellotta
2 CONC . Charles gave up all rights to Sicily and agreed to the marriage of his daughter Leonora and King Frederick; the treaty was ratified by the pope in 1303. Charles spent his last years quietly in Naples, which city he improved and 
2 CONC embellished.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in Naples in August 1309, and was succeeded by his son Robert the Wise.
2 CONT
