2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_I_of_Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles I King of Hungary[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 	12 July 1312 – 16 July 1342
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	1288
2 CONT
2 CONT Birthplace 	Naples, Kingdom of Naples
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	16 July 1342 (aged 54)
2 CONT
2 CONT Place of death 	Visegrád, Kingdom of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor 	Otto III, Duke of Bavaria
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor 	Louis I of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Consorts 	Maria of Bytom, Beatrix of Luxembourg, Elisabeth of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT Offspring 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Katharine of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Hungary (?)
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis I of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew, Duke of Calabria
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen, Duke of Slavonia
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal House 	House of Anjou-Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Charles Martel of Anjou
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Klementia of Habsburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles I of Hungary (Hungarian: Károly Róbert, Croatian: Karlo Robert, Slovak: Karol Róbert), (1288, Naples, Italy – 16 July 1342, Visegrád, Hungary[1]), is also known as Charles Robert, Charles Robert of Anjou, and Charles Rober
2 CONC t of Anjou-Hungary, King of Hungary[1] (1308-1342). 
2 CONT
2 CONT He belonged to the royal house of Anjou-Hungary, was a patrilineal descendant of the Capetian dynasty of Anjou (Anjou-Sicily) and a matrilineal descendant of the Árpád dynasty. His claim to the throne of Hungary was contested by s
2 CONC everal pretenders. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, although he was only a child when his grandfather, King Charles II of Naples sent him to Hungary in 1300, Charles could strengthen his rule in the kingdom against his opponents and the powerful magnates following a l
2 CONC ong series of internal struggles. Charles also carried out numerous important political and economical reforms: he established the so called honor system which made the powerful barons dependent of his favour and he introduced new
2 CONC  coins with a constantly high purity of gold. Charles's foreign policy largely stemmed from dynastic alliances. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His most successful achievement was the mutual defense union with Poland and Bohemia against the Habsburgs. Charles also endeavoured to enforce his or his descendants' claim to the Kingdom of Naples, but he could achieve only sham
2 CONC  results. Nevertheless, he was one of the most successful rulers of the Kingdom of Hungary whose efforts established his successor's achievements.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Struggle for Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Struggles with the magnates
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 The restoration of the royal power
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 Foreign policy
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 5.1 Deterioration of the southern frontier
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 6 Death
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 7 Marriages and children
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 7.1 First marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 7.2 Second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 7.3 Third marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT           o 7.4 Mistress
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 8 Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 9 Titles
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 10 References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 11 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT Childhood
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was the only son of Charles Martel, Prince of Salerno and his wife Clementia, a daughter of King Rudolph I of Germany. His paternal grandmother, Mary, a daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary, declared her claim to Hungary 
2 CONC following the death of her brother, King Ladislaus IV of Hungary, but the majority of the country accepted the rule of her distant cousin, King Andrew III. Nevertheless, Mary transferred her claim to Hungary to her eldest son, Cha
2 CONC rles Martel on 6 January 1292, who was also the heir to the Kingdom of Naples, but he was never able to enforce his claim against King Andrew III and died on 19 August 1295.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father's death, the child Charles inherited the claim to Hungary, but his grandfather, King Charles II of Naples appointed his younger son (Charles' paternal uncle), Robert to his heir in Naples on 13 February 1296. This
2 CONC  decree was confirmed by Pope Boniface VIII, the overlord of the Kingdom of Naples, on 27 February 1297, so Charles lost his claim to the throne of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Struggle for Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT In the beginning of 1300, a powerful magnate, Paul Šubić accepted Charles' title to the kingdom and invited him to Hungary. His grandfather accepted the invitation and granted Charles a smaller amount of money and sent him to Hung
2 CONC ary to enforce his claim against King Andrew III. Charles disembarked in Split in August 1300 and he went to Zagreb where he was accepted as King of Hungary by Ugrin Csák, another influential magnate of the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT When King Andrew III died on 14 January 1301, Charles' partisans took him to Esztergom where the Archbishop Gregory Bicskei crowned him with an occasional crown because the Holy Crown of Hungary was guarded by his opponents. The m
2 CONC ajority of the magnates of the kingdom, however, did not accept his rule and proclaimed Wenceslaus, the son of Wenceslaus II of Bohemia king. The young Wenceslaus accepted the election and engaged the daughter of King Andrew III a
2 CONC nd he was crowned with the Holy Crown of Hungary in Székesfehérvár by Archbishop John of Kalocsa.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his opponent's coronation, Charles withdrew to Slavonia where his partisans strengthened his rule. In September 1302, he laid siege to Buda, but he could not occupy the capital of the kingdom and had to withdraw to Slavonia 
2 CONC again. Pope Boniface VIII confirmed Charles' claim to Hungary on 31 May 1303 and his maternal uncle, King Albert I of Germany also provided him military assistance. In the summer of 1304, King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia arrived to H
2 CONC ungary in order to help his son to strengthen his rule in the kingdom. However, the King of Bohemia had to realise soon that his son's position in Hungary was unstable; therefore he decided to retreat and his son followed him. On 
2 CONC hearing his opponents retreat, Charles made an alliance with Duke Rudolph I of Austria and they attacked Bohemia but they could not occupy Kutná Hora and Charles had to retreat to Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nevertheless, the majority of the Hungarian magnates did not accept Charles' rule. In August 1305, his opponent, Wenceslaus, who had inherited Bohemia from his father, renounced his claim to Hungary on behalf of Otto III, Duke of 
2 CONC Bavaria, who was a grandson of King Béla IV of Hungary. Otto arrived to Hungary soon and he was crowned with the Holy Crown of Hungary in Székesfehérvár by the Bishops of Veszprém and Csanád on 6 December. However, Otto was not ab
2 CONC le to strengthen his rule, either. In the course of 1306, Charles occupied Esztergom, Spiš Castle, Zvolen and some other fortresses in the northern parts of the kingdom, and in the next year he also occupied Buda.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1307, Duke Otto III visited the powerful Voivode of Transylvania, Ladislaus Kán, but the latter arrested him. On 10 October 1307, the magnates presented at the assembly in Rákos proclaimed Charles king, but the most powerf
2 CONC ul aristocrats (Máté Csák, Amadé Aba and Ladislaus Kán) ignored him. At the end of the year, Ladislaus Kán set Charles' opponent free and Otto left the country, but the Voivode of Transylvania denied to hand over the Holy Crown of
2 CONC  Hungary to Charles, whose legitimacy could be questioned without the coronation with the Holy Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Struggles with the magnates
2 CONT
2 CONT After Otto's escape, Charles stood alone as claimant to the throne of Hungary, but large parts of his kingdom were under the rule of powerful aristocrats and even his alleged partisans ignored his royal prerogatives. His position 
2 CONC slightly strengthened when the legate of Pope Clement V arrived to Hungary in June 1308, who persuaded Máté Csák to recognise Charles' reign on their meeting in the Monastery of Kékes. On 27 November 1308, Máté Csák was also prese
2 CONC nt at the assembly in Pest where Charles was again proclaimed King of Hungary. Following the assembly, the synod of the prelates in Buda confirmed the theory of the inviolability of the king and the bishops also summoned Ladislaus
2 CONC  Kán to return the Holy Crown of Hungary to Charles. However, the Voivode of Transylvania denied the request; therefore the Papal legate had a new crown made for Charles and he was crowned with the new crown by Archbishop Tamás of
2 CONC  Esztergom on 15 June 1309. Finally, under the threats of the Papal legate, Ladislaus Kán handed over the Holy Crown and Charles was, for the third time, crowned with the Holy Crown on 27 August 1310 by the Archbishop of Esztergom
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1311, Máté Csák laid siege to Charles' capital, Buda, but Charles forced back his attack. Shortly afterwards, the citizens of Košice murdered Amade Aba, who had been one of Charles' main partisans, but Charles too
2 CONC k the side of the citizens against Amade Aba's sons; consequently, the latters allied themselves with Máté Csák. Charles laid siege to Máté Csák's castle, Šariš Castle in May 1312, but he was forced to retreat by the troops of the
2 CONC  powerful magnate. Then the allied armies of Máté Csák and Amade Aba's sons marched against Košice but Charles defeated them in the Battle of Rozgony on 12 July 1312. After the victory, Charles managed to occupy some castles of Am
2 CONC ade Aba's sons in the counties of Abaúj, Torna and Sáros.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1314, Charles reoccupied from Austria Devín Castle, taking advantage of the internal conflicts in the Holy Roman Empire, and in the first half of 1315 he managed to reoccupy also the Castle of Visegrád from Máté Csák. At about 
2 CONC that time, he married Mary of Bytom, a daughter of Duke Casimir of Bytom.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the course of May 1316, Charles was struggling against the family Kőszegi, but some magnates of the eastern part of his kingdom, lead by Kopasz Borsa, rebelled against him and offered the Holy Crown to King Andrew of Halych, wh
2 CONC o himself was also a descendant of King Béla IV of Hungary. Charles' troops, however, defeated the rebels and occupied their castles in the counties of Bihar, Szolnok, Borsod and Kolozs. In May 1317, his armies also suppressed the
2 CONC  revolt of Amade Aba's sons, and he could occupy the Castle of Komárom from Máté Csák in October.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles raised the funds of his military actions by seizing ecclesiastical properties; therefore the prelates made an alliance in 1318 and they demanded that the Estates of the realm be summonded to a general assembly. However, th
2 CONC e Estates did not raised objections against the king's policies at their assembly in July. During the year, his troops occupied several fortresses of the deceased Ladislaus Kán's sons in Transylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his first wife's death, the widowed Charles married Beatrix of Luxemburg, daughter of Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor and sister of King John I of Bohemia, probably in September 1318. In the summer of 1319, he led his armies a
2 CONC gainst King Stefan Uroš II Milutin of Serbia, who had occupied the southern part of the Kingdom of Hungary, and defeated the Serbian troops near Macsó. After his victory, Charles reoccupied Beograd and also the territory of the Ba
2 CONC nate of Macsó. At that time, Charles began to reorganise the financial basis of the royal power by declaring that it is only the king who is entitled to open new custom-houses in the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT His second wife, Beatrix and her only child died at its birth on October 11, 1319. Charles, having widowed for the second time, married Elisabeth, a daughter of King Wladislaus I of Poland on 6 July 1320.
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of Máté Csák on 21 March 1321, the most powerful aristocrat in the kingdom, resulted in the disintegration of his provinces and Charles' troops could occupy all the fortresses of the deceased baron till the end of the ye
2 CONC ar. In January 1322, the towns in Dalmatia rebelled against the rule of Ban Mladen Subić, whose family had been among Charles' first supporters. Charles, taking advantage of the situation, went to Dalmatia and arrested the powerfu
2 CONC l Ban and enstrengthened his power in Croatia and Dalmatia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The restoration of the royal power
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles carried out numerous important political and economical reforms. In the beginning of 1323, he renounced the royal prerogative of undermining the currency and introduced a new tax (lucrum camaræ) in order to ensure the perm
2 CONC anency of the royal revenues. In the same year, Charles transferred his seat to Visegrád from Timişoara.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coats of Arms of Charles I of Anjou, King of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles established the so called honor system: instead of large donations, faithful servants of the king were given an office (in Latin honor), thus they became the keeper of royal property (including castles) in the counties and
2 CONC  the representative of the king. However, these offices were not given for eternity, because the king could deprive his people of their office any time. Most powerful honors often rotated among the members of aristocracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles successfully curbed inflation, introducing new coins with a constantly high purity of gold. Florins minted, from 1325, in a newly established mint in Kremnica became soon the popular international means of payment througho
2 CONC ut Europe. The reform of the currency and of the whole fiscal system greatly contributed to enrich the treasury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Foreign policy
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles's foreign policy largely stemmed from dynastic alliances and he also endeavoured to strengthen his rule over the neighbouring territories that had accepted the supremacy of the Kings of Hungary in the course of the 13th ce
2 CONC ntury.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles lead, already in 1324, his armies against Voivode Basarab I of Wallachia who had occupied the Banat of Severin from the Kingdom of Hungary and defeated him. Wallachian sepherds rolled down cliffs from hills in a canyon. Ch
2 CONC arles rebuilt quickly his armies. Bassarab paid tributes to Charles.
2 CONT
2 CONT His most successful achievement was the mutual defense union with Poland and Bohemia against the Habsburgs, accomplished by the convention of Trenčín in 1335, confirmed the same year at the brilliant two-month congress of Visegrád
2 CONC . Not only did all the princes of Central Europe compose their differences and enjoy splendid entertainment during the months of October and November: the immediate result of the congress was a combined attack by the Hungarians an
2 CONC d Poles upon Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor and his ally the Habsburg Duke Albert II of Austria, which resulted in favor of Charles in 1337.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles's desire to unite the kingdoms of Hungary and Naples under his eldest son Louis I was dashed by Venice and by the Pope, who both feared Hungary might become the dominant Adriatic power. Nevertheless he was more than compen
2 CONC sated for this disappointment by his compact in 1339 with his ally and brother-in-law, Casimir III of Poland, whereby it was agreed that Louis should succeed to the Polish throne on the death of the childless Casimir. Finally his 
2 CONC younger son, Andrew, Duke of Calabria was promised the crown of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deterioration of the southern frontier
2 CONT
2 CONT The Árpád kings had succeeded in encircling their whole southern frontier with six military colonies or banates, comprising, roughly speaking, Little Wallachia (southern part of present-day Romania) and the northern parts of prese
2 CONC nt-day Bulgaria, Serbia and Bosnia. Charles redistributed these territories and proselytized the residents of the region to consolidate his reign.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he managed to expand his kingdom, the adverse effect was converting most of the old banates into semi-independent and violently anti-Hungarian principalities. The predominant religion of the area was Greek-Orthodox, and f
2 CONC orceful proselytization to Catholicism provoked rebellion. Natural dynastic competition with the Orthodox Serbian and Bulgarian tsars and the emergence of a new Wallachia also contributed to the uprising.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prior to 1320, Western Wallachia (Oltenia) was regarded by the Hungarians as part of the banate of Szörény (Severin). When the Wallachian ruler, Basarab I showed signs of disobedience, Charles lead his army into Wallachia, though 
2 CONC poor supplies caused him to return after occupying several towns. On his return 9 November 1330, the Hungarian army got lost between the mountains. The Wallachians and Hungarians signed a peace treaty and Basarab vowed to show the
2 CONC m out of the mountain pass, but in fact the Wallachians trapped the Hungarians in an ambush at Posada. In the Battle of Posada, King Charles barely escaped, by exchanging clothes with one of his knights. This incident marked the b
2 CONC eginning of Wallachia as an independent voivodeship.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unknown to Charles, the Ottoman Turks had already secured Asia Minor under the sultans Osman I and Orhan I and planned to invade south-eastern Europe to consolidate their realm. The south-eastern European sovereignties were keener
2 CONC  on securing their regimes than on co-ordinating their defences. Their diversity helped the Ottomans expand their dominion into the region.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles died on 16 July 1342, and was laid beside the high altar in Székesfehérvár, the ancient burial place of the Árpáds.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and children
2 CONT
2 CONT First marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles married three times.[2] His first wife was Maria of Bytom, a member of the Piast dynasty.[3] She was a daughter of Casimir, Duke of Bytom and his wife Helena.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles and Maria possibly had two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Katharine of Hungary, married Henry II of Świdnica and was mother of Anne of Świdnica; Anna was the third wife of Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor and mother of Wenceslaus, King of the Romans.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Elisabeth of Hungary (d.1367), married Boleslaw of Opole.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some believe that Katherine and Elisabeth were daughters of Elisabeth of Poland, Charles' third wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Second marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1318, Charles married his second wife Beatrix of Luxembourg. She was a daughter of Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor and Margaret of Brabant. Beatrix was a younger sister of both John of Bohemia and Marie de Luxembourg, Queen of Fr
2 CONC ance. In November 1319, Beatrix died after giving birth to a stillborn child.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Third marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 6 July 1320, Charles married his third wife Elisabeth of Poland. She was a daughter of Władysław I the Elbow-high and Jadwiga of Greater Poland.[5] Her maternal grandparents were Boleslaus of Greater Poland and Jolenta of Hunga
2 CONC ry. They had five sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles (1321).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Ladislaus (Belgrade, 1 November 1324 – 24 February 1329).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Louis I of Hungary (1326–1382).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Andrew, Duke of Calabria (1327–1345).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Stephen, Duke of Slavonia (1332–1354).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that Charles' two daughter: Katherine and Elisabeth were daughter of Elisabeth of Poland, rather than Maria of Bytom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mistress
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles had a mistress named Elisabeth Csák, daughter of George Csák. They were parents to Coloman of Hungary, Bishop of Győr (1318 - 1375/1376). [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles
2 CONT
2 CONT King of Hungary, Dalmatia, Rama, Croatia, Cumania, Bulgaria, Galicia and Lodomeria
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. "Charles I". Encyclopædia Britannica. http://www.britannica.com/eb/article-9022561/Charles-I#90690.hook. Retrieved on 2008-05-02. 
2 CONT
2 CONT    2.  Profile of Charles in "Medieval Lands" by Charles Cawley
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Profile of Maria in "Medieval Lands" by Charles Cawley
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Profile of Beatrix in "Medieval Lands" by Charles Cawley
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Profile of Władysław in "Medieval Lands" by Charles Cawley
2 CONT
2 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica, Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Martel_of_Anjou
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Martel of Anjou
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Martel (8 September 1271 – 12 August 1295) of the Angevin dynasty, also known as Charles I Martel, (French: Charles Martel d'Anjou, Italian: Carlo Martello) was the eldest son of king Charles II of Naples and Maria of Hung
2 CONC ary, the daughter of King Stephen V of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 18-year-old Charles Martel was set up by Pope Nicholas IV and the ecclesiastical party as the titular King of Hungary (1290–1295) as successor of his maternal uncle, the childless Ladislaus IV of Hungary against whom the Pope 
2 CONC had already earlier declared a crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT He never managed to govern the Kingdom of Hungary, where an agnate of the Árpád dynasty, his cousin Andrew III of Hungary actually ruled that period. Charles Martel was, however, successful in asserting his claims in parts of Croa
2 CONC tia, a kingdom then in personal union with Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Martel died young in Naples, during the lifetime of his parents. His son, Charles (or Charles Robert), would ultimately succeed where he had failed in winning the throne of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was apparently known personally to Dante: in the Divine Comedy the poet speaks warmly of and to Charles's spirit when they meet in the Heaven of Venus.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Klementia of Habsburg (d.1295), daughter of Rudolph I, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles I of Hungary (1288–1342), King of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrix (1290–1354, Grenoble), married on 25 May 1296 Jean II de La Tour du Pin, Dauphin du Viennois
2 CONT
2 CONT Clémence d'Anjou (February 1293 – 12 October 1328, Paris), married in Paris on 13 August 1315 Louis X of France
