2 CONT *'Friaries: Black friars of Pontefract', in A History of the County of York: Volume 3, ed. William Page (London, 1974), pp. 271-273. British History Online http://www.british-history.ac.uk/vch/yorks/vol3/pp271-273 [accessed 16 Apr
2 CONC il 2019]. “Edmund de Lacy, son of John de Lacy, Earl of Lincoln, and Margaret de Quincy, was born in 1227. He early attached himself to Richard Wych, and after the bishop's death, 3 April 1253, he determined to establish a house o
2 CONC f Friars Preachers on his own estates. With due deliberation he chose the town of Pontefract; and accompanied by many discreet men, both religious and secular, he went to the spot and laid the foundation stone with his own hand, s
2 CONC aying, 'To the honour of our Lady Mary, mother of God and Virgin, and of St. Dominic, confessor, to whose brethren I assign this place, and also of St. Richard, bishop and confessor, formerly my lord and dearest friend, I wishing 
2 CONC to found a church in this place lay the first stone !' Whereupon the stone immediately split into three parts, as though to proclaim approval of the choice of the three patron saints. This took place probably about 1256, some six 
2 CONC years before Richard Wych was formally canonized. (fn. 3) Edmund, dying on 22 July 1257, left his heart to be buried in the Dominican church of Pontefract. ...”
2 CONT * http://www.pontarc.org.uk/
