2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT HGCRP-Tomo I-pg. 175 -Tab. 17
2 CONT
2 CONT Constança de Hohenstaufen (1249-9 de Abril de 1302) foi princesa da Sicília e rainha de Aragão de 1275 até à sua morte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Era filha de Beatriz de Sabóia (1230-c.1259) e de Manfredo, rei da Sicília (1232 — 26 de Fevereiro de 1266). Era neta materna de Ana de Borgonha e do conde Amadeu IV de Sabóia e neta paterna da condessa Branca de Lancia e de Frede
2 CONC rico II de Hohenstauffen, Imperador do Sacro Império Romano Germânico, rei da Sicília, sendo Constança por isso descendente da poderosa família Hohenstaufen que reinou no Sacro Império Romano Germânico e na Sicília durante a Idade
2 CONC  Média.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Constantia of Sicily
2 CONT
2 CONT Constantia or Constance (1249 - 9 April 1302) was the Queen consort of Peter III of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the only daughter of Manfred of Sicily and his first wife Beatrice of Savoy. Her paternal grandparents were Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor and his mistress Bianca Lancia. Her maternal grandparents were Amadeus IV of Savo
2 CONC y and his first wife Anne of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was a daughter of Eudes III, Duke of Burgundy and his second wife Alice of Vergy.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 13 June 1262, Constantia married Prince Peter of Aragon, eldest son of James I of Aragon and his second wife Violant of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Manfred was killed in the Battle of Benevento (26 February 1266) while fighting against his rival Charles of Anjou, posthumous son of Louis VIII of France and Blanche of Castile. As his eldest daughter she could be considered his 
2 CONC heiress but did not pursue her claim at this time.
2 CONT
2 CONT James I died on 27 July 1276 and Peter succeeded to the throne. Constance became his Queen consort. With the War of the Sicilian Vespers (1282 - 1302), at first Peter and then their sons claimed the throne of Sicily in her right. 
2 CONC The War resulted in the partition of the Kingdom of Sicily to the "Kingdom of Trinacria" (the island of Sicily) under her descendants and the Kingdom of Naples under the descendants of Charles of Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter III died on 2 November 1285 and Constantia remained a widow. She ended her days as a nun in Barcelona.
2 CONT
