1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isabelle de France (1242-1271)
2 CONT
2 CONT Un article de Wikipédia, l'encyclopédie libre.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabelle_de_France_(1242-1271)
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle de France, née le 2 ou le 18 mars 1242, morte à Hyères le 27 avril 1271, comtesse de Champagne et reine de Navarre, fille de Louis IX, roi de France, et de Marguerite de Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elle épousa à Melun le 6 avril 1258 Thibaud II (1239 † 1270), roi de Navarre et comte de Champagne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thibaud II mourut au retour de la huitième croisade, et elle ne lui survécut que quelques mois. Ils n'avaient pas eu d'enfants.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabelle_of_France,_Queen_of_Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of France, Queen of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Isabelle of France, Queen of Navarre)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT For other people named Isabella of France, see Isabella of France (disambiguation).
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen consort of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure 	1255-1270
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Theobald II of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT House 	House of Capet
2 CONT
2 CONT House of Champagne
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Louis IX of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Margaret of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	2 March 1241
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	17 April 1271(aged 30)
2 CONT
2 CONT Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Provins, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of France (2 March 1241 – 17 April 1271) was a daughter Louis IX of France and Margaret of Provence. She was married to Theobald II of Navarre, eldest son of Theobald I of Navarre and Margaret of Navarre on 6 April 1255. 
2 CONC Isabelle became Queen consort of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents
2 CONT
2 CONT [show]
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 1 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 2 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 3 Ancestors
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 4 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT     * 5 See also
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was the second of eleven children and the eldest surviving child on the death of her sister Blanche in 1243. Of the eleven siblings, eight of them lived to adulthood. Isabella's brother was Philip III of France, who succe
2 CONC eded their father in 1270. Isabella's sisters included Blanche, Princess of Castile, Margaret, Duchess of Brabant and Agnes, Duchess of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella's paternal grandparents were Louis VIII of France and his wife Blanche of Castile, daughter of Alfonso VIII of Castile and Eleanor of England. Isabella's maternal grandparents were Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence an
2 CONC d Beatrice of Savoy. Eleanor of Provence was Isabella's aunt, this made her cousin of Edward I of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis IX wanted to make peace with Navarre so he married Isabella off to Theobald. Some written parental directives of Louis IX for his daughter survive, an unusual feature since the preparation of royal ladies for marriage was us
2 CONC ually entrusted to their mothers, not their fathers. The Archbishop of Rouen celebrated the marriage between Isabella and Theobald II, King of Navarre and Count of Champagne, on 6 April 1255 in Melun. The bridegroom was 18 and the
2 CONC  bride 13 years old. They are usually said to have remained childless, but a contemporary Spanish chronicle states that Isabella and Theobald produced and lost a child around 1258, and a rather later Spanish chronicler says that I
2 CONC sabella died in 1271, shortly after Theobald's death, "siendo prenada," presumably meaning that she died in childbirth. Contemporary reports of the couple's extreme modesty had it that they refused to disrobe completely when retir
2 CONC ing for the night, and that as she lay dying, Isabella ordered her attendants to see that her corpse was well shrouded so that nothing of it should become visible when it was boiled to remove the flesh from her bones. It seems lik
2 CONC ely, however, that the couple did not have a chaste or "white" marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella receiving a messenger from her father
2 CONT
2 CONT Together with her husband and her father, the very pious Isabella travelled to the Seventh Crusade in July 1270. Her father died there in August of the same year. Then, in December, Isabella's husband died of an epidemic while in 
2 CONC Sicily. After the deaths of both her father and husband, Isabella returned to France and lived in Provence until her death only two months later in 1271.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella is buried next to her husband in Provins.
2 CONT
