2 CONT ******************************************************************
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip III the Bold (in April 3, 1245 - October 5, 1285) reigned as King of France from 1270 to 1285. A member of the Capetian dynasty, he was born in Poissy, the son of Louis IX of France and of Marguerite Berenger of Provence (1
2 CONC 221 - 1295). 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of twenty-five he ascended to the throne. Indecisive, and dominated by the policies of his father, he followed the dictates of others, first of Pierre de la Broce and then of his uncle Charles I of Anjou, king of Naples
2 CONC . 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1285, the last year of his reign, Philippe made an unsuccessful attempt to annex the kingdom of Aragon. In the aftermath of this struggle, while retreating from Girona, Philippe III died on October 5, 1285 at Perpignan (in the 
2 CONC present-day département of Pyrénées-Orientales). He lies buried with his wife, Isabella of Aragon (1247 - 1271) in Saint Denis Basilica. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip III married (on 28 May 1262) Isabella of Aragon, daughter of James I of Aragon, and had the following children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT LOUIS - (1266 - May 1276) 
2 CONT
2 CONT PHILIPPE IV - (1268 - November 29, 1314) 
2 CONT
2 CONT CHARLES de Valois - (March 12, 1270 - December 16, 1325) 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Isabella, he married on August 21, 1274, 
2 CONT
2 CONT MARIE de  Brabant, daughter of 1276 - May 19, 1319) (married: Marguerite d' Artois) 
2 CONT
2 CONT BLANCHE - (1278 - March 19, 1305) (married: Rudolph III, duke of Austria) 
2 CONT
2 CONT MARGUERITE - (1282 - February 14, 1317) (married: Edward I of England) 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Philippe III's son, Philippe IV, succeeded him on the throne. 
