2 CONT Margaret of England
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen consort of Scots
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure	26 December 1251 – 26 February 1275
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse	Alexander III of Scotland m. 1251; dec. 1275
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, Queen of Norway Alexander, Prince of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT House	House of Plantagenet (by birth) House of Dunkeld (by marriage)
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	Henry III of England Mother	Eleanor of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT Born	29 September 1240 Windsor Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT Died	26 February 1275 (aged 34) Cupar Castle
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial	Dunfermline Abbey, Fife
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of England (29 September 1240 – 26 February 1275) was Queen of Scots as the wife of King Alexander III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret was the second child of King Henry III of England and his wife, Eleanor of Provence, and was born at Windsor Castle. Margaret’s first appearance in historical record comes when she was three years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married on 26 December 1251, when she was eleven years old, at York Minster, to King Alexander III of Scotland, who was ten years of age. The couple had three children:[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret (28 February 1261 – 9 April 1283), who married King Eric II of Norway. Alexander (21 January 1264 Jedburgh – 28 January 1284 Lindores Abbey). David (20 March 1272 – June 1281 Stirling Castle); buried in Dunfermline Abbey.
2 CONC  Margaret is said to have been unhappy in Scotland, and created some tensions between England and Scotland by writing to her family in England that she was poorly treated in Scotland.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT It was said that Margaret was responsible for the death of a young courtier, who reputedly had killed her uncle Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester.[3] While walking along the River Tay, she became annoyed with the young man.
2 CONC  She jokingly pushed him into the river, but he was swept to his death by a powerful current before anyone could help.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret died on 26 February 1275 at Cupar Castle, and was buried at Dunfermline Abbey, Fife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of England House of Plantagenet
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: 29 September 1240 Died: 26 February 1275
2 CONT
2 CONT Royal titles Vacant Title last held by Marie de Coucy Queen consort of Scotland 1251–1275 Vacant Title next held by Yolande de Dreux
2 CONT
2 CONT Scottish royal consorts
2 CONT
