1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====en.Wikipedia (29 Aug 2024): [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_of_Cornwall Richard of Cornwall]
2 CONT * '''Wikipedia continually updates.''' Thus, its not ideal to copy and paste quoted matter from Wikipedia to Geni's person-profile Overviews. 
2 CONT * '''Please be content with going to this webpage''' to scrutinized its merit. In its current state, ''it is among the best Wikipedia profile pages.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Wikitree: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Plantagenet-39 Richard (Plantagenet) of England (1209 - 1272)]
2 CONT * '''Please click the hyperlink above to review the Biography and rationales provided by the Wikitree curator.'''
2 CONT ** '''Born''' 5 Jan 1209 in Winchester Castle, Hampshire, England
2 CONT ** '''Son of''' John (Plantagenet) of England and Isabella (Angoulême) de Lusignan
2 CONT ** '''Husband of''' Isabel (Marshal) of Cornwall – married 30 Mar 1231 in Fawley, Buckinghamshire, England
2 CONT ** '''Husband of''' Sancha (Provence) of Cornwall – married 22 Nov 1243 in Westminster Abbey, Westminster, Middlesex, England
2 CONT ** '''Husband of''' Beatrix (Falkenburg) Countess of Cornwall — married 16 Jun 1269 in Kaiserslautern, Germany
2 CONT ** '''Died''' 2 Apr 1272 at age 63 in Berkhamstead Castle, Hertfordshire, England
2 CONT * '''NOTES'''
2 CONT ** 1. Richardson, Douglas. ''Royal Ancestry: A Study In Colonial And Medieval Families'', in 5 vols. (Salt Lake City, Utah, 2013). CORNWALL. Richard of England is #6 on page 298, Volume II. 
2 CONT ** 2. Cawley, Charles. ''Medieval Lands: A Prosopography of Medieval European Noble and Royal Families'', Online at Foundation for Medieval Genealogy Website. [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLAND,%20Kings%201066-1603.htm#Richa
2 CONC rddied1272 Profile for Richard, Earl of Cornwall].
2 CONT ** 3. M. Duffy. ''Royal Tombs of Medieval England'' 2003 p. 20, 22, 23, 72-74 
2 CONT ** 4. ''History of St. Mary’s Abbey of Buckfast in the county of Devon: A.D. 760–1906'', p. 7
2 CONT **  ''Calendar of Inquisitions Post Mortem and Other Analogous Documents preserved in the Public Record Office'' (H.M. Stationery Office, London, 1904) Vol. 1: Henry III. [https://archive.org/details/cu31924011387796/page/273/mode
2 CONC /1up Page 273-5]: #808. "'''Richard King of Almain''' ... Sir [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Plantagenet-40 Edmund], his son, aged 22 on the day of St. Stephen last, is his heir.”
2 CONT
2 CONT '''<nowiki>-------------------------------</nowiki>'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Medlands: '''''England, Earls 1138-1142''''' (20 June 2024) [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL1.htm#_Toc57456160 Earls of Cornwall 1227-1300]
2 CONT * RICHARD, son of JOHN King of England & his second wife Isabelle Ctss d'Angoulême (Winchester Castle 5 Jan 1209-Berkhamstead Castle, Herts 2 Apr 1272, bur Hayles Abbey, Gloucestershire). The Chronicle of Ralph of Coggeshall recor
2 CONC ds the birth in 1209 of "''Ricardus secundus filius regis''"[229]. 
2 CONT * Matthew Paris records that "''Isabel Anglorum regina''" gave birth in 1208 to “''Johanni regi filium legitimum... Ricardum''”[230]. 
2 CONT * He was designated Comte de Ponthieu before 14 Aug 1225. Created '''Earl of Cornwall''' 30 May 1227. In 1236, he was suggested as intermediary to negotiate an Anglo/imperial alliance proposed by Emperor Friedrich II but did not t
2 CONC ake up the post[231]. 
2 CONT * The Continuator of Florence of Worcester records that "''Ricardus comes Cornubiæ frater Henrici regis Angliæ''" went to Jerusalem in [1239][232]. 
2 CONT * Implementing a crusading vow taken in 1236, Richard arrived at Acre in Palestine 11 Oct 1240, re-established some order in the kingdom of Jerusalem, and left in May 1241[233]. 
2 CONT * He renounced the county of Ponthieu in Dec 1243. Richard was one of the proposed candidates to replace Heinrich Raspe as anti-king of Germany in 1247, and in 1250 may have been offered the Sicilian crown by Pope Innocent IV[234]
2 CONC . 
2 CONT * According to Matthew Paris, he refused the kingdom of Sicily on the Pope's terms, after which the Pope offered it to King Henry who accepted it on behalf of his son Edmund[235]. 
2 CONT * In Dec 1256, Richard was offered the German crown by the archbishops of Köln and Mainz to whom promises of payment of 8,000 marks had each been made, supported by Ludwig II Duke of Bavaria, who was betrothed to the daughter of K
2 CONC ing Henry III with a dowry of 12,000 marks, and subsequently by Otakar II King of Bohemia[236]. 
2 CONT * Richard accepted the offer before the English parliament, and sailed for Germany. The offer was confirmed by a limited election outside Frankfurt 13 Jan 1257, entry into the city being barred by Arnold Archbishop of Trier[237]. 
2 CONT * He was crowned '''RICHARD King of Germany''' on 17 May 1257 at Aachen Cathedral with his wife[238]. 
2 CONT * Although his rival Alfonso X King of Castile had initially enjoyed the support of France and the Pope, King Henry III's peaceful settlement with Louis IX King of France in Jan 1257 provided the basis for Pope Alexander IV to mod
2 CONC ify his own position and on 30 Apr 1259 he secretly invited Richard to Rome for his imperial coronation, although Richard was unable to leave England because of the deteriorating relations with the barons[239]. 
2 CONT * He was elected Roman senator in Apr 1261, with the support particularly of cardinals John of Toledo and Ottobono Fieschi[240]. 
2 CONT * Pope Urban IV was less sympathetic to his cause, obtaining a more powerful military ally to protect his position in Italy in Charles Comte d'Anjou. The dispute was unresolved by the Papal Bull ''Qui Cœlum'' dated 27 Aug 1263 whi
2 CONC ch found that both candidates for the German throne were king-elect. However, in 1266 Pope Clement IV appears to have favoured Richard's candidacy over a possible election of Konradin. After the latter's execution in 1268, Friedri
2 CONC ch von Meissen emerged as a new possible candidate for the German throne and Richard renewed his efforts to increase his authority in Germany, in particular by his third marriage to a relative of Engelbert Archbishop of Köln[241].
2 CONC
2 CONT * Richard suffered an apoplectic stroke in [Oct] 1271 which paralysed his left side and affected the balance of his mind[242]. 
2 CONT * The ''Annales Halesiensibus'' record the death "''1272 Berhamstede IV Non Apr'''” of "''rex Ricardus Alemanie''” and his burial "''apud Heles"[243]. 
2 CONT * The Continuator of Florence of Worcester records the death "IV Non Apr apud Berhamstede''" [1272] of "''Ricardus Alemannia rex''" and his burial "''apud monasterium de Hayles''" which he had founded[244]. 
2 CONT * A writ dated 5 Apr "56 Hen III", after the death of “Richard king of Almain”, records "Edmund his son, aged 22 on the day of St Stephen last, is his heir"[245]. 
2 CONT * '''m firstly''' (Fawley, Buckinghamshire 13 or 30 Mar 1231) as her second husband, '''ISABEL Marshal''', widow of GILBERT de Clare Earl of Hertford and Gloucester, daughter of WILLIAM Marshal Earl of Pembroke & his wife Isabella
2 CONC  de Clare (Pembroke Castle 9 Oct 1200-Berkhamstead Castle, Hertfordshire 15 or 17 Jan 1240, bur Beaulieu Abbey, Hampshire).  The ''Annals of Tewkesbury'' record the marriage “''III Kal Apr…apud Falle juxta Merlawe''” in 1231 of “'
2 CONC 'Ysabel comitissa Gloucestriæ''” and “''Ricardo comiti Cornubiæ, fratri Henrici regis Angliæ''”[226]. Her (second) marriage is recorded by Matthew Paris, who names her "''Ysabellam comitissam Gloverniæ''" sister of William Marshal
2 CONC l Earl of Pembroke, specifying that the marriage took place in April[227].  The ''Annales Cambriæ'' record the marriage in 1231 of "''Ricardus comes Cornubiæ''" and "''Isabellam cometissam Gloucestriæ''"[228].  The ''Annales Londo
2 CONC nienses'' record the marriage in 1231 of "''Ricardus frater regis''" and "''Isabellam comitissam Gloverniæ, relictam Gileberti de Clare''"[229]. The ''Annales Londonienses'' record the death in 1240 of "''comitissa Gloverniæ uxor 
2 CONC comitis Ricardi''" in childbirth[230].  The ''Annals of Tewkesbury'' record the death “''XVI Kal Feb…apud Berkhamstede''” in 1239 of “''Isabella comitissa Gloucestriæ et Hertfordiæ, Cornubiæ et Pictaviæ''” and her burial “''apud B
2 CONC ellum Locum Cisterciensis ordinis''”[231]. Matthew Paris records that she died of jaundice contracted in childbirth[232].  
2 CONT * '''m secondly''' (Contract 17 Jul 1242, Westminster Abbey 23 Nov 1243) '''SANCHA de Provence''', daughter of RAYMOND BERENGER IV Comte de Provence & his wife Béatrice de Savoie (Aix-en-Provence [1225]-Berkhamstead Castle, Buckin
2 CONC ghamshire 5 or 9 Nov 1261, bur Hayles Abbey, Gloucestershire).  "''Petrus de Sabaudia''" acted as proxy for the marriage of "''Richardo comite Cornubie''" and "''Sanccie filie…Raimundi Berengarii comitis provincie''", recorded in 
2 CONC a charter dated 17 Jul 1242[233].  The'' Annales Londonienses'' record the marriage "''die Sanctæ Ciciliæ''" in 1243 of "''Ricardus frater regis''" and "''Beatrix uxor comitis Provinciæ…filiam suam Cyntiam''"[234]. The marriage is
2 CONC  recorded and dated by Matthew Paris, who confirms the bride’s parentage[235]. '''She was crowned Queen of Germany with her husband 17 May 1257 at Aachen Cathedral'''[236]. The ''Annales Halesiensibus'' record the death "''1261 V 
2 CONC Id Nov…apud Berhamstede" of "Sanchia regina Alemannie''" and her burial "''apud Heiles''"[237].  The ''Annals of Osney'' record the death “''die Mercurii proximo ante festum beati Martini apud Berchamstede''” in 1261 of “''Schench
2 CONC ia regina Alemanniæ, soror Elianoræ reginæ Angliæ''” and her burial “''ad domum de Hayles''”[238].  
2 CONT * '''m thirdly''' (Kaiserslauten Stiftskirche 16 Jun 1269) '''BEATRIX [van Valkenburg],''' daughter of [DIRK [II] Heer van Valkenburg & his first wife Bertha van Limburg] (-17 Oct 1277, bur Oxford, church of the Franciscan Friars 
2 CONC Minor). The ''Annales Halesiensibus'' record the marriage in 1267 of ''"rex Riccardus Alemannie''" and "''Beatricem cuius avunculus fuit archiepiscopus Colonie''"[239].  The same relationship is hinted by the charter dated 13 Sep 
2 CONC 1271 in which "''Richardus…romanorum rex''" refers to "''E. Coloniensi archiepiscopi''" as "''affinis nostri''"[240].  '''The primary source which confirms her parentage more precisely has not yet been identified,although the refe
2 CONC rence to "Falkestan" (presumably in error for Valkenburg/Falkenburg) in the record of her death quoted below suggests that she must have been the daughter of Dietrich [II] von Valkenburg if Engelbert Archbishop of Köln was her unc
2 CONC le.'''  If this is correct, the date of her marriage suggests that she was the daughter of Dietrich [II]’s first wife, but this is not without doubt as other primary sources show marriages celebrated when the bride was aged 12 at 
2 CONC that time. This marriage was arranged to bolster Richard's support in Germany, through the bride's influential uncle Engelbert Archbishop of Köln[241]. The ''Annals of Osney'' record the death “''in vigilia Sancti Lucæ Evangelistæ
2 CONC ''” in 1277 of “''Beatrix de Falkestan, regina Alemanniæ, uxor regis Ricardi''” and her burial “''in ecclesia fratrum Minorum Oxoniæ''”[242].  
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Earl Richard & his first wife had four children:
2 CONT # '''JOHN''' (Marlow, Buckinghamshire 31 Jan 1232-Marlow, Buckinghamshire 22/23 Sep 1232, bur Reading Abbey).  The ''Annals of Tewkesbury'' record the birth “II Kal Feb” in 1231 of “''Ricardo comiti Cornubiæ filius…Johannes''”, an
2 CONC d in a later passage his death “''apud Merlawe X Kal Oct''” in 1232 and burial “''apud Radinges''”[264]. The ''Annals of Dunstable'' record that “''Johannes filius Ricardi comitis Cornubiæ''” was born and died in 1232[265]. 
2 CONT #  '''ISABELLA''' (Marlow, Buckinghamshire [8] Sep 1233-Marlow, Buckinghamshire 6 Oct 1234, bur Reading Abbey).  The ''Annals of Tewkesbury'' record the birth “''circa Nativitatem beatæ Virginis''” in 1233 of “''filia Ricardo comi
2 CONC ti Cornubiæ…Isabel''”, and in a later passage her death “''circa festum Sanctæ Fidis''” and burial “''juxta fratrem suum apud Radinges''”[266]. 
2 CONT # '''HENRY''' (Haughley Castle, Suffolk 2, 4 or 12 Nov 1235–murdered Viterbo, Italy 13 Mar 1271, bur 21 May 1271 Hayles Abbey, Gloucestershire). The ''Annals of Tewkesbury'' record the birth “''V Non Nov''” in 1235 of “''filius Ri
2 CONC cardo comiti Cornubiæ…Henricus''”[267]. His parentage is recorded by Matthew Paris when he reports that he was knighted in 1257[268]. His father hoped that Henry would inherit his rights to the throne of Germany.  Henry visited Ro
2 CONC me in 1270 on his journey back from the crusade[269]. The Continuator of William of Tyre records that he was murdered by his cousins Guy and Simon de Montfort at Viterbo[270], either in the Church of San Lorenzo or the Church of S
2 CONC an Silvestro or the Cathedral of St Niccolo. The ''Annales Londonienses'' record that "''Henricus filius regis Alemanniæ''" was murdered "''a Simone et Guidone filiis Simonis de Monteforti apud Biterbe, in Quadragesima"[271].  The
2 CONC  Annals of Osney record the burial “XII Kal Jun” in 1271 of “domini Henrici filii regis Ricardi Alemanniæ…in abbatia de Hayles''”[272].  '''m''' (Windsor Castle 5 or 15 May 1269, repudiated 1270) as her second husband, '''CONSTANC
2 CONC E''' de Moncada Ctss de Bigorre Vicomtesse de Marsan, widow of Infante don ALFONSO de Aragón, daughter of GASTON [VII] de Moncada Vicomte de Béarn & his wife Mathe Ctss de Bigorre ([1245/50]-26 Apr 1310). The marriage contract bet
2 CONC ween “''Gastonem vicecomitem Bearnensem dominum Montis-Cathani et Castriveteris...primogenitam nostram dominam Constantiam” and “Henrico regis Alemanniæ primogenito''” is dated 1268, and names “''domina Matha coniuge nostra''”[273
2 CONC ]. The ''Annales Londonienses'' record the marriage in 1269 of "''Ricardus rex Alemanniæ…Henricus eiusdem regis filius''" and "''filiam Gastuni de Byerne"''[274].  The ''Chronicle of Thomas Wykes'' records that “''Romanorum regis 
2 CONC primogenitus…Henricus” repudiated “uxore sua…filia…Gastonis de Biern''a” in 1270[275]. She married thirdly ([Jun/Aug] 1279) as his second wife, Aimon Comte de Genève. Edward I King of England recorded the marriage contract between
2 CONC  “''sa chere cosyne...Constance jadis femme de...Henri de Alemaine nostre cosyne''” and “''Edmun Genenue neuuz le esveke de Lengris et nostre cosyn''” by charter dated 1279[276]. 
2 CONT # NICHOLAS (b and d Berkhamstead Castle, Buckinghamshire 17 Jan 1240, bur Beaulieu Abbey, Hampshire).  '''The primary source which confirms his parentage has not been identified.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Earl Richard & his second wife had [three] children:
2 CONT # ['''daughter''' (-after 25 Nov 1256).  "''L…comes palatinus Rheni, dux Bawarie''" confirmed his betrothal "''cum filia fratris…regis Anglie''", or in case of impossibility "''cum filia sororis eiusdem''", by charter dated 25 Nov
2 CONC  1256[277].  This betrothal was arranged to confirm Duke Ludwig's agreement to support the candidature of Richard Earl of Cornwall as king of Germany, her dowry being 12,000 marks[278]. Duke Ludwig’s support for Earl Richard is co
2 CONC nfirmed in a charter dated 26 Nov 1256[279]. It is assumed that this daughter, concerning whom no other record has yet been found, was born from her father’s second marriage, as daughters from his first marriage would probably hav
2 CONC e been considered to old for betrothal at that date. '''Betrothed''' (Bacharach 26 Nov 1256) to '''LUDWIG II "der Strenge" Duke of Bavaria''', son of OTTO II "dem Erlauchten" Duke of Bavaria & his wife Agnes von Braunschweig (Heid
2 CONC elberg 13 Apr 1229–Heidelberg 2 Feb 1294, bur Kloster Fürstenfeld).] 
2 CONT # '''RICHARD''' (Wallingford Castle, Berkshire Jul 1246–Wallingford Castle, Berkshire 15 Aug 1246, bur Grove Mile). His birth and death are recorded by Matthew Paris, although he does not name him or give the places or precise dat
2 CONC es of the events[280]. '''The primary source which confirms his name has not been identified.''' 
2 CONT # '''EDMUND''' (Berkhamstead Castle, Buckinghamshire 26 Dec 1249–Ashridge Abbey, Herts 24/25 Sep or 1 Oct 1300, bur Hayles Abbey, Gloucestershire). His parentage is recorded by Matthew Paris who records his birth[281].  A writ dat
2 CONC ed 5 Apr "''56 Hen III''", after the death of "Richard king of Almain", records "Edmund his son, aged 22 on the day of St Stephen last, is his heir"[282]. He succeeded his father 1272 as Earl of Cornwall, invested 13 Oct 1272.  “'
2 CONC 'Edmundus filius quondam Ricardi regis Alemanniæ et comitis Cornubiæ''” donated property to Wallingford Church, Berkshire[283]. The ''Annals of Worcester'' record the death “Kal Oct” in 1300 of “''Edmundus comes Cornubiæ''”[284]. 
2 CONC  '''m''' (Ruislip Chapel, Middlesex 6 Oct 1272, divorced 1293/94) '''MARGARET de Clare''', daughter of RICHARD de Clare Earl of Gloucester and Hertford & his wife Matilda de Lacy ([1249/50]-either before 16 Sep 1312 or Feb 1313, b
2 CONC ur Chertsey Abbey, Surrey). The ''Chronica de Fundatoribus et Fundatione'' of Tewkesbury Abbey names “''Isabella primogenita, Margareta et Roysea''” as the three daughters of “''Ricardus de Clare secundus filius et hæres…Gilberti 
2 CONC et Isabellæ''” and his wife “''Matildem…filiam comitis Lincolniæ''”[285]. The Continuator of Florence of Worcester records the marriage "''in crastine Sanctæ Fidis''" 6 Oct [1272] of "''Eadmundus de Alemannia comes Cornubiæ''" and
2 CONC  "''Margaretam sororem Gileberti comitis Gloverniæ''"[286].  
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Mistress (1): [JOANNA [de Vautort]...
2 CONT # '''Earl Richard had [one possible illegitimate child by Mistress (1)]:'''
2 CONT ## 8  ['''JOAN''' [de Cornwall]. Her possible parentage is indicated by the passage from Sir William Pole, quoted above under her supposed mother. The same passage also confirms her marriage.  William Pole also records her descend
2 CONC ants (no sources cited)[287]. As noted above, none of Pole’s information concerning Joanna and her supposed mother has been verified. ''' m RICHARD [I] Champernon''', son of HENRY Champernon of Clyst Champernon & his wife —.] 
2 CONT # '''Earl Richard had one illegitimate child by [Mistress (1)/Mistress (2)]:'''
2 CONT ## 9. '''RICHARD de Cornwall''' (-killed siege of Berwick [31 Mar] 1296). '''The primary source which confirms his parentage, in particular the identity of his mother, has not been found.''' – [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGL
2 CONC ISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL1.htm#RichardCornwalldiedafter1280B See below].
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Earl Richard had one illegitimate son by Mistress (3):
2 CONT # '''WALTER de Cornwall''' (-1313). Kennett records that Edmund Earl of Cornwall “gave lands to Walter de Cornwall his base brother” (undated)[288]. '''The primary source which confirms his parentage has not been identified.''' 
2 CONT
