2 CONT REF:  "Falls the Shadow" Sharon Kay Penman: During Simon de Montfort'sbarons war, March 31, 1264 Londoners riot (London was very pro deMontfort), burn townhouses of the hated William de Lusignan, alsoprominent Royalist Lord Philip
2 CONC  Basset, also Richard of Cornwall'sWestminster manor, & his beloved Isleworth manor 7 miles away.  Richardovernight goes from trying to mediate a solution to, like Edward,ardently desiring a battlefield confrontation.
2 CONT Richard was not popular with his brother Henry III's subjects as he wasintelligent, pragmatic, had business acumen, & a lack of rancor; thesequalities were disdained in favor of reckless courage, battlefieldprowess, & open-handed 
2 CONC generosity, which was expected of Lords. Richard'scourage was never suspect; but he was not an innovative nor brilliantmilitary commander. At Battle of Lewes on 14 May 1264, Richard commandedthe left flank of the Royalist forces. 
2 CONC  After Prince Edward left thebattle to pursue with unwholesome savagery the rebel Londoner flank ofMontfort's forces, Montfort, keeping a reserve force (a tactic unheardof) came in & attacked Henry & obliterated his force; Henry f
2 CONC leed to thepriory.  Gilbert de Clare's center column forces met Richard's & took theday.  Richard with his son Edmund fled into a windmill.  When de Clare'sforces captured the, they jeered "come out king of the millers". JohnGiffa
2 CONC rd's squire was the one who officially accepted Richard's royalsurrender, adding to Richard's discomposure (that he had surrendered to asquire rather than a knight).  Upon his presentation to Simon deMontfort, Simon made the lad, 
2 CONC John Befs of Tewkesbury, a knight.
