1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beatrice_of_Hungary,_Dauphine_of_Viennois <br> 
2 CONT '''Beatrice of Hungary, dauphine of Viennois'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice of Hungary (1290 – 1343 or 1354)[1] was the elder daughter of Charles Martel of Anjou and his wife Klementia of Habsburg. She was a member of the Capetian House of Anjou.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice's maternal grandparents were Rudolph I of Germany and his first wife, Gertrude of Hohenburg. Her paternal grandparents were Charles II of Naples and his wife, Maria Arpad of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice was the second of three children. Her siblings were Charles I of Hungary and Clementia of Hungary. Charles claimed his father's succession wrights on Hungary and her sister, Clementia married Louis X of France and was mot
2 CONC her of John I of France, the baby King.
2 CONT
2 CONT In total, Beatrice had thirty five maternal cousins and at least forty two paternal cousins. This was due to her grandparents, maternal and paternal having many children.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Life
2 CONT
2 CONT When Beatrice was just six years old, in 1296, at Naples, she was married to John II of Viennois, who was ten years her senior. The couple had two sons
2 CONT
2 CONT >#    Guigues VIII (1309 † 1333), dauphin of Viennois.
2 CONT >#    Humbert II (1312 † 1355), dauphin of Viennois.
2 CONT
2 CONT When her husband died in 1319, Beatrice became a nun at Cîteaux. She remained here until 1340, when she transferred to the Abbaye des Ayes. Her son founded for her the convent of Saint-Just dans le Royannais.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice died in 1343. She died in the convent which her sons had founded for her. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HUNGARY.htm#Beatrixdied1354
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.         BEATRIX of Hungary (Naples after 25 Mar 1290-Convent of Saint-Just, Royannais [1354]).  The Aymari Rivalli De Allobrogibus records the marriage of "Joannes Delphinus" and "Beatricem, Caroli secundi Siciliæ et Hierusalem
2 CONC  regis neptem, Caroli regis Ungariæ filiam", and her dowry "Serrum Castrum et ius sibi in Aragrandi et Bassamolio" agreed by contract "III Non Jun" in 1298[1013].  The marriage contract between "Karolum secundum…regem Hierusalem e
2 CONC t Siciliæ…pro Dom. Beatrice minore septennio nepte sua primogenita bonæ memoriæ…principis Dom. Karoli primogeniti eius Hungariæ regis" and "Dom. Humberti Dalphini Viennen. et Albonis comitis dominique de Turre ac Joannis Dalphini 
2 CONC primogeniti sui" is dated 25 May 1296[1014].  When her husband died, she became a nun at Cîteaux.  Abbesse de Val de Bressieu, diocese of Grenoble until 15 Feb 1340, when she transferred to the Abbaye des Ayes.  Her son founded fo
2 CONC r her the convent of Saint-Just dans le Royannais.  The Aymari Rivalli De Allobrogibus records the burial of "Beatrix Humberti mater" at "cœnobio Sancti Justi"[1015].  Valbonnais quotes accounts dated May 1355 relating to the jewe
2 CONC lry of "l´ancienne Dauphine", concluding that they relate to Beatrix of Hungary and that she must therefore have died in 1354[1016].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (contract Naples 25 May 1296) JEAN de la Tour du Pin et de Coligny, son of HUMBERT de la Tour du Pin et de Coligny, Dauphin de Viennois & his wife Anne Dauphine de Viennois [Bourgogne-Capet] (before [1277]-Pont de Sorgues,
2 CONC  Avignon 4 Mar 1319, bur Grenoble église Saint-André).  He succeeded his father in 1307 as JEAN II Dauphin de Viennois, Comte d'Albon. 
