2 CONT Maria's coronation
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Brabant (13 May 1254, Leuven – 10 January 1321, Murel), Queen consort of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a daughter of Henry III, Duke of Brabant 
2 CONT
2 CONT and Adelaide of Burgundy, daughter of Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria became the second wife of Philip III of France on 21 August 1274 at Vincennes. She was crowned at Ste-Chapelle on 24 June 1275. She was the mother of Louis d'Evreux, 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche, wife of Rudolph I of Bohemia, 
2 CONT
2 CONT and Marguerite (wife of Edward I of England).
2 CONT
2 CONT  Due to her influence Philip became involved in the affairs of the Angevins and in hostilities with the Kingdom of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the death of her husband the King in 5 October 1285, she withdrew to a convent near Meulan. She died there 36 years later, and was buried in the Cordeliers convent in Paris.
2 CONT
