1 OCCU Duchess of Athens 1308-1311, Countess of Brienne, Countess consort of Joanna; Duchess consort of Athens (1308 - 1311), Countess consort of Brienne; Duchess consort of Athens; AKA "Joanna of Châtillon", Countess consort of Brienne
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Joanna of Châtillon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Jeanne de Châtillon)
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna of Châtillon (or Joan, French: Jeanne; (c. 1285- 16 January 1354) was the wife of Walter V of Brienne (1305) and duchess of Athens by marriage (1308–1311). She was the daughter of Gaucher de Chatillon, Constable of France a
2 CONC nd Isabelle de Dreux. Her paternal grandparents were Gaucher IV de Chatillon and Isabelle de Villehardouin. Her maternal grandparents were Robert de Dreux, Viscount of Chateaudun and Isabelle de Villebéon.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1305, Joanna married Walter V of Brienne, the son of Hugh of Brienne, Count of Brienne and Lecce, and Isabella de la Roche. The marriage produced two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter VI of Brienne, Count of Brienne, Lecce, and Conversano, titular Duke of Athens (died 19 September 1356), married firstly Margharita of Anjou-Tarent, and secondly, Jeanne de Brienne. His children by both wives died young, so
2 CONC  his titles, possessions, and claims were inherited by his sister Isabella.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Brienne, Countess of Lecce and Conversano, claimant to the Duchy of Athens, and the Kingdom of Jerusalem (1306- 1360), married Walter of Enghien, by whom she had eleven children. Mary, Queen of Scots and Queen consort 
2 CONC Elizabeth Woodville were her direct descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 15 March 1311, her husband Walter was killed in the Battle of Halmyros against the Catalan Company. Joanna may have tried to hold the Acropolis of Athens against them, but eventually surrendered it.[1] She returned with her son
2 CONC  Walter II to France, though her retainers continued to possess Argos and Nauplia under Walter of Foucherolles.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1318, Joanna and her father sent a request to the Republic of Venice seeking money and ships for knights and infantry to Negroponte or Nauplia. The request, however, was refused, as the Briennist vassals in Greece had tur
2 CONC ned to the Catalans in the meanwhile. As late as the next year, however, Walter of Foucherolles was still commanding his vessels in the Argolid to remain loyal to Joanna and the young Walter. By constant petition to the King of Na
2 CONC ples, the King of France, and the Pope, Joanna kept her claim to Athens alive for her son until he was old enough to campaign for his rights in the Aegean. In January 1321, Philip V of France mediated the suit brought against her 
2 CONC by her own son, who was suing for the payment of some of his father's great debt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna herself retained her ducal title until her death. Her tomb, in the church of Saint Jacobin in Troyes has the inscription Duchess d'Athènes.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT ^ The Libre de los fechos indicates that la muller del dicho duch [Walter] qui era muerto con un su fiio, & alli [there, meaning Athens] se defendió por un tiempo.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]References
2 CONT
2 CONT Setton, Kenneth M. (general editor) A History of the Crusades: Volume II — The Later Crusades, 1189 – 1311. Robert Lee Wolff and Harry W. Hazard, editors. University of Wisconsin Press: Milwaukee, 1969.
2 CONT
2 CONT Setton, Kenneth M. (general editor) A History of the Crusades: Volume III — The Fourteenth and Fifteenth Centuries. Harry W. Hazard, editor. University of Wisconsin Press: Madison, 1975.
2 CONT
2 CONT Setton, Kenneth M. Catalan Domination of Athens 1311–1380. Revised edition. Variorum: London, 1975.
