1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Mikołaj Mały''' - '''książę ziębicki''' w latach 1341-1358. [http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miko%C5%82aj_Ma%C5%82y_Zi%C4%99bicki Wikipedia PL]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicholas_the_Small
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas the Small (Mikołaj Mały) (1322/1327 – 23 April 1358) was a Duke of Ziębice from 1341 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born
2 CONT 1322/1327
2 CONT Died
2 CONT 23 April 1358
2 CONT Noble family
2 CONT Silesian Piasts
2 CONT Spouse(s)
2 CONT Agnes Krušina of Lichtenberg
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT Ludmila [Anna?, Elisabeth?]
2 CONT Bolko III of Ziębice
2 CONT Henry I of Ziębice
2 CONT Agnes
2 CONT Guta
2 CONT Katharina
2 CONT Father
2 CONT Bolko II of Ziębice
2 CONT Mother
2 CONT Bonne or Guta/Judith of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the only son of Duke Bolko II of Ziębice and his wife Guta. He was born between 1322 and 1327. He had a sister, Małgorzata, but he did not know whether she was older or younger.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life	Edit
2 CONT Nicholas received a good education in the Cistercian monastery of Heinrichau [pl] (Henryków). After his father's death on 11 June 1341, he took control of the government of his domains. He almost immediately went to Prague, where 
2 CONC on 24 August he paid homage to King John of Bohemia. The huge debts left by his father forced Nicholas on 14 October 1343 to promise not to sell any of his lands without John's consent. In addition, if he did sell, the King had th
2 CONC e first option to buy. He also agreed that, if the male line of the Dukes of Ziębice became extinct, the duchy would be annexed to the Bohemian Kingdom on condition that appropriate dowries be provided to the late Duke's daughters
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Financial problems as early as 1343 forced Nicholas to sell the district of Zobten (Sobótka) to his cousin Bolko II the Small for 1,000 grzywnas of silver. Three years later, and for the same amount, he sold Frankenstein (Ząbkowic
2 CONC e) and the monastery of Kamenz (Kamieniec Ząbkowicki) to the Bohemian magnate Heinrich von Haugwitz. A combination of favorable circumstances enabled him to sell the same land in 1348, this time definitively, to Emperor Charles IV
2 CONC  for 6,000 grzywnas. Finally, in 1350 Nicholas sold the town of Wąsów to the Bishop of Breslau, Przecław of Pogorzela. As a result, of the vast domains he inherited from his father, Nicholas retained only Münsterberg, Strehlen (St
2 CONC rzelin), Kanth (Kąty Wrocławskie) and Patschkau (Paczków).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1355, Nicholas took part in the coronation of Charles IV as Holy Roman Emperor in Rome. After a long stay in Prague, he returned to his duchy a year later. The Duke then went on a pilgrimage to Palestine between 1357 and 1358.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas died in Hungary on 23 April 1358 during his return trip. His body was brought to the duchy and buried in the monastery in Heinrichau.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT By 23 October 1343, Nicholas married Agnes (d. 16 July 1370), daughter of Hynek (also named Hermann or Hajman) Krušina z Lichtemburka, a Bohemian magnate.[1] They had six children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ludmila [Anna?, Elisabeth?] (b. ca. 1344 – d. ca. 1372), married by 1360 to Duke Siemowit III of Masovia
2 CONT Bolko III (b. ca. 1348 – d. 13 June 1410)
2 CONT Henry I (b. ca. 1350 – d. aft. 8 August 1366)
2 CONT Agnes (b. ca. 1351/53? – d. October 1434), Abbess of St. Klara, Strehlen
2 CONT Guta (b. ca. 1354? – d. 2 September 1413), Abbess of St. Klara, Breslau (bef. 1380)
2 CONT Katharina (b. ca. 1355/58? – d. ca. 1396), a nun in St. Klara, Strehlen.
2 CONT In his will, Nicholas left to his wife the regency of his lands on behalf of their infant sons and effective rule over Strehlen.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Marek, Miroslav. "Genealogy of the House of Krušina z Lichtemburka". Genealogy.EU.[self-published source].[better source needed] In some sources, Agnes is erroneously named the daughter of "Herman, Landgrave of Leuchtenberg", who
2 CONC  historically never existed. Elisabeth Krušina z Lichtemburk, Agnes' older sister, married Boček z Kunštátu, another Bohemian magnate, and they were the paternal great-grandparents of George of Poděbrady, later King of Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT External links
2 CONT Cawley, Charles, SILESIA, Medieval Lands database, Foundation for Medieval Genealogy
2 CONT Marek, Miroslav. "Complete Genealogy of the House of Piast". Genealogy.EU.
2 CONT Genealogical database by Herbert Stoyan
2 CONT Genealogy of the Dukes of Ziębice
