2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY Marguerite Alleman was the daughter of Humbert Alleman, seigneur d'Aubonne, and Agnès de Joinville. She became the first wife of Rodolphe, comte de Gruyère, eldest son of Pierre IV, comte de Gruyère, and Catherine de La 
2 CONC Tour. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A charming legend is associated with Marguerite's efforts to conceive an heir. According to the story, she was so sadly afflicted that she had borne no heir, that she had no longer joy in her castle, no comfort with her lord. Vain
2 CONC ly journeying to distant shrines, as vainly invoking the aid of sorcerers and magicians, she went one day, clad as one of her poor subjects, to pray in the chapel at the foot of the Gruyère hill. There, as the November day was clo
2 CONC sing, poor Jean the cripple, well known through the country, came also to tell his beads. Very simple and kindly was poor Jean, with always the same blessing for those who gave him food or mocked him with cruel jeers. Perceiving i
2 CONC n the shadow a poor woman sadly weeping, he gave her all his day's begging, a piece of black bread with a morsel of coarse cheese, repeating his usual blessing, 'May God and our Lady grant thee all thy noble heart desires'. That e
2 CONC vening, at the close of a feast laid for Rodolphe's companions after the day's hunt, Marguerite offered each knight a little of this bread and cheese, with a moving story of poor Jean and a prayer that all should wish what her hea
2 CONC rt so long and vainly had desired. Nine months later, so concludes the tale, a son and heir was born to Marguerite. He was also named Rodolphe, and would have progeny. Marguerite died about 1363.
