2 CONT Hugh III, Duke of Burgundy From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh III of Burgundy (1142 – August 25, 1192, in Acre) was duke of Burgundy between 1162 and 1192. Hugh was the eldest son of duke Eudes II and Marie De Champagne, daughter of Theobald and Mathilda of Carinthia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The rule of Hugh III marked the ending of a period of relative peace in the duchy of Burgundy. Hugh was a belligerent man and soon was involved in conflicts against king Louis VII of France over their borders. When Philip Augustus
2 CONC  succeeded Louis in 1180, Hugh seized the opportunity and forced several men to change alliance to Burgundy. Philip II was not happy with the loss of his vassals and invaded the duchy, besieging Chatillon. The town fell and with i
2 CONC t, its garrison, commanded by Eudes, Hugh's heir. A peace was negotiated and Hugh had to pay a high ransom for his son and give up ambitions over French territory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh then turned his energies to the Holy Land, embarking in the Third Crusade in the retinue of Philip II. He was the most trusted ally of Richard, the Lionheart and fought with him against Saladin. When Philip returned to France
2 CONC , he left Hugh in charge of the French troops. Hugh played a major role in the victory of the battle of Arsuf (September 7, 1191) and the conquest of Acre, where he died in the following year.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1187, Hugh transferred the capital of Burgundy to Dijon, and endeavoured to turn the city into a major commercial centre.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married twice:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Firstly, in 1165, to Alice of Lorraine (1145-1200), daughter of Matthias I, Duke of Lorraine; he repudiated her in 1183.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Secondly, in 1183, to Béatrice of Albon (1161-1228), Countess of Albon and Dauphine of Viennois, daughter of Guigues, Count of Albon and Dauphin of Viennois.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his first marriage, he produced:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Eudes III (1166 - 1218), his successor in the Duchy
2 CONT     * Alexander (1170 - 1205), Lord of Montagu, founder of the line of Seigneurs of Montagu
2 CONT     * Douce (1175 - c.1219), married in 1196 Simon of Semur (d.1219), Lord of Luzy
2 CONT     * Alix (b.1177), married Béraud VII, Lord of Mercoeur
2 CONT
2 CONT By his second marriage, he produced:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * André Guigues VI (1184 - 1237), Dauphin of Viennois
2 CONT     * Mahaut (1190 - 1242), married in 1214 John I, Count of Châlon and Auxonne (1190 - 1267)
2 CONT     * Anne (1192 - 1243), married in 1222 Amadeus IV (1197 - 1253), Count of Savoy
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References
2 CONT
2 CONT     * The Royal Ancestry Bible Royal Ancestors of 300 Colonial American Families by Michel L. Call (chart 2718) ISBN 1-933194-22-7
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] See also
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Dukes of Burgundy family tree
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Wikipedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_III,_Duke_of_Burgundy Hugh III, Duke of Burgundy From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search
