1 NOTE     FGRA
1 NOTE levde 1168.
1 NOTE Judith of Babenberg (c. late 1110s/1120 - after 1168), (Jutta, sometimes called Julitta or Ita in Latin sources), was a daughter of Agnes of Germany and her second husband Leopold III of Austria. The chronicler Otto of Freising wa
2 CONC s one of her older brothers; Conrad III of Germany her half-brother. Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor was her nephew.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married c. 1133 William V, Marquess of Montferrat, by whom she bore at least eight children. Of their five sons, four became prominent in the affairs of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and of Byzantium:
2 CONT
2 CONT William Longsword, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon, father of Baldwin V of Jerusalem 
2 CONT Conrad, King of Jerusalem 
2 CONT Boniface, his successor to Montferrat and founder of the Kingdom of Thessalonica 
2 CONT Frederick, Bishop of Alba 
2 CONT Renier, married into the Byzantine imperial family 
2 CONT and three daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes, who married Count Guido Guerra III Guidi of Ventimiglia. The marriage was annulled on grounds of childlessness before 1180, when Guido remarried, and Agnes entered the convent of Santa Maria di Rocca delle Donne. 
2 CONT Adelasia or Azalaïs (d. 1232), who married Manfred II, marquess of Saluzzo, c. 1182, and was regent for her grandson Manfred III. 
2 CONT An unidentified daughter, who married Albert, marquess of Malaspina. 
2 CONT She was still living in 1168, but seems to have died before her husband went to the Kingdom of Jerusalem after their grandson Baldwin's coronation as King of Jerusalem in the 1180s.
