2 ADDR
1 OCCU amante dell’Imperatore Federico II e madre di Re Manfredi, detta moglie di Federico II “in articulo mortis”.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MONFERRATO,%20SALUZZO,%20SAVONA.htm#BiancaLanciaMistrEmpFriedrich
2 CONT
2 CONT c)         BIANCA Lancia ([1205/12][804]-[1233/34]).  The Thomas Tusci Gesta Imperatorum et Pontificum refers to the mother of "Manfredus" as "sorore marchionis Lancee…filia domne Blanca"[805].  The Historia Sicula of Bartolomeo d
2 CONC i Neocastro names "domina Blanca…de Lancea de Lombardia" as the fifth wife of "dominus Fridericus secundus…Romanorum…imperator"[806].  The Historia Manuscrita Mediolanensi records that Emperor Friedrich II married "filiam Bonefaci
2 CONC i Castellani" by whom he fathered "Regem Manfredum"[807].  A "confirmatio matrimonii in articulo mortis" in [1233/34] is recorded by Matthew of Paris, in the form of a declaration of her son Manfred[808].  The Cronica Fratris Sali
2 CONC mbene de Adam refers to the mother of "Manfredus…filius Friderici" as "marchionis Lancee neptis", specifying that she married the Emperor "in obitu"[809].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mistress''' ([1231/34]) of Emperor FRIEDRICH II, son of Emperor HEINRICH VI & his wife Constance of Sicily (Iesi, Ancona 26 Dec 1194-Castel Fiorentino near Lucera, Foggia, 13 Dec 1250, bur 25 Feb 1251 Palermo Cathedral). 
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT - http://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/bianca-lancia_%28Dizionario-Biografico%29/
2 CONT
2 CONT - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bianca_Lancia
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly born in Agliano in Piedmont, where her ancestors of the Ghibelline Lancia (or Lanza) noble family, so-called since Manfred I (fl. 1160–1214) had been lancifer pikeman of Emperor Frederick Barbarossa, served as margraves. 
2 CONC There is no source which definitively states who her parents were, historians have offered three theories that she was a granddaughter of Manfred I Lancia:
2 CONT
2 CONT #as daughter of his daughter (maybe also named Bianca), by a son (name unknown) of Guglielmo di Moncucco and Belda di Agliano.
2 CONT #as daughter of his son Bonifacio di Agliano by an unknown wife.
2 CONT #'''as daughter of his son Manfred II, possible from a marriage to a lady called Bianca, of the Maletta family.''' '''If she was a daughter of Manfred II, she had seven siblings: four brothers (Manfred III, Guglielmo, Galvano and 
2 CONC Frederick) and three sisters (Beatrice, Agnes and Isabella). '''
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT Bianca Von Hohenstaufen (born Lanza) MyHeritage Family Trees Parents: Bonifacio Ier Lanza, Constanza Maletta Brother: Galvano Lanza Husband: Friedrich Ii Von Hohenstaufen Son: Manfred Von Hohenstaufen 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT Bianca Lancia d'Agliano (also called Beatrice and Blanche); was an Italian noblewoman, who was the mistress and later wife of emperor Frederick II of Hohenstaufen, although the marriage, conducted while she was on her deathbed, wa
2 CONC s considered non-canonical.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Relationship with Frederick II
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Marriage and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Death
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 References
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Born ca. 1200/1210 in Agliano Terme, Bianca was a member of the Lancia (or Lanza) family of Piedmont, so-called since her grandfather Manfred I had been lancifer of emperor Frederick Barbarossa. There is no source which definitive
2 CONC ly states who her parents were, historians have offered three theories:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.That she was a granddaughter of Manfred I Lancia, as daughter of his daughter (maybe named Bianca), by a son (name unknown) of Guglielmo di Moncucco and Belda di Agliano.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.That she was a granddaughter of Manfred I Lancia, as daughter of his son Bonifacio di Agliano by an unknown wife.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.That she was a granddaughter of Manfred I Lancia, as daughter of his son Manfred II, possible from a marriage to a lady called Bianca, of the Maletta family.
2 CONT
2 CONT If she was a daughter of Manfred II, she had seven siblings: four brothers (Manfred III, Guglielmo, Galvano and Frederick) and three sisters (Beatrice, Agnes and Isabella). Apparently, Bianca would be the third child and first bor
2 CONC n daughter.[2][3] But this is only one of three competing theories.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Relationship with Frederick II
2 CONT
2 CONT Bianca lived most of her life in the Castle of Boro, the ancestral residence of the Lancia family. She met Frederick II, who was then married to Yolande of Jerusalem, in 1225 at Agliano, near Asti. Thenceforth, it is said, the two
2 CONC  maintained a romantic relationship.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to some historians, Bianca was the only true love of Frederick's life; others consider this a romantic exaggeration. It was certainly beneficial to the interests of the Lancia family, who were favoured by the Emperor wit
2 CONC h political posts in Italy (Manfredo III was appointed Imperial Vicar of the Holy Roman Empire's northern Italian territories and Podesta of Alessandria, Milan and Chieri; Galvano became Imperial Vicar of Tuscany, Podesta of Padov
2 CONC a, Prince of Salerno, Count of Fondi and Grand Marshal of Sicily; and Frederick was appointed Count of Squillace and Viceroy of Apulia). Nonetheless, the relationship of Bianca and Frederick was the longest to all the affairs of t
2 CONC he Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Queen Isabella of England, Frederick's third wife, in 1241, he endowed Bianca with the castle of Monte Sant'Angelo, located in the cities of Vieste and Siponto. By the terms of the will of William II of Sicily, 
2 CONC the castle was the traditional dowry of the Sicilian queens consort.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bianca died either in the castle of Paternò or the castle of Gioia del Colle.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage and Children
2 CONT
2 CONT The Chronicle of Salimbene di Adam, and also Matthew of Paris claimed that a "confirmatio matrimonii in articulo mortis" ("marriage ceremony in the moments of death") took place between Bianca and Frederick when she was dying. Bia
2 CONC nca, apparently, desired the marriage for the salvation of her soul and the safety of their children's future.[4][5] This marriage however was not deemed canonical by the Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick and Bianca had three children together:
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance (1230-1307), who married the Emperor of Nicea, John Vatatzes, and thereupon her name changed to Anna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Manfred (1232-1266), who succeeded his father as ruler of Sicily (initially as regent, before usurping the throne for himself).
2 CONT
2 CONT Violante (1233-1264), who married Richard Sanseverino, Count of Caserta.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Death
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not known exactly when she died. There are several conflicting reports. Although the Medlands database gives 1233/1234, Medlands is not reliable for unsourced claims. This date lingers in various reference works but appears 
2 CONC based only on the assumption that she died after the birth of her last child, and before the marriage of her husband to his next wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are credible reports that she married Frederick while she was on her deathbed, and that her son was 12 at this time. Bianca may have died a short while before the wedding of her daughter to Ioannes Batatzes in 1244.[6] One s
2 CONC ource states that she died 20 years after the start of her relationship with Frederick, however the same source states that she died 20 years before events that were occurring in 1256. All of these cannot be simultaneously true.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ "The mother of Manfred, King of Sicily, was a noblewoman named Blanche, of the family of the Marquis of Lancia and Loreto, in southern Lombardy. She was the daughter of Boniface, Count of Agliano (near Asti), one of the princi
2 CONC pal fiefs of Lancia. Even contemporaries were unsure of the exact degree of her relationship with the Marquis of Lancia, who had accompanied Emperor Frederick II to the East. They could not tell if she were his niece or his grandd
2 CONC aughter." [Reference: Source: Paul Crawford, The 'Templar of Tyre': Part III of the 'Deeds of the Cypriots' (Crusade Texts in Translation 6) (2003), pg. 18, footnote 4]. Reference provided by Douglas Richardson on soc.genealogy.me
2 CONC dieval 22 January 2008
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.^ Monferrato MONFERRATO, SALUZZO, SAVONA
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.^ Genealogy of the Lancia family
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.^ Matthew of Paris, Mon. Germ. Hist. Scriptores XXVIII pp. 360-361
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.^ Cronica Fratris Salimbene di Adam, Ordinis Minorem, MGH SS XXXII p.349
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.^ By this time, Frederick II's longtime enemy, the Duke Frederick of Austria became his most important ally, and, by reforce this new alliance, were make plans to a marriage between the Emperor and the teenager niece of the Duke
2 CONC , Gertrude. She refused the marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References
2 CONT
