1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''NOTE:''' Medlands is unsure of his enumeration in succession by using brackets: [III]. FrancoGene shows it as IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Medlands '''''(1 Jun 2021)''''': [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BURGUNDY%20Kingdom.htm#GuillaumeIIIMacondied1155 Burgundy Kingdom Nobility]
2 CONT * '''Etienne I & his wife had four children: ''' 
2 CONT ** c)  GUILLAUME de Mâcon (-27 Sep 1155). The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "comes Renaldus [de Burgundia] fratrem comitem Guilelmum" when recording his marriage[89].  '''He succeeded in 1120 as Comte de Mâcon. Com
2 CONC te d'Auxonne 1127.''' Guillaume enjoyed some influence in Vienne as shown by the following document: Konrad III King of Germany confirmed the rights of the archbishop and chapter over Vienne, specifying that he removed (“''alienam
2 CONC us''”) “''Willelmum Matisconensem comitem''” [Guillaume [III] Comte de Mâcon] from “''predicta urbe''”, by charter dated 6 Jan 1146[90].  He joined the Second Crusade 1147-1149.  Regent of Bourgogne-Comté for his niece, he attempt
2 CONC ed to deprive her of her inheritance.   
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Medlands '''''(8 Feb 2023)''''': [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BURGUNDIAN%20NOBILITY.htm#GuillaumeIIIMacondied1155B Burgundy Duchy Nobility]
2 CONT * '''GUILLAUME [III] de Mâcon''', son of ETIENNE [I] "Tête-Hardie" Comte de Mâcon [ Bourgogne-Comté ] & his wife Beatrix of Lotharingia (before 1102-27 Sep 1155). 
2 CONT * The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "''comes Renaldus [de Burgundia] fratrem comitem Guilelmum''" when recording his marriage[169].  
2 CONT * '''He succeeded in 1120 as Comte de Mâcon. Comte d'Auxonne 1127.'''
2 CONT * He joined the Second Crusade 1147-1149.  
2 CONT * Guillaume enjoyed some influence in Vienne as shown by the following document: Konrad III King of Germany confirmed the rights of the archbishop and chapter over Vienne, specifying that he removed (“''alienamus''”) “''Willelmum 
2 CONC Matisconensem comitem''” [Guillaume [III] Comte de Mâcon] from “''predicta urbe''”, by charter dated 6 Jan 1146[170].  
2 CONT * “''Raynaldus Burgundiæ comes''” donated property to Besançon cathedral, with the consent of “''fratris nostri Guillermi comitis et...collateralis nostræ Agathæ Lotharingiæ ducis filiæ''”, by charter dated 1148[171].  
2 CONT * '''Regent of Bourgogne-Comté''' for his niece Beatrix, whom he attempted to deprive of her inheritance: one of Sigebert’s continuators records, when recording Beatrix’s marriage, that “''filiam comitis Burgundionum Reinaldi''” h
2 CONC ad been imprisoned by “''patruus suus comes Wilhelmus''” after her father died[172].  
2 CONT * “''Comes Stephanus...et fratrem meum comitem Gerardum''” settled disputes with Charlieu abbey, with the consent of “''matris nostræ...comitis Willelmi patris nostri et avi nostri domini Theobaldi de Treva''”, by charter dated 28
2 CONC  Oct 1157[173]. 
2 CONT * '''m PONCE de Traves''' Dame de Traves, daughter of THIBAUT Seigneur de Traves & his wife Alix --- (-15 Apr after 1157). The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines names "Aleyde comitissa qui fuit unica heres de Treva, relicta 
2 CONC Theobaldi de Rogemont" as wife of "comitem Guilelmum"[174]. Bouchard points out that Thibaut [I] Seigneur de Rougemont was still alive when Comte Guillaume is alleged to have married Ponce de Traves and suggests that the chronicle
2 CONC  confused her with her mother[175]. It is more likely that Alberic confused Ponce with her sister Alix, who is reported to have married Thibaut [II] Seigneur de Rougemont. "''Willelmus Masticonensis comes et Burgundie''" donated "
2 CONC ''abbatiam de Balma''" to Cluny by charter dated 14 Jun 1147, confirmed by "''Poncia comitissa uxore mea et filiis meis Stephano et Gerard''o"[176]. The necrology of La Charité records the death “''XVII Kal Mai''” of “''Poncia com
2 CONC itissa Burgundiæ''”[177]. 
2 CONT * Mistress (1): ---.  The name of Comte Guillaume's mistress is not known.  The name of her son suggests that she was Italian. 
2 CONT * Comte Guillaume [III] & his wife had two children:'''...etc:'''
2 CONT * Comte Guillaume [III] had one illegitimate child by Mistress (1):''':..etc.'''
2 CONT <nowiki>------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT ====it.Wikipedia '''''(28 mar 2024)''''': [https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guglielmo_IV_di_Borgogna Guglielmo IV di Borgogna] – Auto-Translation
2 CONT
2 CONT ..in French Guillaume IV (également appelé Guillaume III de Muncon) (c.1088 – before October 1157), was count of Auxone from 1127, and, from 1148 to 1156, also regent of the Franche-Commeas, usurping the title of Count of Burgundy
2 CONC , Count of Macon and Count of Vienne from 1148 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the second-born male son of the Count of Macon and Count of Vienne Stephen I said the Ardito, who, in 1097, also became regent of the Franche-Comté of Burgundy and Beatrice of Lorraine (- ca. 1116), daughter of the Cou
2 CONC nt of Metz and Chatenoy and Duke of Lorraine, Gerardo I, as confirmed by document no. 3791 of the Recueil des chartes de l'abbaye de Cluny. Tome 5 (although Beatrice's name is not mentioned, but is confirmed by the note of the nex
2 CONC t page[[1][2][2]; William is in fact mentioned in the Chronica Albrici Monachi Trium Fontium together with her sister Isabella and her brother, Rinaldo (Elizabeth sorem comitis Raynaldi de Burgundia et comititis Guilelmi de Ultras
2 CONC agonnam)[[3]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen I, according to document no. 365 of the Chartes et documents de Saint-Bénigne de Dijon des origines à 1300 (Dijon) II (not consulted)[[4] and according to the Chronique de l'abbaye de Saint-Bénigne de Dijon was the son of 
2 CONC the Count of Burgundy, Count of Macon and Count of Vienne, William I, known as the Great or the Aardo[5].1087Stefania di Borgogna 1035 – after 1088), of which the ascendants are not known exactly[[6] who, according to the most acc
2 CONC redited version, was Countess Vienne, daughter of Gerard of Vienne.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the grandson of Guy of Burgundy (1050 - 1124), future pope with the name of Callisto II and Raymond of Burgundy (about 1062 – 11071107), future Count of Galicia and Coimbra and future father of King Alfonso VII of Cast
2 CONC ile who will be called the Emperor of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 11021102, his father, Stephen, died in the Holy Land, after the Second Battle of Ramla; according to the chronicler of the First Crusade, Albert of Aachen, canon and guardian of the church of Aachen, was beheaded along with Ste
2 CONC phen of Blois[[7] after being captured by the Saracens at Ramla[. Even William of Tyre, archbishop, in the twelfth century, of the city of Tyre, in present-day Lebanon, in his Historia rerum in partibus transmarinis gestarum, repo
2 CONC rts the death of [Stephen; William together with his elder brother, Rinaldo took over the titles of the Count of Vienne and Count of Mucon, while his cousin, William II called the German remained the only Count of Burgundy and als
2 CONC o became regent., and Cloister: Nobility and the Church in Burgundy, 980-1198, the regency was exercised by his uncle, Guy of Burgundy[, the future pope.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1119 his uncle, Guy of Burgundy (1050 - 1124), who, in 1085, had been appointed administrator of the Archbishopric of Besan?on [11]and, in 1088, according to Orderico Vitale, had become archbishop of Vienne[[12] ascended to the
2 CONC  papal throne with the name of Callisto II.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1125, on the death of his cousin, William II called the German, following a plot of his barons],[13] in the title of Earl of Burgundy, his son, William, called the Child, took over him who, in 1127, according to Anselmi continu
2 CONC atio Sigeberti died murdered by his subjects, at only seventeen years old, while, in a church, on 27 February (3 Kalend. Martii) prayed before the altar[[14]; the Annales Sancti Disibodi also confirmed the murder (Wilhelmus prince
2 CONC ps Burgundi? a suis occiditur).[15] According to the Veterum Scriptorum, Tome VI, Fundatio monasteries beati Maria de Altaripa, the assassination was anticipated by one year on 9 February 1126 (1126, 5 Idu Februari), at Payerne (a
2 CONC pud Paterniacum).[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT That same year, 1127, William acquired Auxonne County[17].
2 CONT
2 CONT After William's death, called the Child, without heirs[,[18] according to the German historian Horst Fuhrmann, in his Deutsche Geschichte im hohen Mittelalter. Von der Mitte des 11. bis zum Ende des 12 the new king of Germany, Lot
2 CONC hair II of Supplimburgo, to impose his authority in the kingdom of Arles or of the two Burgundians, granted to ]William's uncle, known as the Child, Conrad I of Z?hringen, the title and functions of Rector Burgundiae,[18] with the
2 CONC  aim of safeguarding the interests of the kingdom of Germany in the counties of the kingdom of the kingdom of the city. The county of Burgundy, however, was ruled by his brother, Rinaldo, with the title of count[10].
2 CONT
2 CONT Taking advantage of the struggle between the aspirants to the imperial crown, Lothair II of Supplimburgo elected king of the Romans, at Worms, in 1125 (which will later be crowned Emperor in 1133), with the support of the clergy a
2 CONC nd of Pope Honourius II, and the designated successor of Henry V, the Duke of Swabia, Frederick II,[[19], who did not accept the election and who, in 1127, had been elected to the Romans.re di Germaniaof Hohenstaufen, who, the fol
2 CONC lowing year, received the iron crown in Milan (therefore he was king of Italy),[[20] his brother, Rinaldo III proclaimed the independence of the county from the empire, obviously without asking permission from one of the two kings
2 CONC ; but Rinaldo defeated by Conrad I of Z?hringen Rector Burgundiae, was handed over to Lothair, who conbusted the county to him[10].[21]authority only the lands east of the Jura massif.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1146 and 1147, William joined those who participated in the Second Crusade[17].
2 CONT
2 CONT In those years his brother, Rinaldo, was recognized the title franc-compte, which means "free count", from which the name of the Franche-Comté region took [place; William at that time remained at the side of his brother: in fact, 
2 CONC according to document 103 of the Lettre touchant Beatrix comtesse de Chalon, dated 1148 and concerning a donation made to the Cathedral of Besancon, by his brother, Rinaldo,Raynaldus Burgundiæ comessister-in-law, Agatha of Lorrain
2 CONC e (with the consent of our brother William the Count and of our sweetest collateral Agatha???)[22]
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of Rinaldo, according to the Obituaires de la province de Sens. Tome 1 / Tome 1 / Partie 1 /I, Prieuré de Saint-Martin-des-Champs, took place on January 15 (XVIII Kal Feb)[[23] of 1148, as confirmed by note 8 of the same
2 CONC  Obituaires de la province de Sens. Tome 1 / Tome 1 / Partie 1 / I, Prieuré de Saint-Martin-des-Champs[[24]; the date of death, January 15, is also confirmed by the Necrologium ecclesi? sancti Petri Matisconensis[[25]; William rem
2 CONC ained the only count of Macon and Vienne, while, in the title of Countess of Burgundy, he was succeeded by the only daughter of Rinaldo, Beatrice.[26]
2 CONT
2 CONT William, did not settle for the regency but tried to appropriate the assets and titles of Beatrice, keeping her practically captive, tried to oust her niece, seizing all her assets[27].
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Frederick Barbarossa, in 11521152, after confirming, Rector of Burgundy, Bertoldus IV of Z?hringen, in place of his father, Conrad I of Z?hringen, who died in that same year, gave him the task of countering the lusts of Wi
2 CONC lliam, granting him full autonomy in Burgundy and Provence,[ but seems to be without great success].[28]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1153, the court of the kingdom of Arles or of the two Burgundians, presided over by Frederick himself, was summoned to Besancon to impose its authority on the kingdom; William presented itself but it seems that no measures were
2 CONC  taken against him, for the possession of the county of Burgundy[28].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1156, on the day of Pentecost, Frederick Barbarossa, who had divorced his first wife, Adelaide of Vohburg, married Beatrice, ]in Wurzburgburg, and was under the way, under the attempt of William to take possession of the county
2 CONC  of Burgundy. However, William continued to rule the county of Burgundy until his death, which occurred not long after, before October 1157; in fact, on October 28 of that year, the two legitimate sons, Stephen and Gerard, with th
2 CONC eir mother, Ponzia or Alice of Traves, according to the Histoire généalogique des sires de Salins au comté de Bourgogne, put an end to a dispute with the amanCharlieu[29].
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death, the county of Auxonne and the lordship of Traves went to his eldest son Stephen, while the counties of Vienne and Macon went to his second son, Gerard.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Chronica Albrici Monachi Trium Fontium Guglielmo had married the heir of the lordship of Traves, Alice, widow of Tebald of Rogemont (Aleyde comitissa qui fuit unica heres de Treva, relicta Theobaldi de Rogemont)[3
2 CONC 0] daughter, according to the Histoire généalogique des sires de Salins au comté de Bourgigne.10641122[29]Recueil des chartes de l'abbaye de Cluny. Tome 5, concerning a donation (abbatiam de Balma) made by Guglielmo (Ego Willelmus
2 CONC  Masticonensis comes et Burgundie) at the church of Cluny (cluniacensi ecclesiae), dated 14 June 1147, instead William had married Alice's sister, Ponzia (about 1090 - April 15, after 1158), who a signatory figure, along with his 
2 CONC sons, Stefano and Gerardo[31]] ’) Even the Lettre touchant Beatrix comtesse de Chalon does not help to clarify, as in document 111, he cites as William's wife, Alice (Adalasia comitissa uxor eius)[32], while, in document 110, when
2 CONC  he mentions his death (without specifying the year), on April 15 (XVII Kal Mai), cites it as Ponzia (Ponciaitissa Burgundi?).[33]
2 CONT
2 CONT >1. '''Stephen'''[30] (?-1173), Count of Auxonne; followed in the title: Stephen III (?-1241), John called the Sage (1190-1267), also Count of Chalon and finally Hung of Chalons (1220-1266), who married Adelaide I of Burgundy, bec
2 CONC ame Count of Burgundy.
2 CONT >2. '''Gerard'''[30] (1142-1184), Count of Vienne and Macon; followed by: William V of Macon (?-1224), Gerard II of Macon (?-1222) and finally Alice of M?con, last Countess of Vienne and of Macon whom married John of Dreux.
2 CONT > 
2 CONT >'''From a mistress whose name or the ascendants are known, William had an illegitimate [17]son:'''
2 CONT >'''Malaspina''' (after 1170), which appears as a witness in document No. 4122 of the Recueil des chartes de l'abbaye de Cluny. Tome 5[31].
2 CONT
