2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_IV_of_France wikipedia - '''Philip IV of France''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip IV (April–June 1268 – 29 November 1314), called the Fair (French: le Bel), son and successor of Philip III, reigned as King of France from 1285 until his death. He was the husband of Joan I of Navarre, by virtue of which he
2 CONC  was King of Navarre (as Philip I) and Count of Champagne from 1284 to 1305. The nickname Philip "the Fair" or "the Handsome" comes from his appearance; it had nothing to do with his actions as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the House of Capet, Philip was born at the Palace of Fontainebleau at Seine-et-Marne, the son of King Philip III and Isabella of Aragon. Philip was nicknamed the Fair (le Bel) because of his handsome appearance, but hi
2 CONC s inflexible personality gained him other epithets, from friend and foe alike.
2 CONT
2 CONT # Father: Philippe III , King Of France b: 1 May 1245 in Poissy, Yvelines, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Princess Of Aragon Isabelle b: 1243-1247 in Montpellier, Herault, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Joan I , Of Navarre, Queen Of France b: 14 Jan 1271/72 in Bar-Sur-Seine, Aube, France
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Married: 16 Aug 1284 in Notre Dame De Paris, Seine, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Isabelle , Queen Of England b: 1292 in Paris, Seine, France
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Marguerite , Princess Of France b: 1286 in PARIS,SEINE,FRANCE
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Louis X "the Headstrong" , King Of France b: 4 Oct 1289 in Paris,Seine,France
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Blanche , Princess Of France b: 1290 in PARIS,SEINE,FRANCE
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Philippe V , King Of France b: 1291-1292 in Paris, Seine, France
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Charles IV "the Fari" , King Of France b: 1295 in Clermont, Oise, France
2 CONT
2 CONT    7. Prince Of France Robert b: 1297 in PARIS,SEINE,FRANCE
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip IV of France
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_IV_of_France
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip IV the Fair
2 CONT
2 CONT King of France and Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT Count of Champagne
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 	5 October 1285 – 29 November 1314
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation 	6 January 1286, Reims
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor 	Philip III
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor 	Louis X
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Joan I of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis X
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip V
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles IV
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle, Queen of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Philip III
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Isabella of Aragon
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	April–June 1268
2 CONT
2 CONT Fontainebleau, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	29 November 1314 (aged 46)
2 CONT
2 CONT Fontainebleau, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Saint Denis Basilica
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip IV (April–June 1268 – 29 November 1314), called the Fair (French: le Bel), son and successor of Philip III, reigned as King of France from 1285 until his death. He was the husband of Joan I of Navarre, by virtue of which he
2 CONC  was King of Navarre (as Philip I) and Count of Champagne from 1284 to 1305. The nickname Philip "the Fair" or "the Handsome" comes from his appearance; it had nothing to do with his actions as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Youth
2 CONT
2 CONT A member of the House of Capet, Philip was born at the Palace of Fontainebleau at Seine-et-Marne, the son of King Philip III and Isabella of Aragon. Philip was nicknamed the Fair (le Bel) because of his handsome appearance, but hi
2 CONC s inflexible personality gained him other epithets, from friend and foe alike. His fierce opponent Bernard Saisset, bishop of Pamiers, said of him, "He is neither man nor beast. He is a statue."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His education was guided by Guillaume d'Ercuis, the almoner of his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT As prince, just before his father's death, he negotiated the safe passage of the royal family out of Aragon after the unsuccessful Aragonese Crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Consolidation of the royal demesne
2 CONT
2 CONT As a king, Philip was determined to strengthen the monarchy at any cost. He relied, more than any of his predecessors, on a professional bureaucracy of legalists. Because to the public he kept aloof and left specific policies, esp
2 CONC ecially unpopular ones, to his ministers, he was called a "useless owl" by his contemporaries. His reign marks the French transition from a charismatic monarchy – which could all but collapse in an incompetent reign – to a bureauc
2 CONC ratic kingdom, a move, under a certain historical reading, towards modernity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip married queen Joan of Navarre (1271–1305) on 16 August 1284. The primary administrative benefit of this was the inheritance of Joan in Champagne and Brie, which were adjacent to the royal demesne in Ile-de-France and became
2 CONC  thus effectively united to the king's own lands, forming an expansive area. During the reigns of Joan herself, and her three sons (1284–1328), these lands belonged to the person of the king; but by 1328 they had become so entrenc
2 CONC hed in the royal domain that king Philip VI of France (who was not an heir of Joan) switched lands with the then rightful heiress, Joan II of Navarre, with the effect that Champagne and Brie remained part of the royal demesne and 
2 CONC Joan received compensation with lands in western Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Kingdom of Navarre in the Pyrenees was not so important to contemporary interests of the French crown. It remained in personal union 1284–1329, after which it went its separate way. Philip gained Lyon for France in 1312.
2 CONT
2 CONT War with the English
2 CONT
2 CONT As Duke of Aquitaine, the English king Edward I was a vassal to Philip, and had to pay him homage. Following the Fall of Acre in 1291 however, the former allies started to show dissent.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1293, following a naval incident between the Normans and the English, Philip summoned Edward to the French court, but the latter, busy harassing Scotland, refused to appear. Philip used this pretext to strip Edward of all his p
2 CONC ossessions in France, thereby initiating hostilities with England.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The outbreak of hostilities with England in 1294 was the inevitable result of the competitive expansionist monarchies, triggered by a secret Franco-Scottish pact of mutual assistance against Edward I, who was Philip's brother-in-l
2 CONC aw, having married Philip's sister Marguerite; inconclusive campaigns for the control of Gascony to the southwest of France were fought in 1294–98 and 1300–03. Philip gained Guienne but was forced to return it. The search for inco
2 CONC me to cover military expenditures set its stamp on Philip's reign and his contemporary reputation. Pursuant to the terms of the Treaty of Paris (1303), the marriage of Philip's daughter Isabella to the Prince of Wales, heir of Phi
2 CONC lip's enemy, celebrated at Boulogne, 25 January 1308, was meant to seal a peace; instead it would produce an eventual English claimant to the French throne itself, and the Hundred Years War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Drive for income
2 CONT
2 CONT In the shorter term, Philip expelled the Jews for oppressive lending practices from French territories on 22 July 1306 (see The Great Exile of 1306).  He was criticized by his enemies in the Catholic Church[4] for his spendthrift 
2 CONC lifestyle. He debased the coinage. When he also levied taxes on the French clergy of one half their annual income, he caused an uproar within the Roman Catholic Church and the papacy, prompting Pope Boniface VIII to issue the Bull
2 CONC  Clericis laicos, forbidding the transference of any church property to the French Crown and prompting a drawn-out diplomatic battle with the King. In order to condemn the pope, Philip convoked an assembly of bishops, nobles and g
2 CONC rand bourgeois of Paris, a precursor to the Etats Généraux that appeared for the first time during his reign, a measure of the professionalism and order that his ministers were introducing into government. Philip emerged victoriou
2 CONC s, after having sent his agent William Nogaret to arrest Boniface at Anagni, when the French archbishop Bertrand de Goth was elected pope as Clement V and the official seat of the papacy moved to Avignon, an enclave surrounded by 
2 CONC French territories, commencing the captive Avignon Papacy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Flanders
2 CONT
2 CONT He suffered a major embarrassment when an army of 2,500 noble men-at-arms (Knights and Squires) and 4,000 infantry he sent to suppress an uprising in Flanders was defeated in the Battle of the Golden Spurs near Kortrijk on 11 July
2 CONC  1302. Philip reacted with energy to the humiliation and personally defeated the Flemings at Mons-en-Pévèle two years later. Finally, in 1305, Philip forced the Flemish to accept a harsh peace treaty after his success at the battl
2 CONC e of Mons-en-Pévèle; the peace exacted heavy reparations and humiliating penalties, and added the rich cloth cities of Lille and Douai, sites of major cloth fairs, to the royal territory. Béthune, first of the Flemish cities to yi
2 CONC eld, was granted to Mahaut, Countess of Artois, whose two daughters, to secure her fidelity, were married to Philip's two sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Suppression of the Knights Templar
2 CONT
2 CONT Numerous complaints of usury across France prompted Philip to expel Jewish moneylenders and disband the Templars, a monastic order of knights who had acted as bankers for over two hundred years to serve the needs of the Crusades. 
2 CONC As debts spiraled out of control, anger toward banking and usury abounded across Europe. In 1300, the Pope called for the first Christian jubilee where all debts could be forgiven. Templars did not forgive debts. So, banning usury
2 CONC  altogether would free French subjects and the king of debt. On Friday the 13th, October 1307, hundreds of Knights Templar were arrested and tortured into admitting heresy. Legally, Knights Templar answered only to the Pope.  Pope
2 CONC  Clement V asked for trials, but it was too late. Philip had ordered the Templars burnt at the stake. In 1314, the last Grand Master of the Templars, Jacques de Molay was set upon a stake over a fire in Paris; and accursed both Ki
2 CONC ng Philip and Pope Clement V from the flames, vowing they would be dead within a year. Both King and Pope died within that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The crown passed through Philip's three sons, who also each died relatively young, without producing direct male heirs. By 1328, the Capet Dynasty ended, and the crown passed to the House of Valois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Expulsion of the Jews
2 CONT
2 CONT While King Edward ordered the Jews to leave England in 1290, Philip the Fair expelled the Jews from France in 1306, for oppressive money-lending policies.  In 1315,  the Jews were allowed back for 12 years of guaranteed residence,
2 CONC  free from government interference if they promised to limit usury. In 1322, the Jews were expelled again by the Kings' successor, since usury became a problem again. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tour de Nesle affair
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1314, the daughters-in-law of Philip IV-Margaret of Burgundy, Queen of France Wife of Louis X; and Blanche of Burgundy wife of Charles IV-were accused of adultery, and their alleged lovers (Phillipe d'Aunay and Gauthier d'Aunay
2 CONC ) tortured, flayed and executed in what has come to be known as the Tour de Nesle Affair (French: Affaire de la tour de Nesle). A third daughter-in-law, Jeanne II, Countess of Burgundy, wife of Philip V, was accused of knowledge o
2 CONC f the affairs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Crusades and diplomacy with Mongols
2 CONT
2 CONT See Main article: Franco-Mongol alliance
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip had various contacts with the Mongol power in the Middle East, including reception of the embassy of the Turkic/Mongol monk Rabban Bar Sauma.[8] Bar Sauma presented an offer of a Franco-Mongol alliance with Arghun of the Mo
2 CONC ngol Ilkhanate in Baghdad. Arghun was seeking to join forces between the Mongols and the Europeans, against their common enemy the Muslim Mamluks. In return, Arghun offered to return Jerusalem to the Christians, once it was re-cap
2 CONC tured from the Muslims. Philip seemingly responded positively to the request of the embassy, by sending one of his noblemen, Gobert de Helleville, to accompany Bar Sauma back to Mongol lands.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT There was further correspondence between Arghun and Philip in 1288 and 1289,[10] outlining potential military cooperation. However, Philip never actually pursued such military plans.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1305, the new Mongol ruler Oljeitu sent letters to Philip,[11] the Pope, and Edward I of England. He again offered a military collaboration between the Christian nations of Europe and the Mongols against the Mamluks. Euro
2 CONC pean nations attempted another Crusade, but were delayed, and it never took place.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 4 April 1312, another Crusade was promulgated at the Council of Vienne. In 1313, Philip "took the cross", making the vow to go on a Crusade in the Levant, thus responding to Pope Clement V's call. He was, however, warned agains
2 CONC t leaving by Enguerrand de Marigny[12] and died soon after in a hunting accident.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomb of Philip IV in the Basilica of St Denis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip IV's rule signaled the decline of the papacy's power from its near complete authority. His palace located on the Île de la Cité is represented today by surviving sections of the Conciergerie. He suffered a cerebral ictus du
2 CONC ring a hunt at Pont-Sainte-Maxence (Forest of Halatte) and died a few weeks later in Fontainebleau, where he was born. He is buried in the Basilica of St Denis. He was succeeded by his son Louis X.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Philip IV and Jeanne of Navarre were:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Marguerite (1288–c.1294)
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. Louis X - ( 4 October 1289–5 June 1316)
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Blanche (died c.1294)
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Philip V - (1292/93–3 January 1322)
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Charles IV - (1294–1 February 1328)
2 CONT
2 CONT    6. Isabelle - (c. 1295–23 August 1358)
2 CONT
2 CONT    7. Robert (born 1297, died 1308 at St-Germaine-en-Laye)
2 CONT
2 CONT All three of his sons reaching adulthood would become kings of France, and his surviving daughter, as consort of Edward II, was queen of England.
