1 OCCU 2nd Queen Consort of Edward I of England, Queen Of England, Queen of England, Princess of France
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_France,_Queen_of_England
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00003852&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birth:''' Between Jan 8 1279 and Jan 7 1280 - France, Lot-et-Garonne, Aquitaine, France
2 CONT '''Death:''' Feb 22 1316 - Marlborough, Wiltshire, England
2 CONT '''Husband:''' Edward I " Longshanks" Plantagenet King of England
2 CONT '''Children:''' Eleanor Plantagenet, Edmund Woodstock Kent Plantagenent, Thomas Plantagenet
2 CONT //////////////////////////////////////////////
2 CONT Eleanor was born in Castile, now Spain, daughter of Ferdinand III of Castile and Joan, Countess of Ponthieu. Both the court of her father and her half-brother Alfonso X of Castile were known for its literary atmosphere. Growing up
2 CONC  in such an environment probably influenced her later literary activities as queen. The young couple married at the monastery of Las Huelgas, Burgos on 1 November 1254. Edward and Eleanor were second cousins once removed, as Elean
2 CONC or's great-grandmother Eleanor of England was a daughter of King Henry II and Eleanor of Aquitaine. Arranged royal marriages in the Middle Ages were not always happy, but available evidence indicates that Eleanor and Edward were d
2 CONC evoted to each other. Edward is among the few medieval English kings not known to have conducted extramarital affairs or fathered children out of wedlock. The couple were rarely apart; she accompanied him on military campaigns in 
2 CONC Wales, famously giving birth to their son Edward on 25 April 1284 in a temporary dwelling erected for her amid the construction of Caernarfon Castle. Edward followed her body to burial in Westminster Abbey, and erected memorial cr
2 CONC osses at the site of each overnight stop between Lincoln and Westminster. Based on crosses in France marking Louis IX's funeral procession, these artistically significant monuments enhanced the image of Edward's kingship as well a
2 CONC s witnessing his grief. The best preserved is that at Geddington.That Edward remained single until he wed Marguerite of France in 1299 is often cited to prove he cherished Eleanor's memory. 
2 CONT //////////////////////////////////////////////
2 CONT '''Source:''' http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_France_%28died_1318%29
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of Edward's beloved first wife, Eleanor of Castile, at the age of 49 in 1290, left him reeling in grief. However, it was much to Edward's benefit to make peace with France to free him to pursue his wars in Scotland. Addi
2 CONC tionally, with only one surviving son, Edward was anxious to protect the English throne with additional heirs. In summer of 1291, the English king had betrothed his son and heir, Edward, to Blanche of France in order to achieve pe
2 CONC ace with France. However, hearing of her renowned beauty, Edward decided to have his son's bride for his own and sent emissaries to France. Philip agreed to give Blanche to Edward on the following conditions: that a truce would be
2 CONC  concluded between the two countries and that Edward would give up the province of Gascony. Edward agreed to the conditions and sent his brother Edmund Crouchback, Earl of Lancaster, to fetch the new bride. Edward had been deceive
2 CONC d, for Blanche was to be married to Rudolph III of Habsburg, the eldest son of King Albert I of Germany. Instead, Philip offered her younger sister Margaret to marry Edward (then 55). Upon hearing this, Edward declared war on Fran
2 CONC ce, refusing to marry Margaret. After five years, a truce was agreed upon under the influence of Pope Boniface VIII. A series of treaties in the first half of 1299 provided terms for a double marriage: Edward I would marry Margare
2 CONC t and his son would marry Isabella of France, Philip's youngest surviving child. Additionally, the English monarchy would regain the key city of Guienne and receive £15,000 owed to Margaret as well as the return of Eleanor of Cast
2 CONC ile's lands in Ponthieu and Montreuil as a dower first for Margaret, and then Isabella of France.[3]
2 CONT ////////////////////////////////////////////
2 CONT ====Source: www.celtic-casimir.com
2 CONT >'''Marguerite de France''' (1279-1317)
2 CONT >Born 1279
2 CONT >Died 14 February 1317 Marlborough Castle 
2 CONT >Married September 1299 Canterbury Cathedral
2 CONT >Edward I "Longshanks" 
2 CONT >King of England 1272-1307 
2 CONT >Born 17 June 1239 Westminster Palace 
2 CONT >Died 7 July 1307 Burgh on the Sands nr.Carlisle
2 CONT
2 CONT For political reasons Edward I agreed to remarry. At first Blanche of France was the candidate, but for unknown reasons she was replaced by her younger sister, Margaret. Margaret was about twenty years old when she married the six
2 CONC ty-year-old widower and was the first English queen since the conquest not to be consecrated and crowned. This marriage seems to have been happy enough as two sons and a daughter were born. However, only the two sons survived into
2 CONC  adulthood. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Edward I's death, Margaret remained on good terms with the new king Edward II, her stepson. In January 1308 she accompanied him to France where he married Margaret's niece, Isabella of France. After this marriage she went to
2 CONC  live at Marlborough Castle where she died in 1317. 
2 CONT /////////////////////////////////////////
2 CONT Princess Marguerite Hardi CAPET of Castile 
2 CONT Born: 1279, Paris, France
2 CONT Married: 8 Sep 1299, Canterbury Cathedral, Kent, England
2 CONT Died: 14 Feb 1316-1317, Marlborough Castle, Wiltshire, England
2 CONT Buried: Grey Friars, Church, London, England  
2 CONT Cause of Death: Natural Causes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Another name for Marguerite was Marguerite LE HARDI. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Marguerite married Edward I "Atheling" PLANTAGENET King of England—son of Henry III PLANTAGENET King of England and Eléanor BÉRENGAR, comtesse de Provence—on 8 Sep 1299 in Canterbury Cathedral, Kent, England
2 CONT >Edward I "Atheling" PLANTAGENET King of England was born on 17 Jun 1239 in Westminster Palace, London, England 1056. 
2 CONT >Edward was christened on 21 Jun 1239 in Westminster, Middlesex, England. 
2 CONT >Edward died on 7 Jul 1307 in Burgh-On-Sands, Northumberland 1056. 
2 CONT >Edward was buried on 28 Oct 1307 in Westminster Abbey, London, England.)
2 CONT >www.findagrave.com
2 CONT //////////////////////////////////////////////
2 CONT Marguerite de France  Birth 1279  Paris, City of Paris, Île-de-France, France Death 14 Feb 1318 (aged 38\'9639)  Marlborough, Wiltshire Unitary Authority, Wiltshire, England Burial  Grey Friars London  London, City of London, Grea
2 CONC ter London, England   Memorial ID 8333258
2 CONT
2 CONT British Monarch. Queen consort of King Edward I "Longshanks". Daughter of Philip III the Bold of France and Mary of Brabant. Married Edward I in 1290 as his second queen. The marriage was an affectionate one, and the couple had 3 
2 CONC children, Thomas, Edmund, and Eleanor. Bio by: Kristen Conrad
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Parents''': Philippe III of France, 1245\'961285; Marie de Brabant  1256\'961321  
2 CONT '''Spouse:''' Edward I  1239\'961307
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''Siblings'''
2 CONT >Louis d'Evreux  1276\'961319 
2 CONT >Blanche de France  1285\'961305  Half Siblings
2 CONT >Louis de France  1265\'961276 
2 CONT >Philippe IV of France  1268\'961314 
2 CONT >Robert de France  1269\'961276 
2 CONT >Charles of Valois  1270\'961325 (m. 1290)  Children
2 CONT >Thomas Plantagenet of Brotherton  1300\'961338 
2 CONT >Edmund Plantagenet of Woodstock  1301\'961330 
2 CONT >Eleanor Plantagenet  1306\'961311 
2 CONT >'''Source:''' Leo van de Pas  [http://brigittegastelancestry.com/royal/bio/margueritefrancebio1279.html]
