4 CONT Eleanor [Eleanor of Castile] (1241–1290), queen of England, consort of Edward I, was the daughter of Ferdinand III of Castile (1201–1252) and his second wife, Jeanne de Dammartin (d. 1279), heir to the French county of Ponthieu. A
4 CONC ncient claims to Gascony in Aquitaine, raised in 1252–3 by Eleanor's half-brother Alfonso X, were transferred to Edward [see ] upon his marriage to Eleanor at the convent of Las Huelgas near Burgos on 1 November 1254. Though diplo
4 CONC matically advantageous the marriage was unpopular in England, as it was feared Edward's bride would bring a crowd of Castilians in her wake. Eleanor came to England in October 1255, but little is recorded of her before the barons'
4 CONC  wars. She supported Edward's turn to the Lusignans in 1258, and was abroad with him between 1260 and 1263. When he returned from France in February 1263, mercenaries whom Eleanor obtained from Ponthieu were among the troops he in
4 CONC stalled in Windsor Castle. Contrary to general belief, she did not leave England during the crisis. She lived at Windsor after the battle of Lewes, when ordered her to join him (17 June 1264), probably at Montfort's wishes as it w
4 CONC as thought she might be hiring Castilian mercenaries. After the battle of Evesham she secured grants of rebels' lands and began to acquire the rich estates for which she became notorious.
4 CONT
4 CONT In August 1270 Eleanor and Edward left England for the Holy Land [see also ]. At Acre in June 1272 an assassin wounded Edward with a poisoned knife; his life was despaired of until a surgeon cut the inflamed flesh from his arm. Th
4 CONC e legend that Eleanor instead sucked poison from the wound is first found in the Historia ecclesiastica by the Dominican Ptolemy of Lucca (d. 1327?), who gave it only as a popular story. Camden first published it in England in Bri
4 CONC tannia (1586). Walter of Guisborough's chronicle states merely that Eleanor, weeping and lamenting, was led from Edward's bedside before the operation.
4 CONT
4 CONT Following Henry III's death the couple returned to England and were crowned together on 19 August 1274. With Edward's support Eleanor from 1275 expanded her estates by securing English knights' debts to Jewish moneylenders, and th
4 CONC en taking over lands pledged for the debts. She obtained much land in this way between 1278 and 1281; thereafter she acquired more estates by purchase than through Jewish debts, but continued to collect such debts and probably exa
4 CONC cted usury on them. Archbishop Pecham warned her in 1283 that this was causing scandal, and in 1286 wrote of continued outcry and gossip. By 1290 Eleanor's lands were worth upwards of £2500 yearly; the revenue was needed, but such
4 CONC  economic activity was unprecedented for a queen. English reactions are seen in the Dunstable annals' description of Eleanor as ‘a Spaniard by birth, who acquired many fine manors’ (Ann. mon., 3.362), and Guisborough's verse notin
4 CONC g her craving for land:
4 CONT
4 CONT     The king would like to get our gold
4 CONT     The queen, our manors fair to hold.
4 CONT     (Chronicle, 216)
4 CONT
