1 NOTE {geni:about_me} a short summary from Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of Acre
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess of Hertford
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess of Gloucester
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert de Clare, 7th Earl of Gloucester, 6th Earl of Hertford m. 1290; dec. 1295
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Ralph de Monthermer m. 1297; wid. 1307
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilbert de Clare, 7th Earl of Hertford
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor de Clare
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret de Clare
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth de Clare
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary de Monthermer
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan de Monthermer
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas de Monthermer, 2nd Baron Monthermer
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward de Monthermer
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Edward I Longshanks
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Eleanor of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT Born: April 1272
2 CONT
2 CONT Acre, Kingdom of Acre
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 23 April 1307(1307-04-23) (aged 35)
2 CONT
2 CONT Clare Castle, Clare
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: 23 April 1307
2 CONT
2 CONT Clare Priory, Suffolk
2 CONT
2 CONT =================================================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT "Joan of Acre (April 1272 – 23 April 1307) was an English princess, a daughter of King Edward I of England and Queen Eleanor of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name "Acre" derives from her birthplace in the Holy Land while her parents were on a crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married twice; her first husband was Gilbert de Clare, 7th Earl of Gloucester, one of the most powerful nobles in her father's kingdom; her second husband was Ralph de Monthermer, a squire in her household whom she married
2 CONC  in secret.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan is most notable for the claim that miracles have allegedly taken place at her grave, and for the multiple references to her in literature."
2 CONT
2 CONT =============================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT Links:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_of_Acre
