1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Edward II, King of England==
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Reign:''' 7 July 1307 – 20 January 1327 
2 CONT * '''Coronation:''' 25 February 1308
2 CONT * '''Predecessor:''' Edward I Longshanks
2 CONT * '''Successor:''' Edward III of Windsor
2 CONT * '''Consort:''' Isabella of France
2 CONT * '''Issue:'''
2 CONT ** Edward III of Windsor
2 CONT ** John of Eltham, Earl of Cornwall
2 CONT ** Eleanor, Countess of Guelders
2 CONT ** Joan, Queen of Scots
2 CONT ** Adam Fitzroy ''-- illegitimate, with unknown mother''
2 CONT * '''House:''' House of Plantagenet
2 CONT * '''Father:''' Edward I Longshanks
2 CONT * '''Mother:''' Eleanor of Castile
2 CONT * '''Born:''' 25 April 1284 - Caernarfon Castle, Gwynedd
2 CONT * '''Died:''' 21 September 1327 - Berkeley Castle, Gloucestershire
2 CONT * '''Burial:''' Gloucester Cathedral, Gloucestershire
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Edward II''' (25 April 1284 – 21 September 1327), also called Edward of Caernarfon, was King of England from 1307 until he was deposed by his wife Isabella in January 1327. He was the sixth Plantagenet king, in a line that bega
2 CONC n with the reign of Henry II. Between the strong reigns of his father Edward I and son Edward III, the reign of Edward II was considered by some to be disastrous for England, marked by alleged incompetence, political squabbling an
2 CONC d military defeats.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Edward fathered at least five children by two women, he was rumoured to have been bisexual. His inability to deny even the most grandiose favours to his unpopular male favourites (first a Gascon knight named Piers Gaveston, 
2 CONC later a young English lord named Hugh Despenser) led to constant political unrest and his eventual deposition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I had pacified Gwynedd and some other parts of Wales and the Scottish lowlands, but never exerted a comprehensive conquest. However, the army of Edward II was devastatingly defeated at Bannockburn, freeing Scotland from Eng
2 CONC lish control and allowing Scottish forces to raid unchecked throughout the north of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to these disasters, Edward II is remembered for his probable death in Berkeley Castle, allegedly by murder, and for being the first monarch to establish colleges at Oxford and Cambridge: Oriel College at Oxford and Kin
2 CONC g's Hall, a predecessor of Trinity College, at Cambridge. ''(Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 4.0)''
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
