1 NOTE He was beheaded.
1 NOTE Edmund Plantagenet, husband of Margaret Wake, was born Aug. 5, 1301,surnamed of Woodstock, from the place of his birth, 2nd son of KingEdward I by his 2nd wife Margaret, daughter of Philip, the Hardy, King ofFrance, son of Louis I
2 CONC X, Saint Louis. "Edmundo de Woodstock" was summonedto Parliament by writ Aug. 2, 1320, about two years before he attainedhis majority. He had previously been in the wars of Scotland and hadobtained considerable territorial grants 
2 CONC from the crown. In the next yearhe was created Earl of Kent, and had a grant of the castle of Okham, inthe County of Rutland, and shrievalty of the county. About the same timehe was constituted Governor of the castle of Tunbridge 
2 CONC in Kent; and uponthe breaking out of the insurrection, under Thomas Plantagenet, Earl ofLancaster (son of Edmund Plantagenet, 2nd son of Henry III, King ofEngland), he was commissioned by the King (Edward II) to pursue thatrebelli
2 CONC ous prince, and to lay siege to the Castle of Pontefract. The Earlof Lancaster was subsequently made a prisoner at Boroughbridge, and thisEdmund Plantagenet, Earl of Kent, was one of those who condemned him todeath. Edmund espouse
2 CONC d the cause of his half-brother Edward II and wasbeheaded by Edward III.
1 NOTE Edmund of Woodstock, 1st Earl of Kent (5 August 1301 – 19 March 1330) was a member of the English Royal Family.    He was born at Woodstock in Oxfordshire, the son of Edward I Longshanks, King of England and his second wife, Queen
2 CONC  Marguerite. He was 62 years younger than his father, who died when Edmund of Woodstock was only seven. Reportedly, he enjoyed his father`s favour. He was summoned to Parliament by writ of summons on 5 August 1320, by which he is 
2 CONC held to have become Baron Woodstock. On 28 July 1321 he was created Earl of Kent.    Kent was married to Margaret Wake, 3rd Baroness Wake of Liddell, daughter of John Wake, 1st Baron Wake of Liddell by Joan de Fiennes, sometime be
2 CONC tween October and December in 1325 at Blisworth in Northamptonshire.    In 1327, after the execution and forfeiture of the Earl of Arundel, Kent held the castle and honour (land) of Arundel, although he was never formally invested
2 CONC  with the titles appropriate to this barony. He was the father of Joan of Kent, through whom the earldom eventually passed into the Holland family.    Kent was sentenced to death by Sir Robert de Hauville for treason, having suppo
2 CONC rted his half-brother, the deposed King Edward II, by order of the Regents the Earl of March and Queen Isabella, before the outer walls of Winchester Castle. It was said that he believed Edward II to be still alive and had conspir
2 CONC ed to rescue him from prison. Such was public hostility to the execution that "he had to wait five hours for an executioner, because nobody wanted to do it", until a convicted murderer offered to do the deed in exchange for a pard
2 CONC on.    He was buried on 31 March at the Church of the Dominican Friars in Winchester.    Kent`s execution was the beginning of the end for March`s regency. Thereafter, in October 1330, King Edward III assumed the full powers of Ki
2 CONC ng with the support of Kent`s cousin, the powerful Earl of Lancaster. March was executed that same year for, inter alia, having assumed the royal powers. The children and widow of the Earl of Kent were treated as members of Edward
2 CONC  III`s Royal Household.
