1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Medlands (22 Jan 2024): [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#AlexanderIIIdied1286 Scotland, Kings]
2 CONT 1. '''ALEXANDER''' (Roxburgh 4 Sep 1241-between Burntisland and Kinghorn, Fife 19 Mar 1286). The Chronicle of Melrose records the birth "on the day of the translation of St Cuthbert the day before Non Sep" in 1241 of "the eldest c
2 CONC hild of…Alexander king of Scotland…Alexander" at Roxburgh[636]. He succeeded his father in 1249 as '''ALEXANDER III "the Glorious" King of Scotland''', crowned 13 Jul 1249 at Scone Abbey, Perthshire. A description of his inaugurat
2 CONC ion ceremony is contained in the chronicle of Fordun[637]. The Continuator of Florence of Worcester records the death "''XIV Kal Apr''" [1286] of "''Alexander rex Scotiæ''"[638]. He died after his horse plunged over a cliff. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ''firstly'' (York Minster 26 Dec 1251) '''MARGARET of England''', daughter of '''HENRY III King of England''' & his wife '''Eléonore de Provence''' (Windsor Castle 5 Oct 1240-Cupar Castle, Fife 26 Feb 1275, bur Dunfermline
2 CONC  Abbey, Fife). Her birth is recorded by Matthew Paris[639]. Matthew Paris also records her marriage, as well as the splendour and extravagance of the marriage banquets[640]. The Annals of Burton record the marriage “''die Natalis 
2 CONC Domini apud Eboracum''” in 1251 of “''rex Scotiæ, filius regis Alexandri, puer parvulus ix annorum''” and “''Margaretam filiam regis Henrici Angliæ…eiusdem ætatis''”[641]. The ''Annals of Tewkesbury'' record the marriage “''apud E
2 CONC boracum…circa festum beati Stephani''” in 1251 of “''dominus rex…filiam suam primogenitam''” and “''regi Scotiæ''”[642]. The ''Annales Londonienses'' record the marriage "''apud Eboracum''" in 1252 of "''Henricus rex Margaretam fi
2 CONC liam suam''" and "''regi Scotiæ''"[643]. The Continuator of Florence of Worcester records the death in 1275 of "''Margareta regina Scotie et Beatrix comitissa Britanniæ, filiæ Henrici''"[644]. The ''Extracta ex Cronicis Scocie'' r
2 CONC ecords the death "''IV Kal Mar apud castrum de Cupro''" of "''Margareta regina Scocie, soror…regis Anglie''" and her burial "''Dunfermling iuxta regem Dauid''"[645]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' ''secondly'' (Jedburgh Abbey 1 Nov 1285) as her first husband, '''YOLANDE de Dreux''', daughter of '''ROBERT [IV] Comte de Dreux''' & his wife '''Beatrix Ctss de Montfort''' ([1269]-2 Aug 1322). The Continuator of Florence
2 CONC  of Worcester records the marriage in [1285] of "''Alexander rex Scotorum''" and "''filiam comitis de Drues''"[646]. John of Fordun’s Scotichronicon (Continuator) records the betrothal "''post festum Purificationis beatæ Mariæ vir
2 CONC ginis''" of "''rex Alexander''" and "''Joletam sive Jolandam…filiam…comitis de Droco sive Droys''", dated to [1284/85] from the context, and in a later passage their marriage "''die S. Calixti''" in 1285[647]. The ''Extracta ex Cr
2 CONC onicis Scocie'' records the marriage "''apud Jedwode die Sancti Calixti''" in 1285 of "''Alexander rex tercius''" and "''domina Ioleta filia comitis de Droco''", with some details of the celebration[648]. She married secondly (129
2 CONC 2) Arthur de Bretagne, who succeeded in 1305 as Arthur II Duke of Brittany. The necrology of Port-Royal records the death "''IV Non Aug''" of "''dame Yoland, royne d'Escosse, duchesse de Bretaigne et contesse de Montfort''"[649]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ====King Alexander III & his first wife had three children:
2 CONT # '''MARGARET''' (Windsor Castle 28 Feb 1261-Tönsberg 9 Apr 1283, bur Christ Kirk, Bergen). &c….
2 CONT # '''ALEXANDER''' (Jedburgh, Roxburghshire 21 Jun 1264-Lindores Abbey, Fife 28 Jan 1283, bur Dunfermline Abbey, Fife). &c….
2 CONT # '''DAVID''' (20 Mar 1273-Stirling Castle 1278 or end Jun 1281, bur Dunfermline Abbey, Fife). &c….
2 CONT
2 CONT ===<nowiki>-------------------------</nowiki>===
2 CONT
2 CONT ====en.Wikipedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_III_of_Scotland Alexander III of Scotland]
2 CONT '''Alexander III''' (Medieval Gaelic: Alaxandair mac Alaxandair; Modern Gaelic: Alasdair mac Alasdair) (4 September 1241 – 19 March 1286), King of Scots, was born at Roxburgh, the only son of '''Alexander II''' by his second wife 
2 CONC '''Marie de Coucy'''. Alexander's father died on 6 July 1249 and he became king at the age of eight, inaugurated at Scone on 13 July 1249.
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried: Dunfermline Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT Consorts: '''Margaret of England''' and '''Yolande de Dreux'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Offspring (by Margaret of England)	
2 CONT * '''Margaret, Princess of Scotland''' (1260/61–1283), who married Eirik II of Norway
2 CONT * '''Alexander, Prince of Scotland''' (21 January 1263 Jedburgh – 28 January 1283 Lindores Abbey); buried in Dunfermline Abbey
2 CONT * '''David of Scotland''' (20 March 1272 – June 1281 Stirling Castle); buried in Dunfermline Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Royal House: House of Dunkeld
2 CONT
2 CONT The years of his minority featured an embittered struggle for the control of affairs between two rival parties, the one led by Walter Comyn, Earl of Menteith, the other by Alan Durward, Justiciar of Scotia. The former dominated th
2 CONC e early years of Alexander's reign. At the marriage of Alexander to Margaret of England in 1251, Henry III seized the opportunity to demand from his son-in-law homage for the Scottish kingdom, but Alexander did not comply. In 1255
2 CONC  an interview between the English and Scottish kings at Kelso led to Menteith and his party losing to Durward's party. But though disgraced, they still retained great influence, and two years later, seizing the person of the king,
2 CONC  they compelled their rivals to consent to the erection of a regency representative of both parties.
2 CONT
2 CONT On attaining his majority at the age of 21 in 1262, Alexander declared his intention of resuming the projects on the Western Isles which the death of his father thirteen years before had cut short. He laid a formal claim before th
2 CONC e Norwegian king Haakon. Haakon rejected the claim, and in the following year responded with a formidable invasion. Sailing around the west coast of Scotland he halted off the Isle of Arran, and negotiations commenced. Alexander a
2 CONC rtfully prolonged the talks until the autumn storms should begin. At length Haakon, weary of delay, attacked, only to encounter a terrific storm which greatly damaged his ships. The Battle of Largs (October 1263) proved indecisive
2 CONC , but even so, Haakon's position was hopeless. Baffled, he turned homewards, but died in Orkney on 15 December 1263. The Isles now lay at Alexander's feet, and in 1266 Haakon's successor concluded the Treaty of Perth by which he c
2 CONC eded the Isle of Man and the Western Isles to Scotland in return for a monetary payment. Norway retained only Orkney and Shetland in the area. In 1284, Alexander invested the title of Lord of the Isles in the head of the Macdonald
2 CONC  family, Angus Macdonald, and over the next two centuries the Macdonald lords operated as if they were kings in their own right, frequently opposing the Scottish monarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander had married Princess Margaret of England, a daughter of King Henry III of England and Eleanor of Provence, on 26 December 1251. She died in 1274, after they had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Margaret (28 February 1260 – 9 April 1283), who married King Eirik II of Norway
2 CONT # Alexander, Prince of Scotland (21 January 1264 Jedburgh – 28 January 1284 Lindores Abbey); buried in Dunfermline Abbey
2 CONT # David (20 March 1272 – June 1281 Stirling Castle); buried in Dunfermline Abbey
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Lanercost Chronicle, Alexander did not spend his decade as a widower alone: "he used never to forbear on account of season nor storm, nor for perils of flood or rocky cliffs, but would visit none too creditably nu
2 CONC ns or matrons, virgins or widows as the fancy seized him, sometimes in disguise."
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of Alexander's reign, the death of all three of his children within a few years made the question of the succession one of pressing importance. In 1284 he induced the Estates to recognize as his heir-presumptive hi
2 CONC s granddaughter Margaret, the "Maid of Norway". The need for a male heir led him to contract a second marriage to Yolande de Dreux on 1 November 1285.
2 CONT
2 CONT But the sudden death of the king dashed all such hopes. Alexander died in a fall from his horse in the dark while riding to visit the queen at Kinghorn in Fife on 19 March 1286, having spent the evening at Edinburgh Castle oversee
2 CONC ing a meeting with royal advisors. He was advised by them not to make the journey over to Fife because of weather conditions, but travelled anyway. Alexander became separated from his guides and it is assumed that in the dark his 
2 CONC horse lost its footing. The 44-year old king was found dead on the shore the following morning with a broken neck. Some texts have said that he fell off a cliff. Although there is no cliff at the site where his body was found ther
2 CONC e is a very steep rocky embankment - which would have been fatal in the dark. After Alexander's death, his strong realm was plunged into a period of darkness that would eventually lead to war with England. Had Alexander, who was a
2 CONC  strong monarch, lived, things might have worked out differently (Ashley 2002, p. 156). He was buried in Dunfermline Abbey.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Alexander left no surviving children the heir to the throne was his unborn child by Queen Yolande. When Yolande's pregnancy ended in a still-birth in November of 1286, Alexander's granddaughter Margaret became the heir. Margare
2 CONC t died, still uncrowned, on her way to Scotland in 1290. The inauguration of John Balliol as king on 30 November 1292 ended the six years of interregnum when the Guardians of Scotland governed the land.
2 CONT
2 CONT ====Sources
2 CONT * Scott, Robert McNair. Robert the Bruce: King of Scots, 1996
2 CONT * Ashley, Mike (2002), British Kings & Queens, Carroll & Graf, ISBN 0-7867-1104-3 .
2 CONT
