1 OCCU Count of Bar 1302-1336, Count of Bar 1302 - Nov. 1336, Count of Bar, 1302-death; Captured in war against Frederick in 1313 and ransomed in 1314
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Edouard I de Bar, Comte de Bar1 M, #104564, b. 1294, d. 1337
2 CONT
2 CONT      Edouard I de Bar, Comte de Bar was born in 1294.
2 CONT
2 CONT  He was the son of Henry III de Bar, Comte de Bar and Eleanor of England.1 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Marie de Bourgogne, daughter of Robert II de Bourgogne, Duc de Bourgogne and Agnes de France, in 1310.3
2 CONT
2 CONT  He died in 1337, drowning in a shipwreck. 2
2 CONT      Edouard I de Bar, Comte de Bar gained the title of Comte de Bar.2
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p10457.htm#i104564
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_I_of_Bar
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Edward I of Bar
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I (circa 1293 – November 1336), grandson and namesake of Edward I of England, was the Count of Bar from 1302 to his death. He was a minor when he succeeded his father, Henry III, as count and ruled under the regency of his 
2 CONC grandfather, as his mother, Eleanor of England, was dead since 1298.
2 CONT
2 CONT The county was governed on Edward's behalf by John of Puisaye, Theobald, Bishop of Liège, and Renaud, Bishop of Metz.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1308, he accompanied Frederick IV of Lorraine into battle. In 1310, he married Mary, daughter of Robert II, Duke of Burgundy, and was declared to have attained his majority. Then he purchased the lordship of Stenay from his unc
2 CONC le John, the aforementioned lord of Puisaye. In 1313, he was captured in war against Frederick and not ransomed until 1314. He constructed a hydraulic forge at Moyeuvre-Grande in 1323. In 1324, he was again allied in military oper
2 CONC ations with the duke of Lorraine, and also with the King of Bohemia, John, and the Archbishop of Trier, Baldwin of Luxembourg. This operations was the War of Metz, for each of the allied lords was owed something by the citizens of
2 CONC  Metz. Edward demanded compensation for garrisoning the city with his own troops during a conflict with the bishop of Verdun
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1336, Edward died in a shipwreck off the coast of Famagusta, Cyprus, while en route to a Crusade. By his wife, he left three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry IV, his successor
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor (died 1332), married (1330) Rudolph, Duke of Lorraine, son of Frederick IV
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice, married Guy I of Gonzaga, Lord of Mantua
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]In Popular Culture
2 CONT
2 CONT In conspiracy theories, such as the one promoted in The Holy Blood and the Holy Grail, Edward I of Bar has been alleged to be the fourth Grand Master of the Priory of Sion.
2 CONT
2 CONT PVD's first cousin 19 times removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/133956015/%C3%A9douard-de_bar
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I (died November 1336), grandson and namesake of King Edward I of England, was the Count of Bar from 1302 to his death. He was a minor when he succeeded his father, Henry III, as count and ruled under the regency of his gra
2 CONC ndfather, as his mother Eleanor was dead since 1298.
2 CONT
2 CONT The county was governed on Edward's behalf by John of Puisaye, Theobald, Bishop of Liège, and Renaud, Bishop of Metz.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1308, he accompanied Frederick IV of Lorraine into battle. In 1310, he married Mary, daughter of Robert II, Duke of Burgundy, and was declared to have attained his majority. Then he purchased the lordship of Stenay from his unc
2 CONC le John, the aforementioned lord of Puisaye. In 1313, he was captured in war against Frederick and not ransomed until 1314. He constructed a hydraulic forge at Moyeuvre-Grande in 1323. In 1324, he was again allied in military oper
2 CONC ations with the duke of Lorraine, and also with the King of Bohemia, John, and the Archbishop of Trier, Baldwin of Luxembourg. This operations was the War of Metz, for each of the allied lords was owed something by the citizens of
2 CONC  Metz. Edward demanded compensation for garrisoning the city with his own troops during a conflict with the bishop of Verdun
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1336, Edward died in a shipwreck off the coast of Famagusta, Cyprus, while en route to a Crusade. By his wife, he left three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry IV, his successor
2 CONT Eleanor (died 1332), married (1330) Rudolph, Duke of Lorraine, son of Frederick IV
2 CONT Beatrice, married Guido Gonzaga, Lord of Mantua (they became paternal grandparents of Francesco I Gonzaga)
2 CONT
2 CONT In conspiracy theories, such as the one promoted in The Holy Blood and the Holy Grail, Edward I of Bar has been alleged to be the fourth Grand Master of the Priory of Sion.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward I, Count of Bar
2 CONT Spouse(s) Mary of Burgundy
2 CONT Noble family House of Montbéliard
2 CONT Father Henry III, Count of Bar
2 CONT Mother Eleanor of England
2 CONT Died November 1336
2 CONT Famagusta
