1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Peter I of Bourbon (Pierre Ier, Duc de Bourbon in French; 1311 – 19 September 1356) was the second Duke of Bourbon, from 1342 to his death. Read more on Wikipedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Since 2007, the English Wikipedia page of Peter I, Duke of Bourbon has received more than 148,522 page views. His biography is available in 23 different languages on Wikipedia. Peter I, Duke of Bourbon is the 3,837th most popular 
2 CONC politician (down from 3,707th in 2019), the 1,218th most popular biography from France (down from 1,213th in 2019) and the 298th most popular French Politician.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter I, Duke of Bourbon is most famous for his role in the French Wars of Religion. He was a leader of the Catholic League and was a member of the House of Bourbon.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_I,_Duke_of_Bourbon
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00002075&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Ier, né en 1311, mort à Poitiers en 1356, fut duc de Bourbon de 1342 à 1356.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il était fils de Louis Ier, duc de Bourbon et comte de la Marche, et de Marie d'Avesnes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Il fit ses premières armes en 1341 sous les ordres du duc de Normandie, le futur roi Jean II de France et combattit en Bretagne, alors en pleine guerre. Il permit notamment au prétendant français Charles de Blois de prendre posses
2 CONC sion du duché.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1346, il combattit à Crécy et y fut blessé. En 1355, il fut envoyé dans le Languedoc avec le titre de lieutenant général du roi, et accomplit sa mission avec succès. Il se battit à la bataille de Poitiers et y fut tué, en faisa
2 CONC nt rempart de son corps devant son roi.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Famille [modifier]
2 CONT
2 CONT Il épousa en 1336 Isabelle de Valois (1313-1383), fille de Charles de France, comte de Valois, d'Alençon, de Chartres, d'Anjou, du Maine et de Mahaut de Saint-Pol. Ils eurent huit enfants :
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis II (1337 † 1410), duc de Bourbon 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne (1337 † 1378), mariée en 1350 à Charles V (1338 † 1380), roi de France 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (1339 † 1361), mariée en 1352 à Pierre Ier le Cruel (1334 † 1369), roi de Castille et de León 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne (1341 † 1402), mariée en 1355 à Amédée VI († 1383), comte de Savoie 
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine (1342 † 1427), mariée en 1359 à Jean VI († 1388), comte d'Harcourt et d'Aumale et baron d'Elbeuf 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite de Bourbon (1344 † ap.1416), mariée en 1359 à Arnaud VIII Amanieu (1338 † 1401), sire d'Albret, comte de Dreux 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (1345 † ) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie (1347 † 1401), prieure de Poissy 
2 CONT
2 CONT Précédé par Pierre Ier de Bourbon Suivi par 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Ier 
2 CONT
2 CONT duc de Bourbon Louis II 
2 CONT
2 CONT Récupérée de « http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre_Ier_de_Bourbon »
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter I of Bourbon (1311 – 19 September 1356, Poitiers) was the second Duke of Bourbon, from 1342 to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter was son of Louis I of Bourbon, whom he also succeeded as Grand Chamberlain of France, and Mary of Avesnes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Peter is reported to have been somewhat mentally unstable, a trait of nervous breakdowns presumably hereditary that showed clearly for example in his daughter Joan of Bourbon, the queen, and in her son, king Charles VI of Fra
2 CONC nce, as well as in Peter's only surviving son, Duke Louis II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early career
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter I took part in several of the early campaigns of the Hundred Years War which broke out in 1337.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1339 he took part in Jean de Marigny, Bishop of Beauvais's failed attack on Bordeaux.
2 CONT
2 CONT In autumn 1341 he took part in the John, Duke of Normandy's campaign in Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was present at the coronation of Pope Clement VI at Avignon 19 May 1342.
2 CONT
2 CONT Summer 1342 he was together with the Raoul I of Brienne, Count of Eu given command of the covering force protecting France from attacks from the north while king Philip VI campaigned in Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1343 he and the Dauphin of Viennois were the French ambassadors at a peace conference at Avignon, but the negotiations were fruitless as the Edward III of England declined to send any but the most junior member of the em
2 CONC bassy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant in Languedoc
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 August 1345 Peter I was appointed by Philip VI as his lieutenant on the south-west march. His opponent was to be Henry, Earl of Derby (later Earl and Duke of Lancaster) who completed disembarking his army at Bordeaux the day 
2 CONC after Peter I's appointment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter I arrived to take up his lieutenancy in Languedoc in September. By then the Earl of Derby had already opened his campaign, throwing the French defences into disarray with the capture of Bergerac and the destruction of the Fr
2 CONC ench army present there the previous month. Bourbon set up headquarters at Angoulême and begun an extensive recruitment campaign to raise a new army, command of which fell to the Duke of Normandy. However on 21 October the Earl of
2 CONC  Derby won another crushing victory outside Auberoche over parts of this force. The Duke of Normandy abandoned his campaign once he heard the news. In early November he disbanded his army and left for the north.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Earl of Derby exploited the absence of a French commander in the field to lay siege to the important fortress-city of La Réole. Bourbon proclaimed the arrière-ban in Languedoc and the march provinces in an attempt to find troo
2 CONC ps to relieve the siege. However the results were poor as many of the potential recruits were still on their way home from the army just disbanded by John of Normandy. Attempts by John I, Count of Armagnac to raise troops from his
2 CONC  domains in the Rouergue also produced little. Early January 1346 the garrison of La Réole marched away under truce.
2 CONT
2 CONT Winter 1346 Bourbon kept his winter quarters at the provincial capital of Agen, a city which quickly was becoming isolated as many of the lesser towns were captured or defected to the English. Spring however opened with the so far
2 CONC  greatest French effort in the south-west. Bourbon and the Bishop of Beauvais raised a new army at Toulouse, in part financed by the Pope whose nephew had been captured by Derby the previous year, while John of Normandy brought wi
2 CONC th him a substantial number of nobles from the north including such dignitaries as the Eudes IV, Duke of Burgundy, Raoul II of Brienne, Count of Eu the Constable of France, both Marshals and the Master of Crossbowmen. In April Nor
2 CONC mandy laid siege to the town of Aiguillon which controlled the confluence between the Lot and the Garonne. There they still remained in August when John of Normandy was urgently recalled to the north to help stop Edward III who ha
2 CONC d landed in Normandy. And so the French 1346 campaign in the south ended having accomplished nothing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Diplomatic missions
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1347 he took part in fruitless negotiations with the English outside Calais in the days just before that city's capitulation.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 February 1354 he was together with the Guy, Cardinal of Boulogne appointed as King John II's commissioners to King Charles II of Navarre, empowered to offer whatever Charles wanted. The two met the King of Navarre in the cast
2 CONC le of Mantes, accompanied by the two dowager Queens and droves of courtiers and ministers, most of who more or less openly sympathized with Charles of Navarre. The treaty concluded 22 February granted to Charles of Navarre a consi
2 CONC derable part of Lower Normandy which he was to hold with the same rights as the Duke of Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1355 he was sent together with the Chancellor of France Pierre de la Forêt on a diplomatic mission to Avignon where they were to meet with an English embassy led by Henry of Lancaster and Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of 
2 CONC Arundel. The purpose of the mission was to formally ratify a peace treaty based on a draft drawn up at Guînes the previous year. However since then French policy had changed, the French ambassadors had only come to reject the Engl
2 CONC ish demands and had nothing new to offer. Negotiations therefore quickly broke down and the conference ended having accomplished nothing except prolonging the existing truce a few more months until 24 June.
2 CONT
2 CONT May 1355 when it became apparent that open war was about to break out between the King of France and a King of Navarre allied to England the Duke of Bourbon belonged to the party fronted by the Dowager Queens who lobbied John II o
2 CONC n Charles of Navarre's behalf. In the end John II gave way and on 31 May agreed to pardon Charles of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT In July the Duke of Bourbon and the Chancellor met with English ambassadors to negotiate the extension of the truce. As both the French and English governments had decided to resume the war these negotiations were naturally quite 
2 CONC empty and fruitless.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter was killed in the Battle of Poitiers 19 September 1356.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter I, Duke of Bourbon
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter I of Bourbon (1311 – September 19, 1356, Poitiers) was the second Duke of Bourbon, from 1342 to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter was son of Louis I of Bourbon, whom he also succeeded as Grand Chamberlain of France, and Mary of Hainaut.
2 CONT
2 CONT Duke Peter is reported to have been mentally somewhat instable, a trait of nervous breakdowns presumably hereditary that showed clearly for example in his daughter Jeanne de Bourbon, the queen, and in her son, king Charles VI of F
2 CONC rance, as well as in Peter's only surviving son, Duke Louis II.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 25 January 1326 he married Isabella of Valois, daughter of Charles, Count of Valois and his third wife Mahaut of Chatillon. Peter and Isabella had only one son, Louis, and seven daughters. These were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne (1338–1378), married King Charles V of France
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche (1339–1361, Medina-Sidonia), married King Pedro of Castile in 1353 in Valladolid, poisoned by her husband
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonne (1341 – January 19, 1402, Château de Mâcon), married Amadeus VI, Count of Savoy in 1355 in Paris
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherine (1342–1427, Paris), married John VI, Count of Harcourt
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret (1344–1416), married Arnaud Amanieu, Viscount of Tartas
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabelle (b. 1345)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary (1347–1401, Poissy), Prioress of Poissy
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter died in the Battle of Poitiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Foundation for Medieval Genealogy Accessed November 17, 2007
2 CONT
