2 CONT
2 CONT ii)         PHILIPPA de Milly (-after [1304]).  Minieri Riccio names “Filippa” as daughter of Geoffroy de Milly by his second wife, adding that she inherited all the fiefs of her mother and her maternal grandparents, noting that s
2 CONC he married firstly “il milite Ugo de Sully detto Rosso, col quale procreò Giovanni Gualtieri”, secondly “il milite Guido de Vademont” (from whom she was recorded as widow 29 Apr 1302), and thirdly “Filippo di Fiandra conte di Chie
2 CONC ti e di Loreto”, after whose death she returned to Naples[412].  Her family origin and two marriages are confirmed by the following document: a charter dated Jan 1299 names “Philippa de Miliaco, veuve d’Hugues dit Rufus de Suliaco
2 CONC  et à présent femme de Gui de Vaudémont chevalier”[413].  Charles II King of Sicily [Anjou-Capet] declared that, should Guy de Vaudémont die during the campaign against Sicily (in revolt), “sa femme Philippe” should retain her dow
2 CONC er (agreed by a second charter of the same date) even though childless, by charter dated 2 Jul 1299[414].  The testament of Philippe de Flandre, dated 19 Feb 1308 at Naples, bequeathed half of all his properties in Flanders or els
2 CONC ewhere to “sua seconda moglie Filippa di Milly”, and appointed as his heirs “i suoi figliuoli Ludovico e Filippo”, whom he obliged to pay dowry to “Margarita loro sorella”[415].  Philippa de Milly petitioned Charles II King of Sic
2 CONC ily for funds on behalf of “gli altri due suoi figliuoli Ludovico e Filippo e la figliuola Margarita qui de...progenie comitum Flandrie processerunt”, noting that “l’unico suo figluolo Giovanni Gualtieri de Sully detto Rosso avuto
2 CONC  dal primo marito” had “amplas facultates” from the succession of his father[416].  Philippa de Milly died 15 Jun 1309 at Gaeta[417].  
2 CONT
