2 CONT >'''From Wikipedia: [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_II,_Count_of_Flanders "Louis II of Flanders"]'''
2 CONT >In 1347, he married Margaret of Brabant (1323–1368), daughter of John III, Duke of Brabant. They had three children:
2 CONT >1. Pierre (died young)
2 CONT >2. Charles (predeceased his father)
2 CONT >3. Margaret III of Flanders (1348–1405)
2 CONT
2 CONT >>'''From [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_Brabant,_Countess_of_Flanders Wikipedia ''(6 Jun 2022)'': Margaret of Brabant]:'''
2 CONT >>Margaret of Brabant (9 February 1323 – 1380.[1]) was a countess consort of Flanders. She was the second daughter of Duke John III of Brabant and Mary of Évreux. She was the only child of Duke John to have children. In 1347 she m
2 CONC arried Louis II of Flanders, who was then sixteen years old and already count of Flanders.[2] On 13 April 1350 their daughter, Margaret III, Countess of Flanders, was baptized.[3] Through this daughter, their only surviving child,
2 CONC  Brabant came under the influence of Burgundy when she married Philip the Bold.
2 CONT >>On 26 September 1371, Margaret and her mother-in-law, Margaret of France, countess of Artois, with their attendants, were guests of Philip the Bold, duke of Burgundy for supper in Lens.[4] Soon afterwards, Margaret left Flanders
2 CONC  and went to reside permanently, far from her husband, in the county of Réthel, where she died in April 1380 (after Easter).[5] 
2 CONT >>'''References'''
2 CONT >>1. Paul Thomas ''Textes historiques sur Lille et le Nord de la France avant 1789'', Revue du Nord Année 1930, Volume 16, Numéro 62, pp. 97-112, p. 105
2 CONT >>2. Anselme. ''Histoire généalogique et chronologique de la maison royale de France''. Vol. 2, p. 739.
2 CONT >>3.  Anselme. ''Histoire généalogique et chronologique de la maison royale de France''. Vol. 2, p. 741.
2 CONT >>4. Famiglietti, R.C. (2015). ''Audouin Chauveron''. Vol. 2. p. 161.
2 CONT >>5. Famiglietti, R.C. (2015). ''Audouin Chauveron''. Vol. 2. p. 159.
2 CONT
