1 NOTE Tysk kg
1 NOTE KING OF GERMANY 1273-1291
1 NOTE Rudolf I of the House of Hapsburg, of Austria, ruled Germany in 1257 during the Great Interregnum, as a nominal emperor.  His rule coincided with that of Richard, earl of Cornwall who had also been elected in 1256.  Rudolf I ruled
2 CONC  until 1273, and was then made Holy Roman Emperor until he died in 1291.   He was succeeded by Adolf of Nassau.  Another rival emperor during the Great Interregnum was Alfonso of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolf I Duke of Hapsburg, Holy Roman Emperor, ousted Ottakor of Bohemia as King of the Empire of Austria in 1276, and took over the empire.  He ruled until 1282, and was succeeded by his son duke Albert I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ottokar II of Bohemia had possessed the Babenberg lands of Austria between 1246 and 1278. Rudolf I of German House of Hapsburg defeated Ottokar II in 1278, and those lands then passed into the Hapsburg rule until 1806.  The line o
2 CONC f Hapsburg Holy Roman Emperors from 1438-1806 turned Austria into one of the most dynamic states in Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rudolf I of the house of Hapsburg-Austria finally won out in the election for Holy Roman Emperor in 1273.  After the death of Conrad the Salian in 1254, Rudolf was one of four vying for the elected office.  He ruled as Holy Roman 
2 CONC Emperor from 1273 to 1291.  His successor was Adolf of Nassau.
