2 CONT '''Philippe de Coucy'''1 
2 CONT F, #107249, '''b. 1367, d. circa October 1411'''
2 CONT Last Edited=24 Oct 2016
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=0.27%
2 CONT '''     Philippe de Coucy was born in 1367.1 She was the daughter''' of Enguerrand VII de Coucy, Sire de Coucy and Isabella of England.1 '''She and Robert de Vere, 9th Earl of Oxford were divorced in 1387.2 She married Robert de V
2 CONC ere, 9th Earl of Oxford''', son of Thomas de Vere, 8th Earl of Oxford and Matilda de Ufford, '''before 5 October 1376.2 She died circa October 1411.'''
2 CONT '''     After her marriage, Philippe de Coucy was styled as Countess of Oxford.3 In 1397 she inherited the English estates of her father.'''3
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Families: The Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 94. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Families.
2 CONT 2.[S37] BP2003 volume 3, page 3464. See link for full details for this source. Hereinafter cited as. [S37]
2 CONT 3.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extinc
2 CONC t or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 73. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT From: https://www.thepeerage.com/p10725.htm#i107249
2 CONT ____
2 CONT '''Robert de Vere, 9th Earl of Oxford''', KG (16 January 1362 – 22 November 1392) was a favourite and court companion of King Richard II of England. He was the ninth Earl of Oxford and the first Duke of Ireland and the only Marque
2 CONC ss of Dublin. He was also the first person to be created a Marquess.
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT '''Robert de Vere''' was the only son of Thomas de Vere, 8th Earl of Oxford and Maud de Ufford.[1] He succeeded his father as earl in 1371, and was created Marquess of Dublin in 1385. The next year he was created Duke of Ireland. 
2 CONC He was thus the first marquess, and only the second non-princely duke (after Henry of Grosmont, 1st Duke of Lancaster in 1337), in England. King Richard's close friendship with de Vere was disagreeable to the political establishme
2 CONC nt.[citation needed] This displeasure was exacerbated by the earl's elevation to the new title of Duke of Ireland in 1386.[2] His relationship with King Richard was very close and rumoured by Thomas Walsingham to be homosexual.[3]
2 CONT '''Robert, Duke of Ireland, was married to Philippa de Coucy, the King's first cousin''' (her mother, Isabella, was the sister of the King's father, Edward, the Black Prince and the eldest daughter of Edward III). Robert had an af
2 CONC fair with Agnes de Launcekrona, a lady-in-waiting of Richard's queen, Anne of Bohemia. '''In 1387, the couple were separated and eventually divorced; Robert''' took Launcekrona as his second wife.
2 CONT Downfall
2 CONT Since '''Robert''' was hugely unpopular with the other nobles and magnates, his close relationship with King Richard was one of the catalysts for the emergence of an organised opposition to Richard's rule in the form of the Lords 
2 CONC Appellant.
2 CONT In 1387, '''Robert''' led Richard's forces to defeat at the Battle of Radcot Bridge outside Oxford, against the forces of the Lords Appellant. He fled the field and his forces were left leaderless and compelled into ignominious su
2 CONC rrender. He travelled abroad into exile after Radcot Bridge.
2 CONT He was attainted and sentenced to death in absentia by the Merciless Parliament of 1388, which also made him forfeit his titles and lands. People associated with him were also affected, for the parliament dismissed his Irish Admin
2 CONC istration, composed of John Stanley, his deputy, who had been serving as Lord Lieutenant of Ireland, James Butler, 3rd Earl of Ormond, the governor, Bishop Alexander de Balscot of Meath, Lord Chancellor of Ireland, and Sir Robert 
2 CONC Crull, Lord High Treasurer of Ireland.[4]
2 CONT Death
2 CONT He died in or near Louvain in 1392 of injuries sustained during a boar hunt. Three years later, on the anniversary of his death, 22 November 1395, Richard II had his embalmed body brought back to England for burial. It was recorde
2 CONC d by the chronicler Thomas Walsingham that many magnates did not attend the re-burial ceremony because they 'had not yet digested their hatred' of him. The king had the coffin opened to kiss his lost friend's hand and to gaze on h
2 CONC is face one last time.[5]
2 CONT Succession
2 CONT After Ireland's death, his uncle Sir Aubrey de Vere, was restored to the family titles and estates, becoming 10th Earl of Oxford. The Marquessate of Dublin became extinct.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_de_Vere,_9th_Earl_of_Oxford
2 CONT ___
2 CONT '''Robert De VERE (1° D. Ireland)'''
2 CONT Born: 16 Jan 1362
2 CONT Acceded: 1371
2 CONT Died: 1392, Louvain, Belgium
2 CONT Buried: Earls Colne
2 CONT Notes: Knight of the Garter. 1° Marquess of Dublin and 9° E. Oxford. The Complete Peerage vol.X,p.227-232. Once the favourite of Richard II, he died in exile and poverty at Louvain. He was attainted in 1387. Died after being injur
2 CONC ed at a boar hunt. 
2 CONT Father: Thomas De VERE (8° E. Oxford)
2 CONT Mother: Maud De UFFORD
2 CONT '''Married 1: Phillipa De COURCY (b. 1367)''' (dau. of Enguerrand Le Brun, E. Bedford, and Isabella Plantagenet) '''5 Oct 1376 Divorce 1387'''
2 CONT Married 2: Agnes LANCECRONE
2 CONT From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/VERE.htm#Robert%20De%20VERE%20(1%C2%B0%20D.%20Ireland)
2 CONT ________
2 CONT Enguerrand VII de Coucy, KG (1340 – 18 February 1397), also known as Ingelram de Coucy and Ingelram de Couci, was a medieval French nobleman and the last Lord of Coucy. He became a son-in-law of King Edward III of England followin
2 CONC g his marriage to the king's daughter, Isabella of England, and the couple was subsequently granted several English estates, among them the title Earl of Bedford. Coucy fought in the Battle of Nicopolis in 1396 as part of a failed
2 CONC  crusade against the Ottoman Empire, but was taken prisoner and contracted the bubonic plague. He died in captivity the following year at Bursa.
2 CONT Coucy had no surviving legitimate sons. Fierce legal disputes were fought over the succession of his lordship of Coucy, which, as a result, passed to the crown lands of France.
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT Coucy became Lord of Coucy at the death of his father, Enguerrand VI, Lord of Coucy, during the sequence of battles ending with the Battle of Crécy in 1346. He also gained the titles of 4th Lord Gynes, Sire d' Oisy, in the distric
2 CONC t of Marle, and the Sire de La Fère. His mother, Catherine of Austria, oldest daughter of Leopold I, Duke of Austria, had died in 1349, during a wave of the Black Death. Coucy first became involved in the war against England at th
2 CONC e age of fifteen, serving among the barons of Picardy in the battalion of Moreau de Fiennes. In 1358, at the age of eighteen, Coucy acted as a leader during the suppression of the peasant revolt known as the Jacquerie.
2 CONT Between England and France
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT In 1365, the wealthy Coucy was betrothed and married to the 33-year-old Isabella of England, who has been described as an over-indulged, willful, and wildly extravagant princess. To care for her personal needs, her father settled 
2 CONC a substantial annual income on her for life, as well as gifts of costly jewellery, and properties that included manors, castles, and priories. Coucy was her choice as a husband, as she wished to marry for love after the failure of
2 CONC  previous betrothal negotiations with several noble houses of Europe. Coucy received, as part of the marriage settlement, the restoration of former Coucy lands in Yorkshire, Lancaster, Westmorland, and Cumberland, England. He was 
2 CONC also released as a hostage for the French treaty requirements, with no payment of ransom. In November 1365, after their marriage on 27 July, the couple was given leave to travel to France. Their daughter, Marie de Coucy, was born 
2 CONC in April 1366 at Coucy in Picardy, France. During a subsequent visit to England with his new family, Coucy was created as Earl of Bedford, and was inducted into the Order of the Garter.[1] In 1367, Coucy's '''second daughter, Phil
2 CONC ippa de Coucy, was born in England'''. At this time, Coucy was presented with additional French lands, receiving the title Count of Soissons, which had come to King Edward III through the payment of ransom.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT Coucy married as his first wife, Isabella of England,[3] and had two children by her:
2 CONT *Marie de Coucy, Countess of Soissons (1366–1405),[4] married Henry of Bar, Marquis de Pont-à-Mousson and Lord of Marle.
2 CONT *'''Philippa de Coucy (1367–1411),[4] married Robert de Vere, 9th Earl of Oxford.'''
2 CONT Coucy married as his second wife, Isabelle of Lorraine,[5] daughter of John I, Duke of Lorraine and Sophie of Württemberg, and had one child by her:
2 CONT *Isabelle de Coucy (1386–1411), married Philip II, Count of Nevers[6]
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enguerrand_VII_de_Coucy
2 CONT ____
2 CONT Isabella of England (16 June 1332 – c. 5 October 1382) was the eldest daughter of King Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainault, and the wife of Enguerrand de Coucy, Earl of Bedford, by whom she had two daughters. She was ma
2 CONC de a Lady of the Garter in 1376.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT Isabella's husband had been brought to England in 1360 as a hostage exchanged for the freedom of King John II of France, an English prisoner. They married on 27 July 1365, at Windsor Castle, by which time Isabella was in her thirt
2 CONC ies.[2] Her father, Edward III, gave her a dowry of £4,000 and a large lifetime annual income, together with expensive amounts of jewellery and lands; de Coucy was restored to his family's lands in Yorkshire, Lancaster, Westmorlan
2 CONC d and Cumberland, and was released as a hostage without any need for ransom.
2 CONT In November 1365, Isabella and her husband were permitted to enter France; their first daughter, Marie, was born at the family lands at Coucy in April 1366.[2] They later returned for a visit to England; on this occasion, Enguerra
2 CONC nd was made Earl of Bedford on 11 May 1366, which made Isabella Countess of Bedford as well as Lady of Coucy. After the birth of Isabella's '''second daughter, Philippa, in 1367''', Enguerrand and Isabella were also made Count and
2 CONC  Countess of Soissons by Edward. Because her husband also served the King of France as a military leader, he was frequently away from home; consequently, Isabella, though living principally with Enguerrand at Coucy, made frequent 
2 CONC visits to her family in England. She was made a Lady of the Garter in 1376.
2 CONT Isabella bore two children by her marriage to Enguerrand de Coucy:
2 CONT *Marie de Coucy (April 1366 – 1405), suo jure Countess of Soissons. She married Henry of Bar, a nephew of Charles V of France, by whom she had children. After her father's death, she disputed the inheritance of his lands with her 
2 CONC stepmother, Isabelle of Lorraine, before dying suddenly. After her death, her patrimony was absorbed into the French royal estates.
2 CONT *'''Philippa de Coucy (1367–1411), who was born at Eltham, and named after Isabella's mother.[2] She married Robert de Vere, the Earl of Oxford, in 1371, and lived thereafter in England. She died childless.'''
2 CONT Death
2 CONT Isabella was at her father's side when he died on 21 June 1377 having been urgently summoned home from France by couriers the previous April.[5]
2 CONT After the accession of Richard II, Isabella's nephew, in August 1377, Enguerrand resigned all of his English ties and possessions. Isabella then died in England, separated from her husband and eldest daughter, Marie.
2 CONT Her death was between 17 June and 5 October 1382.[6][7] She was buried in Greyfriars Church, Newgate, London. Seven years after her death, her husband took as his second wife, Isabelle, the daughter of John I, Duke of Lorraine and
2 CONC  Sophie of Württemberg.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella,_Countess_of_Bedford
2 CONT ______
2 CONT Isabella PLANTAGENET (C. Bedford)
2 CONT Born: 16 Jun 1332, Woodstock Palace, Oxfordshire, England
2 CONT Died: BEF 4 May 1379, London, England
2 CONT Buried: Greyfriars Church, Newgate, London, Middlesex, England
2 CONT Father: EDWARD III PLANTAGENET (King of England)
2 CONT Mother: Phillippa of Hainault (Queen of England)
2 CONT Married: Enguerrand Le BRUN de Courcy (7º E. Bedford) 27 Jul 1365, Windsor Castle, Windsor, Berkshire, England
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT 1. Mary De COURCY (b. 1366) (m. Robert (Henry) De Barr, Marquess Pont-â-Mousson)
2 CONT 2. '''Phillippa De COURCY (b. 1367) (m. Robert De Vere, 1º D. Ireland)'''
2 CONT From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/PLANTAGENET.htm#Isabella%20PLANTAGENET%20(C.%20Bedford)
2 CONT ____
2 CONT '''Sir Robert de Vere'''
2 CONT Birth	16 Jan 1362
2 CONT England
2 CONT Death	1392 (aged 29–30)
2 CONT Leuven, Arrondissement Leuven, Flemish Brabant, Belgium
2 CONT Burial	Colne Priory
2 CONT Earls Colne, Braintree District, Essex, England
2 CONT Find a Grave Memorial ID: 112244684
2 CONT Knight of the Garter, 9th Earl of Oxford, hereditary Chamberlain of England, Chief Justice of Chester, Chief Justice of North Wales. A favorite companion of King Richard II.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son and heir to Sir Thomas de Vere and Maud de Ufford. Grandson of Sir John de Vere and Maud de Badlesmere, Sir Ralph de Ufford and Maud of Lancaster.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Robert married Philippe de Coucy, the younger daughter and co-heiress''' of Enguerrand de Coucy, Earl of Bedford and Isabel, the daughter of King Edward III, and the first cousin of the current king, Richard II. '''They were ma
2 CONC rried on or before 05 Oct 1376, and had no issue. They were divorced in 1387.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondly, Robert married Agnes de Lancecrone, by whom he had been carrying on an affair for years. Agnes was a Lady-in-Waiting for the Queen, Anne of Bohemia. There is no listing of their marriage date or children.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1382, King Richard II granted the Chamberlainship to Aubrey de Vere and his family, and in 1385, '''granted Robert Marquess of Dublin''' for life, also granting him the territory and lordship of Ireland with quasi-regal powers,
2 CONC  making '''Robert all powerful.''' On 13 Oct 1386, the Marquessate was revoked and he was made Duke of Ireland, and given Ireland and all its islands.
2 CONT
2 CONT The king granted '''Robert''' the royal castle of Berkhamstead, Hertfordshire for his residence for one year. His relationship with the king was frowned upon, rumored to be sexual.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 10th of 1387, '''Robert''' met with Gloucester, Arundel and Warwick accused '''Robert''' of treason, and pressured Richard to dismiss Michael de la Pole as his chancellor. On the 19th of December, the battle at Radcot 
2 CONC Bridge over the river Thames now outside Oxford involved Irish forces loyal to King Richard led by de Vere against the army of the recently formed Lords Appellant, the armies led by Henry Bolingbroke, Earl of Derby. Robert managed
2 CONC  to abandon the battlefield, escaping by means of the fog bank and swam the Thames, leaving his men leaderless with no choice but to surrender.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Robert''' left England for Utrecht and then Paris, then to Brabant. Back in England, the Lords Appellant managed to purge Richard's household, '''Robert''' was found guilty of treason, all his properties confiscated, his honour
2 CONC s forfeited.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''In 1389, the Pope (Urban or Boniface) declared his divorce null and invalid''', leaving Agnes in despair.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir Robert''' died at Louvain (then France, now Belgium) from an injury he received while boar hunting. He was originally buried at Louvain, until King Richard has his body exhumed and brought back to England, and buried at Col
2 CONC ne Priory. It is said King Richard had his coffin opened so that he might, for one last time, view the body and kiss the hand of his closest friend.
2 CONT
2 CONT After '''Robert's death''', the titles and estates were restored to his grandfather's brother, Sir Aubrey de Vere, who became the 10th Earl of Oxford. Agnes was present in 1396 when Isabel of France was delivered to Richard for ma
2 CONC rriage after Anne's death in 1394. Philippe made an agreement with her sister, Mary, so that Philippe became the sole heiress to their parents, and she was with Queen Isabel in 1401 when she crossed the channel, returning to Calai
2 CONC s after Richard's death in 1400.
2 CONT Family Members
2 CONT Parents
2 CONT Thomas de Vere
2 CONT 1335–1371
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud de Ufford Vere
2 CONT 1345–1413
2 CONT From: https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/112244684/robert_de_vere
