1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Descendant of kings [https://www.geni.com/people/William-the-Conqueror-king-of-England/5597380726000028489 William the Conqueror], [https://www.geni.com/people/Stephen-I-King-of-England/6000000000424802839?through=
2 CONC 6000000003051107855 Stephen I of England], [https://www.geni.com/people/Louis-VII-the-Young-king-of-France/6000000005791896564 Louis VII of France] and [https://www.geni.com/people/Manuel-II-Palaiologos-byzantine-emperor/600000000
2 CONC 0670279300 Manuel II byzantine emperor]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Palaeologus Thomas Palaiologos] or Palaeologus (Greek: Θωμάς Παλαιολόγος, Thōmas Palaiologos) (1409 – May 12, 1465) was Despot in Morea from 1428 until the Ottoman conquest in 1460. After the d
2 CONC esertion of his older brother to the Turks in 1460, Thomas Palaiologos became the most legitimate claimant to the Byzantine throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Palaeologus
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Life
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Imperial heirs
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Ancestry
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Palaiologos was the youngest surviving son of the Eastern Roman Emperor Manuel II Palaiologos and his wife Helena Dragaš. His maternal grandfather was Constantine Dragaš. His brothers included the Byzantine emperors John VI
2 CONC II Palaiologos and Constantine XI Palaiologos , as well as Theodore II Palaiologos and Demetrios Palaiologos, despots in Morea, and Andronikos Palaiologos, despot in Thessalonica. As a younger son, Thomas was never expected to rei
2 CONC gn, but his children became the only surviving heirs of the defunct Palaiologan dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like other imperial sons, Thomas Palaiologos was made a Despot (despotēs), and from 1428 joined his brothers Theodore and Constantine as governor of Morea. After the retirement of Theodore in 1443, he governed together with Consta
2 CONC ntine, until the latter became emperor (Constantine XI) in 1448. Thomas remained governor of Morea, but was forced to share rule with his older brother Demetrios from 1449. The Byzantine holdings in Morea had expanded considerably
2 CONC  at the expense of the Latin Principality of Achaea. After last war in 1430 virtually the entire peninsula was under Byzantine rule, and Thomas married Catherine Zaccaria, the daughter of the last Prince of Achaea Centurione II Za
2 CONC ccaria, succeeding to his father-in-law's possessions in 1432.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this period of success, the fortunes of Byzantine Morea declined, as the collegiate government by several brothers caused increasing friction. This became especially acute after the arrival of the ambitious Demetrios, who to
2 CONC ok a pro-Ottoman stance as opposed to Thomas' pro-western orientation. From 1447 the despots had become tributary vassals of the Ottoman Sultan. At the onset of the siege of Constantinople by Mehmed II of the Ottoman Empire, an Ot
2 CONC toman army was sent with orders to blockade Morea. After the conquest of Constantinople by Mehmed II on May 29, 1453, maintaining the status quo, the Sultan ordered the two brothers to continue as joint rulers in Morea.
2 CONT
2 CONT This order had been accepted for the first two years because of the Kantakouzenos family's revolt which started in 1453 during the fall of Constantinople by Demetrius I Kantakouzenos' grandchild Manuel. Only in the next year did t
2 CONC he forces of the Palaiologos brothers destroy the rebel forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT In these circumstances, and without Constantine XI to maintain peace in the family, Thomas sought western support against both the Ottomans and his competitive pro-Ottoman brother Demetrios. He allied with Genoa and the Pope, and 
2 CONC defeated Demetrios, who fled seeking help from the Ottomans in 1460. The Ottoman army duly attacked Morea and quickly breached the Hexamilion wall across the Isthmus of Corinth, which was too long to be effectively manned and defe
2 CONC nded by Thomas' forces. Thomas escaped with his family to Italy, where he had already been recognized as the legitimate heir to the Byzantine Empire at Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT The commanders of the garrisons of the fortified cities in Morea, deserted by their rulers, chose individually whether to fight or surrender, depending on their own will and circumstances. In the final battle of the Roman Empire i
2 CONC n its Byzantine incarnation, Graitzas Palaiologos, the military commander of the city of Salmenikos defeated Mehmed II, who after a month of siege returned home without conquering that "unimportant city". In the following year Gra
2 CONC itzas received an offer to become general of the Republic of Venice, which he accepted, thus leaving Salmenikos to the Ottomans.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Imperial heirs
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fall of Morea, Thomas lived in Rome, recognized throughout Christian Europe as the rightful Emperor of the East. To create greater support for his situation Thomas changed his religion to Catholicism in his last years of
2 CONC  life. After his death in 1465, the position of rightful Byzantine emperor fell to his older son Andreas Palaiologos, born in Mistra around 1453.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mehmed II conquered the Empire of Trebizond, de facto the last free territory of the ancient Roman state, in the year 1461. Nevertheless, Mehmed had already proclaimed himself "Roman Emperor" upon capturing Constantinople in 1453.
2 CONT
2 CONT Playing a great political game, Pope Paul II arranged in 1472 a marriage between the Catholic daughter of Thomas, Zoe Palaiologina (renamed Sophia), and Grand Prince Ivan III of Russia, with the hope of making Russia a Catholic co
2 CONC untry. This attempt to unite churches failed. Nonetheless, because of this marriage, Moscow began in the following century its imperial policy of "third Rome". Moreover, Thomas' great-grandson was Ivan IV of Russia, the first empe
2 CONC ror (tsar) of Russia to be crowned as such (the imperial title had already come into use under Ivan III and his son Vasili III of Russia). The last known descendant of Zoe/Sophia was Maria of Staritsa, wife of Livonia's king Magnu
2 CONC s . She died in 1610.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage with Catherine (Caterina) Zaccaria of Achaea, Thomas Palaiologos had at least four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena Palaiologina, who married Prince Lazar II of Serbia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew (Andreas) Palaiologos, who succeeded as claimant to the Byzantine throne
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel Palaiologos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zoe Palaiologina (renamed Sophia), who married Grand Prince Ivan III of Russia
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Thomas Palaiologos
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Palaiologos or Palaeologus (Greek: Θωμάς Παλαιολόγος, Thōmas Palaiologos) (1409 – May 12, 1465) was Despot in Morea from 1428 until the Ottoman conquest in 1460. After the desertion of his older brother to the Turks in 1460
2 CONC , Thomas Palaiologos became the most legitimate claimant to the Byzantine throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Palaiologos was the youngest surviving son of the Eastern Roman Emperor Manuel II Palaiologos [1] and his wife Helena Dragaš. His maternal grandfather was Constantine Dragaš. His brothers included the Byzantine emperors Joh
2 CONC n VIII Palaiologos [2] and Constantine XI Palaiologos , as well as Theodore II Palaiologos and Demetrios Palaiologos, Despots of the Morea, and Andronikos Palaiologos, Despot of Thessalonica. As youngest son, Thomas was never expe
2 CONC cted to reign, but his children became the only surviving heirs of the defunct Palaiologan dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like other imperial sons, Thomas Palaiologos was made a Despot (despotēs), and from 1428 joined his brothers Theodore and Constantine in the Morea. After the retirement of Theodore in 1443, he governed together with Constantine, u
2 CONC ntil the latter became emperor (Constantine XI) in 1448. Thomas remained Despot of the Morea, but was forced to share the rule with his older brother Demetrios from 1449. The Byzantine holdings in Morea had expanded considerably a
2 CONC t the expense of the Latin Principality of Achaea. After the last war in 1430 virtually the entire peninsula was under Byzantine rule, and Thomas married Catherine Zaccaria, the daughter of the last Prince of Achaea Centurione II 
2 CONC Zaccaria, succeeding to his father-in-law's possessions in 1432.
2 CONT
2 CONT After this period of success, the fortunes of Byzantine Morea declined, as the collegiate government by several brothers caused increasing friction. This became especially acute after the arrival of Demetrios, who took a pro-Ottom
2 CONC an stance as opposed to Thomas' pro-western orientation. From 1447 the Despots had become vassals of the Ottoman Sultan. At the onset of the siege of Constantinople by Mehmed II of the Ottoman Empire, an Ottoman army was sent with
2 CONC  orders to raid in the Morea, preventing help from being sent to The City. After the conquest of Constantinople by Mehmed II on May 29, 1453, to maintain the status quo, the Sultan ordered the two brothers to continue as joint rul
2 CONC ers in Morea.
2 CONT
2 CONT This order had been accepted for the first two years because of the Kantakouzenos family's revolt which started in 1453 during the fall of Constantinople by Demetrius I Kantakouzenos' grandchild Manuel. Only in the next year did t
2 CONC he forces of the Palaiologos brothers destroy the rebel forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT In these circumstances, and without Constantine XI to maintain peace in the family, Thomas sought western support against both the Ottomans and his competitive pro-Ottoman brother Demetrios. He allied with Genoa and the Pope, and 
2 CONC defeated Demetrios, who fled seeking help from the Ottomans in 1460. The Ottoman army duly attacked Morea and quickly breached the Hexamilion wall across the Isthmus of Corinth, which was too long to be effectively manned and defe
2 CONC nded by Thomas' forces. Thomas escaped with his family to Italy, where he had already been recognized as the legitimate heir to the Byzantine Empire at Rome.
2 CONT
2 CONT The commanders of the garrisons of the fortified cities in Morea, deserted by their rulers, chose individually whether to fight or surrender, depending on their own will and circumstances. In the final battle of the Roman Empire i
2 CONC n its Byzantine incarnation, Graitzas Palaiologos, the military commander of the city of Salmenikos defeated Mehmed II, who after a month of siege returned home without conquering that "unimportant city". In the following year Gra
2 CONC itzas received an offer to become general of the Republic of Venice, which he accepted, thus leaving Salmenikos to the Ottomans.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Imperial heirs
2 CONT
2 CONT After the fall of Morea, Thomas lived in Rome, recognized throughout Christian Europe as the rightful Emperor of the East. To create greater support for his situation Thomas changed his religion to Catholicism in his last years of
2 CONC  life. After his death in 1465, the position of rightful Byzantine emperor fell to his older son Andreas Palaiologos, born in Mistra around 1453.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mehmed II conquered the Empire of Trebizond, de facto the last free territory of the ancient Roman state, in the year 1461. Nevertheless, Mehmed had already proclaimed himself "Roman Emperor" upon capturing Constantinople in 1453.
2 CONT
2 CONT In an effort to reunite the Catholic Church and the Orthodox Church, Pope Paul II arranged in 1472 a marriage between the Catholic daughter of Thomas, Zoe Palaiologina (renamed Sophia), and Grand Prince Ivan III of Russia, with th
2 CONC e hope of making Russia a Catholic country. This attempt to unite churches failed. Nonetheless, because of this marriage, Moscow began in the following century its imperial policy of "third Rome". Moreover, Thomas' great-grandson 
2 CONC was Ivan IV of Russia, the first emperor (tsar) of Russia to be crowned as such (the imperial title had already come into use under Ivan III and his son Vasili III of Russia). The last known descendant of Zoe/Sophia was Maria of S
2 CONC taritsa, wife of Livonia's king Magnus . She died in 1610.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Portraits
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Palaiologos [3]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Family
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage with Catherine (Caterina) Zaccaria of Achaea, Thomas Palaiologos had at least four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena Palaiologina, who married Prince Lazar II of Serbia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew (Andreas) Palaiologos, who succeeded as claimant to the Byzantine throne
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel Palaiologos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zoe Palaiologina (renamed Sophia), who married Grand Prince Ivan III of Russia
