1 OCCU Connétable de Champagne
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Voir [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_II_de_Dampierre '''Wikipedia...''']
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_II_of_Dampierre
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy II of Dampierre (died 18 January 1216) was constable of Champagne, and Lord of Dampierre, Bourbon and Montluçon. He was the only son of William I of Dampierre, Lord of Dampierre, and Ermengarde of Mouchy. William I of Dampierr
2 CONC e was the son of Guy I, Lord of Dampierre and Viscount of Troyes, and Helvide de Baudémont.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy participated in the Third Crusade as a member of an advance party who initiated the Siege of Acre in the fall of 1189.[1] His name and arms are in the Salles des Croisades of the Palace of Versailles[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Castle chapel of Moëslains.
2 CONT Guy was the eldest son of William I of Dampierre[3] and Ermengarde of Toucy, lady of Champlay and daughter of Ithier III de Toucy and Elisabeth of Joigny.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the lifetime of his father, Guy received the Lordship of Moëslains and maybe the viscounty of Troyes. During this time, he signed charters under the name of Guy de Moëslains.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1174, Guy succeeded his father as Lord of Dampierre, Saint-Dizier and Saint-Just. Like his father before him, he is also named constable of Champagne, but this position was not hereditary.[4][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1189, he participated in the Third Crusade as a member of an advance party who initiated the Siege of Acre in the fall of the year.[1][6][7] After the departure of King Philip Augustus, he stayed in the Holy Land and fought at 
2 CONC the Battle of Arsuf. He came back to Dampierre in late 1192.[6] His name and arms are in the Salles des Croisades of the Palace of Versailles[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Church of Dampierre.
2 CONT In 1194, Guy fought with Philip Augustus against the English and was at the battle of Fréteval in the rearguard that covered the escape of the King. In 1202, he participated in the conquest of Normandy and fought at the battle of 
2 CONC Mirebeau, the siege of Château Gaillard and the catch of Falaise, Caen, Bayeux and Rouen.[9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1199, for service rendered, the King gave him the Lordship of Montluçon.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1210, Guy was sent at the head of a royal army sent by Philip Augustus to confiscate the lands of Count Guy II of Auvergne.[12] He took Nonette, the castle of Tournoël and Riom in December 1213, and removed Guy of Auvergne from
2 CONC  power. He was named by the King constable of Auvergne and managed the county for him. [12][13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy also fought at the battle of Bouvines and his participation was decisive in the French victory.[14]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the war of the Succession of Champagne, Guy supported the Countess-Regent Blanche of Navarre and her son Theobald against the pretender Erard of Brienne-Ramerupt and his wife Philippa of Champagne.[15]
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy died on 18 January 1216 and was buried in Saint-Laumer Abbey at Blois.[16]
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Castle of Dampierre.
2 CONT In the year 1196 he married Mathilde of Bourbon, daughter of Archambaud de Bourbon and Alix of Burgundy,[17] and had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Archambaud of Dampierre,[17] Lord of Bourbon and Montluçon after his father's death.
2 CONT William II of Dampierre, Lord of Dampierre, Saint-Dizier and Moëslains, married Margaret II of Flanders[17]
2 CONT Guy III of Dampierre, Lord of Saint-Just after his father's death.[18]
2 CONT Philippa Mahaut of Dampierre, married in 1205 to Guigues IV, Count of Forez.[18]
2 CONT Marie of Dampierre, married around 1201 to Hervé of Vierzon. Then widowed, she married secondly in 1220 to Henri I de Sully.[18]
2 CONT Joan of Dampierre.
2 CONT Margaret of Dampierre.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/GXDG-52F
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Dampierre-13
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/156638933/guy-de_dampierre-bourbon
2 CONT
2 CONT Seigneur, Gui II, de Dampierre, de Bourbon, III.
2 CONT ---
2 CONT Father: Guillaume I, seigneur de Dampierre.
2 CONT Mother: Hermengarde de Moucy (Mouchy-en-Beauvaisis?).
2 CONT
2 CONT SIBLINGS:
2 CONT - Milon de Dampierre.
2 CONT - Élisabeth de Dampierre-sur-l'Aube.
2 CONT - Odette de Dampierre-sur-l'Aube.
2 CONT - Hélvis (Helvise) de Dampierre-sur-l'Aube.
2 CONT
2 CONT HALF-SIBLINGS:
2 CONT - Guellaume I, comte de Mello.
2 CONT - Beatrix de Mello.
2 CONT - Agnès de Mello, dame de Voisines.
2 CONT ---
2 CONT b.~1140 (possibly as late as 1155)
2 CONT d. 1215
2 CONT ---
2 CONT Husband of Mahaud [AKA Mathilde -ed.], dame de Bourbon.
2 CONT ---
2 CONT Guy II de Dampierre, marié à Mahaud, dame de Bourbon; après avoir été Seigneur de Dampierre, de Saint Dizier et Saint Just, il devient chef de la race des Bourbon et fonde la célèbre Maison de Bourbon. Guillaume de Dampierre Bourb
2 CONC on, son fils, marié à Marguerite de Flandres, a pour fils Jean de Dampierre-Flandres, qui suit, et qui lui succède dans la Seigneurie de Dampierre, de Saint Dizier, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: "Dampierre, antiquités nationales, françaises, européenes, etc: origines des ...", par 'Abbé, Stephane Maistre, livre II, Chapitre Premier, pages 49, PARIS, 1884. Published by, F. Watteler et Cie., Libraries, 5, rue du Che
2 CONC rche-Midi, 5.
2 CONT Source [link]: https://books.google.com/books?id=ObU5AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA49&
2 CONT ---
2 CONT CHILDREN:
2 CONT - Philippa Mahaut de Dampierre
2 CONT - Archambaud VIII "le Grand" de Dampierre, seigneur de Bourbon
2 CONT - Guillaume II, seigneur de Dampierre
2 CONT - Marie de Dampierre-sur-l'Aube
2 CONT - Gui III de Dampierre, seigneur de Saint Just
2 CONT - Jeanne de Dampierre
2 CONT
2 CONT See also, SOURCE: Geni
2 CONT Source [link]: http://www.geni.com/people/Guy-II-de-Dampierre-seigneur-de-Dampierre-et-de-Bourbon/6000000001107642774
2 CONT ---
2 CONT Né vers 1140, il était fils de Guillaume Ier, seigneur de Dampierre, et d'Ermengarde de Mouchy. Il avait en apanage Moëslains et Dampierre, il devint seigneur de Saint-Dizier et de Saint-Just, connétable de Champagne à la mort de 
2 CONC son père. Son nom et ses armes figurent dans la première des salles des croisades du château de Versailles car il était au côté de son suzerain Henry II à la troisième croisade. Il épousa en 1196 Mathilde, dame de Bourbon, fille d
2 CONC 'Archambaud de Bourbon et d'Alix de Bourgogne et petite-fille d'Archambaud VII de Bourbon, laquelle venait de divorcer de Gaucher IV de Salins. Ils eurent:
2 CONT
2 CONT - Archambaud VIII, (1189 † 1242), seigneur de Bourbon, connétable de France.
2 CONT - Guillaume II (1196 † 1231).
2 CONT - Philippée († 1223), mariée en 1205 à Guigues IV d'Albon († 1241), comte de Forez.
2 CONT - Marie, mariée vers 1210 à Hervé de Vierzon, puis en 1220 à Henri Ier de Sully.
2 CONT
2 CONT Après son mariage, il administra la seigneurie de Bourbon avec sa femme. Entre 1210 et 1212, il est à la tête d'une armée de Philippe Auguste contre Guy II, comte d'Auvergne. Il est accompagné de l'archevêque de Lyon Renaud II de 
2 CONC Forez et du chef mercenaire Lambert Cadoc. L'objectif est de mater Guy II d'Auvergne, qui a dévasté l'abbaye royale de Mozac et fait prisonnier son propre frère Robert d'Auvergne, évêque de Clermont. Guy II de Dampierre écrase les
2 CONC  troupes de Guy II d'Auvergne. En 1213, Philippe Auguste lui donne le comté d'Auvergne. Il est présent au côté du roi à la bataille de Bouvines, il a fondé le prieuré d'Epineusval et l'église paroissiale de Saint-Dizier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy II de Dampierre, mort le 18 janvier 1216 [1215? -ed.], fut connétable de Champagne (1174-1216), seigneur de Dampierre, de Bourbon et de Montluçon.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: Wikipedia
2 CONT Source [link]: https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_II_de_Dampierre
2 CONT ---
2 CONT Il mourut l'année suivante 1215 et fut inhumé à Blois, en l'église de Saint-Laumer, à laquelle il avoit fait plusieurs dons.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: "Histoire Généalogique Et Chronologique de la Maison Royale de Bourbon", Tome 1, pages 31-34, par Nicolas Louis Achaintre - Chez Mansut Fils, Editeur, rue de l'école de Médecine, No. 4, Paris, 1825.
2 CONT Source [link]: https://books.google.com/books?id=FzwWAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA31&
2 CONT
2 CONT https://dbpedia.org/page/Guy_II_of_Dampierre
2 CONT
