1 NAME Gauthier V de Brienne, duke of Athens
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1275
2 ADDR
3 CITY Brienne-le-Château
3 STAE Champagne
3 CTRY France
1 BURI
2 DATE ABT 1311
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 MAR 1311
2 ADDR
3 CITY Thebes
3 STAE Central Greece
3 CTRY Greece
1 OCCU Count of Brienne, Duke of Athens, Count of Brienne; Duke of Athens, Conversano, and Lecce (1296 - Mar. 15, 1311); Duke of Athens, and Lecce (Aug. 9, 1296 - Mar. 15, 1311
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Walter V of Brienne (c. 1275 – March 15, 1311) was born in Brienne-le-Château, Aube, Champagne, France. He was the son of Hugh de Candie des Brienne, known as Hugh of Brienne, Count of Brienne and Lecce, and Isabel
2 CONC la de la Roche, daughter of Guy I of la Roche, Duke of Athens. He was the heir of the Brienne claim to the Kingdom of Jerusalem and of Cyprus, as well as to Taranto and Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter spent his youth as a hostage in Sicily, in the castle of Agosta. On the death of his father Hugh in 1296, Walter inherited the titles of Count of Brienne, Conversano and Lecce. Like his father, he took up arms in the servic
2 CONC e of Naples, but was captured in an ambush at Gagliano in 1300. He was freed in 1302 with the signing of the Treaty of Caltabellotta. The death of his mother's first cousin, Guy II of la Roche, in 1308 brought him the Duchy of Ath
2 CONC ens. There he found himself hard pressed by the Despot of Epirus, the Emperor Andronicus II Palaeologus and the Lord of Vlachia. In 1310, he hired the Catalan Company, then ravaging the Byzantine empire, to fight the Greeks encroa
2 CONC ching on his territory. After the Company had successfully reduced his enemies, he attempted to expel the Company from Athens with their pay in arrears. The Company refusing this, Walter marched out with a strong force of French k
2 CONC nights from Athens, the Morea and Naples and Greek foot from Athens. Walter's army met the Catalans at the Battle of Halmyros on the river Cephissus in Boeotia on March 15, 1311. The Catalans won a devastating victory, killing Wal
2 CONC ter and almost all of his chivalry, and seizing his Duchy of Athens, excepting only the Lordship of Argos and Nauplia. His son Walter VI of Brienne succeeded him in all his titles; the Catalan Company nominated one of the survivin
2 CONC g knights, Roger Deslaur, as their leader and new Duke of Athens by conquest.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1306 he married Jeanne de Châtillon and had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Walter VI of Brienne (c. 1304–1356), his successor as Count of Brienne and Lecce and Lord of Argos and Nauplia, as well as the titular Duke of Athens
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Isabella of Brienne (1306–1360), married Walter of Enghien and succeeded her brother on his death
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Halmyros, of Orchomenos, or of the Cephissus was fought on March 15, 1311 between the Frankish Greek forces of Walter V of Brienne and the mercenaries of the Catalan Company, resulting in a devastating victory for th
2 CONC e Catalans.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Walter V of Brienne
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter V of Brienne (c. 1275 – March 15, 1311) was born in Brienne-le-Château, Aube, Champagne, France. He was the son of Hugh de Candie des Brienne, known as Hugh of Brienne, Count of Brienne and Lecce, and Isabella de la Roche, 
2 CONC daughter of Guy I of la Roche, Duke of Athens. He was the heir of the Brienne claim to the Kingdom of Jerusalem and of Cyprus, as well as to Taranto and Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter spent his youth as a hostage in Sicily, in the castle of Agosta. On the death of his father Hugh in 1296, Walter inherited the titles of Count of Brienne, Conversano and Lecce. Like his father, he took up arms in the servic
2 CONC e of Naples, but was captured in an ambush at Gagliano in 1300. He was freed in 1302 with the signing of the Treaty of Caltabellotta. The death of his mother's first cousin, Guy II of la Roche, in 1308 brought him the Duchy of Ath
2 CONC ens. There he found himself hard pressed by the Despot of Epirus, the Emperor Andronicus II Palaeologus and the Lord of Vlachia. In 1310, he hired the Catalan Company, then ravaging the Byzantine empire, to fight the Greeks encroa
2 CONC ching on his territory. After the Company had successfully reduced his enemies, he attempted to expel the Company from Athens with their pay in arrears. The Company refusing this, Walter marched out with a strong force of French k
2 CONC nights from Athens, the Morea and Naples and Greek foot from Athens. Walter's army met the Catalans at the Battle of Halmyros on the river Cephissus in Boeotia on March 15, 1311. The Catalans won a devastating victory, killing Wal
2 CONC ter and almost all of his chivalry, and seizing his Duchy of Athens, excepting only the Lordship of Argos and Nauplia. His son Walter VI of Brienne succeeded him in all his titles; the Catalan Company nominated one of the survivin
2 CONC g knights, Roger Deslaur, as their leader and new Duke of Athens by conquest.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1306 he married Jeanne de Châtillon and had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter VI of Brienne (c. 1304–1356), his successor as Count of Brienne and Lecce and Lord of Argos and Nauplia, as well as the titular Duke of Athens
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Brienne (1306–1360), married Walter of Enghien and succeeded her brother on his death
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh	Count of Brienne
2 CONT
2 CONT 1296–1311	Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter VI
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy II	Duke of Athens
2 CONT
