1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mechteld von Kleve (also known as Mathilde van Kleef, Mathilde de Clèves, or Mathilde von Kleve) was born around 1235 and passed away in 1304, at the age of 68–70.
2 CONT Immediate Family:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Father: Dietrich Graf von Kleve gen. von Dislaken
2 CONT         Dietrich was the Count of Kleve and belonged to a noble family in the Rhineland region, which was part of the Holy Roman Empire at the time. The von Kleve family was significant in the area and had ties to various German t
2 CONC erritories.
2 CONT     Mother: Elisabeth I 'Ysabeau' van Brabant
2 CONT         Elisabeth was from the Brabant family, which was also highly influential in the Low Countries. The Brabant family was closely linked to the Duchy of Brabant and had connections to the Holy Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Husband: Gerard van Limburg, seigneur de Durbuy
2 CONT         Mechteld married Gerard van Limburg, who was the seigneur (lord) of Durbuy, a small principality located in the Ardennes region. The Limburg family was a prominent noble family with vast lands in the Low Countries and Germ
2 CONC any.
2 CONT     Children:
2 CONT         Catharina van Limburg, dame de Durbuy
2 CONT         Isabella van Limburg
2 CONT         Ermgarde van Luxemburg Durbuy
2 CONT         Marguerite de Luxembourg Durbuy
2 CONT         Agnès de Durbuy
2 CONT         3 other children (Names not provided)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mechteld and Gerard had several children, many of whom were likely involved in the politics and noble circles of the Low Countries and Luxembourg, as seen with their daughter's marriages to other noble families.
2 CONT Siblings and Half-Siblings:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Sister: Elisabeth of Cleves-Dinslaken
2 CONT         Elisabeth was Mechteld’s full sister and was also part of the von Kleve family. Elisabeth’s noble background would have played an important role in establishing ties and alliances with other powerful families in the region
2 CONC .
2 CONT     Half-Sister: Mathilde von Wassenberg
2 CONT         Mathilde was Mechteld’s half-sister, indicating that their father had other children from a different relationship. The Wassenberg family was a noble family in the region, possibly connected to the Duchy of Limburg and oth
2 CONC er territories.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and Legacy:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mechteld passed away in 1304 at the age of 68–70, after a long and influential life. Her marriage to Gerard van Limburg and her noble lineage placed her at the center of a web of political alliances between the Limburg, Luxembourg
2 CONC , and Cleves families.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her descendants, particularly through her daughters, would continue to hold significant influence in the region. The Limburg family, with its control over territories like Durbuy, maintained strong ties with the Luxembourg and oth
2 CONC er noble families in the Low Countries. Her children, including Catharina van Limburg and Isabella van Limburg, likely helped to secure political connections and alliances that lasted for generations.
2 CONT Historical Context:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mechteld’s lifetime coincided with a period of great political change in the Low Countries and surrounding regions. The 13th and 14th centuries saw the consolidation of noble power, particularly among families like the Limburgs, L
2 CONC uxembourgs, and Cleves. The House of Limburg and the House of Luxembourg were key players in the region’s politics, and Mechteld’s family would have had to navigate the complex alliances and conflicts of the period.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Limburg family, which Mechteld married into, had connections to the Holy Roman Empire, and their holdings in Durbuy placed them at the crossroads of important trade and political routes. Mechteld’s marriage to Gerard van Limbu
2 CONC rg would have strengthened the ties between the noble houses of the Limburg, Luxembourg, and Cleves families.
2 CONT Conclusion:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mechteld von Kleve was a significant noblewoman of the 13th–14th centuries, with strong family ties to the von Kleve and Limburg families. Through her marriage to Gerard van Limburg and her children’s marriages, she solidified her
2 CONC  family's position in the Low Countries and Luxembourg. Her descendants carried on her legacy of political alliances, contributing to the ongoing influence of her family.
