2 CONT Margaret of Soissons
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret or Margaretha of Soissons (d. ca. 1350) Was the only daughter of Hugo of Soissons and Johanna of Argies.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1306 she succeeded her father as Countess of Soissons. Margaret was married to John of Beaumont (1288-1356), son of John II, Count of Holland. She was mother of:
2 CONT
2 CONT Johanna of Hainault (1323-1350)
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Canon of Cambrai.
2 CONT
2 CONT William, Canon of Cambrai, Beauvais and Le Mans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Amalrik, Canon of Cambrai, Dole and Tours.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reinout, Canon of Cambrai.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis II, Count of Blois
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Louis I of Châtillon)
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis I of Châtillon (d. August 26, 1346, battle of Crécy), son of Guy I of Blois-Châtillon and Marguerite of Valois, was count of Blois and lord of Avesnes 1342–1346.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1340 in Soissons, he married Jeanne of Avesnes, Countess of Soissons (d. 1350), daughter of John of Avesnes, Lord of Beaumont. They had three children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis (d. 1372)
2 CONT
2 CONT John (d. 1381)
2 CONT
2 CONT Guy (d. 1397)
