1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BURGUNDIAN%20NOBILITY.htm#GuichardVBeaujeudied1265
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         GUICHARD [VI] de Beaujeu (-8 or 9 May 1265).  "Humbertus dominus Belli Joci" names his son "Guichardum…" in his testament dated Jul 1248[704].  He succeeded his father in 1250 as Seigneur de Beaujeu.  The Anniversary Bo
2 CONC ok of Notre-Dame de Beaujeu records the death "VIII Id Mai" of "Guichardus dominus Bellijoci, Belleville sepultus", referring on the same date to "Humbertus pater eius qui decessit in partibus transmarinis…domina Margarita soror e
2 CONC iusdem domina de Monte S Johannis"[705].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (Papal dispensation 14 Nov 1250, [1260]) as her first husband, BLANCHE de Salins, daughter of JEAN [I] "le Sage" Comte de Chalon [Bourgogne-Comté] & his second wife Isabelle de Courtenay (-1306).  Pope Innocent IV issued a
2 CONC  dispensation for the marriage of “nobili viro G. nato quondam...domini Bellijoci” and “Blanca nata J. comitis Burgundiæ domini Salinensis”, despite “quarto consanguinitatis gradu”, dated 14 Nov 1250[706].  “Jehans cuens de Bourgo
2 CONC igne et sires de Salins” granted “Rochefort, Saint-Juliain, Orgelet et Valnantois” to “es enfanz que nous avons de…Ysabel…Johan, Estevenet et Perrenin…dame Blanche dame de Biaujuel”, by charter dated 25 Mar 1263[707].  “Jean comte
2 CONC  de Bourgogne seigneur de Salins” requested Thibaut V Comte de Champagne to take homage for Fontaine-Macon from “Blanche sa fille jadis épouse de Guichard seigneur de Beaujeu” by charter dated 1267[708].  She married secondly (126
2 CONC 8) Béraud [VII] Seigneur de Mercœur.  "Blanche de Châlon dame de Belleville, veuve de Guichard sire de Beaujeu et ensuite de Béraud de Mercœur fils de Béraud de Mercœur" donated property to the frères mineurs de Villefranche by ch
2 CONC arter dated Jun 1282[709].  A charter dated [1/28] Nov 1282 records an agreement between the monks of Cluny and "Ludovicum dominum Bellijoci", and names “dominam Blanchiam, relictam domini Guichardi, quondam domini Bellijoci”[710]
2 CONC . 
