1 NOTE     ES I:5
1 NOTE Tysk-Rom.kejsare. Konung o Kejsare
1 NOTE Henry VI (November 1165 - 28 September 1197) was King of Germany from 1190 to 1197, Holy Roman Emperor from 1191 to 1197 and King of Sicily from 1194 to 1197.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT Born in Nijmegen, Henry was the son of the emperor Frederick I, Holy Roman Emperor and Beatrix of Burgundy, and was crowned King of the Romans at Bamberg in June 1169, at the age of four. After having taken the reins of the Empire
2 CONC  from his father, who had gone on the Crusade, in 1189-1190 he suppressed a revolt by Henry the Lion, former duke of Saxony and Bavaria and relative of Frederick.
2 CONT
2 CONT Constance of Sicily was betrothed to Henry in 1184, and they were married on 27 January 1186. Constance was the sole legitimate heir of William II of Sicily, and, after the latter's death in November 1189, Henry had the possibilit
2 CONC y of adding the Sicilian crown to the imperial one, as his father had died crossing the Saleph River in Cilicia, now part of Turkey June 10, 1190.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coronation as Emperor
2 CONT Holy Roman Emperor 
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1191, in Rome, Henry and Constance were crowned Emperor and Empress by Pope Celestine III. The crown of Sicily, however, was harder to gain, as the barons of southern Italy had chosen a grandson of Roger II, Tancred, coun
2 CONC t of Lecce, as their king. Henry began his work besieging Naples, but he had to return to Germany (where Henry the Lion had revolted again) after his army had been heavily hit by an epidemic. Constance, who stayed behind in the pa
2 CONC lace at Salerno, was betrayed by the Salernitans, handed over to Tancred, and only released on the intervention of Celestine III, who in return recognized Tancred as King of Sicily. Henry had a stroke of fortune when Leopold V, Du
2 CONC ke of Austria, gave him his prisoner, king Richard I of England, whom he kept in Trifels Castle. Ignoring his excommunication by Pope Celestine III for imprisoning a former crusader, Henry managed to exact from the English a ranso
2 CONC m of 150,000 silver marks, a huge sum for that age, and with this money, he could raise a powerful army to conquer southern Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was granted free passage in Northern Italy, signing with the Italian communes a treaty in January 1194. The following April he also reached a settlement with Henry the Lion. In February Tancred died, leaving as heir a young 
2 CONC boy, William III. Henry met little resistance and entered Palermo, capital city of the Kingdom of Sicily, on 20 November, and was crowned on 25 December. He is also said to have had the young William blinded and castrated, while m
2 CONC any Sicilian nobles were burned alive. Some, however, like the Siculo-Greek Eugene of Palermo, transitioned into the new Hohenstaufen government with ease.
2 CONT
2 CONT At that point, Henry was the most powerful monarch in the Mediterranean and Europe, since the Kingdom of Sicily added to his personal and Imperial revenues an income without parallel in Europe. Henry felt strong enough to send hom
2 CONC e the Pisan and Genoese ships without giving their governments the promised concessions in Southern Italy, and even received tribute from the Byzantine Empire. In 1194 his son, Frederick, the future emperor and king of Sicily and 
2 CONC Jerusalem, was born. Henry secured his position in Italy, naming his friend Conrad of Urslingen as Duke of Spoleto and giving the Marche to Markward of Anweiler.
2 CONT
2 CONT His next aim was to make the imperial crown hereditary. At the Diet of Würzburg, held in April 1196, he managed to convince the majority of the princes to vote for his proposal, but in the following one at Erfurt (October 1196) h
2 CONC e did not achieve the same favourable result.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT In 1197 the tyrannical power of the foreign King in Italy spurred a revolt, especially in southern Sicily, where Arabs were the majority of the population, which his German soldiers suppressed mercilessly. In the same year Henry p
2 CONC repared for a Crusade, but, on 28 September, he died of malaria in Messina.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His son Frederick II was to inherit both the Kingdom of Sicily and the Emperor crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was fluent in Latin and, according to Alberic of Troisfontaines, was "distinguished by gifts of knowledge, wreathed in flowers of eloquence, and learned in canon and Roman law". He was a patron of poets and poetry, and he al
2 CONC most certainly composed the song "Kaiser Heinrich", now among the Weingarten Song Manuscripts.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to his rank and with Imperial Eagle, regalia, and a scroll, he is the first and foremost to be portrayed in the famous Codex Manesse, a fourteenth century manuscript showing 140 reputed poets (see Minnesänger), and at l
2 CONC east three poems are attributed to a young and romantically minded Henry VI. In one of those he describes a romance which makes him forget all his earthly power, and neither riches nor royal dignity can outweigh his yearning for t
2 CONC hat lady (ê ich mich ir verzige, ich verzige mich ê der krône - before I give her up, I'd rather give up the crown).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT See also
2 CONT Kings of Germany family tree. He was related to every other king of Germany. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT ^ In 1197, although "the well-prepared crusade of Emperor Henry VI aimed at winning the Holy Land, it also aimed at attaining the ancient goal of Norm[an] policy in the E[ast]: the conquest of the Byz[antine] Empire." See Werner H
2 CONC ilgemann and Hermann Kinder, The Anchor Atlas of World History, Volume I: From the Stone Age to the Eve of the French Revolution, trans. Ernest A. Menze (New York: Anchor Books, Doubleday, 1974), 153; "Henry pressed territorial an
2 CONC d political claims against Constantinople, demanding territories the Normans had held in 1185 and using a remote family connection to pose as the avenger of the deposed emperor Isaac II. . . . even Pope Innocent III was frightened
2 CONC  by the German emperor's claims of world domination. As events turned out, however, Henry died suddenly in 1197 before he could carry out his plans for eastward expansion." See Timothy E. Gregory, A History of Byzantium (Malden: B
2 CONC lackwell Publishing, 2005), 273. 
2 CONT  Wikimedia Commons has media related to: Henry VI, Holy Roman Emperor  
2 CONT Alberic of Troisfontaines, Chronicon 
2 CONT David Abulafia, Frederick II 
1 NOTE KING OF SICILY; KING OF GERMANY 1190-1197; LEADER OF THE 4TH CRUSADE (1202-1204)
1 NOTE Henry VI, German emperor (1190-7), was born in 1165, the son of Frederick I, Barbarossa, and Constance, heiress to the kingdom of Sicily. One of the main objects of his policy was to make himself master of that island. Another was
2 CONC  to abolish the elective character of the imperial sovereignty, and have it declared hereditary in his own family. Both objects failed of realization. See Toeche�s Kaiser Heinrich VI (1867). [World Wide Illustrated Encyclopedia, 1
2 CONC 935]
1 NOTE He ruled from 1190 to 1197.
1 NOTE Erik v. Born:Krönt i Rom.Dog i Messina innan han hunnit förverkliga sina högtflygande planer.      Henry VI (Holy Roman Empire) (1165-97), Holy Roman emperor (1191-97) and king of Sicily (1194-97), born in Nijmegen (in present-day
2 CONC  Netherlands). When his father, Emperor Frederick I Barbarossa departed in 1189 on the Third Crusade, Henry became regent for him. In 1190 Henry put down a rebellion of nobles led by Henry the Lion, duke of Saxony. In the followin
2 CONC g year, after his father's death, Henry was crowned Holy Roman emperor at Rome. Claiming the crown of Sicily through his wife, Henry then advanced against Tancred, king of Sicily (died 1194). He failed to take Naples, however, and
2 CONC  was forced to return to Germany, where the German nobles, led by Henry the Lion, were in revolt. In 1192 the emperor captured and held for ransom King Richard I, the Lion-Hearted, of England, brother-in-law of Henry the Lion. In 
2 CONC return for Richard's release in 1194, the emperor was able to bring Henry the Lion to terms and thus restore peace in Germany. In the same year Tancred died, and Henry gathered a large army and invaded Italy. He conquered Sicily w
2 CONC ith little difficulty and was crowned king. Henry tried unsuccessfully in 1196 to make the imperial crown hereditary in the Hohenstaufen family. He did, however, succeed in securing the eventual succession of his son Frederick as 
2 CONC Emperor Frederick II. Henry was succeeded by Otto IV (of Brunswick).     "Henry VI (Holy Roman Empire)," Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c) 1993 Microsoft Corporation. Copyright (c) 1993 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation
