2 CONT ------------------------
2 CONT =Beatrice d'Este, Queen of Hungary=
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Beatrice D'Este (Queen Consort of Hungary))
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice D'Este (1215–before 8 May 1245), Queen Consort of Hungary and Croatia as the third wife of King Andrew II of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice was the only child of Marquis Aldobrandino I of Este but her mother's name and origin is unknown. Since her father died in the year of her birth, she was educated by her uncle, Marquis Azzo VII of Este.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the beginning of 1234, the elderly King Andrew II of Hungary, who had been widowed for the second time in 1233, visited the court of the Este family and fell in love with the young Beatrice. Her uncle gave his consent to the ma
2 CONC rriage only on the condition that both King Andrew and Beatrice renounced the dowry and any claim of her father's inheritance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their marriage was celebrated on 14 May 1234 in Székesfehérvár, and King Andrew promised in their conjugal contract that he would grant 5,000 pounds as marriage portion to Beatrice and Beatrice would also receive 1,000 pounds as h
2 CONC er annual revenue. However, the relationship between Beatrice and her husband's sons became tense soon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following her husband's death on 21 September 1235, her stepson, King Béla IV of Hungary ascended the throne and he wanted to banish Beatrice from Hungary. Moreover, when the widowed Beatrice announced that she had been pregnant, 
2 CONC her stepson accused her of adultery and ordered her arrest. Beatrice could escape from Hungary only with the assistance of the ambassadors of Frederick II, Holy Roman Empire who had arrived to the deceased king's funerals.
2 CONT
2 CONT She went to the Holy Roman Empire, where she bore his husband's posthumous son, Stephen whose legitimacy, however, never was confirmed by his brothers. Following the birth of her child, Beatrice was planning to live in the court o
2 CONC f his uncle, but Marquis Azzo VII denied her claim.
2 CONT
2 CONT She spent the following years wandering in Italy, and she never gave up her son's claims to receive ducal revenues from Hungary. She tried to persuade the Republic of Venice to support her son during the war with Hungary, but the 
2 CONC Serenissima promised to King Béla IV that it would not support Beatrice and her son in the peace of 30 June 1244.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pope Innocent IV granted her revenues of 35 monasteries in Italy.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage and child
2 CONT
2 CONT 14 May 1234: Andrew II of Hungary (c. 1177 – 21 September 1235)
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen (1236 – 10 April 1271)
2 CONT
