2 CONT ===Wife dispute:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boniface_del_Vasto
2 CONT
2 CONT Boniface is sometimes said to have married Alice of Savoy, the daughter of Peter of Savoy.[3] This is unlikely, however, since Boniface and Alice were first-cousins-once-removed (Boniface's mother Bertha and Alice's grandmother Ad
2 CONC elaide were sisters), and thus far too closely related to be permitted to marry according to canon law.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boniface married Agnes, daughter of Hugh, Count of Vermandois.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''' it happened: explanation in his [https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonifacio_del_Vasto#Matrimoni_e_discendenza wiki ITA] '''' --> ''Boniface initially married Alice of Savoy[6], daughter of Count Peter I of Savoy[7], "desponsata"
2 CONC  (despondency) by her recently deceased brother Anselm. The "desponsalia" was a ceremony, the meaning of which is considered by some to be equivalent to a wedding and by others only as a promise of marriage[8].
2 CONT The marriage was then annulled by Pope Gregory VII, who adhered to the first theory and considered it a marriage between relatives, not permitted by canon law. Before this step, on 3 November 1078, the pope entrusted Cunibert, bis
2 CONC hop of Turin, William, bishop of Asti, and the bishop of Alba, with the task of stopping him from the marriage, an attempt which was unsuccessful[9].
2 CONT In the meantime, the following children had already been born from the marriage:''
2 CONT
2 CONT
