1 NOTE       ?? Line 5615: (New PAF RIN=10376)
2 CONT       1 NAME Leopold III "The Saint", Margrave Of /AUSTRIA/
2 CONT BIRTH: Ordinance Index File, son of Leopold II of Austria and Ida, Countess of Cham.
1 NOTE Saint Leopold III (1073 - November 15, 1136) was the Margrave of Austria in 1095-1136. He is the patron saint of Austria, of the city of Vienna, of Lower Austria, and, jointly with Saint Florian, of Upper Austria. His feast day is
2 CONC  November 15.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold was the son of Margrave Leopold II and Ida of Formbach-Ratelnberg. He married twice. His first wife may have been one of the von Perg family, who died in 1105. His second wife was Agnes, the widowed sister of Emperor Henry
2 CONC  V whom he had supported against her father Henry IV. This connection to the Salians raised the importance of the House of Babenberg, to which important royal rights over the margravate of Austria were granted. Also, Agnes had inf
2 CONC luential connections through her previous marriage, one of her sons being Conrad III of Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold called himself "Princeps Terræ", a reflection of his sense of territorial independence. He was considered a candidate in the election of the Kaiser of The Holy Roman Empire in 1125, but declined this honour.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mainly remembered for the development of the country and, in particular, the founding of several monasteries. His most important foundation is Klosterneuburg (1108). According to legend, the Virgin Mary appeared to him and l
2 CONC ed him to a place where he found the veil of his wife Agnes, who had lost it years earlier. He established the monastery of Klosterneuburg there. He subsequently expanded the settlement to become his residence.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold also founded the monasteries of Heiligenkreuz, Kleinmariazell and Seitenstetten which developed a territory still largely covered by forest. All of these induced the church to canonize him in 1485.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold also fostered the development of cities, such as Klosterneuburg, Vienna and Krems. The last one was granted the right to mint but never attained great importance.
2 CONT
2 CONT The writings of Henry of Melk and Ava of Göttweig, which are the first literary texts from Austria, date back to Leopold's time.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried in the Klosterneuburg Monastery, which he founded. His skull is kept in an embroidered reliquary, which leaves the forehead exposed; it also wears an archducal crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1663, under the rule of his namesake Emperor Leopold I, he was declared patron saint of Austria instead of Saint Koloman.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no school on November 15 (Leopoldstag) in Vienna and Lower Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT The brothers Joseph and Michael Haydn, each of whom sang in the choir of St. Stephen's Cathedral, both sang in that capacity at Klosterneuburg on this day. Joseph Haydn later became the more famous composer of the two. Michael Hay
2 CONC dn later (1805) wrote a Mass in honour of Leopold, the Missa sub titulo Sancti Leopoldi.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT By his first marriage, possibly to a lady of the Perg family:
2 CONT
2 CONT Adalbert, d. 1138 
2 CONT By his second wife, Agnes of Germany, widow of Frederick I, Duke of Swabia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold IV 
2 CONT Henry II Jasomirgott. 
2 CONT Berta, m. Henry III, Burggraf of Regensburg. 
2 CONT Agnes, m. Wladyslaw II of Poland. 
2 CONT Ernst. 
2 CONT Otto of Freising, Bishop and biographer of his nephew (from his mother's first marriage), Emperor Frederick I "Barbarossa". 
2 CONT Conrad, Bishop of Passau and Archbishop of Salzburg. 
2 CONT Elizabeth, m. Hermann II of Winzenburg. 
2 CONT Judith, m. William V of Montferrat. 
2 CONT Gertrude, m. King Vladislaus II of Bohemia. 
2 CONT According to the Continuation of the Chronicles of Klosterneuburg, there may have been up to seven others (possibly from multiple births) stillborn or died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT Karl Lechner, Die Babenberger, 1992. 
2 CONT Brigitte Vacha & Walter Pohl, Die Welt der Babenberger: Schleier, Kreuz und Schwert, Graz, 1995. 
