2 CONT Maria of Montferrat
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Montferrat, Queen of Jerusalem (1192 – 1212) was the daughter of Conrad of Montferrat and Isabella, Queen of Jerusalem. Her father was murdered on 28 April 1192 in Tyre by the Hashshashin. Her mother married Henry II of C
2 CONC hampagne on 5 May, when already noticeably pregnant (The Old French Continuation of William of Tyre and Imad ad-Din al-Isfahani remarked on this). Maria was thus born posthumously.
2 CONT
2 CONT On her mother's death in 1205, Maria became Queen of Jerusalem, aged thirteen. Her mother's half-brother John of Ibelin, Lord of Beirut, acted as regent for Maria.
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 14, 1210, Maria married John of Brienne (c. 1150–1237) in Acre in an arranged marriage. Their daughter Yolande of Jerusalem (also known as Isabella) was born in 1212, but Maria died shortly after, probably from puerpe
2 CONC ral fever. John became regent for his daughter Yolande, now Queen of Jerusalem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria's line died out in 1268, when her great-grandson Conrad III of Jerusalem was executed in Southern Italy. After that, her next half-sister's (Alice of Champagne's) descendants became heirs-general of Queen Isabella of Jerusal
2 CONC em.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_of_Montferrat
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Montferrat (or Maria of Jerusalem) (1192–1212) was Queen of Jerusalem, the daughter of Conrad of Montferrat and Isabella, Queen of Jerusalem. She was known in her youth as The Marquise, because of her father's title.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 28, 1192, while the rivalry between Guy de Lusignan and Maria's father was about to find a term and Richard I of England was about to finish Third Crusade and return to England, Conrad was assassinated. Her mother remarri
2 CONC ed in haste on May 5 to Henry II, Count of Champagne, nephew of King Richard and Philip II of France. At the time of the wedding, Isabella was already visibly pregnant with Maria. Maria, the posthumous child was born during the su
2 CONC mmer of 1192.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry II of Champagne died in 1197, from this marriage Maria had gained three half-sisters. Amalric II of Cyprus married Isabella, he became joint ruler of Jerusalem with Isabella. He died on April 1, 1205. Isabella died shortly t
2 CONC hereafter and Marie became queen of Jerusalem, at the age of thirteen, while her stepbrother Hugh, from the first marriage of Amalric, became King of Cyprus and married Maria's half-sister, Alice of Champagne. The half-brother of 
2 CONC her mother, John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut, acted as regent on behalf of Maria, wisely and to the satisfaction of the inhabitants of the kingdoms. Failing to conduct operations to reconquer the territories lost in 1187, he
2 CONC  maintained the kingdom within its limits, a policy of peace with Al-Adil I, brother of Saladin, who had come to his estate by eliminating the other heirs[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1209, Maria was seventeen and the regency expired, so the government believed it best for Maria to marry so she could secure her post as queen. The assembly of barons and prelates decided to seek advice from Philip II of France
2 CONC , who offered one of his followers, John of Brienne. However John was not a very rich man. To overcome his lack of fortune and to enable him to fund his sovereign obligations (court and army) King Philip and Pope Innocent III each
2 CONC  paid him the sum of 40 000 livres[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage was celebrated on September 4, 1210, then the couple were crowned King and Queen of Jerusalem on October 3, 1210 in Tyre Cathedral. John continued the peace policy of John of Ibelin. In 1212, Maria of Montferrat gave 
2 CONC birth to a daughter, Isabelle (1212–1228) or Yolande, but died shortly afterwards, probably from puerperal fever. John retained the crown but only as regent on behalf of his daughter who married (in 1225) to Frederick II, Holy Rom
2 CONC an Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT The lineage of Maria died out in 1268, with the death of her great-grand-grandson Conradin (or Conrad III of Jerusalem). He was executed in southern Italy on the orders of Charles of Anjou, who took the Kingdom of Sicily. After hi
2 CONC m, the descendants of Maria's younger half-sister (Alice of Champagne) inherit her kingdom.
