2 CONT
2 CONT Béla IV of Hungary
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT   (Redirected from Bela IV of Hungary)
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla IV c.1270
2 CONT
2 CONT Béla IV (Hungarian: IV. Béla, Croatian: Bela III, Slovak: Belo IV), (1206 – 3 May 1270), King of Hungary and Croatia (1214-1270), Duke of Styria (1254-1258). Béla was present, at the age of seven, when a group of conspirators kill
2 CONC ed his mother, and he could never forgive his father's generosity towards the conspirators' accomplices. Shortly afterwards, he was crowned junior king (rex iunior) and he governed several provinces of the Kingdom of Hungary durin
2 CONC g his father's reign. He endeavoured to restore the royal power that had declined since the death of his grandfather, King Béla, which resulted in permanent conflicts between Béla and his father. When he ascended the throne, he de
2 CONC termined to revive his grandfather's internal policy which made him unpopular among his barons. However, he soon had to face the threat of the Mongol invasion of Europe; therefore he granted asylum to the Cumans in order to streng
2 CONC then his military force. Nevertheless, the Mongol armies defeated his troops in the decisive battle and he had to escape to the farthest fortress of his kingdom while the Mongols were pillaging the country. When the Mongol troops 
2 CONC were withdrawn unexpectedly, Béla returned and commenced to reconstruct his devasteted kingdom; he patronized the towns, persuaded the construction of fortresses and encouraged the immigration. His success is reflected by his popu
2 CONC lar epithet, "the Second Founder of our Country", in Hungary. During the second period of his reign, he could even expand his rule over the neighbouring countries. However, his last years were characterized by his permanent confli
2 CONC cts with his eldest son, Stephen.
2 CONT
