1 NOTE roi de Hongrie (1235-1270)
1 NOTE     ES II:155
1 NOTE King of Hungary
1 NOTE RBS : King of Hungary between 1235 and 1270. He was a witness where Kuthen, Kahn of the Polowets was given asylum from the marauding Gengis Khan, with his people, the Cumans, by King Bela IV in 1239. Béla IV, Király Magyarország w
2 CONC as routed on the banks of the Sajó River by Mongols under Batu Khan in 1241. Béla fled to Dalmatia, and for a year the kingdom of Hungary did not exist. So nearly complete was the country's destruction that Hungarians, when referr
2 CONC ing to total calamity, use the word tatárjárás, meaning Tatar invasion. When the Mongols withdrew because of dynastic troubles, Béla began to reconstruct his realm, a task that occupied the rest of his reign. He battled Frederick 
2 CONC II, Duke of Austria, who died as a result of the battle on 15 June 1246.
1 NOTE KING OF THE MAGYARS 1235-1270
1 NOTE from Wikipedia
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=e7d61def-c565-4ea0-a9b3-985dd1ba5da5&tid=10145763&pid=-227715008
1 NOTE from Wikipedia
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=e7d61def-c565-4ea0-a9b3-985dd1ba5da5&tid=10145763&pid=-227715008
1 NOTE He ruled from 1235 to 1270. He was driven from the country by the Mongol invasion of 1241 and fled to Dalmatia. Approximately half the population was killed by the invading armies. He was eventually able to reestablish himself and
2 CONC  defeated the Tartar Nogai Khan in 12?1. He had two sons and seven daughters, the most famous being St. Margaret of Hungary.
