2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Poland
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Kujavia (Polish: Elżbieta Łokietkówna; 1305 – 29 December 1380) was Queen consort of Hungary and regent of Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a member of the Polish royal clan of Piast, the daughter of Władysław I the Elbow-high, prince of Kujavia and Jadwiga of Greater Poland, she was the sister of Casimir III the Great, King of Poland and the last ruler of Pia
2 CONC st dynasty, who died in 1370.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married on 6 July 1320 to Charles Robert (Charles I), King of Hungary, as his third wife. While at court in Hungary, Elisabeth is credited as having been the first to introduce perfume, then known as Hungary Water, to Euro
2 CONC pe and the western world.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her eldest son king Louis I of Hungary succeeded in 1370 her brother Casimir also as king of Poland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Polish Piasts died out in 1370, when Elisabeth's brother Casimir III left only female issue and had only one grandson. There were other Piast princes in Silesia and in Masovia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since arrangements had been made for Louis' succession as early as 1355, he became King of Poland upon his uncle's death in right of (and practically together with) his mother Elisabeth of Poland, who -acting as regent in Poland- 
2 CONC held much of the practical power until her death in 1380.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth and Charles Robert had five sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles (1321)
2 CONT
2 CONT Ladislaus (Belgrade, November 1, 1324 – February 24, 1329)
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis I of Hungary (1326–1382)
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrew, Duke of Calabria (1327–1345)
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen, Duke of Slavonia (1332–1354)
