1 CONT Count of Savoy 1189-1233 [Ref: Tapsell p. 240] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Tommaso I di SAVOIA, C of Savoy. Born 20 May 1178. Died 1 Mar 1233. He married Beatrix de GENEVE, About 1196. [ES II:190, XI:158] [Ref: Richard Borthwick 26 Jan 1999 msg to SGM] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Apparently the name Thomas was given to him in honour of St. Thomas Becket, whose cult was raging at the time of his birth. There had been a series of minorities in the line of the counts of Savoy, including the father and grandfa
1 CONC ther of Thomas, and his coming of age may well have been brought forward from experience of the activities of regents. 
1 CONT As ruler Thomas I opened a golden age for Savoy, as he started the great push northwestwards which carried the area controlled by his successors over the Rhone into Bresse and to the Saone between Maçon and Chalon. Even more signi
1 CONC ficant was his victory over Berthold V of Zähringen, which cleared the way for the conquest by his son Peter II of what are now the cantons of Vaud and Fribourg. Thomas was also more successful than his father in reproducing his l
1 CONC ine; with his wife Beatrice de Genève he raised eight sons and six daughters. 
1 CONT From the time of Thomas I the counts were effectively rulers of a minor kingdom and there were very few states of any size in which the central power had so successfully established its authority. Savoy's greatest days were under 
1 CONC Peter and Philip, the sons of Thomas. Savoy prospered and continued to expand from this time into the sixteenth century. [Ref: Leo van de Pas, _Genealogics_ citing Christopher Cope's, _The Lost Kingdom of Burgundy_, Paget p. 67, E
1 CONC S (Schwennicke) II:190 & ES (Isenburg) II:110 online at: http://www.genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00027292&tree=LEO] 
1 CONT
1 CONT "The reign of HUMBERT's son, Count Thomas I (1189-1233), opened a golden age for Savoy for he started the great advance north-westwards which carried his successors' sway over the Rhone into Bresse and to the Saone between Macon a
1 CONC nd Chalon. Even more significant was his victory over Berthold V of Zahringen which cleared the way for the conquest by his son Peter II (1263-8) of what is now the cantons of Vaud and Fribourg. Thomas was also more successful tha
1 CONC n his father in reproducing his race for his bride, MARGARET, daughter of the COUNT OF GENEVOIS, who raised eight sons and six daughters. [Thomas m. MARGARET (abt 1180 - 8 Apr 1257), dau. of WILLIAM I, COUNT OF GENEVA (1130 - 27 J
1 CONC ul 1195) and heiress of Faucigni.] According to the chronicler of Hautecombe Abbey, the Westminster of Savoy, MARGARET was being taken in 1196 to France by her ambitious father to become the third wife of Richard Lionhart's enemy,
1 CONC  PHILIP AUGUSTUS. Thomas had fallen passionately in love with her and, ambushing her party in a narrow gorge in Bugey, defied the KING OF FRANCE and carried her off to the altar." 
1 CONT "From the time of Thomas I the counts were effectively rulers of a minor kingdom and there were very few states of any size in which the central power had so successfully established its authority." 
1 CONT Savoy's greatest days were under Thomas' sons, Peter and Philip. Savoy prospered and even continued to expand from this time on into the sixteenth 
1 CONT century. [Ref: Jim Stevens 1 Nov 1996 msg to SGM, quoting Christopher Cope's, _The Lost Kingdom of Burgundy_] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas I of Savoy 
1 CONT Thomas I or Tommaso I (1178 – March 1, 1233) was Count of Savoy from 1189-1233. He was the son of Humbert III of Savoy and Beatrice of Viennois. His birth was seen as miraculous; his monkish father had despaired of having a male h
1 CONC eir after three wives. Count Humbert sought counsel from St. Anthelm, who blessed Humbert three times, and it was seen as a prophecy come true when Thomas was born shortly after Anthelm himself died on June 26, 1178. He was named 
1 CONC in honor of St. Thomas Becket. 
1 CONT Thomas possessed the martial abilities, energy, and brilliance that his father lacked, and Savoy enjoyed a golden age under his leadership. He had taken over effective rule of Savoy by August 1191, and despite his youth he began t
1 CONC he push north-west into new territories. He conquerored Vaud, Bugey, and Carignano. He supported the Hohenstaufens, and was known as "Thomas the Ghibelline" due to his career as Imperial Vicar of Lombardy. 
1 CONT In 1195 he ambushed the party of Count William I of Geneva, which was escorting the count's daughter, Marguerite, to France for her intended wedding to King Philip II of France. Thomas carried off Marguerite and married her himsel
1 CONC f, producing some eight sons and six daughters. 
1 CONT 1. Amedeo, his immediate successor 
1 CONT 2. Umberto, d. between March and November 1223 
1 CONT 3. Tommaso, lord and then count in Piedmont and founder of a line that became the Savoy-Achaea 
1 CONT 4. Aimone, d. August 30, 1237, Lord of Chablais 
1 CONT 5. Guglielmo, Bishop of Valenceand Dean of Vienne 
1 CONT 6. Amadeo of Savoy, Bishop of Maurienne 
1 CONT 7. Pietro, who resided much in England, became Earl of Richmond, and ultimately in 1263 became the disputed count of Savoy 
1 CONT 8. Filippo, archbishop of Lyon, who resigned, through marriage became Count Palatine of Burgundy and ultimately in 1268 became the disputed count of Savoy 
1 CONT 9. Bonifacio who became archbishop of Canterbury 
1 CONT 10. Beatrice of Savoy, d. 1265 or 1266, married in December 1219 to Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence (1209-1245) and was mother of four Queens-consort 
1 CONT 11. Alasia of Savoy, abbess of the monastery of St Pierre in Lyon 
1 CONT 12. Ágatha of Savoy, abbess of the monastery of St Pierre in Lyon 
1 CONT 13. Margherita of Savoy, d. 1273, married in 1218 to Hartmann I of Kyburg 
1 CONT 14. Avita of Savoy [Ref: Widipedia, online at: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_I,_Count_of_Savoy] 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Children
1 CONT 1.	 Amadeo IV Count of Chablais & Aosta & Savoy b: 1197 in Savoy, France
1 CONT 2.	 Thomas de Savoie , Count of Flanders b: ABT 1198 in Carbonierres, Savoy, France
1 CONT 3.	 Beatrice of Savoy b: ABT 1200 in Chambery, Savoie, Rhone-Alpes, France
1 CONT 4.	 Marguerite de Savoie b: ABT 1202 in Carbonierres, Savoy, France
1 CONT 5.	 Piers of Savoy , Earl of Richmond b: 1203 in Susa, Savoy/Piedmont, Italy
1 CONT 6.	 Agnes de Savoy b: ABT 1205 in Savoy, France
1 CONT 7.	 Philip Count of Savoy , Count of Burgundy b: 1207 in Evian-les-Bains, Haute-Savoie, Rhone-Alpes, France
1 CONT 8.	 Guiscard I de Charron , Steward of Richmond b: ABT 1215 in Charron, Ain, Rhone-Alpes, France
1 CONT WIKIPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas I of Savoy
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas I or Tommaso I (c. 1176 – March 1, 1233) was Count of Savoy from 1189-1233. He was the son of Humbert III of Savoy and Beatrice of Viennois. His birth was seen as miraculous; his monkish father had despaired of having a mal
1 CONC e heir after three wives. Count Humbert sought counsel from St. Anthelm, who blessed Humbert three times, and it was seen as a prophecy come true when Thomas was born shortly after Anthelm himself died on June 26, 1178. He was nam
1 CONC ed in honour of Saint Thomas Becket.
1 CONT Thomas was still a minor when his father died on 4 March 1189, and a council of regency was established, comprising of his mother Beatrice, his father's cousin Boniface I of Montferrat, and the Bishop of Saint-Jean-de-Maurienne. H
1 CONC e had reached his majority by August 1191. Thomas possessed the martial abilities, energy, and brilliance that his father lacked, and Savoy enjoyed a golden age under his leadership. Despite his youth he began the push north-west 
1 CONC into new territories. In the same year he granted Aosta Valley the "Carta delle Franchigie", recognising the right to administrative and political autonomy. This right was maintained up until the eve of the French Revolution. Late
1 CONC r he conquered Vaud, Bugey, and Carignano. He supported the Hohenstaufens, and was known as "Thomas the Ghibelline" due to his career as Imperial Vicar of Lombardy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT In 1195 he ambushed the party of Count William I of Geneva, which was escorting the count's daughter, Marguerite, to France for her intended wedding to King Philip II of France. Thomas carried off Marguerite and married her himsel
1 CONC f, producing some eight sons and six daughters.
1 CONT 1.	Amedeo, his immediate successor
1 CONT 2.	Umberto, d. between March and November 1223
1 CONT 3.	Tommaso, lord and then count in Piedmont and founder of a line that became the Savoy-Achaea
1 CONT 4.	Aimone, d. August 30, 1237, Lord of Chablais
1 CONT 5.	Guglielmo (William of Savoy), Bishop of Valence and Dean of Vienne
1 CONT 6.	Amadeo of Savoy, Bishop of Maurienne
1 CONT 7.	Pietro, who resided much in England, became Earl of Richmond, and ultimately in 1263 became the disputed count of Savoy
1 CONT 8.	Filippo, archbishop of Lyon, who resigned, through marriage became Count Palatine of Burgundy and ultimately in 1268 became the disputed count of Savoy
1 CONT 9.	Bonifacio who became archbishop of Canterbury
1 CONT 10.	Beatrice of Savoy, d. 1265 or 1266, married in December 1219 to Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence (1209-1245) and was mother of four Queens-consort
1 CONT 11.	Alasia of Savoy, abbess of the monastery of St Pierre in Lyon
1 CONT 12.	Ágatha of Savoy, abbess of the monastery of St Pierre in Lyon
1 CONT 13.	Margherita of Savoy, d. 1273, married in 1218 to Hartmann I of Kyburg
1 CONT 14.	Avita of Savoy
1 CONT
1 CONT Further reading:
1 CONT •	Francesco Cognasso, Il Piemonte nell’Età Sveva (Turin, 1968)
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by Humbert III
1 CONT Count of Savoy
1 CONT Succeeded by Amadeus IV
