0 @N6000000002187750146@ NOTE Humbert III of Savoy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Humbert III (b. 1135 - d. 1189), surnamed the Blessed, was Count of Savoy from 1148 to 1189. According to Cope, "Humbert III, who reigned from 1149 to 1189...was a man of irresolute spirit who was disconsolate at being born a prin
1 CONC ce and preferred the seclusion of a monestery. He only renounced his chosen state of celibacy so as to give his land an heir."
1 CONT
1 CONT His first wife died young; his second marriage ended in divorce. Humbert gave up and became a Carthusian monk. However, the nobles and common people of Savoy begged him to marry yet again, which he reluctantly did. This third wife
1 CONC  gave him two more daughters, and Humbert attempted to return to the monastic life yet again. Finally he was prevailed upon to marry for a fourth time, and this wife, Beatrice, produced the son who would ultimately succeed him.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married four times:
1 CONT
1 CONT Faidiva (Italian) daughter of Alphonse I of Toulouse 
1 CONT Gertrude of Flanders, whom he divorced and confined. She was freed thanks to Robert, bishop of Cambrai and returned to the court of her brother, Philip of Flanders 
1 CONT Clemenza of Zähringen (married 1164), daughter of Conrad I of Zähringen. They had 2 daughters: 
1 CONT Sofia, (1165-1202), married Azzo IV of Este 
1 CONT Alicia, (1166-1178), betrothed to John of England 
1 CONT Beatrice of Viennois and had 1 son 
1 CONT Tommaso (born 1178) 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Cope, Christopher. The Lost Kingdom of Burgundy. 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Amadeus III Count of Savoy Succeeded by:
1 CONT Thomas I 
0 @N6000000003949883097@ NOTE who reigned from 1149 to 1189, . . . was a man of irresolute spirit who was disconsolate at being born a prince and preferred the seclusion of a monestery.  He only renounced his chosen state of celibacy so as to give his land an 
1 CONC heir . . . "  Two wives produced only a daughter.  "Humbert gave up and became a Carthusian monk . . . As the ancient chronicle
1 CONT narrates, the barons, knights, and people of Savoy went to the abbot to beg him  to restore their count to them, but his marriage . . .  again only produced a daughter; Humbert was about to withdraw to the abbey of Aulps when he w
1 CONC as prevailed on to marry yet again, and his fourth wife [GERTUDE, dau. of THIERRY, COUNT OF FLANDERS] gave him an heir who fortunately had the kingly qualities which Humbert lacked."
1 CONT   Weis' "Ancestral Roots . . ." (133:36), however, has the third wife, BEATRIX dau. of GIRARD, COUNT OF VIENNE/MACON (as THOMAS' mother.
1 CONT    On a close call, I have chosen to go with Weis. 
0 @N6000000004303404396@ NOTE WIKIPEDIA:
1 CONT
1 CONT Humbert III of Savoy
1 CONT
1 CONT Humbert III, the Blessed
1 CONT
1 CONT For other persons named Humbert, see Humbert (disambiguation).
1 CONT Humbert III (1135–1189), surnamed the Blessed, was Count of Savoy from 1148 to 1189. His parents were Amadeus III of Savoy and Mahaut (or Mafalda, or Matilda) of Albon, the sister of Guy IV of Dauphinois. His memorial day is March
1 CONC  4.
1 CONT According to Cope[1], "Humbert III, who reigned from 1149 to 1189...was a man of irresolute spirit who was disconsolate at being born a prince and preferred the seclusion of a monastery. He only renounced his chosen state of celib
1 CONC acy so as to give his land an heir."
1 CONT His first wife died young; his second marriage ended in divorce. Humbert gave up and became a Carthusian monk. However, the nobles and common people of Savoy begged him to marry yet again, which he reluctantly did. This third wife
1 CONC  gave him two more daughters, and Humbert attempted to return to the monastic life yet again. Finally he was prevailed upon to marry for a fourth time, and this wife, Beatrice, produced the son who would ultimately succeed him.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married four times
1 CONT •	Faidiva (Italian) (d. abt 1154) daughter of Alphonse I of Toulouse
1 CONT •	Gertrude of Flanders (m. abt 1155), whom he divorced and confined. She was freed thanks to Robert, bishop of Cambrai and returned to the court of her brother, Philip of Flanders
1 CONT •	Clemenza of Zähringen (married 1164), daughter of Conrad I of Zähringen. They had 2 daughters:
1 CONT •	Sofia, (1165-1202), married Azzo IV of Este
1 CONT •	Alicia, (1166-1178), betrothed to John of England
1 CONT •	Beatrice of Viennois and had 1 son:
1 CONT •	Tommaso (born 1178)
1 CONT
1 CONT External links
1 CONT •	Humbert at Patron Saints Index
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT 1.	^ Cope, Christopher. The Lost Kingdom of Burgundy
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by Amadeus III
1 CONT Count of Savoy
1 CONT Succeeded by Thomas I
1 CONT FROM LEO VAN DE PAS:
1 CONT
1 CONT BIOGRAPHY
1 CONT
1 CONT Umberto, known as 'the Blessed', was born on 1 August 1136, the son of Amadeo III, Comte de Savoie, and Mathilde d'Albon. In 1148, when only twelve years old, he succeeded his father as Count of Savoy. 
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Christopher Cope (_The Lost Kingdom of Burgundy_), 'Humbert III, who reigned from 1148 to 1189, was a man of irresolute spirit who was disconsolate at being born a prince and preferred the seclusion of a monastery. He
1 CONC  only renounced his chosen state of celibacy so as to give his land an heir'. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Before 3 January 1151 he married Faydiva whose origins are unknown. Faydiva died about 1154 and a year later he married Gertrud of Flanders. Umberto confined Gertrud for reasons unknown, but she was rescued by a cleric from Flande
1 CONC rs, Robert, provost of Arie, who profited from his efforts through the high regard of her brother and later became bishop-elect of Cambrai. However on 4 October 1174, while still not consecrated, he was murdered at the behest of J
1 CONC acques d'Avesnes. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Before 1162 Umberto III and Gertrud were divorced. At this point he gave up his attempts at parenthood and became a Carthusian monk. However, the nobles and common people of Savoy begged him to marry yet again, which he reluctantl
1 CONC y did. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1164 he married Klementia von Zähringen and they became the parents of two daughters, whereupon Umberto attempted to return to the monastic life yet again. Klementia died between 1173 and 1175, and he was prevailed upon to marr
1 CONC y for a fourth time. About 1175 he married Beatrice de Maçon and by her fathered his son and heir, Thomas. When Umberto III died on 4 March 1189, his son was not quite twelve.
1 CONT
1 CONT ----------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage 1 Faydiva b: ABT 1136 in France
1 CONT •	Married: 3 JAN 1150/51 in 1st wife 1
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage 2 Gertrude of Flanders b: ABT 1140 in Alsace & Lorraine, France
1 CONT •	Married: 1155 in 2nd wife - divorced bef. 1162 1
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage 3 Klementia von Zahringen b: ABT 1140 in Zahringen, Germany
1 CONT •	Married: 1164 in 3rd wife 1
1 CONT Children
1 CONT 1.	 Sofie de Savoy b: ABT 1165 in Savoy, France
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage 4 Beatrice de Macon b: ABT 1161 in Macon, Saone-et-Loire, Bourgogne, France
1 CONT •	Married: 1175 in 4th wife 1
1 CONT Children
1 CONT 1.	 Thomas (Tomaso) I Count of Savoy b: 20 MAR 1176/77 in Carbonierres-les-Bains, Rhone, Rhone-Alpes, France
