0 @N6000000002187840649@ NOTE Margaret of Brabant
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Margaret of Brabant (4 October 1276–14 December 1311, Genoa), daughter of John I, Duke of Brabant and Margaret of Flanders. She was the wife of Count Henry of Luxemburg and after his coronation 1308, Queen of Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT She had been married with Henry 1292 in the context of a peace agreement (due to the enmity, which resulted after the battle of Worringen) of the two houses. She bore in the marriage, which ran according to all reports lucky, a so
1 CONC n, Johann of Luxembourg (1296-1346), and two daughters, Maria (1304-24) and Beatrix (1305-19). Margarete accompanied its husband on its Italy course, got sick however during the siege of Brescia and deceased only few months later 
1 CONC in Genova, where she lies also buried.
0 @N6000000002187700624@ NOTE Leopold I of Austria (Habsburg)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Leopold I (born August 4, 1290 in Vienna, died February 28, 1326 in Strassburg) was a Duke of Austria and Styria from the Habsburg family. He was the third son of King Albert I and brother of Duke Frederick I the Handsome (III as 
1 CONC King of the Romans). After the death of his parents, he became the head of the House of Habsburg. As the administrator of Further Austria, he incurred a decisive loss against the Swiss in the Battle of Morgarten in 1315. He also s
1 CONC truggled to have his brother Frederick elected as King. After Frederick's capture by King Louis the Bavarian after the Battle of Mühldorf he struggled for his release.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert I Duke of Austria
1 CONT Co-ruler to
1 CONT Frederick I ---- 
