0 @N6000000002187820661@ NOTE Charles II of Naples
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles II, known as the Lame (Fr. le Boiteux) (born c. 1248, died 5 May 1309, Naples) was the King of Naples and Sicily, titular king of Jerusalem, and Prince of Salerno. He was a son of Charles I of Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT He had been captured by Roger of Lauria in the naval battle at Naples in 1284. When his father died, he was still a prisoner of Peter III of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1288 King Edward I of England mediated to make peace, and Charles was liberated only to retain Naples alone. Sicily was left to the Aragonese. Charles was also to induce his cousin Charles of Valois to renounce for twenty thous
1 CONC and pounds of silver the kingdom of Aragon which given him by Pope Martin IV to punish Peter for having invaded Sicily, but which the Valois had never effectively occupied.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles was then released, leaving three of his sons and sixty Provençal nobles as hostages, promising to pay 30,000 marks and to return a prisoner if the conditions were not fulfilled within three years. He went to Rieti, where t
1 CONC he new Pope Nicholas IV, immediately absolved him from all the conditions he had sworn to observe, crowned him King of Sicily in 1289, and excommunicated King Alfonso III of Aragon. Charles of Valois, in alliance with Castile, pre
1 CONC pared to take possession of Aragon. Alfonso, being hard pressed, had to promise to withdraw the troops he had sent to help his brother James in Sicily, to renounce all rights over the island, and pay a tribute to the Holy See.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso died childless in 1291 before the treaty could be carried out, and James took possession of Aragon, leaving the government of Sicily to the third brother Frederick.
1 CONT
1 CONT The new Pope Boniface VIII, elected in 1294 at Naples under the auspices of King Charles, mediated between the latter and James, and a most dishonourable treaty was signed: James was to marry Charles’s daughter Bianca and was prom
1 CONC ised the investiture by the pope of Sardinia and Corsica, while he was to leave the Angevin a free hand in Sicily and even to assist him if the Sicilians resisted.
1 CONT
1 CONT An attempt was made to bribe Frederick into consenting to this arrangement, but being backed up by his people he refused, and was afterwards crowned king of Sicily. The ensuring war was fought on land and sea but Charles, though a
1 CONC ided by the pope, his cousin Charles of Valois and James, was unable to conquer the island, and his son the prince of Taranto was taken prisoner at the battle of La Falconara in 1299. Peace was at last made in 1302 at Caltabellott
1 CONC a. Charles gave up all rights to Sicily and agreed to the marriage of his daughter Leonora and King Frederick; the treaty was ratified by the pope in 1303. Charles spent his last years quietly in Naples, which city he improved and
1 CONC  embellished. He died in August 1309, and was succeeded by his son Robert the Wise.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles II of Naples is known in Italian history books for one particularly crude episode, that is for his order to one of his commanders, Giovanni da Barletta, to destroy the town of Lucera, the last surviving Muslim community in
1 CONC  Italy, as a way to celebrate the first Jubilaeum proclaimed by Pope Boniface VIII. The entire population of about 20,000 Arabs and Italian Muslims was exterminated in the summer of year 1300, ending five centuries of Muslim prese
1 CONC nce on Italian soil.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1270, he married Maria of Hungary (c. 1257 – March 25, 1323), the daughter of Stephen V of Hungary. They had fourteen children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles Martel d'Anjou, titular King of Hungary 
1 CONT Saint Louis of Toulouse (February 9, 1275, Nocera – August 19, 1298, Chateau de Brignoles), Bishop of Toulouse 
1 CONT Robert of Naples, King of Naples 
1 CONT Philip I of Taranto, Prince of Achaea and Taranto, Despot of Romania, Lord of Durazzo, titular Emperor of Constantinople 
1 CONT Raymond Berengar (1281–1307), Count of Provence, Prince of Piedmont and Andria 
1 CONT John (1283 – aft. March 16, 1308), a priest 
1 CONT Tristan (1284–bef. 1288) 
1 CONT Peter (1291 – August 29, 1315, Battle of Montecatini), Count of Gravina 
1 CONT John of Gravina (1294 – April 5, 1336, Naples), Duke of Durazzo, Prince of Achaea, and Count of Gravina, married March 1318 (div 1321) Matilda of Hainault (November 29, 1293–1336), married November 14, 1321 Agnes of Périgord (d. 1
1 CONC 345) 
1 CONT Marguerite (1273– December 31, 1299), Countess of Anjou and Maine, married at Corbeil August 16, 1290 Charles of Valois 
1 CONT Blanca (1280 – October 14, 1310, Barcelona), married at Villebertran November 1, 1295 James II of Aragon 
1 CONT Leonora, (August 1289 – August 9, 1341, Monastery of St. Nicholas, Arene), married at Messina May 17, 1302 Frederick III of Sicily 
1 CONT Maria (1290 – c. 1346), married at Palma de Majorca September 20, 1304 Sancho I of Majorca, married 1326 Jaime de Ejerica (1298 – April 1335) 
1 CONT Beatrice (1295 – c. 1321), married April 1305 Azzo VIII, Margrave d'Este (d. 1308), married 1309 Bertrand III of Baux, Count of Andria (d. 1351) 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Charles I King of Naples
1 CONT 1285–1309 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Robert 
1 CONT Prince of Achaea
1 CONT 1285–1289 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Isabella 
1 CONT Count of Anjou
1 CONT 1285–1290 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Charles III 
