1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT Blanche (1240 – April 29, 1243) 
1 CONT Isabelle (March 2, 1241 – January 28, 1271), married Theobald V of Champagne 
1 CONT Louis (February 25, 1244 – January 1260) 
1 CONT Philippe III (May 1, 1245 – October 5, 1285) 
1 CONT Jean (born and died in 1248) 
1 CONT Jean Tristan (1250 – August 3, 1270), married Yolande of Burgundy 
1 CONT Pierre (1251–84), Count of Perche and Alençon; Count of Blois and Chartres in right of his wife, Joanne of Châtillon 
1 CONT Blanche (1253–1323), married Ferdinand de la Cerda, Infante of Castille 
1 CONT Marguerite (1254–71), married John I, Duke of Brabant 
1 CONT Robert, Count of Clermont (1256 – February 7, 1317). He was the ancestor of King Henry IV of France. 
1 CONT Agnes of France (ca 1260 – December 19, 1327), married Robert II, Duke of Burgundy 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death and legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Reliquary of Saint Louis (end 13th c.) Basilica of Saint Dominic, Bologna, ItalyDuring his second crusade, Louis died at Tunis, August 25, 1270, and was succeeded by his son, Philip III. Louis was traditionally believed to have di
1 CONC ed from bubonic plague but is thought by modern scholars to be dysentery. The Bubonic Plague didn't hit Europe until 1348, so the likelihood of him contracting and ultimately dying for the Bubonic Plague was very slim.
1 CONT
1 CONT Christian tradition states that some of his entrails were buried directly on the spot in Tunisia, where a Tomb of Saint-Louis can still be visited today, whereas other parts of his entrails were sealed in an urn and placed in the 
1 CONC Basilica of Monreale, Palermo, where they still remain. His corpse was taken, after a short stay at the Basilica of Saint Dominic in Bologna, to the French royal necropolis at Saint-Denis, resting in Lyon on the way. His tomb at S
1 CONC aint-Denis was a magnificent gilt brass monument designed in the late 14th century. It was melted down during the French Wars of Religion, at which time the body of the king disappeared. Only one finger was rescued and is kept at 
1 CONC Saint-Denis.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Veneration as a saint
1 CONT Saint Louis 
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis IX of France was revered as a saint and painted in portraiture well after his death (such portraits may not accurately reflect his appearance). This portrait was painted by El Greco ca 1592–95. 
1 CONT King of France, Confessor 
1 CONT Born 25 April 1214(1214-04-25), Poissy, France 
1 CONT Died 25 August 1270 (aged 56), Tunis in what is now Tunisia 
1 CONT Venerated in Roman Catholic Church 
1 CONT Canonized 1297 by Pope Boniface VIII 
1 CONT Feast 25 August 
1 CONT Attributes Depicted as King of France, generally with a crown, holding a sceptre with a fleur-de-lys on the end, possibly with blue clothing with a spread of white fleur-de-lys (coat of arms of the French monarchy) 
1 CONT Patronage France, French monarchy; hairdressers; passementiers (lacemakers) 
1 CONT  Saints Portal 
1 CONT Pope Boniface VIII proclaimed the canonization of Louis in 1297; he is one of the few royals in French history to have been declared a saint.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis IX is often considered the model of the ideal Christian monarch. Because of the aura of holiness attached to his memory, many Kings of France were called Louis, especially in the Bourbon dynasty, who directly descended from 
1 CONC one of his younger sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Congregation of the Sisters of Saint Louis is a Roman Catholic religious order founded in 1842 and named in his honour.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Places named after Saint Louis
1 CONT The cities of San Luis Potosí in Mexico, Saint Louis, Missouri, Saint-Louis du Sénégal in Senegal, Saint-Louis in Alsace, as well as Lake Saint-Louis in Quebec, and the Mission San Luis Rey de Francia in California are among the m
1 CONC any places named after the king.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Cathedral Saint-Louis in Versailles, Basilica of St. Louis, King of France in St. Louis, Missouri, the Cathedral-Basilica of St. Louis in St. Louis, Missouri, and the French royal Order of Saint Louis (1693–1790 and 1814–30) w
1 CONC ere also created after the king. The Saint Louis Cathedral in New Orleans is named after him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Many places in Brazil called São Luís in Portuguese are named after Saint Louis.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Famous portraits
1 CONT A portrait of St. Louis hangs in the chamber of the United States House of Representatives.
1 CONT
1 CONT Saint Louis is also portrayed on a frieze depicting a timeline of important lawgivers throughout world history in the Courtroom at the Supreme Court of the United States.
1 CONT
