0 @N6000000002187755564@ NOTE Philip III of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip III the Bold ( French: Philippe III le Hardi) (April 3, 1245 – October 5, 1285) reigned as King of France from 1270 to 1285. A member of the Capetian dynasty, he was born in Poissy, the son of Louis IX of France and of Marg
1 CONC uerite Berenger of Provence (1221 - 1295).
1 CONT
1 CONT At the age of twenty-five he ascended to the throne. Indecisive, and dominated by the policies of his father, he followed the dictates of others, first of Pierre de la Broce and then of his uncle Charles I of Anjou, king of Naples
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1285, the last year of his reign, Philip, in order to help his uncle Charles, who had lost Sicily to King Pedro III of Aragon, made an unsuccessful attempt to conquer Aragon. In the aftermath of this struggle, while retreating 
1 CONC from Girona, Philippe III died on October 5, 1285 at Perpignan (in the present-day département of Pyrénées-Orientales). He lies buried with his wife, Isabella of Aragon (1247 - 1271) in Saint Denis Basilica.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT On 28 May 1262, Philip III married Isabella of Aragon, daughter of James I of Aragon and his second wife Yolande of Hungary, daughter of Andrew II of Hungary, and had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis - (1266 - May 1276) 
1 CONT Philippe IV - (1268 - November 29, 1314) 
1 CONT Charles de Valois - (March 12, 1270 - December 16, 1325) 
1 CONT After Isabella's death, he married on August 21, 1274, Marie de Brabant, daughter of Henry III of Brabant and Adelaide of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT The children of Philippe III and Marie de Brabant were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis d'Evreux - (May 1276 - May 19, 1319) (married: Marguerite d' Artois) 
1 CONT Blanche - (1278 - March 19, 1305) (married: Rudolph III, duke of Austri
1 CONT Marguerite - (1282 - February 14, 1317) (married: Edward I of England) 
1 CONT King Philippe III's son, Philippe IV, succeeded him on the throne.
0 @N6000000002322325294@ NOTE In his reign, the power of the monarchy was enlarged and the royal domain
1 CONT extended, though his foreign policy and military ventures were largely
1 CONT unsuccessful.
