1 CONT Ruled 1216-1272
0 @N6000000001783522035@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry III was the son and successor of John "Lackland" as King of England, reigning for 56 years from 1216 to his death. His contemporaries knew him as Henry of Winchester. He was the first child king in England since the reign of
1 CONC  Ethelred the Unready. Despite his long reign, his personal accomplishments were slim and he was a political and military failure. England, however, prospered during his century and his greatest monument is Westminster, which he m
1 CONC ade the seat of his government and where he expanded the abbey as a shrine to Edward the Confessor.
1 CONT
1 CONT He assumed the crown under the regency of the popular William Marshal, but the England he inherited had undergone several drastic changes in the reign of his father. He spent much of his reign fighting the barons over the Magna Ca
1 CONC rta and the royal rights, and was eventually forced to call the first "parliament" in 1264. He was also unsuccessful on the Continent, where he endeavoured to re-establish English control over Normandy, Anjou, and Aquitaine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Appearance
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Nicholas Trevet, Henry was a thickset man of medium height with a narrow forehead and a drooping left eyelid (inherited by his son, Edward I).
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Married on 14 January 1236, Canterbury Cathedral, Canterbury, Kent, to Eleanor of Provence, with at least five children born:
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward I (b. 17 January 1239 - d. 8 July 1307)
1 CONT Margaret (b. 29 September 1240 - d. 26 February 1275), married King Alexander III of Scotland
1 CONT Beatrice (b. 25 June 1242 - d. 24 March 1275), married to John II, Duke of Brittany
1 CONT Edmund (16 January 1245 - d. 5 June 1296)
1 CONT Katharine (b. 25 November 1253 - d. 3 May 1257), deafness was discovered at age 2.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's reign ended when he died in 1272, after which he was succeeded by his son, Edward I. His body was laid, temporarily, in the tomb of Edward the Confessor while his own sarcophagus was constructed in Westminster Abbey.
0 @N6000000003904597737@ NOTE
1 CONT He succeeded to the title of King Henry III of England on 19 October 1216.2 He was crowned King of England on 28 October 1216 at Gloucester Cathedral, Gloucester, Gloucestershire, England <pd42.htm>, and styled 'Rex Anglaie, Domin
1 CONC us Hiberniae, Dux Normanniae, et Dux Aquitaniae.1,4' He abdicated as Duke of Normandy in December 1259.2 He was only 9 years old when he came to the throne which he occupied for 56 years. While he was a minor the land was ruled by
1 CONC  the Earl of Pembroke and Hubert de Burgh. His personal rule was weak and ineffective. Many followers from his wife's country were given important positions and the English barons became restless. By 1258 Henry was compelled to ha
1 CONC nd power to these barons, led by Simon de Monfort. War broke out between the barons and Henry, and he was defeated and made prisoner at Lewes. He had to agree that a new Great Council or Parliament, as it was now called for the fi
1 CONC rst time, be set up. The members of this parliament would be chosen half by the King and half by the barons. In 1265 his son Edward defeated the barons at Evesham and de Monfort was killed. After this, although Henry remained King
1 CONC , the real ruler was Edward. Henry's most lasting contribution to his country was his advancement of the design of Gothic architecture. In particular he instituted the building of a new Abbey at Westminster and in Oct 1269 the rel
1 CONC ics of the Saint, Edward the Confessor, were laid in a shrine behind its altar. He has an extensive biography in the Dictionary of National Biography.5
