0 @N6000000000437071653@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> I, Of England "Longshanks" Ruled 1272-1307 --------------------- Longshanks), king of England, eldest son of Henry III. <kingh.html> and his queen, Eleanor of Provence, was born in 1239. 
1 CONC Atten years of age he was named governor of Gascony, and married in 1254 the Princess Eleanor of Castile. He took a prominent part in state affairs during the latter part of his father's reign, and showed that ability, quick energ
1 CONC y, and decision of character which distinguished him throughout his reign. In the barons' war, which began in 1261, he had generally the conduct of the royal forces; was defeated and taken prisoner by De Montfort <earla-m.html> at
1 CONC  Lewes, in 1264;escaped the next year, and defeated De Montfort at Evesham, thus securing theliberty of his father, and ended the war by the reduction of the Isle of Ely in1267. He soon after took the cross, and set out to join St
1 CONC . Louis in the crusade, but did not arrive in the Holy Land till 1271. After various successes anda narrow escape from assassination - -his wife, it is said, sucking the poisonfrom his arm --he set out on his return, arriving in E
1 CONC ngland in August, 1274. He had been proclaimed king on the death of his father nearly two years previously, and was crowned, with his queen, soon after his arrival. War filled up the greater part of his reign. The principal events
1 CONC  are the conquest of Wales andthe wars with Scotland. Llewellyn, prince of Wales, refusing to attend the English parliament and do homage, was defeated by Edward in 1277 ; and having again revolted, was again defeated, and at last
1 CONC  slain in 1282. Edward built many castles in Wales, and settled the government by the statute of Rhuddlan. He treated the Jews with great cruelty and injustice, hung hundreds of them on a chargeof clipping the coin, and in 1290 ba
1 CONC nished them. In 1291 the numerous competitors for the crown of Scotland submitted their claims to Edward's decision, which was in favour of John de Baliol <kingscot.html>. Baliol did homage to Edward,and was made to feel his depen
1 CONC dence too keenly; so that war soon broke out between the two kingdoms. Then came the terrible devastation of Scotland, temporary submission, insurrection of Wallace <knight.html>, his victory of Stirling, his defeat at Falkirk, nu
1 CONC merous invasions and truces, capture and execution of the great patriot leader, fresh revolt, and coronation of Robert Bruce <kingscot.html> in 1306, and a final expedition against the Scots in the following year,which was cut sho
1 CONC rt by the death of Edward at Burgh-on-the- Sands, near Carlisle, 7th July, 1307. Very great and important legislative changes took place inthis reign. Edward left by his first wife, four sons and nine daughters; and byhis second, 
1 CONC Margaret of France, whom he married in 1299, two sons and one daughter. Margaret survived him.
0 @N6000000000437721643@ NOTE Alias:<ALIA> King /Edward/I, "Longshanks" REFERENCE: 1932
0 @N6000000003098013083@ NOTE Reign: 1272-1307; Of the Plantagenets, House of Anjou.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1270 Edward left England to join the Seventh Crusade. The first years of Edward's reign were a period of the consolidation of his power. He suppressed corruption in the administration of justice, restricted the jurisdiction of 
1 CONC the ecclesiastical courts to church affairs, and eliminated the papacy's overlordship over England. In 1290 Edward expelled all Jews from England. In 1296, after invading and conquering Scotland, he declared himself king of that r
1 CONC ealm. The conquest of Scotland became the ruling passion of his life. He was, however, compelled by the nobles, clergy and commons to desist in his attempts to raise by arbitrary taxes the funds he needed for campaigns. In 1307 Ed
1 CONC ward set out for the third time (at age 68) to subdue the Scots, but he died en route near Carlisle on 7 Jul 1307.
1 CONT
1 CONT ***********
1 CONT Reigned 1272-1307 In the Barons war 1264-67 he defeated the Barons at Evesham (1265) as King he is noted for encouraging Parliamentary institutions at the expense of feudalism and for subduing Wales on which he imposed the English
1 CONC  system of administration. He later tried to assert his authority over Scotland and died while on his way to fight Robert Bruce. ****************
1 CONT Crowned: 19 August 1274 at Westminster Abbey, Middlesex  Married: (1st) October 1254 at Las Huelgas, Castile; (2nd) 10 September 1299 at Canterbury Cathedral  Spouse: (1st) Eleanor daughter of Ferdinand III, King of of Castile & L
1 CONC eon; (2nd) Margaret daughter of Philip III, King of France  Offspring: (1st) Eleanor, Joan, John, Henry, Julian (alias Katherine), Joan, Alfonso, Margaret, Berengaria, Mary, Alice, Elizabeth, Edward, Beatrice & Blanche; (2nd) Thom
1 CONC as, Edmund & Eleanor; (Illegitimate) supposedly one  Contemporaries: Robert Burnell (Chancellor, 1272-1288); Alexander III (King of Scotland, 1249-1286); Robert Bruce; William Wallace; Philip IV (King of France, 1285-1314); Llywel
1 CONC yn ap Gruffydd
1 CONT Edward I, nicknamed "Longshanks" due to his great height and stature, was perhaps the most successful of the medieval monarchs. The first twenty years of his reign marked a high point of cooperation between crown and community. In
1 CONC  these years, Edward made great strides in reforming government, consolidating territory, and defining foreign policy. He possessed the strength his father lacked and reasserted royal prerogative. Edward fathered many children as 
1 CONC well: sixteen by Eleanor of Castille before her death in 1290, and three more by Margaret.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward held to the concept of community, and although at times unscrupulously aggressive, ruled with the general welfare of his subjects in mind. He perceived the crown as judge of the proper course of action for the realm and its
1 CONC  chief legislator; royal authority was granted by law and should be fully utilized for the public good, but that same law also granted protection to the king's subjects. A king should rule with the advice and consent of those whos
1 CONC e rights were in question. The level of interaction between king and subject allowed Edward considerable leeway in achieving his goals.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward I added to the bureaucracy initiated by Henry II to increase his effectiveness as sovereign. He expanded the administration into four principal parts: the Chancery, the Exchequer, the Household, and the Council. The Chancer
1 CONC y researched and created legal documents while the Exchequer received and issued money, scrutinized the accounts of local officials, and kept financial records. These two departments operated within the king's authority but indepe
1 CONC ndently from his personal rule, prompting Edward to follow the practice of earlier kings in developing the Household, a mobile court of clerks and advisers that traveled with the king. The King's Council was the most vital segment
1 CONC  of the four. It consisted of his principal ministers, trusted judges and clerks, a select group of magnates, and also followed the king. The Council dealt with matters of great importance to the realm and acted as a court for cas
1 CONC es of national importance.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's forays into the refinement of law and justice had important consequences in decreasing feudal practice. The Statute of Gloucester (1278) curbed expansion of large private holdings and established the principle that all pr
1 CONC ivate franchises were delegated by, and subordinate to, the crown. Royal jurisdiction became supreme: the Exchequer developed a court to hear financial disputes, the Court of Common Pleas arose to hear property disputes, and the C
1 CONC ourt of the King's Bench addressed criminal cases in which the king had a vested interest. Other statutes prohibited vassals from giving their lands to the church, encouraged primogeniture, and established the king as the sole per
1 CONC son who could make a man his feudal vassal. In essence, Edward set the stage for land to become an article of commerce.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward concentrated on an aggressive foreign policy. A major campaign to control Llywelyn ap Gruffydd of Wales began in 1277 and lasted until Llywelyn's death in 1282. Wales was divided into shires, English civil law was introduce
1 CONC d, and the region was administered by appointed justices. In the manner of earlier monarchs, Edward constructed many new castles to ensure his conquest. In 1301, the king's eldest son was named Prince of Wales, a title still grant
1 CONC ed to all first-born male heirs to the crown. Edward found limited success in extending English influence into Ireland: he introduced a Parliament in Dublin and increased commerce in a few coastal towns, but most of the country wa
1 CONC s controlled by independent barons or Celtic tribal chieftains. He retained English holdings in France through diplomacy, but was drawn into war by the incursions of Philip IV in Gascony. He negotiated a peace with France in 1303 
1 CONC and retained those areas England held before the war.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's involvement in Scotland had far reaching effects. The country had developed a feudal kingdom similar to England in the Lowlands the Celtic tribal culture dispersed to the Highlands. After the death of the Scottish king, A
1 CONC lexander III, Edward negotiated a treaty whereby Margaret, Maid of Norway and legitimate heir to the Scottish crown, would be brought to England to marry his oldest son, the future Edward II. Margaret, however, died in 1290 en rou
1 CONC te to England, leaving a disputed succession in Scotland; Edward claimed the right to intercede as feudal lord of the Scottish kings through their Anglo-Norman roots. Edward arbitrated between thirteen different claimants and chos
1 CONC e John Baliol. Baliol did homage to Edward as his lord, but the Scots resisted Edward's demands for military service. In 1296, Edward invaded Scotland and soundly defeated the Scots under Baliol Ð Baliol was forced to abdicate and
1 CONC  the Scottish barons did homage to Edward as their king. William Wallace incited a rebellion in 1297, defeated the English army at Stirling, and harassed England's northern counties. The next year, Edward defeated Wallace at the B
1 CONC attle of Falkirk but encountered continued resistance until Wallace's capture and execution in 1304. Robert Bruce, the grandson of a claimant to the throne in 1290, instigated another revolt in 1306 and would ultimately defeat the
1 CONC  army of Edward II at Bannockburn. Edward's campaigns in Scotland were ruthless and aroused in the Scots a hatred of England that would endure for generations.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's efforts to finance his wars in France and Scotland strained his relationship with the nobility by instituting both income and personal property taxes. Meetings of the King's Great Council, now referred to as Parliaments, 
1 CONC intermittently included members of the middle class and began curtailing the royal authority. Parliament reaffirmed Magna Carta and the Charter of the Forest in 1297, 1299, 1300, and 1301; it was concluded that no tax should be le
1 CONC vied without consent of the realm as a whole (as represented by Parliament).
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's character found accurate evaluation by Sir Richard Baker, in A Chronicle of the Kings of England: He had in him the two wisdoms, not often found in any, single; both together, seldom or never: an ability of judgement in h
1 CONC imself, and a readiness to hear the judgement of others. He was not easily provoked into passion, but once in passion, not easily appeased, as was seen by his dealing with the Scots; towards whom he showed at first patience, and a
1 CONC t last severity. If he be censured for his many taxations, he may be justified by his well bestowing them; for never prince laid out his money to more honour of himself, or good of his kingdom."
1 CONT
1 CONT ************MORE NOTES
1 CONT Edward I (born 1239, ruled 1272-1307) was one of England's greatest kings. He was a handsome man, with fair hair and ruddy cheeks, and so tall that he was nicknamed Longshanks. He delighted in tournaments and hunting, but he was a
1 CONC lso practical and hardworking. For seven years before he came to the throne, he was the real ruler for his weak father, Henry III (see Henry, Kings of England ; Montfort ). He was in the Holy Land involved in the crusades when his
1 CONC  father died, but there was no question that he would take the throne.
1 CONT  Edward has been called the "English Justinian" because, like the Roman emperor Justinian, he organized the laws. His laws were not restatements of existing customs but statutes in the modern sense. Many of them, particularly the 
1 CONC land laws, had a long-lasting influence. A statute of 1285 limited church courts to strictly church matters--a change that Edward's great-grandfather, Henry II, had been unable to make because of the murder of Thomas à Becket (see
1 CONC  Becket ; Henry, Kings of England ). Edward also stopped paying a feudal tribute to the pope.
1 CONT
1 CONT Parliament grew in strength during Edward's reign because he continued the policy of Simon de Montfort in summoning to it representatives of the towns and lesser knights. His parliament of 1295 is known as the Model Parliament ( s
1 CONC ee Parliament ). In 1297 he reaffirmed the Magna Carta in the famous confirmation of the charters. All of Edward's moves were not fair and admirable ones, however; he forced Jews out of England in 1290.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after coming to the throne, Edward conquered Wales and gave to his infant son, Edward, the title prince of Wales (see Wales ). Until 1289 the care of his French possessions, principally Aquitaine, in Southern France, absorbed
1 CONC  much of his attention. For the rest of his life, his main concern was Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He conquered the country in 1296; but in 1297 all Scotland rose in revolt against him under the popular leader, William Wallace. Edward defeated Wallace at Falkirk the next year, but the Scots still resisted. Near the end of Edwar
1 CONC d's reign Scotland found a new leader in Robert Bruce. In 1307 King Edward, then 70 years old, led an army toward Scotland but died before he reached the border.
0 @N6000000000742101912@ NOTE England's warrior-king Edward I won
1 CONT                 victories against such renowned foes as
1 CONT                      Baybars, Llewellyn and Wallace. 
1 CONT
1 CONT                                           By Eric Niderost 
1 CONT
1 CONT              A case can be made that Edward I was the greatest English king of the Middle Ages. A
1 CONT              strong ruler, he was a man blessed with a strong sense of duty. Although he was no
1 CONT              democrat, he believed the king should promote the general welfare and place himself above
1 CONT              class or faction--a revolutionary concept in the 13th century. Although he has been called
1 CONT              "the English Justinian" because of his legal codes, Edward was first and foremost a military
1 CONT              man, one of the great generals of the medieval world. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Edward was born in June 1239, the son of King Henry III. Weak and indecisive, Henry was
1 CONT              not a bad man--just a bad king. He was devoted to his family and took great pleasure in art
1 CONT              and architecture. One of his pet projects was the rebuilding of Westminster Abbey in the
1 CONT              Gothic style that was just coming into vogue. Unfortunately, Henry's private virtues became
1 CONT              public vices. Because of his devotion to his wife, he gave the queen's undeserving foreign
1 CONT              relatives places at court. Worse still, Henry's building projects were a drain on the exchequer,
1 CONT              and his excessive piety made him a dupe of the papacy. That mix of piety, politics and
1 CONT              penury--he was always short of funds--bore bitter fruit. Simon de Montfort, leader of the
1 CONT              baronial opposition, led an open revolt that defeated the king at the Battle of Lewes in 1264. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Lewes gave Prince Edward his first real taste of combat. As a headstrong young blade of 25,
1 CONT              he took exception to the London troops of Montfort's army, sincerely believing they had
1 CONT              insulted his mother. When the battle opened on May 14, Edward led a cavalry charge that
1 CONT              scattered the London burghers like dead leaves in a windstorm. Intoxicated by the chase, he
1 CONT              began a single-minded pursuit of his fleeing quarry that took him miles from the battlefield.
1 CONT              Once his thirst for vengeance was appeased, Edward returned to Lewes-- only to find that
1 CONT              Montfort had defeated his father's main army. Assailed from both flanks by Montfort's
1 CONT              knights, the dumbfounded prince was forced to surrender. But a great lesson had been
1 CONT              learned--from then on, with few exceptions, his intellect would govern his passions. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Eventually Edward escaped, joined forces with Roger Mortimer, Earl of Gloucester, and
1 CONT              together they defeated Simon de Montfort at Evesham on August 4, 1265. By then, Edward
1 CONT              was king in all but name, since his father was growing old and was as self-absorbed as ever.
1 CONT              Fired with chivalric zeal and a surfeit of youthful energy, Prince Edward "took the
1 CONT              cross"--that is, declared himself a crusader pledged to free the Holy Land from the grip of the
1 CONT              Muslim "infidels." 
1 CONT
1 CONT              In 1271, Edward reached the Middle East with a small army of 1,000 men and amazed
1 CONT              everyone by chalking up a series of victories over the Muslim forces of the Mamluk Sultan
1 CONT              Baybars Bundukdari of Egypt. The prince captured Nazareth, scoring a moral victory by
1 CONT              liberating the hometown of Jesus Christ, but his forces were too small to consolidate his
1 CONT              gains. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Once, when Edward was resting in his tent, a Muslim assassin broke in and attacked him
1 CONT              with a poisoned knife. The prince quickly killed his assailant but was wounded in the arm.
1 CONT              Soon the limb swelled, and the foul-smelling flesh grew black. Gangrene had set in.
1 CONT              Handicapped by the lack of medical knowledge at the time, the doctors were baffled and lost
1 CONT              hope. But one brave physician cut away the blackened tissue and hoped for the best. By
1 CONT              some miracle, Edward survived. The next year, 1272, a truce was arranged between
1 CONT              Baybars and the Crusaders, enabling Edward to go home at last. While en route to England,
1 CONT              he received word that his father was dead and he was now king in his own right. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              On August 2, 1274, the new king landed at Dover after an absence of four years. Crowds
1 CONT              gave a tumultuous welcome to their new monarch, who, at 6 feet 2 inches, towered over
1 CONT              contemporaries. He was handsome, but his piercing blue eyes were slightly offset by a
1 CONT              drooping left eyelid. Like most of his Plantagenet dynasty, Edward had a volcanic temper that
1 CONT              sometimes erupted into murderous rages. Generally, though, he was too intelligent to let his
1 CONT              anger get the better of him. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              A few years after his accession to the throne, Edward was forced to deal with Wales, the
1 CONT              mountainous land to the west of England. Politically, Wales was a confusing mosaic of
1 CONT              divided loyalties. In the south and central portions of the country, Anglo-Norman barons,
1 CONT              called Marcher lords, managed to subdue and pacify the Welsh tribesmen, but in the north
1 CONT              the situation was different. There, a line of Gwynedd princes high in the mountains of
1 CONT              Snowdonia refused to submit to the English yoke. One Welsh ruler, Llewellyn-ap-Graffyd,
1 CONT              declared himself prince of Wales and set about expanding his domain at the expense of the
1 CONT              Marcher lords. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Initially Edward had little interest in Wales, and he might have accepted Llewellyn's
1 CONT              independence if the latter had rendered lip service to his feudal obligations to the English
1 CONT              crown. But Llewellyn's arrogance seemed to grow with his power, and he refused to render
1 CONT              homage to Edward. Thoroughly aroused, the king was determined to bring his rebellious
1 CONT              vassal to heel. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              In July 1277, in the town of Worcester, Edward gathered one of the biggest armies ever seen
1 CONT              in Britain. The feudal levy summoned 1,000 armored knights, while a number of English
1 CONT              shires--Cheshire, Derbyshire, Shropshire and others--supplied about 15,000 foot soldiers,
1 CONT              including many Welshmen and Gascon crossbowmen. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              The northern Welsh under Llewellyn were not prepared to meet Edward on his own terms,
1 CONT              so they melted back into the misty valleys and snow-dappled peaks of their mountainous
1 CONT              homeland. Natural guerrillas, they lived off the land when fighting and generally preferred
1 CONT              ambushes to pitched battles. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              The men of southern Wales generally had spears, but the northern tribes possessed a
1 CONT              formidable new weapon--the longbow. One chronicler described it as "made of wild elm,
1 CONT              unpolished, rude and uncouth," but in the hands of a trained archer it was a formidable
1 CONT              weapon, hitting targets with such force that a longbow shaft could pierce chain mail and pin a
1 CONT              man to his horse. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Edward advanced along the north Welsh coast, marching slowly up the valleys of the Severn
1 CONT              and the Dee. Leaving a chain of rising fortresses in his wake, Edward continued on until he
1 CONT              reached the mouth of the Conway River. There, the king unveiled his trump card--sea power.
1 CONT              Just off the coast, on the island of Anglesey, was some of the most fertile soil in Wales, the
1 CONT              breadbasket of Llewellyn's tribes. Thanks to ships provided by Edward's Cinque Ports,
1 CONT              Anglesey was quickly taken. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Ringed in by hostile troops and threatened by starvation, Llewellyn sued for peace. After a
1 CONT              few years' respite, however, Llewellyn's brother David raised the standard of revolt. The
1 CONT              1282 rebellion was a replay of the 1277 campaign, but this time Llewellyn was killed in a
1 CONT              chance encounter, and his head was sent to adorn London Bridge. David was captured and
1 CONT              executed, and the rebellion he had hatched collapsed. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Edward decided that only more castles could help sink English roots and stabilize the shifting
1 CONT              political soil of Wales. Luckily for the king, his reign coincided with the great age of medieval
1 CONT              military architecture, and he found a builder of genius in Master James of St. George. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Master James' fertile imagination produced a series of elaborate designs, each adapted to the
1 CONT              particular needs of an individual site. Even today, Conway, Harlech, Rhuddlan, Beaumaris
1 CONT              and Caernarvon castles give an overwhelming impression of strength and majesty. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Wales was pacified, at least for the moment, so Edward turned his attention to Scotland. The
1 CONT              Scottish throne was empty, and there were no less than 13 claimants for it. To solve the
1 CONT              impasse, the claimants asked Edward to be arbiter and choose a candidate among their
1 CONT              number. The English king should have known better; the Scottish succession was a morass of
1 CONT              claims and counterclaims. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              After fevered consultations with barons, lawyers and churchmen, Edward chose John Bailol
1 CONT              as king of the Scots. Bailol was a weakling, but the fractious Scottish nobles stiffened his
1 CONT              backbone enough to defy Edward. Once again, Edward could brook no disobedience from a
1 CONT              man he considered his feudal underling. The English monarch invaded Scotland with a large
1 CONT              army, and in March 1296, he proceeded to besiege the important Scottish town of Berwick.
1 CONT              Feeling overconfident, the citizens of Berwick shouted insults at Edward, in particular making
1 CONT              fun of his "long shanks." 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Mounted on his great warhorse Bayard, Edward personally led the assault on Berwick.
1 CONT              Hooves flailing, Bayard leapt across a ditch, bounded over a low palisade and brought his
1 CONT              royal master into the very heart of the city. Soon English troops poured into the narrow
1 CONT              streets and fighting gave way to a general massacre of the inhabitants. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              In short order Bailol was deposed, and Edward ruled the northern kingdom through a series
1 CONT              of military garrisons. But Edward's brutal conquest had unleashed a sort of early nationalistic
1 CONT              spirit among the Scots. A Scottish knight, William Wallace, gathered an army and managed
1 CONT              to defeat an English force at Stirling Bridge on September 11, 1297. With his prestige on the
1 CONT              line, Edward--though he was now growing old--took to the field once again and invaded
1 CONT              Scotland. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              On July 22, 1298, the English and Scottish armies met at Falkirk. The backbone of Wallace's
1 CONT              forces was his infantry, drawn up in four phalanx-style formations called schiltrons. Bristling
1 CONT              with spears, the schiltrons seemed invulnerable to the kind of cavalry charge favored by
1 CONT              medieval knights. And sure enough, before Edward could fully deploy his unwieldy army, his
1 CONT              knights rushed forward in a headlong charge. Try as they might, the English knights could
1 CONT              make no impression on the prickly Scottish formations, and round one went to the stubborn
1 CONT              Celts. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              But Edward had a surprise waiting in the wings--swarms of Welsh archers, who came
1 CONT              forward in large numbers to discharge their deadly shafts. The schiltrons were quickly
1 CONT              reduced to heaps of dead and wounded men, and the remaining Scottish infantry became
1 CONT              easy prey for Edward's cavalry. Only Wallace and a handful of fugitives escaped the terrible
1 CONT              slaughter, and the back of Scottish resistance seemed broken forever. At Falkirk, Edward
1 CONT              Long Shanks acquired a new nickname: Scottorum malleus (Hammer of the Scots). The
1 CONT              battle validated his reputation as a general and showcased his tactical skills. His adoption of
1 CONT              the Welsh longbow foreshadowed the English triumphs at Crécy, Poitiers and Agincourt. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Eventually, Wallace was captured and hanged, drawn and quartered, but his grisly fate left
1 CONT              the Scots uncowed. Time and again, Edward had to return to Scotland in an attempt to crush
1 CONT              the embers of revolt. Yet every time he returned home, the flame of Scots nationalism would
1 CONT              blaze anew. A new Scottish champion, Robert the Bruce, declared himself king of Scotland
1 CONT              and girded himself for another English invasion. It was not long in coming. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Edward, white-haired and ailing, must have felt he was an English Sisyphus, condemned to
1 CONT              roll the rock of conquest forward again and again. At 69--something akin to 90 by the
1 CONT              standards of the Middle Ages--the king had little reason to find happiness in his waning years.
1 CONT              His son and heir, Prince Edward of Caernarvon, was a homosexual and a worthless
1 CONT              spendthrift, more interested in fine clothes than the arts of war. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              King Edward moved forward toward Scotland, but his battle-scarred and aging body could
1 CONT              not obey the commands of his iron will. He died on July 6, 1307, a short distance from the
1 CONT              Scottish border at Burgh-on-Sands. Later, Edward II would return to Scotland in
1 CONT              force--only to suffer a humiliating defeat at the hands of Robert the Bruce at Bannockburn,
1 CONT              on June 23, 1314, by which Scotland won its independence from England. 
1 CONT
1 CONT              Although he was not the equal of a Caesar or Napoleon, Edward I was still a great
1 CONT              commander who grasped the essentials of war. Even his enemies recognized his military
1 CONT              greatness. Comparing Edward I to his son Edward II, Robert the Bruce once declared, "I am
1 CONT              more afraid of the bones of the father dead, than of the living son; and, by all the saints, it was
1 CONT              more difficult to get a half a foot of the land from the old king than a whole kingdom from the
1 CONT              son!" 
0 @N6000000000742281753@ NOTE Born in June 1239 at Westminster, Edward was named by his father Henry III after the last Anglo Saxon king (and his father's favourite saint), Edward the Confessor. Edward's parents were renowned for their patronage of the arts (h
1 CONC is mother, Eleanor of Provence, encouraged Henry III to spend money on the arts, which included the rebuilding of Westminster Abbey and a still-extant magnificent shrine to house the body of Edward the Confessor), and Edward recei
1 CONC ved a disciplined education - reading and writing in Latin and French, with training in the arts, sciences and music. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1254, Edward travelled to Spain for an arranged marriage at the age of 15 to 9-year-old Eleanor of Castile. Just before Edward's marriage, Henry III gave him the duchy of Gascony, one of the few remnants of the once vast French
1 CONC  possessions of the English Angevin kings. Gascony was part of a package which included parts of Ireland, the Channel Islands and the King's lands in Wales to provide an income for Edward. Edward then spent a year in Gascony, stud
1 CONC ying its administration. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward spent his young adulthood learning harsh lessons from Henry III's failures as a king, culminating in a civil war in which he fought to defend his father. Henry's ill-judged and expensive intervention in Sicilian affairs (lu
1 CONC red by the Pope's offer of the Sicilian crown to Henry's younger son) failed, and aroused the anger of powerful barons including Henry's brother-in-law Simon de Montfort. Bankrupt and threatened with excommunication, Henry was for
1 CONC ced to agree to the Provisions of Oxford in 1258, under which his debts were paid in exchange for substantial reforms; a Great Council of 24, partly nominated by the barons, assumed the functions of the King's Council. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry repudiated the Provisions in 1261 and sought the help of the French king Louis IX (later known as St Louis for his piety and other qualities). This was the only time Edward was tempted to side with his charismatic and politi
1 CONC cally ruthless godfather Simon de Montfort - he supported holding a Parliament in his father's absence.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, by the time Louis IX decided to side with Henry in the dispute and civil war broke out in England in 1263, Edward had returned to his father's side and became de Montfort's greatest enemy. After winning the battle of Lewe
1 CONC s in 1264 (after which Edward became a hostage to ensure his father abided by the terms of the peace), de Montfort summoned the Great Parliament in 1265 - this was the first time cities and burghs sent representatives to the parli
1 CONC ament. (Historians differ as to whether de Montfort was an enlightened liberal reformer or an unscrupulous opportunist using any means to advance himself.) 
1 CONT
1 CONT In May 1265, Edward escaped from tight supervision whilst hunting. On 4 August, Edward and his allies outmanoeuvred de Montfort in a savage battle at Evesham; de Montfort predicted his own defeat and death 'let us commend our soul
1 CONC s to God, because our bodies are theirs ... they are approaching wisely, they learned this from me.' With the ending of the civil war, Edward worked hard at social and political reconciliation between his father and the rebels, an
1 CONC d by 1267 the realm had been pacified. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In April 1270 Parliament agreed an unprecedented levy of one-twentieth of every citizen's goods and possessions to finance Edward's Crusade to the Holy Lands. Edward left England in August 1270 to join the highly respected French 
1 CONC king Louis IX on Crusade. At a time when popes were using the crusading ideal to further their own political ends in Italy and elsewhere, Edward and King Louis were the last crusaders in the medieval tradition of aiming to recover
1 CONC  the Holy Lands. Louis died of the plague in Tunis before Edward's arrival, and the French forces were bought off from pursuing their campaign. Edward decided to continue regardless: 'by the blood of God, though all my fellow sold
1 CONC iers and countrymen desert me, I will enter Acre ... and I will keep my word and my oath to the death'. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward arrived in Acre in May 1271 with 1,000 knights; his crusade was to prove an anticlimax. Edward's small force limited him to the relief of Acre and a handful of raids, and divisions amongst the international force of Christi
1 CONC an Crusaders led to Edward's compromise truce with the Baibars. In June 1272, Edward survived a murder attempt by an Assassin (an order of Shi'ite Muslims) and left for Sicily later in the year. He was never to return on crusade. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, Henry III died on 16 November 1272. Edward succeeded to the throne without opposition - given his track record in military ability and his proven determination to give peace to the country, enhanced by his magnified exp
1 CONC loits on crusade. In Edward's absence, a proclamation in his name delcared that he had succeeded by hereditary right, and the barons swore allegeiance to him. Edward finally arrived in London in August 1274 and was crowned at West
1 CONC minster Abbey. Aged 35, he was a veteran warrior ('the best lance in all the world', according to contemporaries), a leader with energy and vision, and with a formidable temper. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward was determined to enforce English kings' claims to primacy in the British Isles. The first part of his reign was dominated by Wales. At that time, Wales consisted of a number of disunited small Welsh princedoms; the South W
1 CONC elsh princes were in uneasy alliance with the Marcher lords (feudal earldoms and baronies set up by the Norman kings to protect the English border against Welsh raids) against the Northern Welsh based in the rocky wilds of Gwynedd
1 CONC , under the strong leadership of Llywelyn ap Gruffyd, Prince of Gwynedd. In 1247, under the Treaty of Woodstock, Llywelyn had agreed that he held North Wales in fee to the English king. By 1272, Llywelyn had taken advantage of the
1 CONC  English civil wars to consolidate his position, and the Peace of Montgomery (1267) had confirmed his title as Prince of Wales and recognised his conquests. 
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Llywelyn maintained that the rights of his principality were 'entirely separate from the rights' of England; he did not attend Edward's coronation and refused to do homage. Finally, in 1277 Edward decided to fight Llywely
1 CONC n 'as a rebel and disturber of the peace', and quickly defeated him. War broke out again in 1282 when Llywelyn joined his brother David in rebellion. Edward's determination, military experience and skilful use of ships brought fro
1 CONC m England for deployment along the North Welsh coast, drove Llywelyn back into the mountains of North Wales. The death of Llywelyn in a chance battle in 1282 and the subsequent execution of his brother David effectively ended atte
1 CONC mpts at Welsh independence. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Under the Statute of Wales of 1284, Wales was brought into the English legal framework and the shire system was extended. In the same year, a son was born in Wales to Edward and Queen Eleanor (also named Edward, this future king w
1 CONC as proclaimed the first English Prince of Wales in 1301). The Welsh campaign had produced one of the largest armies ever assembled by an English king - some 15,000 infantry (including 9,000 Welsh and a Gascon contingent); the army
1 CONC  was a formidable combination of heavy Anglo-Norman cavalry and Welsh archers, whose longbow skills laid the foundations of later military victories in France such as that at Agincourt. As symbols of his military strength and poli
1 CONC tical authority, Edward spent some £80,000 on a network of castles and lesser strongholds in North Wales, employing a work-force of up to 3,500 men drawn from all over England. (Some castles, such as Conway and Caernarvon, remain 
1 CONC in their ruined layouts today, as examples of fortresses integrated with fortified towns.) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's campaign in Wales was based on his determination to ensure peace and extend royal authority, and it had broad support in England. Edward saw the need to widen support among lesser landowners and the merchants and traders 
1 CONC of the towns. The campaigns in Wales, France and Scotland left Edward deeply in debt, and the taxation required to meet those debts meant enrolling national support for his policies.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT To raise money, Edward summoned Parliament - up to 1286 he summoned Parliaments twice a year. (The word 'Parliament' came from the 'parley' or talks which the King had with larger groups of advisers.) In 1295, when money was neede
1 CONC d to wage war against Philip of France (who had confiscated the duchy of Gascony), Edward summoned the most comprehensive assembly ever summoned in England. This became known as the Model Parliament, for it represented various est
1 CONC ates: barons, clergy, and knights and townspeople. By the end of Edward's reign, Parliament usually contained representatives of all these estates. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward used his royal authority to establish the rights of the Crown at the expense of traditional feudal privileges, to promote the uniform administration of justice, to raise income to meet the costs of war and government, and t
1 CONC o codify the legal system. In doing so, his methods emphasised the role of Parliament and the common law. With the able help of his Chancellor, Robert Burnell, Bishop of Bath and Wells, Edward introduced much new legislation. He b
1 CONC egan by commissioning a thorough survey of local government (with the results entered into documents known as the Hundred Rolls), which not only defined royal rights and possessions but also revealed administrative abuses. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The First Statute of Westminster (1275) codified 51 existing laws - many originating from Magna Carta - covering areas ranging from extortion by royal officers, lawyers and bailiffs, methods of procedure in civil and criminal case
1 CONC s to freedom of elections. Edward's first Parliament also enacted legislation on wool, England's most important export at the time. At the request of the merchants, Edward was given a customs grant on wool and hides which amounted
1 CONC  to nearly £10,000 a year. Edward also obtained income from the licence fees imposed by the Statute of Mortmain (1279), under which gifts of land to the Church (often made to evade death duties) had to have a royal licence. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The Statutes of Gloucester (1278) and Quo Warranto (1290) attempted to define and regulate feudal jurisdictions, which were an obstacle to royal authority and to a uniform system of justice for all; the Statute of Winchester (1285
1 CONC ) codified the policing system for preserving public order. Other statutes had a long-term effect on land law and on the feudal framework in England. The Second Statute of Westminster (1285) restricted the alienation of land and k
1 CONC ept entailed estates within families: tenants were only tenants for life and not able to sell the property to others. The Third Statute of Westminster or Quia Emptores (1290) stopped subinfeudation (in which tenants of land belong
1 CONC ing to the King or to barons subcontracted their properties and related feudal services). 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's assertion that the King of Scotland owed feudal allegiance to him, and the embittered Anglo-Scottish relations leading to war which followed, were to overshadow the rest of Edward's reign in what was to become known as th
1 CONC e 'Great Cause'. Under a treaty of 1174, William the Lion of Scotland had become the vassal to Henry II, but in 1189 Richard I had absolved William from his allegiance. Intermarriage between the English and Scottish royal houses p
1 CONC romoted peace between the two countries until the premature death of Alexander III in 1286. In 1290, his granddaughter and heiress, Margaret the 'Maid of Norway' (daughter of the King of Norway, she was pledged to be married to Ed
1 CONC ward's then only surviving son, Edward of Caernarvon), also died. For Edward, this dynastic blow was made worse by the death in the same year of his much-loved wife Eleanor (her body was ceremonially carried from Lincoln to Westmi
1 CONC nster for burial, and a memorial cross erected at every one of the twelve resting places, including what became known as Charing Cross in London). 
1 CONT
1 CONT In the absence of an obvious heir to the Scottish throne, the disunited Scottish magnates invited Edward to determine the dispute. In order to gain acceptance of his authority in reaching a verdict, Edward sought and obtained reco
1 CONC gnition from the rival claimants that he had the 'sovereign lordship of Scotland and the right to determine our several pretensions'. In November 1292, Edward and his 104 assessors gave the whole kingdom to John Balliol or Baliol 
1 CONC as the claimant closest to the royal line; Balliol duly swore loyalty to Edward and was crowned at Scone. 
1 CONT
1 CONT John Balliol's position proved difficult. Edward insisted that Scotland was not independent and he, as sovereign lord, had the right to hear in England appeals against Balliol's judgements in Scotland. In 1294, Balliol lost author
1 CONC ity amongst Scottish magnates by going to Westminster after receiving a summons from Edward; the magnates decided to seek allies in France and concluded the 'Auld Alliance' with France (then at war with England over the duchy of G
1 CONC ascony) - an alliance which was to influence Scottish history for the next 300 years. In March 1296, having failed to negotiate a settlement, the English led by Edward sacked the city of Berwick near the River Tweed. Balliol forma
1 CONC lly renounced his homage to Edward in April 1296, speaking of 'grievous and intolerable injuries ... for instance by summoning us outside our realm ... as your own whim dictated ... and so ... we renounce the fealty and homage whi
1 CONC ch we have done to you'. Pausing to design and start the rebuilding of Berwick as the financial capital of the country, Edward's forces overran remaining Scottish resistance. Scots leaders were taken hostage, and Edinburgh Castle,
1 CONC  amongst others, was seized. Balliol surrendered his realm and spent the rest of his life in exile in England and Normandy. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Having humiliated Balliol, Edward's insensitive policies in Scotland continued: he appointed a trio of Englishmen to run the country. Edward had the Stone of Scone - also known as the Stone of Destiny - on which Scottish sovereign
1 CONC s had been crowned removed to London and subsequently placed in the Coronation Chair in Westminster Abbey (where it remained until it was returned to Scotland in 1996). Edward never built stone castles on strategic sites in Scotla
1 CONC nd, as he had done so successfully in Wales - possibly because he did not have the funds for another ambitious castle-building programme. 
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1297, Edward was facing the biggest crisis in his reign, and his commitments outweighed his resources. Chronic debts were being incurred by wars against France, in Flanders, Gascony and Wales as well as Scotland; the clergy wer
1 CONC e refusing to pay their share of the costs, with the Archbishop of Canterbury threatening excommunication; Parliament was reluctant to contribute to Edward's expensive and unsuccessful military policies; the Earls of Hereford and 
1 CONC Norfolk refused to serve in Gascony, and the barons presented a formal statement of their grievances. In the end, Edward was forced to reconfirm the Charters (including Magna Carta) to obtain the money he required; the Archbishop 
1 CONC was eventually suspended in 1306 by the new Gascon Pope Clement V; a truce was declared with France in 1297, followed by a peace treaty in 1303 under which the French king restored the duchy of Gascony to Edward. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In Scotland, Edward pursued a series of campaigns from 1298 onwards. William Wallace had risen in Balliol's name and recovered most of Scotland, before being defeated by Edward at the battle of Falkirk in 1298. (Wallace escaped, o
1 CONC nly to be captured in 1305, allegedly by the treachery of a fellow Scot and taken to London, where he was executed.) In 1304, Edward summoned a full Parliament (which elected Scottish representatives also attended), in which arran
1 CONC gements for the settlement of Scotland were made. The new government in Scotland featured a Council, which included Robert the Bruce. Bruce unexpectedly rebelled in 1306 by killing a fellow counsellor and was crowned king of Scotl
1 CONC and at Scone. Despite his failing health, Edward was carried north to pursue another campaign, but he died en route at Burgh on Sands on 7 July 1307 aged 68. 
1 CONT
1 CONT According to chroniclers, Edward requested that his bones should be carried on Scottish campaigns and that his heart be taken to the Holy Land. However, Edward was buried at Westminster Abbey in a plain black marble tomb, which in
1 CONC  later years was painted with the words Scottorum malleus (Hammer of the Scots) and Pactum serva (Keep troth). Throughout the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, the Exchequer paid to keep candles burning 'round the body of the Lo
1 CONC rd Edward, formerly King of England, of famous memory'.
0 @N6000000001177485760@ NOTE **NOTES**
1 CONT Edward I, called Longshanks (1239-1307), king of England (1272-1307), of the house of Plantagenet. He was born in Westminster on June 17, 1239, the eldest son of King Henry III, and at 15 married Eleanor of Castile. In the struggl
1 CONC es of the barons against the crown for constitutional and ecclesiastical reforms, Edward took a vacillating course. When warfare broke out between the crown and the nobility, Edward fought on the side of the king, winning the deci
1 CONC sive battle of Evesham in 1265. Five years later he left England to join the Seventh Crusade. Following his father's death in 1272, and while he was still abroad, Edward was recognized as king by the English barons; in 1273, on hi
1 CONC s return to England, he was crowned. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The first years of Edward's reign were a period of the consolidation of his power. He suppressed corruption in the administration of justice, restricted the jurisdiction of the ecclesiastical courts to church affairs, and eliminat
1 CONC ed the papacy's overlordship over England. 
1 CONT
1 CONT On the refusal of Llewelyn ab Gruffydd (died 1282), ruler of Wales, to submit to the English crown, Edward began the military conflict that resulted, in 1284, in the annexation of Llewelyn's principality to the English crown. In 1
1 CONC 290 Edward expelled all Jews from England. War between England and France broke out in 1293 as a result of the efforts of France to curb Edward's power in Gascony. Edward lost Gascony in 1293 and did not again come into possession
1 CONC  of the duchy until 1303. About the same year in which he lost Gascony, the Welsh rose in rebellion. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Greater than either of these problems was the disaffection of the people of Scotland. In agreeing to arbitrate among the claimants to the Scottish throne, Edward, in 1291, had exacted as a prior condition the recognition by all co
1 CONC ncerned of his overlordship of Scotland. The Scots later repudiated him and made an alliance with France against England. To meet the critical situations in Wales and Scotland, Edward summoned a parliament, called the Model Parlia
1 CONC ment by historians because it was a representative body and in that respect was the forerunner of all future parliaments. Assured by Parliament of support at home, Edward took the field and suppressed the Welsh insurrection. In 12
1 CONC 96, after invading and conquering Scotland, he declared himself king of that realm. In 1298 he again invaded Scotland to suppress the revolt led by Sir William Wallace. In winning the Battle of Falkirk in 1298, Edward achieved the
1 CONC  greatest military triumph of his career, but he failed to crush Scottish opposition. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The conquest of Scotland became the ruling passion of his life. He was, however, compelled by the nobles, clergy, and commons to desist in his attempts to raise by arbitrary taxes the funds he needed for campaigns. In 1299 Edward 
1 CONC made peace with France and married Margaret, sister of King Philip III of France. Thus freed of war, he again undertook the conquest of Scotland in 1303. Wallace was captured and executed in 1305. No sooner had Edward established 
1 CONC his government in Scotland, however, than a new revolt broke out and culminated in the coronation of Robert Bruce as king of Scotland. In 1307 Edward set out for the third time to subdue the Scots, but he died en route near Carlis
1 CONC le on July 7, 1307. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "Edward I," Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c) 1993 Microsoft Corporation. Copyright (c) 1993 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation 
0 @N6000000000682017394@ NOTE Line 16745 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   OCCU King of England
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 16747 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   BIRT DATE ca 1236
1 CONT
1 CONT Line 16749 from GEDCOM File not recognizable or too long:
1 CONT   DEAT DATE ca 1307
1 CONT
1 CONT      EDWARD is the name of 11 English kings.  Three were Saxon kings who ruled in England before the Norman Conquest in 1066.
1 CONT They were Edward the Elder (870?-924), son of Alfred the Great; Edward the Martyr (963?-978); and Edward the Confessor (1002?-1066), the last Saxon king.  People in England give numbers to kings of the same name who have ruled sin
1 CONC ce the Norman Conquest.
1 CONT
1 CONT      Edward I (1239-1307) was named for Edward the Confessor.  He is known as the English Justinian because of his fame as a lawgiver.  A member of the royal family of Anjou, or Plantagenet, he succeeded his father, Henry III, in 
1 CONC 1272.  He started a war against the Welsh in 1277, and finally subdued them in 1283.
1 CONT Wales then became part of the English realm.
1 CONT      Edward I devoted most of his reign to conquering Scotland.
1 CONT In 1292, of the several men who claimed the Scottish throne, Edward chose John de Baliol.  But he insisted that Baliol pay homage to him.  The Scottish people were humiliated by this demand, and revolted.  In 1296, Baliol joined t
1 CONC he rebel forces, but Edward forced him to surrender.  Edward carried back to London the great Stone of Scone, the coronation stone upon which Scottish kings had been crowned for hundreds of years.  Later in the year, the Scots, le
1 CONC d by William Wallace, again rebelled.
1 CONT Edward defeated them in 1298, but the Scots were not pacified.
1 CONT After a long struggle, Robert Bruce was crowned King of Scotland in 1306. Edward died while on his way to subdue the new king.
1 CONT      Edward's Scottish policy delayed the union of the two kingdoms for 400 years.  He also did little to improve relations with France.  But his need for money to supply his army and government led him to call a parliament that w
1 CONC ould represent the entire country.  In 1295, the new parliament met.  It consisted of representatives of the nobility, the church, and the common people.  This system of representation set a model for later parliaments.  Edward re
1 CONC stricted the power of the king by accepting the rule that taxes could be levied and laws made only with the consent of parliament.  He also sponsored laws aimed at destroying the feudal system, and at limiting the power of the chu
1 CONC rch.  Edward married Eleanor of Castile.  After her death he married Margaret, sister of King Philip IV of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT [The World Book]
0 @N6000000001563228243@ NOTE (Research):Edward I, first-born son of King Henry III by Eleanor, daughter of the count of Savoy, succeeded his father to the throne at the age of thirty-four. Edward's changes of direction during their war between Henry III and h
1 CONC is barons earned him a reputation for untrustworthiness even before he came to the throne. He was indeed as capable of faithlessness as his grandfather King John but he was far more successful. One writer characterized him as a le
1 CONC opard, an animal associated with deviousness. However, the same man also attributed to Edward the pride and ferocity of a lion. His outlook and behavior was quite unlike his father's. Edward was a strong leader and conducted the a
1 CONC ffairs of state skillfully. From boyhood, he was dedicated to the practice of arms, for which he won a world-wide reputation surpassing that of any other Christian prince of his time. Edward was elegant in appearance. He was a hea
1 CONC d taller than average in stature. In his youth, his hair was silvery-blonde which turned from fair to dark, while in old age it became a magnificent swan-like white. His forehead was broad, and the rest of his features were regula
1 CONC r, except his left eyelid drooped, which gave him a resemblance to his father. He was not lacking in eloquence when persuasive arguments were needed, despite a lisp. According to Nicholas Trevet, a contemporary chronicler, Edward 
1 CONC I was very tall, with long arms and legs, hence his nickname "Longshanks". His long arms were in proportion to his supple body. No man was ever endowed with greater muscular strength for wielding a sword. His breast swelled above 
1 CONC his stomach, and the length of his legs ensured that he was never dislodged from his seat by the galloping and jumping of horses.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Edward I took a break from military pursuits, he indulged in hunting birds and wild beasts, especially stags. He used to chase these on horseback. When he had one cornered, he would strike it down with his sword, rather than 
1 CONC use a hunting spear. English monarchs, from William the Conqueror, were devoted to the hunt. Although they and their barons made profits out of their forests, they valued them primarily as game covers. Hunting with falcons was a r
1 CONC itual and a source of pleasure. Men, horses and the extremely valuable birds were caparisoned in elegant trappings of fine leather and silk. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Great nobles like the king, possessed their own private forests. They all employed staffs of verderers under the chief huntsman, which was often the king. The staff preserved the game and tended the pack of hounds that was conside
1 CONC red an aristocratic necessity. There were two seasons to hunt deer: November to February and June to September. Summer was the better season because the deer were fat or "in grease". Richard of Cornwall, Edward I's uncle, took 32 
1 CONC bucks in nine days in July 1252 in the forest of Rockingham. By the 14th century, an increasingly literate aristocracy wished to be informed about hunting. William Twici, Edward II's huntsman produced a practical hunting manual " 
1 CONC L'Art de la Venerie". The book of St Albans was published in the 14th century and it ennobled falconry. It laid down an hierarchy of birds to match the hierarchy of society. An eagle, or more curiously a vulture, was fit only for 
1 CONC an emperor. A 'Falcon, properly a female peregrine, was for a prince, and a 'tiercel', male peregrine, for a nobleman. A hunting parson was allowed only a sparrow-hawk. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward I was a strong leader. As king he was not greatly loved but was viewed with respect and feared throughout England. His devotion to his first queen, Eleanor of Castile was striking. They were married in 1254.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first two decades of Edward I's reign witnessed a great surge of governmental energy. The king started in 1274 by instigating county-wide investigations into the usurpation of royal rights and lands following the civil war of 
1 CONC the 1260s, and into abuses by local officials. This was the largest such undertaking since the Doomsday survey nearly 200 years earlier. In 1285 the government attempted to improve peace keeping by stipulating that all men over th
1 CONC e age of 15 should hold specified arms and be ready to use them under official direction and to maintain law and order. Two years later, in 1287, keepers of the peace were appointed in the counties to supervise this militia. There
1 CONC  was no improvement, however, in order and after 1294 the prevailing atmosphere of war, lawlessness in the countryside increased. New commissions of justices were therefore appointed in 1304-5 to deal with offenders. Their session
1 CONC s were known as 'tailgating enquiries after the gangs of men armed with staves who terrorized the population. Many were soldiers who had fought for the king in his wars against the Welsh, Scots and French. They were now sheltering
1 CONC  in his forests. Edwards' wars outside England finally disrupted life within the country. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was renowned for dispensing good justice. His military skills were considerable and frequently exercised in the conquest of Wales. In 1277, when Llywelyn, prince of Wales, surrendered to Edward on humiliating terms, the king be
1 CONC gan to plan a series of fortifications. In this newly-conquered principality, Edward I built castles which were also strong points to sustain his authority. They were impressive symbols of English domination. 'The reign of Edward 
1 CONC I marks the climax of castle building as an exercise of royal authority...it was in the great fortresses by which he guaranteed the conquest of Wales that the medieval castle reached the limits of its capacity as a military device
1 CONC  (King's Works in Wales, 228). The extent of the English military effort required to crush subsequent Welsh resistance intensified Edward's interest and financial commitment to this building program. This was stepped up in the yea
1 CONC rs after 1282. Two of the great 13th century castles in Wales were built by subjects, namely Caerphilly (Gloucester 1267) and Denbigh (de Lacey 1282). Edward's own castles include Kidwelly (1275-1325), Flint (1277-86), Rhuddlan (1
1 CONC 277-82), Conway (1283-9), Caernarvon (1293-1330), Harlech (1283-90), Beaumaris (1295-1330). Each a masterpiece in its own right. Collectively they represent a program of fortress construction unparalleled in scale since the days o
1 CONC f imperial Rome. Massive sums were spent for the five principal strongholds in Snowdonia, Conway, Caernarvon, Criccieth, Harlech and Beaumaris. Beaumaris in Anglesey, Wales, was built 1287-90 with culmination of a concentric plan.
1 CONC  Substantially complete; square inner bailey with six towers and two opposing gatehouses, wall and mural gallery. Outer bailey with 15 towers, two gates, watergate and fortified dock. "No other Edwardian castle presents Such a per
1 CONC fectly scientific system of defense.' Caernarvon, in Caernarvonshire, Wales was built in 1090. It is 'as fair a castle as man ever saw.' A fortress-palace in most advanced military style, incomplete though half a century in buildi
1 CONC ng. Magnificent and extremely substantial remains include stupendous curtain with wall galleries, many great towers and gates. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Twelve lesser castles, three taken over from the Welsh, formed an outer bastion in this encircling wall. All but one of the major castles was accessible to shipping, so that they could be supplied by sea in time of siege.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edwardian castles may be classified into three groups: 
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. Keepless castles in which the flanked curtain wall forms the sole line of defense e.g. Conway.
1 CONT 2. Old castles which adopted a concentric line of defense by extending their site e.g. London Tower.
1 CONT 3. New-plan castles in which the defenses are strictly and deliberately concentric e.g. Beaumaris.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Savoyard architect, Master James of St George incorporated the latest developments in military engineering into his castles. Instead of relying, like earlier fortresses, on a single central strongpoint or "keep", which could b
1 CONC ecome a death trap, the new castles had high enclosure walls studded with massive projecting towers. From these the defenders could sweep the approaches with archery fire, and cover the intervening wall-tops if these fell to an at
1 CONC tacker. Each tower could hold out alone, and in many of the castles, pairs of towers were linked to form powerful gatehouses. What had been the weakest point of a fortress, its entrance, became its strongest feature. The belfry Ed
1 CONC ward I used for assaulting Bothwell was built in Glasgow and payments are recorded for the carpenters, plumbers etc. as well as for materials including lead, wheels, cables, wax. The parts were carried to the siege in 30 wagons, t
1 CONC aking two days to cover the nine miles and involving a bridge over the Clyde and a log road from the bridge to the castle. Sometimes winches and windlasses were used to operate drawbridges and portcullises and for lowering the buc
1 CONC kets down the well. Sometimes these latter were operated by animal power. Rhuddlan was the first of Master James's Welsh castles. Amenities were naturally most developed in the royal castles. In the new castle of Rhuddlan, the cha
1 CONC mber was 'painted', the queen's room had wood specially imported from Gascony and outside its windows Queen Eleanor built a fishpond in the courtyard surrounded by seats for her ladies. The yard itself was laid with 6000 turves an
1 CONC d protected by a fencing of barrel staves. Harlech castle stands as a fighting fortress some 200 feet above the old sea level. Platforms were provided at Harlech for engines in advance of the outer ward on high ground protected by
1 CONC  flanking walls and natural defenses. These were intended to attack ships. At Harlech and Beaumaris the most dramatically sited and the most technically perfect of the castles, Master James raised one circuit of towered walls with
1 CONC in another. The outer defenses were lower than the inner ones and commanded by their fire. "Concentric" fortresses likes these were virtually impregnable. Beaumaris was the last of Edward's Welsh castles to be built. Harlech 's wa
1 CONC lls and towers are in ruins but almost at full height. Edward I had an official, Master Bertram styled ;Machinator', whose main concern seems to have been the construction of engines at Caernarvon and London Tower. The great castl
1 CONC es of Conway and Caernarvon were built as royal residences. At Caernarvon, it was a home and garden as well as fortress. Eleanor built a garden there also and its military importance was softened by its use as a royal residence. E
1 CONC dward recruited a vast army of workmen, masons, and craftsmen from virtually every county in England. The outer walls of Conway Castle, which enclose a royal residence, are almost a mile in circumference. Similar provisions were m
1 CONC ade for the queen's arrival at Conway as Rhuddlan in June 1283 and one of her squires was watering the precious lawn in July. So too in Caernarvon, in time for the royal visit of July 12, 1283, a new lawn was laid. The same castle
1 CONC  had a swan's nest specially built in the middle of its mill-pond. A late 13th and early 14th century architectural feature was the Caernarvon Arch, so called because of the prevalence of this feature at Caernarvon Castle. Doorway
1 CONC  heads are formed of horizontal lintel supported on two corbels instead of the customary arch. Edward brought his queen to Caernarvon to bear the son, the future Edward II, who would be proclaimed the first "prince of Wales' at Ca
1 CONC ernarvon. Wales has been under English rule ever since, in great measure because of the effectiveness of Edward's castle-building. Unlike Wales, Scotland already had many royal and baronial castles on its soil in the late 13th cen
1 CONC tury. The submission of the Scottish magnates to Edward I in 1296 brought all these strongpoints into the king's hands. During Edward II's reign, the castles fell one by one to the Scots, who became the chief beneficiaries of the 
1 CONC royal building program.
1 CONT
1 CONT The significance of Edwardian castles lies in their combination of three elements:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. A replacement of keep by curtain with symmetrical flanking towers.
1 CONT 2. Outer curtain linking two-successive defensive lines which can operate in concert.
1 CONT 3. A powerful Keep-gatehouse with elaborate defenses on the most vulnerable side.
1 CONT
1 CONT 'The Edwardian castles in Wales, which include among their number some of the finest castles ever raised in this country, taken altogether form one defensive system and one comprehensive undertaking which is by far the greatest si
1 CONC ngle achievement in the history of English castle-building.' (Brown, 127). 
1 CONT
1 CONT The astronomical cost of these great works has been well described as 'the premium Edward paid to insure his Welsh conquests against the fire of rebellion'. (J.G. Edwards)
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward I was famed as one who enjoyed the enduring protection of the Lord. When he was a boy, we was once in the middle of a game of chess with one of his knights in a vaulted room. For no apparent reason, he got up and walked awa
1 CONC y. Seconds later, a massive stone, which would have crushed completely anyone who happened to be underneath it, fell from the roof on to the very spot where he had been sitting. He attributed this miraculous escape to the Blessed 
1 CONC Virgin Mary of Walsingham, for whom he felt particular veneration ever afterwards. He had a proud spirit, was reluctant to endure wrongs and quick to forget danger when he was seeking revenge. He was easily placated by a display o
1 CONC f humility. A St Alban's monk, William Rishanger, described one encounter. Once, when Edward I was hunting fowl along a river bank, one of his courtiers on the other side of the river was not taking sufficient care of the falcon, 
1 CONC which had brought down a wild duck among the reeds. Edward rebuked him. When the man seemed to ignore his words, he added threats to which the other, seeing that there was neither bridge nor ford in the vicinity replied, "Oh, I'm 
1 CONC safe enough with this river between us!". This infuriated the prince. Edward I plunged on horseback into the river. He did not know the depth of the river. His horse swam across to the opposite bank, which was hollowed out by the 
1 CONC downward flow of the water. Edward emerged with some difficulty. He drew his sword and set off in pursuit of the courtier who had ridden off. The courtier soon abandoned hope of escape and turned his horse around, baring his head,
1 CONC  knelt humbly and gave himself up to Edward's mercy. The prince sheathed his sword. They returned together in peace. "I have written this account of Edward's character and boyhood in order to give future generations some notion of
1 CONC  the fine actions which were characteristic of his conduct", wrote Rishanger.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward was also prone to fits of typical Plantagenet anger. On one occasion he threw his daughter Elizabeth's coronet into the fire. A formidable man, he personified many of the characteristics expected of a king. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He lavishly endowed an abbey at Vale Royal in Cheshire. His piety did not prevent him from dealing severely with the clergy and the archbishop of Canterbury when they crossed him over taxation in the last decade of his reign. Purs
1 CONC uing his rights with the utmost vigor, Edward I used whatever means he could to manipulate the law. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In the last decade of his reign, Edward I's remarkable successes in war and government began to turn sour. His constant and voracious demands for money and men for his wars with France and Scotland produced strong baronial opposit
1 CONC ion and in 1297 the king averted a major political crisis only by abolishing the most detested tax. the 'maltote', levied on wool. In 1298 a major inquiry into administrative malpractices was set up and in 1300 the king, as a resu
1 CONC lt, agreed to put limits on the foodstuffs and supplies which could be taken for his army. The forest boundaries, which the king's administrators had taken every opportunity to expand, were another major grievance, and in 1301 par
1 CONC liament agreed a subsidy only in return for a careful review and return to ancient limits. From 1303 until his death in 1307, Edward levied taxation more cautiously and borrowed immense sums of money from Italian bankers to financ
1 CONC e his continuing wars. His position in Scotland began to improve. By 1305 it seemed that he had finally conquered that kingdom. In 1306 the new pope released Edward from his promises to parliament over the forests and suspended th
1 CONC e archbishop of Canterbury, Robert Winchelsea, from office. The king managed to defend his treasurer, Walter Langton, from the attacks of the barons. Langton was corrupt and ruthless but the king valued the way in which he had rec
1 CONC onstructed the royal administration after the setbacks of the 1290s and he retained Edward's favor to the end.
1 CONT
1 CONT The best documented chivalrous ceremony of Edward's reign took place in 1306. He prepared for his last Scottish campaign by knighting some 300 young men, including his son Edward. In part his motives were practical. He needed the 
1 CONC fighting men and the money the exercise would produce. Tax raised for the knighting of the king's eldest son was particularly lucrative. The event, however, was also intended to be a great spectacle. The young men, all sons of nob
1 CONC les and knights, were magnificently appareled at royal expense before keeping an overnight vigil in Westminster Abbey and other churches. After which, Prince Edward himself only just knighted, girded on their swords. These had bee
1 CONC n blessed as a sign of the sacramental nature of knighthood. Dubbing was the crucial part of the ritual. A blow with the flat of the hand, it could be, and often was, performed in haste on the battlefield. The climax of the ceremo
1 CONC ny was a magnificent banquet at which roast swans, decorated with golden nets and gilded reeds, formed the centerpiece. These noble birds, much prized as food, belonged exclusively to the king, and Edward swore by them, as well as
1 CONC  by God, that he would avenge himself upon the Scots. 
1 CONT
1 CONT On July 7, 1307, Edward I died at Burgh by the Sands upon his way to fight the Scots once more. He had learned of the evil actions of the new king of Scots and sent orders to the magnates of the realm to meet him at Carlisle on th
1 CONC e 8th of July. In the meantime, the king was so stricken with dysentery that no one, apart from the men of his chamber, was able to speak to him. Rumors spread among the people that the king was dead. Hearing of such tales, the ki
1 CONC ng commanded that everything be ready for an advance upon Scotland. On July 3rd he moved camp from Carlisle over 2 miles. The next day, which was Tuesday he rode another two miles. On Wednesday he rested. On Thursday, King Edward 
1 CONC reached Burgh by Sands and made arrangements to remain there the next day. It was his customary practice to remain in bed each day until the ninth hour. On Friday, July 7th, when his men went to raise him up to eat, he breathed hi
1 CONC s last there in their hands. The king's men concealed his death until his son and the magnates had arrived. Many who spread rumors of his demise were imprisoned. Edward was taken to Westminster Abbey for burial. He was laid to res
1 CONC t in a plain sepulcher whose inscription reads "Edward hammer of the Scots".
0 @N6000000001563228241@ NOTE Time line:
1 CONT 1296 Stone of Scone brought to Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT 1305 Eltham Palace built
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward was a far different character from his father and soon put the country on a footing he preferred. In 1276-77 he brought Llywelyn ap Gruffydd to heel as well as putting the finances of the country back in order after the dis
1 CONC asters of the previous reign. In 1282 he was surprised by a Welsh revolt and by the April of 1283 he had subjugated the whole of Wales, killing Llywelyn and capturing his brother Dafydd. Even this proved insufficient to totally ov
1 CONC erawe Wales and Edward faced further revolts in 1287 and 1295, though neither were as serious as the wars of 1277 and 1282. With the conquest of Wales Edward began a massive castle building project that is still rightly seen as on
1 CONC e of the wonders of the thirteenth century.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Towards the end of his reign Edward became increasingly embroiled in bringing Scotland under his direct rule in a similar manner to that which he had achieved in Wales and this proved by and large to be his undoing. The Welsh cam
1 CONC paigns had made heavy drains on the Exchequer and Edward turned more and more to imposing unjust taxes on his peoples and antagonizing his baronage. The conquest of Scotland failed largely because of Edward's success in Wales, he 
1 CONC had spent his money and this is why no military masterpieces like Caernarfon <caernarf.html> or Harlech <harlech.html> are to be found in Scotland, where Edward had to content himself more with wooden peels like the one he built a
1 CONC t Linlithgow. An increasingly beleaguered Edward, abandoned in some of his campaigns by some of his previously most loyal barons, died crossing the border into Scotland again in 1307.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward I, nicknamed "Longshanks" due to his great height and stature, was perhaps the most successful of the medieval monarchs. The first twenty years of his reign marked a high point of cooperation between crown and community. In
1 CONC  these years, Edward made great strides in reforming government, consolidating territory, and defining foreign policy. He possessed the strength his father lacked and reasserted royal prerogative. Edward fathered many children as 
1 CONC well: sixteen by Eleanor of Castille before her death in 1290, and three more by Margaret. 
1 CONT Edward held to the concept of community, and although at times unscrupulously aggressive, ruled with the general welfare of his subjects in mind. He perceived the crown as judge of the proper course of action for the realm and its
1 CONC  chief legislator; royal authority was granted by law and should be fully utilized for the public good, but that same law also granted protection to the king's subjects. A king should rule with the advice and consent of those whos
1 CONC e rights were in question. The level of interaction between king and subject allowed Edward considerable leeway in achieving his goals. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward I added to the bureaucracy initiated by Henry II to increase his effectiveness as sovereign. He expanded the administration into four principal parts: the Chancery, the Exchequer, the Household, and the Council. The Chancer
1 CONC y researched and created legal documents while the Exchequer received and issued money, scrutinized the accounts of local officials, and kept financial records. These two departments operated within the king's authority but indepe
1 CONC ndently from his personal rule, prompting Edward to follow the practice of earlier kings in developing the Household, a mobile court of clerks and advisers that traveled with the king. The King's Council was the most vital segment
1 CONC  of the four. It consisted of his principal ministers, trusted judges and clerks, a select group of magnates, and also followed the king. The Council dealt with matters of great importance to the realm and acted as a court for cas
1 CONC es of national importance. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's forays into the refinement of law and justice had important consequences in decreasing feudal practice. The Statute of Gloucester (1278) curbed expansion of large private holdings and established the principle that all pr
1 CONC ivate franchises were delegated by, and subordinate to, the crown. Royal jurisdiction became supreme: the Exchequer developed a court to hear financial disputes, the Court of Common Pleas arose to hear property disputes, and the C
1 CONC ourt of the King's Bench addressed criminal cases in which the king had a vested interest. Other statutes prohibited vassals from giving their lands to the church, encouraged primogeniture, and established the king as the sole per
1 CONC son who could make a man his feudal vassal. In essence, Edward set the stage for land to become an article of commerce. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward concentrated on an aggressive foreign policy. A major campaign to control Llywelyn ap Gruffydd of Wales began in 1277 and lasted until Llywelyn's death in 1282. Wales was divided into shires, English civil law was introduce
1 CONC d, and the region was administered by appointed justices. In the manner of earlier monarchs, Edward constructed many new castles to ensure his conquest. In 1301, the king's eldest son was named Prince of Wales, a title still grant
1 CONC ed to all first-born male heirs to the crown. Edward found limited success in extending English influence into Ireland: he introduced a Parliament in Dublin and increased commerce in a few coastal towns, but most of the country wa
1 CONC s controlled by independent barons or Celtic tribal chieftains. He retained English holdings in France through diplomacy, but was drawn into war by the incursions of Philip IV in Gascony. He negotiated a peace with France in 1303 
1 CONC and retained those areas England held before the war. 
1 CONT Edward's involvement in Scotland had far reaching effects. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The country had developed a feudal kingdom similar to England in the Lowlands the Celtic tribal culture dispersed to the Highlands. After the death of the Scottish king, Alexander III, Edward negotiated a treaty whereby Margaret, 
1 CONC Maid of Norway and legitimate heir to the Scottish crown, would be brought to England to marry his oldest son, the future Edward II. Margaret, however, died in 1290 en route to England, leaving a disputed succession in Scotland; E
1 CONC dward claimed the right to intercede as feudal lord of the Scottish kings through their Anglo-Norman roots. Edward arbitrated between thirteen different claimants and chose John Baliol. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Baliol did homage to Edward as his lord, but the Scots resisted Edward's demands for military service. In 1296, Edward invaded Scotland and soundly defeated the Scots under Baliol Ð Baliol was forced to abdicate and the Scottish b
1 CONC arons did homage to Edward as their king. William Wallace incited a rebellion in 1297, defeated the English army at Stirling, and harassed England's northern counties. The next year, Edward defeated Wallace at the Battle of Falkir
1 CONC k but encountered continued resistance until Wallace's capture and execution in 1304. Robert Bruce, the grandson of a claimant to the throne in 1290, instigated another revolt in 1306 and would ultimately defeat the army of Edward
1 CONC  II at Bannockburn. Edward's campaigns in Scotland were ruthless and aroused in the Scots a hatred of England that would endure for generations. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's efforts to finance his wars in France and Scotland strained his relationship with the nobility by instituting both income and personal property taxes. Meetings of the King's Great Council, now referred to as Parliaments, 
1 CONC intermittently included members of the middle class and began curtailing the royal authority. Parliament reaffirmed Magna Carta and the Charter of the Forest in 1297, 1299, 1300, and 1301; it was concluded that no tax should be le
1 CONC vied without consent of the realm as a whole (as represented by Parliament). 
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's character found accurate evaluation by Sir Richard Baker, in A Chronicle of the Kings of England: He had in him the two wisdoms, not often found in any, single; both together, seldom or never: an ability of judgment in hi
1 CONC mself, and a readiness to hear the judgment of others. He was not easily provoked into passion, but once in passion, not easily appeased, as was seen by his dealing with the Scots; towards whom he showed at first patience, and at 
1 CONC last severity. If he be censured for his many taxations, he may be justified by his well bestowing them; for never prince laid out his money to more hon our of himself, or good of his kingdom."
1 CONT
1 CONT As prince he was leader of a reconstituted royalist party, with a more liberal and national spirit, and in 1264 took part in the fighting between the King and the barons.  He was taken prisoner at Lewes, but escaped and defeated S
1 CONC imon de Montfort, Earl of Leicester.  For the rest of his father's reign he controlled policy and appropriated enough of Leicester's ideals not to make the royalist restoration a reaction.  He engaged in a crusade in 1272, reachin
1 CONC g the city of Acre, and was crowned on his return home in 1274. 
1 CONT
1 CONT  At the beginning of his reign he devoted his attention to legislation, based on the principle that to be successful his policy must be popular.  From 1275 to 1290 nearly every year was marked by the enacting of an important law. 
1 CONC  The general effect of his work was to eliminate feudalism in political life, to limit church courts to ecclesiastical matters, and to stop the sending of tribute to Rome.  In 1295 he convoked what has been called the Model Parlia
1 CONC ment, because it first illustrated the type which was to be perpetuated in all subsequent Parliaments. In foreign policy he was energetic.  Llewelyn, Prince of Wales, continued to evade doing homage and Edward invaded that country
1 CONC  in 1277, forcing Llewelyn to surrender.  
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1282 he completed his conquest and later issued the Statute of Wales, providing a scheme of government for North Wales.  The death of Margaret, heir to the Scottish throne, led to a disputed succession and the Scots asked Edwar
1 CONC d to arbitrate between the many claimants.  He accepted on condition that they recognize him as an overlord, and decided in favor of John Baliol.  The Scotch people resented the English claims of overlordship and, in 1297, revolte
1 CONC d under William Wallace.  Edward was then forced to undertake the conquest of Scotland, but although he destroyed the power of Wallace in the battle of Falkirk, this did not end Scotch resistance.  In the meantime he was at war wi
1 CONC th France for the recovery of Gascony and it was not until 1303 that he was able to continue the war with Scotland.  
1 CONT
1 CONT He subdued the country, but had hardly organized the new government, when a fresh revolt broke out under Robert Bruce.  The latter was crowned King of Scotland and for the third time Edward invaded that country.  He took the field
1 CONC  in person, but the effort was too great and he died at Burgh-on-Sands near Carlisle in 1307.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was crowned King 19 Aug 1274, at Westminster by Edward Kilwardby, Archbishop of Canterbury. In 1295 he called together a parliament consisting of representatives of the nobility, the church, and the common people.  This "Model 
1 CONC Parliament" made the beginning of parlimentary government in England. He died of dysentery and bowel hemorrhage at age 68.
