1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward was born in 1239. He was married twice. Firstly at a very young
1 CONT age to Eleanor of Castile, who died in 1290, and whom bore him the
1 CONT children: Eleanor, Joan, Henry, Julian, Joan  of Acre, Alphonso,
1 CONT Isabel, Margaret, Berengaria, Mary, Alice, Elizabeth, Edward, Beatrice
1 CONT and  Blanche. His second wife, Margaret of France, daughter of the King
1 CONT of France, bore him Thomas,  Edmund and Eleanor.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was a special child to his father. He was born very late in
1 CONT Henry's life. He was named  after the canon, Edward the Confessor, and
1 CONT although his title says Edward I, there were three  Edwards previous to
1 CONT him.    It was Henry who arranged for the important marriage of Edward
1 CONT to Eleanor, the half-sister of  Alfonso X, King of Castile and Leon. It
1 CONT was an arranged marriage which bore many children and
1 CONT  was full of love.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was made Overlord of Ireland, before he became King, and was
1 CONT responsible for Gascony  and Wales. He was a typically spoilt
1 CONT adolescent,and liked to spend his time setting up jousting
1 CONT tournaments, in which many lives would be lost at a time. However
1 CONT though he had once recognised  the justice of Simon de Montfort's
1 CONT stance against his father, he rallied to help his father.    It was his
1 CONT role as a general that helped quash De Montfort, and after this he
1 CONT became an exemplary
1 CONT  figure in the ruling of England. He was his father's Regent and
1 CONT succeeded  nchallenged to the  throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT  He did not become King until the age of 35 and was devoted to personal
1 CONT and political integrity. He  was a devoted ruler of England and
1 CONT developed state relations all around the world. He also fought  many
1 CONT wars and used a great deal of the funds of England in these battles. He
1 CONT borrowed heavily  from the Jews in England. They were expert currency
1 CONT manipulators.    The King had tried to involve them in more productive
1 CONT occupations, but against all local feeling, and  the lack of profit to
1 CONT be attained, the Jews went back to their old vocations. In 1290 Edward
1 CONT expelled
1 CONT  the Jews from England. Being unable to borrow money, Edward had to
1 CONT impose high taxes on the  local populations, which was of course highly
1 CONT unpopular.
1 CONT
1 CONT  After having defeated and slain the last Welsh Prince of Wales,
1 CONT Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, Edward  offered his baby son to the Welsh people
1 CONT as the Prince of Wales, in a symbolic gesture. The Welsh  gave the
1 CONT English knowledge of the long bow, one of the most valued weapons
1 CONT before gunpowder,  and Edward encouraged its use.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pacification of the Welsh was not instant, and it took years for
1 CONT the land and power to be  delegated in face of harsh resentment.
1 CONT
1 CONT  War with Scotland was causing great problems. It concerned an attempt
1 CONT to reconcile the centre of  Scotland from Edinburgh to Scone, the
1 CONT natural but unrecognised seat of Scotland. When Alexander  III, King of
1 CONT Scotland died, the crown passed to his three year old grand daughter,
1 CONT Margaret.  When Margaret was aged six, Edward arranged for her to be
1 CONT betrothed to his heir Edward, which  would have led to a peaceful union
1 CONT of England and Scotland. Margaret died in a ship wreck on the  way to
1 CONT her coronation in Scotland, then the succession to the crown was
1 CONT disputed. Edward stepped  in to arbitrate and with a balanced
1 CONT Commission took eighteen months to choose John Balliol.    This
1 CONT decision led to a revolt, which Edward managed to overcome. He declared
1 CONT himself King of  Scotland and carried the Coronation Stone of Scotland
1 CONT from Scone Palace off to England. It was  this that led to the revolt
1 CONT by William Wallace who was eventually defeated by Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1306 Robert the Bruce was declared King of the Scots by the
1 CONT Scottish people and in the  ensuing war he was at first defeated by
1 CONT Edward. In a later campaign to crush Robert the Bruce  Edward died at
1 CONT Burgh by Sands, after being the monarch of England for 35 years.
0 @N6000000001578882114@ NOTE (Research):Edward I, first-born son of King Henry III by Eleanor, daughter of the count of Savoy, succeeded his father to the throne at the age of thirty-four. Edward's changes of direction during their war between Henry III and h
1 CONC is barons earned him a reputation for untrustworthiness even before he came to the throne. He was indeed as capable of faithlessness as his grandfather King John but he was far more successful. One writer characterized him as a le
1 CONC opard, an animal associated with deviousness. However, the same man also attributed to Edward the pride and ferocity of a lion. His outlook and behavior was quite unlike his father's. Edward was a strong leader and conducted the a
1 CONC ffairs of state skillfully. From boyhood, he was dedicated to the practice of arms, for which he won a world-wide reputation surpassing that of any other Christian prince of his time. Edward was elegant in appearance. He was a hea
1 CONC d taller than average in stature. In his youth, his hair was silvery-blonde which turned from fair to dark, while in old age it became a magnificent swan-like white. His forehead was broad, and the rest of his features were regula
1 CONC r, except his left eyelid drooped, which gave him a resemblance to his father. He was not lacking in eloquence when persuasive arguments were needed, despite a lisp. According to Nicholas Trevet, a contemporary chronicler, Edward 
1 CONC I was very tall, with long arms and legs, hence his nickname "Longshanks". His long arms were in proportion to his supple body. No man was ever endowed with greater muscular strength for wielding a sword. His breast swelled above 
1 CONC his stomach, and the length of his legs ensured that he was never dislodged from his seat by the galloping and jumping of horses.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Edward I took a break from military pursuits, he indulged in hunting birds and wild beasts, especially stags. He used to chase these on horseback. When he had one cornered, he would strike it down with his sword, rather than 
1 CONC use a hunting spear. English monarchs, from William the Conqueror, were devoted to the hunt. Although they and their barons made profits out of their forests, they valued them primarily as game covers. Hunting with falcons was a r
1 CONC itual and a source of pleasure. Men, horses and the extremely valuable birds were caparisoned in elegant trappings of fine leather and silk.
1 CONT
1 CONT Great nobles like the king, possessed their own private forests. They all employed staffs of verderers under the chief huntsman, which was often the king. The staff preserved the game and tended the pack of hounds that was conside
1 CONC red an aristocratic necessity. There were two seasons to hunt deer: November to February and June to September. Summer was the better season because the deer were fat or "in grease". Richard of Cornwall, Edward I's uncle, took 32 
1 CONC bucks in nine days in July 1252 in the forest of Rockingham. By the 14th century, an increasingly literate aristocracy wished to be informed about hunting. William Twici, Edward II's huntsman produced a practical hunting manual " 
1 CONC L'Art de la Venerie". The book of St Albans was published in the 14th century and it ennobled falconry. It laid down an hierarchy of birds to match the hierarchy of society. An eagle, or more curiously a vulture, was fit only for 
1 CONC an emperor. A 'Falcon, properly a female peregrine, was for a prince, and a 'tiercel', male peregrine, for a nobleman. A hunting parson was allowed only a sparrow-hawk.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward I was a strong leader. As king he was not greatly loved but was viewed with respect and feared throughout England. His devotion to his first queen, Eleanor of Castile was striking. They were married in 1254.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first two decades of Edward I's reign witnessed a great surge of governmental energy. The king started in 1274 by instigating county-wide investigations into the usurpation of royal rights and lands following the civil war of 
1 CONC the 1260s, and into abuses by local officials. This was the largest such undertaking since the Doomsday survey nearly 200 years earlier. In 1285 the government attempted to improve peace keeping by stipulating that all men over th
1 CONC e age of 15 should hold specified arms and be ready to use them under official direction and to maintain law and order. Two years later, in 1287, keepers of the peace were appointed in the counties to supervise this militia. There
1 CONC  was no improvement, however, in order and after 1294 the prevailing atmosphere of war, lawlessness in the countryside increased. New commissions of justices were therefore appointed in 1304-5 to deal with offenders. Their session
1 CONC s were known as 'tailgating enquiries after the gangs of men armed with staves who terrorized the population. Many were soldiers who had fought for the king in his wars against the Welsh, Scots and French. They were now sheltering
1 CONC  in his forests. Edwards' wars outside England finally disrupted life within the country.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was renowned for dispensing good justice. His military skills were considerable and frequently exercised in the conquest of Wales. In 1277, when Llywelyn, prince of Wales, surrendered to Edward on humiliating terms, the king be
1 CONC gan to plan a series of fortifications. In this newly-conquered principality, Edward I built castles which were also strong points to sustain his authority. They were impressive symbols of English domination. 'The reign of Edward 
1 CONC I marks the climax of castle building as an exercise of royal authority...it was in the great fortresses by which he guaranteed the conquest of Wales that the medieval castle reached the limits of its capacity as a military device
1 CONC  (King's Works in Wales, 228). The extent of the English military effort required to crush subsequent Welsh resistance intensified Edward's interest and financial commitment to this building program. This was stepped up in the yea
1 CONC rs after 1282. Two of the great 13th century castles in Wales were built by subjects, namely Caerphilly (Gloucester 1267) and Denbigh (de Lacey 1282). Edward's own castles include Kidwelly (1275-1325), Flint (1277-86), Rhuddlan (1
1 CONC 277-82), Conway (1283-9), Caernarvon (1293-1330), Harlech (1283-90), Beaumaris (1295-1330). Each a masterpiece in its own right. Collectively they represent a program of fortress construction unparalleled in scale since the days o
1 CONC f imperial Rome. Massive sums were spent for the five principal strongholds in Snowdonia, Conway, Caernarvon, Criccieth, Harlech and Beaumaris. Beaumaris in Anglesey, Wales, was built 1287-90 with culmination of a concentric plan.
1 CONC  Substantially complete; square inner bailey with six towers and two opposing gatehouses, wall and mural gallery. Outer bailey with 15 towers, two gates, watergate and fortified dock. "No other Edwardian castle presents Such a per
1 CONC fectly scientific system of defense.' Caernarvon, in Caernarvonshire, Wales was built in 1090. It is 'as fair a castle as man ever saw.' A fortress-palace in most advanced military style, incomplete though half a century in buildi
1 CONC ng. Magnificent and extremely substantial remains include stupendous curtain with wall galleries, many great towers and gates.
1 CONT
1 CONT Twelve lesser castles, three taken over from the Welsh, formed an outer bastion in this encircling wall. All but one of the major castles was accessible to shipping, so that they could be supplied by sea in time of siege.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edwardian castles may be classified into three groups:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. Keepless castles in which the flanked curtain wall forms the sole line of defense e.g. Conway.
1 CONT 2. Old castles which adopted a concentric line of defense by extending their site e.g. London Tower.
1 CONT 3. New-plan castles in which the defenses are strictly and deliberately concentric e.g. Beaumaris.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Savoyard architect, Master James of St George incorporated the latest developments in military engineering into his castles. Instead of relying, like earlier fortresses, on a single central strongpoint or "keep", which could b
1 CONC ecome a death trap, the new castles had high enclosure walls studded with massive projecting towers. From these the defenders could sweep the approaches with archery fire, and cover the intervening wall-tops if these fell to an at
1 CONC tacker. Each tower could hold out alone, and in many of the castles, pairs of towers were linked to form powerful gatehouses. What had been the weakest point of a fortress, its entrance, became its strongest feature. The belfry Ed
1 CONC ward I used for assaulting Bothwell was built in Glasgow and payments are recorded for the carpenters, plumbers etc. as well as for materials including lead, wheels, cables, wax. The parts were carried to the siege in 30 wagons, t
1 CONC aking two days to cover the nine miles and involving a bridge over the Clyde and a log road from the bridge to the castle. Sometimes winches and windlasses were used to operate drawbridges and portcullises and for lowering the buc
1 CONC kets down the well. Sometimes these latter were operated by animal power. Rhuddlan was the first of Master James's Welsh castles. Amenities were naturally most developed in the royal castles. In the new castle of Rhuddlan, the cha
1 CONC mber was 'painted', the queen's room had wood specially imported from Gascony and outside its windows Queen Eleanor built a fishpond in the courtyard surrounded by seats for her ladies. The yard itself was laid with 6000 turves an
1 CONC d protected by a fencing of barrel staves. Harlech castle stands as a fighting fortress some 200 feet above the old sea level. Platforms were provided at Harlech for engines in advance of the outer ward on high ground protected by
1 CONC  flanking walls and natural defenses. These were intended to attack ships. At Harlech and Beaumaris the most dramatically sited and the most technically perfect of the castles, Master James raised one circuit of towered walls with
1 CONC in another. The outer defenses were lower than the inner ones and commanded by their fire. "Concentric" fortresses likes these were virtually impregnable. Beaumaris was the last of Edward's Welsh castles to be built. Harlech 's wa
1 CONC lls and towers are in ruins but almost at full height. Edward I had an official, Master Bertram styled ;Machinator', whose main concern seems to have been the construction of engines at Caernarvon and London Tower. The great castl
1 CONC es of Conway and Caernarvon were built as royal residences. At Caernarvon, it was a home and garden as well as fortress. Eleanor built a garden there also and its military importance was softened by its use as a royal residence. E
1 CONC dward recruited a vast army of workmen, masons, and craftsmen from virtually every county in England. The outer walls of Conway Castle, which enclose a royal residence, are almost a mile in circumference. Similar provisions were m
1 CONC ade for the queen's arrival at Conway as Rhuddlan in June 1283 and one of her squires was watering the precious lawn in July. So too in Caernarvon, in time for the royal visit of July 12, 1283, a new lawn was laid. The same castle
1 CONC  had a swan's nest specially built in the middle of its mill-pond. A late 13th and early 14th century architectural feature was the Caernarvon Arch, so called because of the prevalence of this feature at Caernarvon Castle. Doorway
1 CONC  heads are formed of horizontal lintel supported on two corbels instead of the customary arch. Edward brought his queen to Caernarvon to bear the son, the future Edward II, who would be proclaimed the first "prince of Wales' at Ca
1 CONC ernarvon. Wales has been under English rule ever since, in great measure because of the effectiveness of Edward's castle-building. Unlike Wales, Scotland already had many royal and baronial castles on its soil in the late 13th cen
1 CONC tury. The submission of the Scottish magnates to Edward I in 1296 brought all these strongpoints into the king's hands. During Edward II's reign, the castles fell one by one to the Scots, who became the chief beneficiaries of the 
1 CONC royal building program.
1 CONT
1 CONT The significance of Edwardian castles lies in their combination of three elements:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. A replacement of keep by curtain with symmetrical flanking towers.
1 CONT 2. Outer curtain linking two-successive defensive lines which can operate in concert.
1 CONT 3. A powerful Keep-gatehouse with elaborate defenses on the most vulnerable side.
1 CONT
1 CONT 'The Edwardian castles in Wales, which include among their number some of the finest castles ever raised in this country, taken altogether form one defensive system and one comprehensive undertaking which is by far the greatest si
1 CONC ngle achievement in the history of English castle-building.' (Brown, 127).
1 CONT
1 CONT The astronomical cost of these great works has been well described as 'the premium Edward paid to insure his Welsh conquests against the fire of rebellion'. (J.G. Edwards)
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward I was famed as one who enjoyed the enduring protection of the Lord. When he was a boy, we was once in the middle of a game of chess with one of his knights in a vaulted room. For no apparent reason, he got up and walked awa
1 CONC y. Seconds later, a massive stone, which would have crushed completely anyone who happened to be underneath it, fell from the roof on to the very spot where he had been sitting. He attributed this miraculous escape to the Blessed 
1 CONC Virgin Mary of Walsingham, for whom he felt particular veneration ever afterwards. He had a proud spirit, was reluctant to endure wrongs and quick to forget danger when he was seeking revenge. He was easily placated by a display o
1 CONC f humility. A St Alban's monk, William Rishanger, described one encounter. Once, when Edward I was hunting fowl along a river bank, one of his courtiers on the other side of the river was not taking sufficient care of the falcon, 
1 CONC which had brought down a wild duck among the reeds. Edward rebuked him. When the man seemed to ignore his words, he added threats to which the other, seeing that there was neither bridge nor ford in the vicinity replied, "Oh, I'm 
1 CONC safe enough with this river between us!". This infuriated the prince. Edward I plunged on horseback into the river. He did not know the depth of the river. His horse swam across to the opposite bank, which was hollowed out by the 
1 CONC downward flow of the water. Edward emerged with some difficulty. He drew his sword and set off in pursuit of the courtier who had ridden off. The courtier soon abandoned hope of escape and turned his horse around, baring his head,
1 CONC  knelt humbly and gave himself up to Edward's mercy. The prince sheathed his sword. They returned together in peace. "I have written this account of Edward's character and boyhood in order to give future generations some notion of
1 CONC  the fine actions which were characteristic of his conduct", wrote Rishanger.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward was also prone to fits of typical Plantagenet anger. On one occasion he threw his daughter Elizabeth's coronet into the fire. A formidable man, he personified many of the characteristics expected of a king.
1 CONT
1 CONT He lavishly endowed an abbey at Vale Royal in Cheshire. His piety did not prevent him from dealing severely with the clergy and the archbishop of Canterbury when they crossed him over taxation in the last decade of his reign. Purs
1 CONC uing his rights with the utmost vigor, Edward I used whatever means he could to manipulate the law.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the last decade of his reign, Edward I's remarkable successes in war and government began to turn sour. His constant and voracious demands for money and men for his wars with France and Scotland produced strong baronial opposit
1 CONC ion and in 1297 the king averted a major political crisis only by abolishing the most detested tax. the 'maltote', levied on wool. In 1298 a major inquiry into administrative malpractices was set up and in 1300 the king, as a resu
1 CONC lt, agreed to put limits on the foodstuffs and supplies which could be taken for his army. The forest boundaries, which the king's administrators had taken every opportunity to expand, were another major grievance, and in 1301 par
1 CONC liament agreed a subsidy only in return for a careful review and return to ancient limits. From 1303 until his death in 1307, Edward levied taxation more cautiously and borrowed immense sums of money from Italian bankers to financ
1 CONC e his continuing wars. His position in Scotland began to improve. By 1305 it seemed that he had finally conquered that kingdom. In 1306 the new pope released Edward from his promises to parliament over the forests and suspended th
1 CONC e archbishop of Canterbury, Robert Winchelsea, from office. The king managed to defend his treasurer, Walter Langton, from the attacks of the barons. Langton was corrupt and ruthless but the king valued the way in which he had rec
1 CONC onstructed the royal administration after the setbacks of the 1290s and he retained Edward's favor to the end.
1 CONT
1 CONT The best documented chivalrous ceremony of Edward's reign took place in 1306. He prepared for his last Scottish campaign by knighting some 300 young men, including his son Edward. In part his motives were practical. He needed the 
1 CONC fighting men and the money the exercise would produce. Tax raised for the knighting of the king's eldest son was particularly lucrative. The event, however, was also intended to be a great spectacle. The young men, all sons of nob
1 CONC les and knights, were magnificently appareled at royal expense before keeping an overnight vigil in Westminster Abbey and other churches. After which, Prince Edward himself only just knighted, girded on their swords. These had bee
1 CONC n blessed as a sign of the sacramental nature of knighthood. Dubbing was the crucial part of the ritual. A blow with the flat of the hand, it could be, and often was, performed in haste on the battlefield. The climax of the ceremo
1 CONC ny was a magnificent banquet at which roast swans, decorated with golden nets and gilded reeds, formed the centerpiece. These noble birds, much prized as food, belonged exclusively to the king, and Edward swore by them, as well as
1 CONC  by God, that he would avenge himself upon the Scots.
1 CONT
1 CONT On July 7, 1307, Edward I died at Burgh by the Sands upon his way to fight the Scots once more. He had learned of the evil actions of the new king of Scots and sent orders to the magnates of the realm to meet him at Carlisle on th
1 CONC e 8th of July. In the meantime, the king was so stricken with dysentery that no one, apart from the men of his chamber, was able to speak to him. Rumors spread among the people that the king was dead. Hearing of such tales, the ki
1 CONC ng commanded that everything be ready for an advance upon Scotland. On July 3rd he moved camp from Carlisle over 2 miles. The next day, which was Tuesday he rode another two miles. On Wednesday he rested. On Thursday, King Edward 
1 CONC reached Burgh by Sands and made arrangements to remain there the next day. It was his customary practice to remain in bed each day until the ninth hour. On Friday, July 7th, when his men went to raise him up to eat, he breathed hi
1 CONC s last there in their hands. The king's men concealed his death until his son and the magnates had arrived. Many who spread rumors of his demise were imprisoned. Edward was taken to Westminster Abbey for burial. He was laid to res
1 CONC t in a plain sepulcher whose inscription reads "Edward hammer of the Scots".
0 @N6000000000337383534@ NOTE King Edward I of England (1272-1307) completed the conquest of Wales and temporarily subdued Scotland. He was the eldest son of HENRY III. In 1254 he was made duke of Gascony and married Eleanor of Castile (d. 1290). In contrast t
1 CONC o his father, Edward showed masterfulness in the disputes with the English barons following the governmental reforms instituted by the Provisions of Oxford (1258). He supported Simon de MONTFORT in 1259 but later changed sides. He
1 CONC  fought for the king at the Battle of Lewes (1264) and himself defeated Montfort decisively at Evesham (1265), restoring royal power. In 1271-72 he was on crusade at Acre. During the years from 1272, when Edward succeeded his fath
1 CONC er, to 1290 striking achievements occurred. Edward conquered the Welsh principality of Llywelyn ap Gruffydd in devastating campaigns in 1277 and 1282-83 and built massive castles to keep it secure. In England he held regular parli
1 CONC aments. A program of legislation strengthened royal control over the court system and reformed the tangled feudal land law. 
1 CONT
1 CONT After 1294 wars in Scotland and France dominated Edward's reign. The death (1290) of Margaret, Maid of Norway, heiress to the Scottish crown, allowed Edward as suzerain to choose a successor, John de BALIOL, and then to claim dire
1 CONC ct rule over Scotland, which he subdued in 1296. In France the conflict concerned the French king's overlordship over Edward's duchy of Gascony. In 1297, Edward attacked France to assert his rights, but the expedition was cut shor
1 CONC t by the rebellion in Scotland of Sir William WALLACE. At the same time the English nobles rebelled, forcing Edward to grant Parliament control over taxes. By a treaty (1303) with PHILIP IV of France, Edward retained Gascony. He f
1 CONC ailed, however, to quell the risings of Wallace and Robert the Bruce (later ROBERT I), and Scotland remained only half- conquered at his death. He was succeeded by his son Edward II. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Costain, Thomas, The Three Edwards (1962).Prestwich, M., Edward I (1988
1 CONT
