0 @N6000000002322264470@ NOTE Reigned 1272-1307
1 CONT In the Barons war 1264-67 he defeated the Barons at Evesham (1265) as
1 CONT King
1 CONT he is noted for encouraging Parliamentary institutions at the expense of
1 CONT feudalism and for subduing Wales on which he imposed the English system
1 CONT of
1 CONT administration. He later tried to assert his authority over Scotland
1 CONT and died
1 CONT while on his way to fight Robert Bruce.
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward I (born 1239, ruled 1272-1307) was one of England's greatest
1 CONT kings. He was a handsome man, with fair hair and ruddy cheeks, and so
1 CONT tall that he was nicknamed Longshanks. He delighted in tournaments and
1 CONT hunting, but he was also practical and hardworking. For seven years
1 CONT before he came to the throne, he was the real ruler for his weak
1 CONT father, Henry III (see Henry, Kings of England; Montfort). He was in
1 CONT the Holy Land involved in the crusades when his father died, but there
1 CONT was no question that he would take the throne.
1 CONT    Edward has been called the "English Justinian" because, like the
1 CONT Roman emperor Justinian, he organized the laws. His laws were not
1 CONT restatements of existing customs but statutes in the modern sense. Many
1 CONT of them, particularly the land laws, had a long-lasting influence. A
1 CONT statute of 1285 limited church courts to strictly church matters a
1 CONT change that Edward's great-grandfather, Henry II, had been unable to
1 CONT make because of the murder of Thomas a Becket (see Becket; Henry, Kings
1 CONT of England). Edward also stopped paying a feudal tribute to the pope.
1 CONT    Parliament grew in strength during Edward's reign because he
1 CONT continued the policy of Simon de Montfort in summoning to it
1 CONT representatives of the towns and lesser knights. His parliament of 1295
1 CONT is known as the Model Parliament (see Parliament). In 1297 he
1 CONT reaffirmed the Magna Carta in the famous confirmation of the charters.
1 CONT All of Edward's moves were not fair and admirable ones, however; he
1 CONT forced Jews out of England in 1290.
1 CONT    Soon after coming to the throne, Edward conquered Wales and gave to
1 CONT his infant son, Edward, the title prince of Wales (see Wales). Until
1 CONT 1289 the care of his French possessions, principally Aquitaine, in
1 CONT Southern France, absorbed much of his attention. For the rest of his
1 CONT life, his main concern was Scotland.
1 CONT    He conquered the country in 1296; but in 1297 all Scotland rose in
1 CONT revolt against him under the popular leader, William Wallace. Edward
1 CONT defeated Wallace at Falkirk the next year, but the Scots still
1 CONT resisted. Near the end of Edward's reign Scotland found a new leader in
1 CONT Robert Bruce. In 1307 King Edward, then 70 years old, led an army
1 CONT toward Scotland but died before he reached the border.
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward was born in 1239. He was married twice. Firstly at a very young
1 CONT age to Eleanor of Castile, who died in 1290, and whom bore him the
1 CONT children: Eleanor, Joan, Henry, Julian, Joan  of Acre, Alphonso,
1 CONT Isabel, Margaret, Berengaria, Mary, Alice, Elizabeth, Edward, Beatrice
1 CONT and  Blanche. His second wife, Margaret of France, daughter of the King
1 CONT of France, bore him Thomas,  Edmund and Eleanor.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was a special child to his father. He was born very late in
1 CONT Henry's life. He was named  after the canon, Edward the Confessor, and
1 CONT although his title says Edward I, there were three  Edwards previous to
1 CONT him.    It was Henry who arranged for the important marriage of Edward
1 CONT to Eleanor, the half-sister of  Alfonso X, King of Castile and Leon. It
1 CONT was an arranged marriage which bore many children and
1 CONT  was full of love.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was made Overlord of Ireland, before he became King, and was
1 CONT responsible for Gascony  and Wales. He was a typically spoilt
1 CONT adolescent,and liked to spend his time setting up jousting
1 CONT tournaments, in which many lives would be lost at a time. However
1 CONT though he had once recognised  the justice of Simon de Montfort's
1 CONT stance against his father, he rallied to help his father.    It was his
1 CONT role as a general that helped quash De Montfort, and after this he
1 CONT became an exemplary
1 CONT  figure in the ruling of England. He was his father's Regent and
1 CONT succeeded  nchallenged to the  throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT  He did not become King until the age of 35 and was devoted to personal
1 CONT and political integrity. He  was a devoted ruler of England and
1 CONT developed state relations all around the world. He also fought  many
1 CONT wars and used a great deal of the funds of England in these battles. He
1 CONT borrowed heavily  from the Jews in England. They were expert currency
1 CONT manipulators.    The King had tried to involve them in more productive
1 CONT occupations, but against all local feeling, and  the lack of profit to
1 CONT be attained, the Jews went back to their old vocations. In 1290 Edward
1 CONT expelled
1 CONT  the Jews from England. Being unable to borrow money, Edward had to
1 CONT impose high taxes on the  local populations, which was of course highly
1 CONT unpopular.
1 CONT
1 CONT  After having defeated and slain the last Welsh Prince of Wales,
1 CONT Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, Edward  offered his baby son to the Welsh people
1 CONT as the Prince of Wales, in a symbolic gesture. The Welsh  gave the
1 CONT English knowledge of the long bow, one of the most valued weapons
1 CONT before gunpowder,  and Edward encouraged its use.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pacification of the Welsh was not instant, and it took years for
1 CONT the land and power to be  delegated in face of harsh resentment.
1 CONT
1 CONT  War with Scotland was causing great problems. It concerned an attempt
1 CONT to reconcile the centre of  Scotland from Edinburgh to Scone, the
1 CONT natural but unrecognised seat of Scotland. When Alexander  III, King of
1 CONT Scotland died, the crown passed to his three year old grand daughter,
1 CONT Margaret.  When Margaret was aged six, Edward arranged for her to be
1 CONT betrothed to his heir Edward, which  would have led to a peaceful union
1 CONT of England and Scotland. Margaret died in a ship wreck on the  way to
1 CONT her coronation in Scotland, then the succession to the crown was
1 CONT disputed. Edward stepped  in to arbitrate and with a balanced
1 CONT Commission took eighteen months to choose John Balliol.    This
1 CONT decision led to a revolt, which Edward managed to overcome. He declared
1 CONT himself King of  Scotland and carried the Coronation Stone of Scotland
1 CONT from Scone Palace off to England. It was  this that led to the revolt
1 CONT by William Wallace who was eventually defeated by Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1306 Robert the Bruce was declared King of the Scots by the
1 CONT Scottish people and in the  ensuing war he was at first defeated by
1 CONT Edward. In a later campaign to crush Robert the Bruce  Edward died at
1 CONT Burgh by Sands, after being the monarch of England for 35 years.
0 @N6000000002322312662@ NOTE From the house of Plantagenet, Edward expelled the Jews from England in 1290,
1 CONT but the conquest of Scotland, however, became the ruling passion of his
1 CONT life. In 1305, he captured Sir William Wallace and executed him, leading to
1 CONT a Scottish revolt and the coronation of Robert de Bruce as King of Scotland.
1 CONT While on his way to subdue the Scots for the third time during his reign,
1 CONT Edward I died en route near Carlisle in 1307.
0 @N6000000002187747719@ NOTE Edward I of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Edward I of England (June 17, 1239 – July 7, 1307), popularly known as "Longshanks" because of his 6 foot 2 inch frame and the "Hammer of the Scots" (his tombstone, in Latin, read, Hic est Edwardvs Primus Scottorum Malleus, "
1 CONC Here lies Edward I, Hammer of the Scots"), achieved fame as the monarch who conquered Wales and who kept Scotland under English domination. He reigned from 1272 to 1307, ascending the throne of England on November 21, 1272 after t
1 CONC he death of his father, King Henry III of England. His mother was Queen consort Eleanor of Provence.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Biography 
1 CONT 2 Military campaigns 
1 CONT 2.1 Crusades 
1 CONT 2.2 Welsh Wars 
1 CONT 2.3 Scottish Wars 
1 CONT 3 Government and law under Edward I 
1 CONT 4 Edward and the Jews 
1 CONT 5 Interesting Facts about Edward I 
1 CONT 6 Issue 
1 CONT 7 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Biography
1 CONT Edward was born at the Palace of Westminster on June 17 or 18, 1239. He married twice; his first marriage, in October 1254, was to Eleanor of Castile which produced sixteen children, and her death in 1290 affected Edward deeply. H
1 CONC e displayed his grief by erecting the Eleanor crosses, one at each place where her funeral cortege stopped for the night. His second marriage, in September 1299, to Marguerite of France (known as the "Pearl of France" by her Engli
1 CONC sh subjects), the daughter of King Philippe III of France (Phillip the Bold) and Maria of Brabant, produced three children.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's character greatly contrasted that of his father, who reigned in England throughout Edward's childhood and consistently tended to favour compromise with his opponents. Edward had already shown himself as an ambitious and i
1 CONC mpatient man, displaying considerable military prowess in defeating Simon de Montfort at the Battle of Evesham in 1265. He gained a reputation for treating rebels and other foes with great savagery. He relentlessly pursued the sur
1 CONC viving members of the de Montfort family, his cousins.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Military campaigns
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Crusades
1 CONT In 1269 Cardinal Ottobono, the Papal Legate, arrived in England and appealed to Prince Edward and his brother Edmund to participate in the Eighth Crusade alongside Louis IX of France. In order to fund the crusade, Edward had to bo
1 CONC rrow heavily from Louis IX and the Jews of England. It is estimated by scholars such as P.R. Coss that Edward raised and spent close to half a million livres. The number of knights and retainers that accompanied Edward on the crus
1 CONC ade was quite small, possibly around 230 knights. Many of the members of Edward's expedition were close friends and family including his wife Eleanor of Castile, his brother Edmund, and his first cousin Henry De Alamain. The origi
1 CONC nal goal of the crusade was to relieve the beleagured Christian stronghold of Acre, but Louis had been diverted to Tunis. By the time that Edward arrived at Tunis, Louis had died of disease. The majority of the French forces at Tu
1 CONC nis returned home, but a small number of them joined Edward who continued onward to Acre to participate in the Ninth Crusade. After a short stop in Cyprus, Edward arrived in Acre with thirteen ships. While in Acre, Edward engaged 
1 CONC in diplomacy with the Mongols hoping to form an alliance against Sultan Baibars of Egypt. This did not come to fruition. In 1271 Hugh III of Cyprus arrived with a contingent of knights. The arrival of the additional forces embolde
1 CONC ned Edward, who engaged in a raid on the town of Ququn. Soon afterward Edward signed a ten year peace treaty with Baibars. Around the same time, Edward was nearly assassinated but warded off his attacker, according to Matthew Pari
1 CONC s, by bludgeoning his would be assassin with a metal tripod. Edward left the Holy Land and returned to England in 1272.
1 CONT
1 CONT Overall, Edward's crusade was insignificant and only gave the city of Acre a reprieve of ten years. However, Edward's reputation was greatly enhanced by his participation in the crusade and was hailed by some contemporary commenta
1 CONC tors as a new Richard the Lionheart. Furthermore, some historians believe Edward was inspired by the design of the castles he saw while on crusade and incorporated similar features into the castles he built to secure portions of W
1 CONC ales, such as Caernarfon Castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Welsh Wars
1 CONT One of Edward's early achievements was the conquest of Wales. Under the 1267 Treaty of Montgomery, Llewelyn ap Gruffydd had extended Welsh territories southwards into what had been the lands of the English Marcher lords, and gaine
1 CONC d the title of Prince of Wales although he still owed homage to the English monarch as overlord. Edward refused to recognise the Treaty which had been concluded by his father. In 1275, pirates in Edward's pay intercepted a ship ca
1 CONC rrying Eleanor de Montfort, Simon de Montfort's only daughter, from France (where her family had lived in exile) to Wales, where she expected to marry Llywelyn. The parties' families had arranged the marriage previously, when an a
1 CONC lliance with Simon de Montfort still counted politically. However, Llywelyn wanted the marriage largely to antagonise his long-standing enemy, Edward. With the hijacking of the ship, Edward gained possession of Eleanor and impriso
1 CONC ned her at Windsor. After Llywelyn repeatedly refused to pay homage to Edward in 1274-5, Edward raised an army and launched his first campaign against the Welsh prince in 1276-77. After this campaign Llywelyn was forced to pay hom
1 CONC age to Edward and was stripped of all but a rump of territory in Gwynedd. But Edward allowed Llywelyn to retain the title of Prince of Wales, and the marriage with Eleanor de Montfort went ahead.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Llywelyn's younger brother, Dafydd (who had briefly been an ally of the English) started another rebellion in 1282. Llywelyn died shortly afterwards in a skirmish. Subsequently, Edward destroyed the remnants of resistance
1 CONC , capturing, brutally torturing and executing Dafydd in the following year. To consolidate his conquest, he commenced the construction of a string of massive stone castles encircling the principality, of which Caernarfon Castle pr
1 CONC ovides a notable surviving example. Wales became incorporated into England under the Statute of Rhuddlan in 1284 and in 1301 Edward created his eldest son Edward Prince of Wales, since which time the eldest son of each English mon
1 CONC arch has borne the same title.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Scottish Wars
1 CONT Edward then turned his attentions to Scotland and on May 10, 1291 Scottish nobles recognised the authority of Edward I. He had planned to marry off his son to the child queen, Margaret of Scotland (Called 'The Maid of Norway') but
1 CONC  when Margaret died the Scottish nobles agreed to have Edward select her successor from the various claimants to the throne, and he chose John Balliol over other candidates. Edward was anxious to impose his overlordship on Scotlan
1 CONC d and hoped that John Balliol would prove the most biddable candidate. Indeed, Edward summoned John Balliol to do homage to him in Westminster in 1293 and made it clear he expected John's military and financial support against Fra
1 CONC nce. But this was too much for Balliol, who concluded a pact with France and prepared an army to invade England.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward gathered his largest army yet and razed Berwick, massacring its inhabitants, proceeding to Dunbar and Edinburgh. The Stone of Destiny was removed from Scone Palace and taken to Westminster Abbey. Until 1996, it formed the s
1 CONC eat on King Edward's Chair, on which all English monarchs since 1308 have been crowned, with the exception of Mary I. In 1996, the stone was returned to Scotland, to return only during royal coronations. Balliol renounced the crow
1 CONC n and was imprisoned in the Tower of London for three years before withdrawing to his estates in France. All freeholders in Scotland were required to swear an oath of homage to Edward, and he ruled Scotland like a province through
1 CONC  English Viceroys.
1 CONT
1 CONT Opposition sprang up (see Wars of Scottish Independence), and Edward executed the focus of discontent, William Wallace, on August 23, 1305, having earlier defeated him at the Battle of Falkirk (1298). His plan to unite the two cou
1 CONC ntries never came to fruition during his lifetime, however, and he died in 1307 at Burgh-by-Sands, Cumberland on the Scottish border, while on his way to wage another campaign against the Scots under the leadership of Robert the B
1 CONC ruce. Against his wishes, Edward was buried in Westminster Abbey. His son, King Edward II of England, succeeded him.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Government and law under Edward I
1 CONT Unlike his father, Henry III of England, Edward I took great interest in the workings of his government and undertook a number of reforms to safeguard the preservation of royal rights and improve the administration of the law. He 
1 CONC spearheaded a wave of nationalism and solidified central authority over the country, a trend also used by contemporary monarchs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Late in his reign, Edward I issued the first trailbaston commissions (1305-7).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Edward and the Jews
1 CONT To help finance his war to conquer Wales, Edward I taxed the Jewish moneylenders. However, the cost of Edward's ambitions soon drained the money-lenders dry. When the Jews could no longer pay, the state accused them of disloyalty.
1 CONC  Already restricted to a limited number of occupations, Edward furthermore abolished their right to lend money at interest with the Statute of Jewry, [1] and eventually restricted their extra-curricular movements and activities. E
1 CONC dward decreed that all Jews wear a yellow patch in the shape of a star attached to their outer clothing to identify them in public (compare Star of David, Yellow badge).
1 CONT
1 CONT In the course of King Edward's persecution of the Jews, he arrested all the heads of Jewish households. The authorities took over 300 of them to the Tower of London and executed them, while killing others in their homes. Finally, 
1 CONC in 1290, the King banished all Jews from the country.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Interesting Facts about Edward I
1 CONT Edward I was known to be fond of falconry and horse riding. The names of his horses have survived: Lyard, his war horse; Ferrault his hunting animal; and his favorite, Bayard. Edward is said to have led the assault on Berwick pers
1 CONC onally, using Bayard to leap over the earthen defenses of the city. 
1 CONT Edward was largely responsible the Tower of London in the form we see today, including notably, the concentric defenses, elaborate entranceways, and the Traitor's Gate. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Issue
1 CONT Children of Edward and Eleanor:
1 CONT
1 CONT Daughter, stillborn in May 1255 in Bordeaux, France. 
1 CONT Katherine, living June 17 1264, died September 5 1264 and buried at Westminster Abbey. 
1 CONT Eleanor, born 18 June 1264 and died 12 October 1297. She married (1) Alfonso III of Aragon, (2) Count Henry III of Bar. 
1 CONT Joan, born January 1265, buried at Westminster Abbey before September 7 1265. 
1 CONT John, born July 13 1266, died August 3 1271 at Wallingford, in the custody of his granduncle, Richard, Earl of Cornwall. Buried at Westminster Abbey. 
1 CONT Henry, born before May 6 1268, died October 16 1274. 
1 CONT Daughter, born May 1271 in Palestine and died before September 1271. 
1 CONT Joan of Acre born May 1271 and died April 7 1307. She married (1) Gilbert de Clare, 7th Earl of Hertford, (2) Ralph Morthermer, 1st Baron Monthermer. 
1 CONT Alphonso, Earl of Chester, born 24 November 1273, died 19 August 1284, buried in Westminster Abbey. 
1 CONT Margaret, born March 15 1275 and died after 1333. She married John II, Duke of Brabant. 
1 CONT Berengaria, born 1 May 1276 and died before June 27 1278, buried in Westminster Abbey. 
1 CONT Daughter, died shortly after birth, January 1278. 
1 CONT Mary, born 11 March 1279 and died 29 May 1332, a nun in Amesbury, Wiltshire (England). 
1 CONT Elizabeth of Rhuddlan, born August 1281 at Rhuddlan, died 5 May 1316. She married (1) John I, Count of Holland, (2) Humphrey de Bohun, 4th Earl of Hereford & 3rd Earl of Essex. 
1 CONT Edward of Caernavon, later Edward II, King of England, born 25 April 1284 at Caernarvon, died 21 September 1327. He married Isabella of France. 
1 CONT Children of Edward and Marguerite:
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas of Brotherton, 1st Earl of Norfolk (1300–1338), married firstly, Alice Hayles and had issue. He married secondly, Mary Brewes and had issu
1 CONT Edmund of Woodstock, 1st Earl of Kent, married Margaret Wake, Baroness Wake of Liddell and had issue. 
1 CONT Eleanor (1306–1311), died young. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
