0 @N6000000001744908682@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled 1272-1307. Edward was already king in all but name when his father died in 1272. He had defeated de Montfort on his father's behalf and displayed all the strength and decisiveness his father lacked. During Edward's reign, Pa
1 CONC rliament met regularly for the first time and became the accepted way of conducting public business.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward was a great reformer. He revised the legal system, and was also a good soldier. He never was able to unify all of the island under one rule, but he did make his son Prince of Wales, and he fought the Scots so hard he earned
1 CONC  the nickname "Hammer of the Scots." Unfortunately, he also expelled the Jews (seen then as killers of Christ) from England; it was more than 300 years before they were allowed to return.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Nicholas Best, "The Kings and Queens of England" (Boston: Bullfinch, 1995).
1 CONT
1 CONT Gen. source: Family tree of Edward Phipps, 39500 Stevenson Place #204, Fremont, CA 95439, 510-794-6850; email <ariod@ix.netcom.com>Ruled 1272-1307. Edward was already king in all but name when his father died in 1272. He had defea
1 CONC ted de Montfort on his father's behalf and displayed all the strength and decisiveness his father lacked. During Edward's reign, Parliament met regularly for the first time and became the accepted way of conducting public business
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward was a great reformer. He revised the legal system, and was also a good soldier. He never was able to unify all of the island under one rule, but he did make his son Prince of Wales, and he fought the Scots so hard he earned
1 CONC  the nickname "Hammer of the Scots." Unfortunately, he also expelled the Jews (seen then as killers of Christ) from England; it was more than 300 years before they were allowed to return.
1 CONT
