0 @N6000000002134158929@ NOTE By his marriage (1254) to Eleanor of Castile Edward gained new claims in France and strengthened the English rights to Gascony. He received from his father the huge appanage of all outlying English dependencies, including Wales, I
1 CONC reland, and the lands in France. After a brief alliance with Simon de Montfort, earl of Leicester, Edward supported his father in the Barons' War (1263?67) and, by revitalizing the royal party and its forces, was responsible for t
1 CONC he crown's triumph. From this time on the young heir was the real ruler of the realm. He joined (1270) the Ninth Crusade and was on his return journey when he learned of his father's death. He did not reach England until 1274, whe
1 CONC n he was crowned.
1 CONT ward's vigorous reign was characterized by constant warfare. Trouble with Llywelyn ap Gruffydd led to his successful conquest (1277?82) of Wales beyond the Welsh Marches, and in 1284 he extended the English administration to Wales
1 CONC . In France from 1286 to 1289 he improved the administration of Gascony.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death in 1290 of Margaret Maid of Norway, Edward asserted his claim to overlordship of Scotland, but John de Baliol (1249?1315), his choice for the throne, soon entered an alliance with Philip IV of France, with whom Edw
1 CONC ard was already on bad terms. Edward's long struggle to conquer Scotland began in 1296. His first campaign was successful; he deposed Baliol and humiliated Scotland by removing the Coronation Stone from Scone to Westminster. But w
1 CONC hile he was heading an expedition against France in 1297 the Scots found a new leader in Sir William Wallace, who defeated the English at Stirling Bridge.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward immediately concluded a truce with Philip IV, and the English claims to Gascony were finally settled favorably in the treaty of 1303. In the meantime Edward invaded Scotland again and won a brilliant but inconclusive victor
1 CONC y at Falkirk (1298). Campaigns in the following years led to Wallace's defeat (1305) and execution, but a new leader, Robert I, arose as king of a still defiant Scotland. Edward commenced an expedition against him in 1307 but died
1 CONC  before reaching the border.
1 CONT
1 CONT Legal and Constitutional Developments
1 CONT Even more important than Edward's military exploits were the legal and constitutional developments of his reign; Edward has been called the English Justinian. He asserted the judicial supremacy of the crown by his quo warranto pro
1 CONC ceedings (inquiries to determine ?by what warrant? private jurisdictions were held), which culminated in the statutes of Gloucester (1278) and of Quo Warranto (1290). By his law of 1285, Circumspecte agatis, he forced church court
1 CONC s to confine themselves to ecclesiastical cases. His three statutes of Westminster (1275, 1285, 1290; see Westminster, Statutes of) formulated the advances of a century of common law and supplemented them.
1 CONT
1 CONT By his Statute of Mortmain (1279), Edward prohibited grants of land to the church without the king's permission. In turn the English clergy, backed by Pope Boniface VIII's bull Clericis laicos (1296), refused in 1297 to contribute
1 CONC  to Edward's campaign against the French until the king boldly denied protection to them and their goods and even threatened to confiscate all church property. This action was mainly prompted by his need for funds, as was his expu
1 CONC lsion (1290) of the Jews from England (which enabled him to seize their property). His expensive wars also necessitated the frequent summoning of Parliament to grant taxes. The so-called Model Parliament of 1295 included represent
1 CONC atives of the shires, boroughs, and lesser clergy, but the composition of Edward's parliaments varied.
1 CONT
1 CONT The increasing resistance of the country to heavy taxation and the refusal of many barons to fight in France in 1297 forced Edward to issue a confirmation of the charters of liberties, including the Magna Carta and those signed by
1 CONC  Henry III. The king also promised that he would collect the nonfeudal forms of taxation only with the consent of Parliament. He did not keep this promise, however, and the last years of his reign were marked by increasing baronia
1 CONC l opposition to the crown. This opposition and the war with Scotland proved to be a disastrous legacy for his son and successor, Edward II.
1 CONT (Longshanks), king of England, eldest son of Henry III. and his queen, Eleanor of Provence, was born in 1239. At ten years of age he was named governor of Gascony, and married in 1254 the Princess Eleanor of Castile. He took a pro
1 CONC minent part in state affairs during the latter part of his father's reign, and showed that ability, quick energy, and decision of character which distinguished him throughout his reign. In the barons' war, which began in 1261, he 
1 CONC had generally the conduct of the royal forces; was defeated and taken prisoner by De Montfort at Lewes, in 1264; escaped the next year, and defeated De Montfort at Evesham, thus securing the liberty of his father, and ended the wa
1 CONC r by the reduction of the Isle of Ely in 1267. He soon after took the cross, and set out to join St. Louis in the crusade, but did not arrive in the Holy Land till 1271. After various successes and a narrow escape from assassinati
1 CONC on - -his wife, it is said, sucking the poison from his arm --he set out on his return, arriving in England in August, 1274. He had been proclaimed king on the death of his father nearly two years previously, and was crowned, with
1 CONC  his queen, soon after his arrival.
1 CONT
1 CONT War filled up the greater part of his reign. The principal events are the conquest of Wales and the wars with Scotland. Llewellyn, prince of Wales, refusing to attend the English parliament and do homage, was defeated by Edward in
1 CONC  1277 ; and having again revolted, was again defeated, and at last slain in 1282. Edward built many castles in Wales, and settled the government by the statute of Rhuddlan. He treated the Jews with great cruelty and injustice, hun
1 CONC g hundreds of them on a charge of clipping the coin, and in 1290 banished them. In 1291 the numerous competitors for the crown of Scotland submitted their claims to Edward's decision, which was in favour of John de Baliol. Baliol 
1 CONC did homage to Edward, and was made to feel his dependence too keenly; so that war soon broke out between the two kingdoms. Then came the terrible devastation of Scotland, temporary submission, insurrection of Wallace, his victory 
1 CONC of Stirling, his defeat at Falkirk, numerous invasions and truces, capture and execution of the great patriot leader, fresh revolt, and coronation of Robert Bruce in 1306, and a final expedition against the Scots in the following 
1 CONC year, which was cut short by the death of Edward at Burgh-on-the- Sands, near Carlisle, 7th July, 1307. Very great and important legislative changes took place in this reign. Edward left by his first wife, four sons and nine daugh
1 CONC ters; and by his second, Margaret of France, whom he married in 1299, two sons and one daughter. Margaret survived him.
0 @N6000000002176622193@ NOTE Reigned 1272-1307
1 CONT In the Barons war 1264-67 he defeated the Barons at Evesham (1265) as
1 CONT King
1 CONT he is noted for encouraging Parliamentary institutions at the expense of
1 CONT feudalism and for subduing Wales on which he imposed the English system
1 CONT of
1 CONT administration. He later tried to assert his authority over Scotland
1 CONT and died
1 CONT while on his way to fight Robert Bruce.
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward I (born 1239, ruled 1272-1307) was one of England's greatest
1 CONT kings. He was a handsome man, with fair hair and ruddy cheeks, and so
1 CONT tall that he was nicknamed Longshanks. He delighted in tournaments and
1 CONT hunting, but he was also practical and hardworking. For seven years
1 CONT before he came to the throne, he was the real ruler for his weak
1 CONT father, Henry III (see Henry, Kings of England; Montfort). He was in
1 CONT the Holy Land involved in the crusades when his father died, but there
1 CONT was no question that he would take the throne.
1 CONT    Edward has been called the "English Justinian" because, like the
1 CONT Roman emperor Justinian, he organized the laws. His laws were not
1 CONT restatements of existing customs but statutes in the modern sense. Many
1 CONT of them, particularly the land laws, had a long-lasting influence. A
1 CONT statute of 1285 limited church courts to strictly church matters a
1 CONT change that Edward's great-grandfather, Henry II, had been unable to
1 CONT make because of the murder of Thomas a Becket (see Becket; Henry, Kings
1 CONT of England). Edward also stopped paying a feudal tribute to the pope.
1 CONT    Parliament grew in strength during Edward's reign because he
1 CONT continued the policy of Simon de Montfort in summoning to it
1 CONT representatives of the towns and lesser knights. His parliament of 1295
1 CONT is known as the Model Parliament (see Parliament). In 1297 he
1 CONT reaffirmed the Magna Carta in the famous confirmation of the charters.
1 CONT All of Edward's moves were not fair and admirable ones, however; he
1 CONT forced Jews out of England in 1290.
1 CONT    Soon after coming to the throne, Edward conquered Wales and gave to
1 CONT his infant son, Edward, the title prince of Wales (see Wales). Until
1 CONT 1289 the care of his French possessions, principally Aquitaine, in
1 CONT Southern France, absorbed much of his attention. For the rest of his
1 CONT life, his main concern was Scotland.
1 CONT    He conquered the country in 1296; but in 1297 all Scotland rose in
1 CONT revolt against him under the popular leader, William Wallace. Edward
1 CONT defeated Wallace at Falkirk the next year, but the Scots still
1 CONT resisted. Near the end of Edward's reign Scotland found a new leader in
1 CONT Robert Bruce. In 1307 King Edward, then 70 years old, led an army
1 CONT toward Scotland but died before he reached the border.
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward was born in 1239. He was married twice. Firstly at a very young
1 CONT age to Eleanor of Castile, who died in 1290, and whom bore him the
1 CONT children: Eleanor, Joan, Henry, Julian, Joan  of Acre, Alphonso,
1 CONT Isabel, Margaret, Berengaria, Mary, Alice, Elizabeth, Edward, Beatrice
1 CONT and  Blanche. His second wife, Margaret of France, daughter of the King
1 CONT of France, bore him Thomas,  Edmund and Eleanor.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was a special child to his father. He was born very late in
1 CONT Henry's life. He was named  after the canon, Edward the Confessor, and
1 CONT although his title says Edward I, there were three  Edwards previous to
1 CONT him.    It was Henry who arranged for the important marriage of Edward
1 CONT to Eleanor, the half-sister of  Alfonso X, King of Castile and Leon. It
1 CONT was an arranged marriage which bore many children and
1 CONT  was full of love.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was made Overlord of Ireland, before he became King, and was
1 CONT responsible for Gascony  and Wales. He was a typically spoilt
1 CONT adolescent,and liked to spend his time setting up jousting
1 CONT tournaments, in which many lives would be lost at a time. However
1 CONT though he had once recognised  the justice of Simon de Montfort's
1 CONT stance against his father, he rallied to help his father.    It was his
1 CONT role as a general that helped quash De Montfort, and after this he
1 CONT became an exemplary
1 CONT  figure in the ruling of England. He was his father's Regent and
1 CONT succeeded  nchallenged to the  throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT  He did not become King until the age of 35 and was devoted to personal
1 CONT and political integrity. He  was a devoted ruler of England and
1 CONT developed state relations all around the world. He also fought  many
1 CONT wars and used a great deal of the funds of England in these battles. He
1 CONT borrowed heavily  from the Jews in England. They were expert currency
1 CONT manipulators.    The King had tried to involve them in more productive
1 CONT occupations, but against all local feeling, and  the lack of profit to
1 CONT be attained, the Jews went back to their old vocations. In 1290 Edward
1 CONT expelled
1 CONT  the Jews from England. Being unable to borrow money, Edward had to
1 CONT impose high taxes on the  local populations, which was of course highly
1 CONT unpopular.
1 CONT
1 CONT  After having defeated and slain the last Welsh Prince of Wales,
1 CONT Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, Edward  offered his baby son to the Welsh people
1 CONT as the Prince of Wales, in a symbolic gesture. The Welsh  gave the
1 CONT English knowledge of the long bow, one of the most valued weapons
1 CONT before gunpowder,  and Edward encouraged its use.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pacification of the Welsh was not instant, and it took years for
1 CONT the land and power to be  delegated in face of harsh resentment.
1 CONT
1 CONT  War with Scotland was causing great problems. It concerned an attempt
1 CONT to reconcile the centre of  Scotland from Edinburgh to Scone, the
1 CONT natural but unrecognised seat of Scotland. When Alexander  III, King of
1 CONT Scotland died, the crown passed to his three year old grand daughter,
1 CONT Margaret.  When Margaret was aged six, Edward arranged for her to be
1 CONT betrothed to his heir Edward, which  would have led to a peaceful union
1 CONT of England and Scotland. Margaret died in a ship wreck on the  way to
1 CONT her coronation in Scotland, then the succession to the crown was
1 CONT disputed. Edward stepped  in to arbitrate and with a balanced
1 CONT Commission took eighteen months to choose John Balliol.    This
1 CONT decision led to a revolt, which Edward managed to overcome. He declared
1 CONT himself King of  Scotland and carried the Coronation Stone of Scotland
1 CONT from Scone Palace off to England. It was  this that led to the revolt
1 CONT by William Wallace who was eventually defeated by Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1306 Robert the Bruce was declared King of the Scots by the
1 CONT Scottish people and in the  ensuing war he was at first defeated by
1 CONT Edward. In a later campaign to crush Robert the Bruce  Edward died at
1 CONT Burgh by Sands, after being the monarch of England for 35 years.
0 @N6000000002176661743@ NOTE From the house of Plantagenet, Edward expelled the Jews from England in 1290,
1 CONT but the conquest of Scotland, however, became the ruling passion of his
1 CONT life. In 1305, he captured Sir William Wallace and executed him, leading to
1 CONT a Scottish revolt and the coronation of Robert de Bruce as King of Scotland.
