0 @N6000000002240749470@ NOTE Reigned 1272-1307
1 CONT In the Barons war 1264-67 he defeated the Barons at Evesham (1265) as
1 CONT King
1 CONT he is noted for encouraging Parliamentary institutions at the expense of
1 CONT feudalism and for subduing Wales on which he imposed the English system
1 CONT of
1 CONT administration. He later tried to assert his authority over Scotland
1 CONT and died
1 CONT while on his way to fight Robert Bruce.
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward I (born 1239, ruled 1272-1307) was one of England's greatest
1 CONT kings. He was a handsome man, with fair hair and ruddy cheeks, and so
1 CONT tall that he was nicknamed Longshanks. He delighted in tournaments and
1 CONT hunting, but he was also practical and hardworking. For seven years
1 CONT before he came to the throne, he was the real ruler for his weak
1 CONT father, Henry III (see Henry, Kings of England; Montfort). He was in
1 CONT the Holy Land involved in the crusades when his father died, but there
1 CONT was no question that he would take the throne.
1 CONT    Edward has been called the "English Justinian" because, like the
1 CONT Roman emperor Justinian, he organized the laws. His laws were not
1 CONT restatements of existing customs but statutes in the modern sense. Many
1 CONT of them, particularly the land laws, had a long-lasting influence. A
1 CONT statute of 1285 limited church courts to strictly church matters a
1 CONT change that Edward's great-grandfather, Henry II, had been unable to
1 CONT make because of the murder of Thomas a Becket (see Becket; Henry, Kings
1 CONT of England). Edward also stopped paying a feudal tribute to the pope.
1 CONT    Parliament grew in strength during Edward's reign because he
1 CONT continued the policy of Simon de Montfort in summoning to it
1 CONT representatives of the towns and lesser knights. His parliament of 1295
1 CONT is known as the Model Parliament (see Parliament). In 1297 he
1 CONT reaffirmed the Magna Carta in the famous confirmation of the charters.
1 CONT All of Edward's moves were not fair and admirable ones, however; he
1 CONT forced Jews out of England in 1290.
1 CONT    Soon after coming to the throne, Edward conquered Wales and gave to
1 CONT his infant son, Edward, the title prince of Wales (see Wales). Until
1 CONT 1289 the care of his French possessions, principally Aquitaine, in
1 CONT Southern France, absorbed much of his attention. For the rest of his
1 CONT life, his main concern was Scotland.
1 CONT    He conquered the country in 1296; but in 1297 all Scotland rose in
1 CONT revolt against him under the popular leader, William Wallace. Edward
1 CONT defeated Wallace at Falkirk the next year, but the Scots still
1 CONT resisted. Near the end of Edward's reign Scotland found a new leader in
1 CONT Robert Bruce. In 1307 King Edward, then 70 years old, led an army
1 CONT toward Scotland but died before he reached the border.
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT Edward was born in 1239. He was married twice. Firstly at a very young
1 CONT age to Eleanor of Castile, who died in 1290, and whom bore him the
1 CONT children: Eleanor, Joan, Henry, Julian, Joan  of Acre, Alphonso,
1 CONT Isabel, Margaret, Berengaria, Mary, Alice, Elizabeth, Edward, Beatrice
1 CONT and  Blanche. His second wife, Margaret of France, daughter of the King
1 CONT of France, bore him Thomas,  Edmund and Eleanor.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was a special child to his father. He was born very late in
1 CONT Henry's life. He was named  after the canon, Edward the Confessor, and
1 CONT although his title says Edward I, there were three  Edwards previous to
1 CONT him.    It was Henry who arranged for the important marriage of Edward
1 CONT to Eleanor, the half-sister of  Alfonso X, King of Castile and Leon. It
1 CONT was an arranged marriage which bore many children and
1 CONT  was full of love.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Edward was made Overlord of Ireland, before he became King, and was
1 CONT responsible for Gascony  and Wales. He was a typically spoilt
1 CONT adolescent,and liked to spend his time setting up jousting
1 CONT tournaments, in which many lives would be lost at a time. However
1 CONT though he had once recognised  the justice of Simon de Montfort's
1 CONT stance against his father, he rallied to help his father.    It was his
1 CONT role as a general that helped quash De Montfort, and after this he
1 CONT became an exemplary
1 CONT  figure in the ruling of England. He was his father's Regent and
1 CONT succeeded  nchallenged to the  throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT  He did not become King until the age of 35 and was devoted to personal
1 CONT and political integrity. He  was a devoted ruler of England and
1 CONT developed state relations all around the world. He also fought  many
1 CONT wars and used a great deal of the funds of England in these battles. He
1 CONT borrowed heavily  from the Jews in England. They were expert currency
1 CONT manipulators.    The King had tried to involve them in more productive
1 CONT occupations, but against all local feeling, and  the lack of profit to
1 CONT be attained, the Jews went back to their old vocations. In 1290 Edward
1 CONT expelled
1 CONT  the Jews from England. Being unable to borrow money, Edward had to
1 CONT impose high taxes on the  local populations, which was of course highly
1 CONT unpopular.
1 CONT
1 CONT  After having defeated and slain the last Welsh Prince of Wales,
1 CONT Llywelyn ap Gruffydd, Edward  offered his baby son to the Welsh people
1 CONT as the Prince of Wales, in a symbolic gesture. The Welsh  gave the
1 CONT English knowledge of the long bow, one of the most valued weapons
1 CONT before gunpowder,  and Edward encouraged its use.
1 CONT
1 CONT The pacification of the Welsh was not instant, and it took years for
1 CONT the land and power to be  delegated in face of harsh resentment.
1 CONT
1 CONT  War with Scotland was causing great problems. It concerned an attempt
1 CONT to reconcile the centre of  Scotland from Edinburgh to Scone, the
1 CONT natural but unrecognised seat of Scotland. When Alexander  III, King of
1 CONT Scotland died, the crown passed to his three year old grand daughter,
1 CONT Margaret.  When Margaret was aged six, Edward arranged for her to be
1 CONT betrothed to his heir Edward, which  would have led to a peaceful union
1 CONT of England and Scotland. Margaret died in a ship wreck on the  way to
1 CONT her coronation in Scotland, then the succession to the crown was
1 CONT disputed. Edward stepped  in to arbitrate and with a balanced
1 CONT Commission took eighteen months to choose John Balliol.    This
1 CONT decision led to a revolt, which Edward managed to overcome. He declared
1 CONT himself King of  Scotland and carried the Coronation Stone of Scotland
1 CONT from Scone Palace off to England. It was  this that led to the revolt
1 CONT by William Wallace who was eventually defeated by Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT  In 1306 Robert the Bruce was declared King of the Scots by the
1 CONT Scottish people and in the  ensuing war he was at first defeated by
1 CONT Edward. In a later campaign to crush Robert the Bruce  Edward died at
1 CONT Burgh by Sands, after being the monarch of England for 35 years.
