0 @N6000000001783235825@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT Edmund Crouchback,Prince of England was the 2nd. son of Henry III; he was created Earl of Lancaster in 1267 by his brother, Edward I
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://users.adelphia.net/~hwaller/Noble.htm>
0 @N6000000001783515693@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward I, popularly known as Longshanks, achieved historical fame as the monarch who conquered large parts of Wales and almost succeeded in doing the same to Scotland. However, his death led to his son Edward II taking the throne 
1 CONC and ultimately failing in his attempt to subjugate Scotland. Longshanks reigned from 1272 to 1307, ascending the throne of England on 20 November 1272 after the death of his father, King Henry III. His mother was queen consort Ele
1 CONC anor of Provence.
1 CONT
1 CONT As regnal post-nominal numbers were a Norman (as opposed to Anglo-Saxon) custom, Edward Longshanks is known as Edward I, even though he is the fourth King Edward, following Edward the Elder, Edward the Martyr, and Edward the Confe
1 CONC ssor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Crusades
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1266, Cardinal Ottobono, the Papal Legate, arrived in England and appealed to Edward and his brother Edmund to participate in the Eighth Crusade alongside Louis IX of France. In order to fund the crusade, Edward had to borrow h
1 CONC eavily from the French king, and persuade a reluctant parliament to vote him a subsidy (no such tax had been raised in England since 1237).
1 CONT
1 CONT The number of knights and retainers that accompanied Edward on the crusade was quite small. He drew up contracts with 225 knights, and one chronicler estimated that his total force numbered 1000 men. Many of the members of Edward'
1 CONC s expedition were close friends and family including his wife Eleanor of Castile, his brother Edmund, and his first cousin Henry of Almain.
1 CONT
1 CONT The original goal of the crusade was to relieve the beleaguered Christian stronghold of Acre, but Louis had been diverted to Tunis. By the time Edward arrived at Tunis, Louis had died of disease. The majority of the French forces 
1 CONC at Tunis thus returned home, but a small number joined Edward who continued to Acre to participate in the Ninth Crusade. After a short stop in Cyprus, Edward arrived in Acre, reportedly with thirteen ships. In 1271, Hugh III of Cy
1 CONC prus arrived with a contingent of knights.
1 CONT
1 CONT Operations during the Crusade of Edward I
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after the arrival of Hugh, Edward raided the town of Qaqun. Because the Mamluks were also pressed by Mongols raid into Syria, there followed a ten year truce, despite Edward's objections.
1 CONT The truce, and an almost fatal wound inflicted by a Muslim assassin, soon forced Edward to return to England. On his return voyage he learned of his father's death. Overall, Edward's crusade was rather insignificant and only gave 
1 CONC the city of Acre a reprieve of ten years. However, Edward's reputation was greatly enhanced by his participation and he was hailed by one contemporary English songwriter as a new Richard the Lionheart.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward was also largely responsible for the Tower of London in the form we see today, including notably the concentric defences, elaborate entranceways, and the Traitor's Gate. The engineer who redesigned the Tower's moat, Brother
1 CONC  John of the Order of St Thomas of Acre, had clearly been recruited in the East.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's first marriage (age 15) was arranged in 1254 by his father and Alfonso X of Castile. Alfonso had insisted that Edward receive grants of land worth 15,000 marks a year and also asked to knight him; Henry had already planne
1 CONC d a knighthood ceremony for Edward but conceded. Edward crossed the Channel in June, and was knighted by Alfonso and married to Eleanor of Castile (age 13) on 1 November 1254 in the monastery of Las Huelgas.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor and Edward would go on to have at least fifteen (possibly sixteen) children. They were:
1 CONT A nameless daughter, b. and d. 1255 and buried in Bordeaux.
1 CONT Katherine, b&d. 1264
1 CONT Joan, b. and d. 1265. She was buried at Westminster Abbey before September 7, 1265.
1 CONT John, born at either Windsor or Kenilworth Castle June or July 10, 1266, died August 1 or 3 1271 at Wallingford, in the custody of his great uncle, Richard, Earl of Cornwall. Buried at Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT Henry, born on July 13 1268 at Windsor Castle, died October 14, 1274 either at Merton, Surrey, or at Guildford Castle.
1 CONT Eleanor, born 1269, died 12 October 1298. She was long betrothed to Alfonso III of Aragon, who died in 1291 before the marriage could take place, and on 20 September 1293 she married Count Henry III of Bar.
1 CONT A nameless daughter, born at Acre, Palestine, in 1271, and died there on 28 May or 5 September 1271
1 CONT Joan of Acre. Born at Acre in Spring 1272 and died at her manor of Clare, Suffolk on April 23, 1307 and was buried in the priory church of the Austin friars, Clare, Suffolk. She married (1) Gilbert de Clare, 7th Earl of Hertford, 
1 CONC (2) Ralph de Monthermer, 1st Baron Monthermer.
1 CONT Alphonso, born either at Bayonne, at Bordeaux24 November 1273, died 14 or 19 August 1284, at Windsor Castle, buried in Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT Margaret, born September 11, 1275 at Windsor Castle and died in 1318, being buried in the Collegiate Church of St. Gudule, Brussels. She married John II of Brabant.
1 CONT Berengaria (also known as Berenice), born 1 May 1276 at Kempton Palace, Surrey and died on June 27, 1278, buried in Westminster Abbey.
1 CONT Mary, born 11 March or 22 April 1278 at Windsor Castle and died 8 July 1332, a nun in Amesbury, Wiltshire, England.
1 CONT Elizabeth of Rhuddlan, born August 1282 at Rhuddlan Castle, Flintshire, Wales, died c.5 May 1316 at Quendon, Essex, in childbirth, and was buried in Walden Abbey, Essex. She married (1) John I, Count of Holland, (2) Humphrey de Bo
1 CONC hun, 4th Earl of Hereford & 3rd Earl of Essex.
1 CONT Edward II of England, also known as Edward of Caernarvon, born 25 April 1284 at Caernarvon Castle, Wales, murdered 21 September 1327 at Berkeley Castle, Gloucestershire, buried in Gloucester Cathedral. He married Isabella of Franc
1 CONC e.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor's death in 1290 affected Edward deeply. He displayed his grief by erecting the Eleanor crosses, one at each place where her funeral cortège stopped for the night.
1 CONT
1 CONT His second marriage, (age 60) at Canterbury on 10 September 1299, to Marguerite of France, (age 17) (known as the "Pearl of France" by her husband's English subjects), the daughter of King Philip III of France (Phillip the Bold) a
1 CONC nd Maria of Brabant, produced three children.  They were:
1 CONT Thomas of Brotherton, later earl of Norfolk, born 1 June 1300 at Brotherton, Yorkshire, died between the 4 August and 20 September 1338, was buried in the abbey of Bury St Edmunds, married (1) Alice Hayles, with issue; (2) Mary Br
1 CONC ewes, no issue.[9]
1 CONT Edmund of Woodstock, 5 August 1301 at Woodstock Palace, Oxon, married Margaret Wake, 3rd Baroness Wake of Liddell with issue. Executed by Isabella of France and Roger Mortimer on the 19 March 1330 following the overthrow of Edward
1 CONC  II.
1 CONT Eleanor, born on 4 May 1306, she was Edward and Margeurite's youngest child. Named after Eleanor of Castile, she died in 1311.
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward's plan to conquer Scotland ultimately failed. In 1307 he died at Burgh-by-Sands, Cumberland on the Scottish border, while on his way to wage another campaign against the Scots under the leadership of Robert the Bruce. Accor
1 CONC ding to a later chronicler tradition, Edward asked to have his bones carried on future military campaigns in Scotland. More credible and contemporary writers reported that the king's last request was to have his heart taken to the
1 CONC  Holy Land. All that is certain is that Edward was buried in Westminster Abbey in a plain black marble tomb, which in later years was painted with the words Edwardus Primus Scottorum malleus hic est, pactum serva, (Here is Edward 
1 CONC I, Hammer of the Scots. Keep Troth). Although in their present form these words were added in the sixteenth century, they may well date from soon after his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 2 January 1774, the Society of Antiquaries opened the coffin and discovered that his body had been perfectly preserved for 467 years. His body was measured to be 6 feet 2 inches (188 cm)
1 CONT <http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_I_of_England>
0 @N6000000002322298533@ NOTE Upon the death of Conrad IV in 1254, Pope Urban IV invested Edmund, son of
1 CONT King Henry III of England, with the Sicilian kingdom in April of 1255. A papal
1 CONT army entered the kingdom, but Manfred resisted successfully and was
1 CONT crowned king of Sicily at Palermo on August 10, 1258. Edmund, age 10 when he
1 CONT was appointed by the pope, was never more than an absentee  titular king.
1 CONT Edmund was, however, a crusader and made the earl of Leicester, and Earl of
1 CONT Lancaster, as well as lieutenant of Gascony.
0 @N6000000002187747882@ NOTE Edmund Crouchback, 1st Earl of Lancaster
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund Crouchback, 1st Earl of Lancaster (January 16, 1245 – June 5, 1296) was the second surviving son of Eleanor of Provence and King Henry III of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund was born in London. He was a younger brother of Edward I of England and Eleanor of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1253 he was invested by Pope Innocent IV in the Kingdom of Sicily and Apulia. At about this time he was also made Earl of Chester. These were of little value as Conrad IV of Germany, the real King of Sicily, was still living an
1 CONC d the Earldom of Chester was transferred to his elder brother Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund soon obtained, however, important possessions and dignities, for soon after the forfeiture of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester in 1265, Edmund received the Earldom of Leicester and of Lancaster and also the honour o
1 CONC f the Stewardship of England and the lands of Nicolas de Segrave.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1271 he accompanied his elder brother Edward on the Ninth Crusade to Palestine. It was because of this he received the nickname Crouchback (or cross back) indicating that he was entitled to wear a cross on his back.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married twice, first to Aveline de Forz, Countess of Albemarle, in 1269, and then in Paris, France on February 3, 1276, to Blanche of Artois. That same year he became the Count of Champagne and Brie in France. With Blanche 
1 CONC he had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Plantagenet, Second Earl of Lancaster (b. 1278) 
1 CONT Henry Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Lancaster (b. 1281) 
1 CONT John Plantagenet, Lord of Beaufort, (b. BEF 1286) 
1 CONT Mary Plantagenet 
1 CONT He died on June 5, 1296 in Bayonne, and was interred on July 15, 1296 at Westminster Abbey, London, England.
0 @N6000000001744804671@ NOTE [s2.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl of Leicester and Lancaster, also titular King of Sicily. Second son of Henry II and Eleanor of Provence; 1st Earl of Lancaster (1267-96). Pope Alexander III conferred the title of King of Sicily upon him in 1255, but Henry II
1 CONC I was unable to provide the money and men Edmund needed to pursue the claim, and in 1263 he resigned the kingship. His brother Edward I created him Lord of Lancaster in 1267. In 1271 Edmund went on a crusade in Palestine; his nick
1 CONC name refers to his crusader's cross. He fought against hte Welsh 1277-82 and the French in Gascony in 1296. [Source: Elizabeth Hallam, The Plantagenet Encyclopedia (NY: Crescent Books, 1996) 66.]Earl of Leicester and Lancaster, al
1 CONC so titular King of Sicily. Second son of Henry II and Eleanor of Provence; 1st Earl of Lancaster (1267-96). Pope Alexander III conferred the title of King of Sicily upon him in 1255, but Henry III was unable to provide the money a
1 CONC nd men Edmund needed to pursue the claim, and in 1263 he resigned the kingship. His brother Edward I created him Lord of Lancaster in 1267. In 1271 Edmund went on a crusade in Palestine; his nickname refers to his crusader's cross
1 CONC . He fought against hte Welsh 1277-82 and the French in Gascony in 1296. [Source: Elizabeth Hallam, The Plantagenet Encyclopedia (NY: Crescent Books, 1996) 66.]
