0 @N6000000001783235814@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT Edmund Crouchback,Prince of England was the 2nd. son of Henry III; he was created Earl of Lancaster in 1267 by his brother, Edward I
1 CONT
1 CONT <http://users.adelphia.net/~hwaller/Noble.htm>
0 @N6000000003463808787@ NOTE Edmund Crouchback, 1st Earl of Lancaster (January 16, 1245 – June 5, 1296) was the second surviving son of Eleanor of Provence and King Henry III of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund was born in London, England. He was a younger brother of Edward I of England and Eleanor of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1253 he was invested by Pope Innocent IV in the Kingdom of Sicily and Apulia. At about this time he was also made Earl of Chester. These were of little value as Conrad IV of Germany, the real King of Sicily, was still living an
1 CONC d the Earldom of Chester was transferred to his elder brother Edward.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund soon obtained, however, important possessions and dignities, for soon after the forfeiture of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester in 1265, Edmund received the Earldom of Leicester and of Lancaster and also the honour o
1 CONC f the Stewardship of England and the lands of Nicolas de Segrave.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1271 he accompanied his elder brother Edward on the Ninth Crusade to Palestine. It was because of this he received the nickname Crouchback (or cross back) indicating that he was entitled to wear a cross on his back.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married twice, first to Aveline de Forz, Countess of Albemarle, in 1269, and then in Paris, France on February 3, 1276, to Blanche of Artois. That same year he became the Count of Champagne and Brie in France. With Blanche 
1 CONC he had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Plantagenet, Second Earl of Lancaster (b. 1278) 
1 CONT Henry Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Lancaster (b. 1281) 
1 CONT John Plantagenet, Lord of Beaufort, (b. BEF 1286) 
1 CONT Mary Plantagenet 
1 CONT He died on June 5, 1296 in Bayonne, and was interred on July 15, 1296 at Westminster Abbey, London, England.
