0 @N6000000003300516139@ NOTE Richard, Earl of Cornwall, was a son of King John of England. He fought against France in the army of his brother, Henry III. Catching the prevailing zeal, he went on a crusade to Palestine between 1240 and 1242. On his return, he
1 CONC  again assisted his brother in a war against France. In 1257, during the interregnum of the Holy Roman Empire, he was chosen to be the Holy Roman Emperor by a bare majority of German electors.
1 CONT Although he exercised all of the rights of a sovereign, he was unable to gain general recognition throughout Germany, because a tenacious minority supported Alfonso X, King of Castile and Leon. Richard's popularity in Germany was 
1 CONC never great, partly because he spent only brief periods of his reign there. However, because of his great wealth, he exercised considerable power throughout Europe.
1 CONT Richard was co-Regent of England with his sister-in-law Queen Eleanor (Au g 1253 - May 1254), and sole Regent (May - Dec, 1254).
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was the founder of Hailes Abbey, where he was buried.
0 @N6000000001220378655@ NOTE
1 CONT Additions, corrections and questions invited.
0 @N6000000001220386587@ NOTE [2753682.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT !Earl of Cornwall. [Magna Charta Sureties]
1 CONT
1 CONT NEWLIN LINE
1 CONT
1 CONT !Westminster, Feb 1225 - Henry invests his brother Richard with the title of Earl of Cornwall . [Chronicle of the Royal Family, p. 56]
1 CONT
1 CONT !London, 4 Jan 1257 -- With the death of King Frederick II of Germany, the ambitious Earl Ric hard of Cornwall has been elected king of Germany.  A deeply pious prince whose loyalty to th e king has never been doubted but often so
1 CONC rely tested, Richard is one of the most thoughtfu l of the day's noblemen.  Married 1. Isabella Marshal; m.2. in 1243 Sanchia of Provence (sist er to the queens of France and England).  The earl is a brilliant businessman, financi
1 CONC ng a re form of the coinage in 1247 in exchange for a half share in the profits. [Chronicle of the Ro yal Family, p. 59]
1 CONT
1 CONT King of the Romans. Son of John Lackland, King of England, and Isabel of Angouleme; m.1. Isab el Marshall; m.2. Sancha of Provence; m.3. Beatrix of Valkenburg; father of Richard de Cornwa ll by illegitimate liaison with Joan de Va
1 CONC utort/Valletort. [Royal Descents, p. 317]
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl of Cornwall, King of the Romans; 2nd son of King John and Isabella of Angouleme; had b y Joan, wid. of Sir Reginald de Vautort/Valetort, at least one illeg. child, Sir Richard de C ornwall. [Ancestral Roots, Line 258, p. 232]
1 CONT
1 CONT Hailes Abbey, near Winchcombe, in Cheltenham, is a ruined Cistercian Abbey founded in 1246 b y Richard, Earl of Cornwall, now in the care of English Heritage. The monastery became famou s in medieval times for its relic of Christ'
1 CONC s blood and is mentioned in Chaucer's "Pardoner' s Tale". ["The Soul of Stanway House" by Elizabeth Parker, BRITISH HERITAGE, February 1998, p . 35]
1 CONT
1 CONT Earl Richard of Cornwal built his castle at Tintagel sometime around 1230. There was no milit arily sound reason for siting his castle on the island. It guarded no trade route or port, an d if the site could be easily defended by 
1 CONC a handful of men, its garrison could as easily be b ottled up by an equally small number and starved into submission. Richard's purpose in buildi ng his castle therefore seems more political than strategic. By making Tintagel his 
1 CONC headquart ers, he may well have intended to equate himself with the legendary Duke of Cornwall, or wit h King Arthur himself. ["Tintagel" by Bruce Heydt, British Heritage, March 1998, p. 29]
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of King John and Isabel of Angouleme; father of:
1 CONT 1. Henry of Almain, d. 1270
1 CONT 2. Edmund of Cornwall, d. 1300
1 CONT [Edward I, p. 572]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1233, Earl Richard of Cornwall acquired Tintagel--by then long deserted and probably alrea dy a place of legends. Richard was the brother of King Henry III, and needed to establish him self among the Cornish, who were always su
1 CONC spicious of outsiders. He was also keen to play a p art in wider European politics and even hoped to be chosen as Holy Roman Emperor. The legen d of King Arthut was one he could turn to his own use.
1 CONT    Tintagel was not an easy site to build on: the Island and mainland courtyards both require d major engineering works to make them level. Nevertheless, Earl Richard acquire the manor o f Bossiney, which included the old legend-h
1 CONC aunted stronghold of Din Tagell, and built a castl e whose inner cuortyard clung like a limpet to the sloping side of the Island. It is as if h e wanted to stand exactly where King Arthur was now believed to have stood. The Earl m
1 CONC ay hav e hoped to benefit from the tales of chivalry and heroism that poets and chroniclers had wove n about the site, presenting himself as a successor to King Arthur, and the heir to the loyal ties of the Cornish people. Nor wou
1 CONC ld the lesson be lost on the European kings and princes, w ho would have been familiar with the stories of King Arthur and whose support Earl Richard ne eded in his bid to be crowned as Holy Roman Emperor. The wildly impractical T
1 CONC intagel Castle w as therefore probably given at least the appearance of military strength.
1 CONT    Even so, the castle does not seem to have incorporated the most up-to-date thinking of th e time. Indeed, the main entrance was quite old-fashioned compared with Earl Richard's castl e at nearby Launceston, where the entrance h
1 CONC ad been updated in the latest fashion. Perhaps th e antiquated look was deliverate--Earl Richard may have wanted a castle that looked as thoug h the famous King Arthur might really have lived there, a ready-aged fantasy castle wor
1 CONC thy o f its legendary setting.
1 CONT    The ruined Dark Age defences were replaced with a strong stone wall, confronting friends a nd enemies alike with a barrier blocking off all sight of what lay beyond. Today the stonewor k is ragged and time-worn, but when first 
1 CONC built it would have been rendered and possibly whit ened with lime. It would have stood out clearly in the landscape, marking the castle of a pow erful nobleman.
1 CONT    By 1233, when Earl Richard built his castle, the neck of land joining the mainland to th e Island must already have been eroded by the sea. There must always have been a low narrow s addle between the two separate parts of the 
1 CONC castle.
1 CONT    When Earl Richard first came here he would have found a grassy hollow with shallow terrace s and possibly the tumbled remains of earlier buildings. To make space to build his Great Hal l, he built a strong retaining wall around
1 CONC  the lower edge of the hollow and tipped in three m etres of soil and stone to level the ground, burying any Dark Age remains.
1 CONT    On this new platform he built his Great Hall--the main reception room of the castle, wher e all formal business would be conducted and where the Earl could impress his guests with lav ish hospitality.
1 CONT    In spite of he early enthusiasm for Tintagel, Earl Richard cannot have come here very ofte n, and, after his death in 1272, his buildings fell into disrepair. [Tintagel Castle, p. 1-19 ]
1 CONT
1 CONT The first castle at Launceston was built by Count Robert of Mortain in 1068 to guard the mai n road into Cornwall. Earl Richard rebuilt the castle as a symbol of his power, adding a tal l keep to the top of the great mound which d
1 CONC ominates the town. [Tintagel Castle, p. 42]
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1227 Henry III gave Berkhamsted Castle, Hertfordshire to his brother Richard, Duke of Corn wall. He may have built a three-storeyed tower along the western part of the curtain: some fo undations of a square tower have been iden
1 CONC tified. [Castles of Britain and Ireland, p. 126][beaufort.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Weir says died Berkhampstead Castle & buried Hayles Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT REF:  "Falls the Shadow" Sharon Kay Penman: During Simon de Montfort's bar ons war, March 31, 1264 Londoners riot (London was very pro de Montfort ), burn townhouses of the hated William de Lusignan, also prominent Royali st Lord 
1 CONC Philip Basset, also Richard of Cornwall's Westminster manor, & h is beloved Isleworth manor 7 miles away.  Richard overnight goes from tryi ng to mediate a solution to, like Edward, ardently desiring a battlefie ld confrontation.
1 CONT Richard was not popular with his brother Henry III's subjects as he was in telligent, pragmatic, had business acumen, & a lack of rancor; these quali ties were disdained in favor of reckless courage, battlefield prowess, & o pen-h
1 CONC anded generosity, which was expected of Lords. Richard's courage w as never suspect; but he was not an innovative nor brilliant military comm ander. At Battle of Lewes on 14 May 1264, Richard commanded the left fla nk of the Royal
1 CONC ist forces.  After Prince Edward left the battle to purs ue with unwholesome savagery the rebel Londoner flank of Montfort's force s, Montfort, keeping a reserve force (a tactic unheard of) came in & attac ked Henry & obliterated 
1 CONC his force; Henry fleed to the priory.  Gilbe rt de Clare's center column forces met Richard's & took the day.  Richa rd with his son Edmund fled into a windmill.  When de Clare's forces captu red the, they jeered "come out king of
1 CONC  the millers". John Giffard's squi re was the one who officially accepted Richard's royal surrender, addi ng to Richard's discomposure (that he had surrendered to a squire rather t han a knight).  Upon his presentation to Simon de
1 CONC  Montfort, Simon made t he lad, John Befs of Tewkesbury, a knight.[harry.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Elected King of the Romans[5463.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Some sources (ie Weir) list his death at Berkhampstead Castle in
1 CONT Hertfordshir e, England and buried in Hayles Abbey.
1 CONT (http://www.genuki.org.uk/big/royalty/e arla-m.html): "Richard, Earl of
1 CONT Cornwall, and titular Emperor of the Romas, w as second son of John, King
1 CONT of England, and his queen, Isabella, and was born at Winchester in 1209.
1 CONT After serving with distinction in France, he went, a bout 1240, to
1 CONT Palestine, where his presence, as nephew of the formidable Rich ard Coeur
1 CONT de Lion gave courage to the Christians and filled the Saracens with 
1 CONT terror.  On his return he had an interview with the Emperor Frederick II.
1 CONT in Sicily, and by his desire attempted to mediate between him and the
1 CONT Pope, G regory IX.; but unsuccessfully.  He arrived in England in 1242;
1 CONT again served in France; mediated more than once between Henry III. and
1 CONT the barons, against whom he ultimately fought; was charged by the king,
1 CONT in 1255, to torture and extort money from the Jews, by which means he got
1 CONT much of his wealth; and in 1256 he accepted the title of King of the
1 CONT Romans, offered him after the death of William, Count of Holland.  He had
1 CONT a rival, however, in Alfonso of Castil e, who was chosen by some of the
1 CONT electors.  Richard was crowned at Aix-la-Cha pelle (now Aachen), with his
1 CONT wife, Sanchia, in May, 1257, and won golden opin ions by his prodigal
1 CONT gifts and expenditure.  He returned to England after two years, but
1 CONT several times revisited Germany and exercised authority in some r espects
1 CONT as Emperor.  He took part on the king's side at the battle of Lewes, and
1 CONT was captured, and kept prisoner more than a year.  The assassination of
1 CONT his eldest son, Henry, a prince of great promise, by the sons of Simon de
1 CONT Mo ntfort, at Viterbo, in 1271, deeply affected him, and he died at
1 CONT Berkhampstea d in April, 1272.  His body was interred in the abbey of
1 CONT Hayles, which he had founded."
