0 @N6000000005598851769@ NOTE Blanche has been styled as one of the most 'beautiful ladies in the wo rld'.  She however was not a good wife for Charles and is said to hav e gone mad.  In early 1314, Philip IV had Blanche and her sister-in-la ws arrested for in
1 CONC trigues against the crown and an affair with the bro thers Philippe and Gautier D'Aunay.  She was imprisoned at Chateau de Gaillard and while in prison gave birth to a child of one of her jaile rs.  She continued to defend herself
1 CONC  agains the charges of Philip IV for seven years.  The twelve peers of France however advised Charles t o seek another wife.  After her release she was repudiated by Charles IV in 1322, then subsequently died in a convent.
0 @N6000000002187845543@ NOTE Robert, Count of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Robert, Count of Burgundy (1300-1315) was the son of Otto IV, Count of Burgundy and Mahaut, Countess of Artois. He was Count of Burgundy between 1302 and 1315. He died in 1315, and was succeeded by his sister, Jeanne.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Otto IV Count of Burgundy
1 CONT 1302–1315 Succeeded by
1 CONT Jeanne II
0 @N6000000002187746465@ NOTE Florent of Hainaut
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Florent of Hainaut (also Floris or Florence; Hainaut is also spelled "Hainault") (c.1255 – 23 January 1297) was Prince of Achaea from 1289 to his death, in right of his wife, Isabella of Villehardouin. He was the son of John I of 
1 CONC Avesnes and Adelaide of Holland. From his father he received the stadholdership (government) of Zeeland.
1 CONT
1 CONT After he left Zeeland, he took up service with Charles II of Naples, who made him constable of the Kingdom of Naples. After his marriage with Isabella (16 September 1289), he had one daughter: Matilda. She would succeed him and he
1 CONC r mother as princess.
1 CONT
1 CONT Florent settled with his wife in Morea. He negotiated the Treaty of Glarentsa with the Byzantine Empire in 1290. The situation for the Franks in Greece was hopeless by this time, however. The fall of the Angevins in Sicily meant t
1 CONC hat they were preoccupied with recouping territory there and few Western governments would send troops to defend Morea. Florent thus made peace and maintained it until 1293, when the Greeks retook Kalamata. Florent did not despair
1 CONC  and did not reopen the war which had been ongoing until his succession: he instead sent an embassy in protest to Michael VIII Palaeologus. The emperor returned Kalamata. In 1296, the Greeks retook the castle of Saint George in Ar
1 CONC cadia. Florent besieged the castle, but died before it could be taken.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Grousset, René. L'Empire du Levant: Histoire de la Question d'Orient.
1 CONT
