0 @N6000000002187700635@ NOTE Elisabeth of Tirol
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Elisabeth de Gorizia de Tirol de Carantania (c. 1262 – 28 October 1312) was Queen consort of the Holy Roman Empire and Duchess of Austria, etc.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT She was the eldest daughter of Meinhard II, Duke of Carinthia, count of Gorizia and Tyrol.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her mother was his wife Elisabeth of Bavaria. She was a daughter of Otto II Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria and his wife Agnes, herself daughter of Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine and Agnes of Hohenstaufen. Her mother was also t
1 CONC he widow of Conrad IV of Germany. Therefore, the young Elisabeth was a half-sister of Conradin, King of Jerusalem etc and Duke of Swabia who was killed in 1268.
1 CONT
1 CONT The younger Elisabeth was from 1274 the wife of the future Albert I of Germany, one of the founders of the House of Habsburg, becoming thus daughter-in-law of the King of the Romans. Her husband, then a Count of Habsburg was inves
1 CONC ted as Duke of Austria and Styria in December 1282 by his father the King Rudolf. They solidified their rule in what was to become the Habsburg patrimony, also by help of Elisabeth's father who in his turn in 1286 was created Duke
1 CONC  of Carinthia.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1298, her husband was finally elected the King upon the end of the reign of Adolf of Nassau. Her husband was murdered on 1 May 1308 by his nephew John "the Parricide".
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
1 CONT
1 CONT She was married in Vienna on 20 December 1274 to the future Albert I of Germany. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Rudolph III (ca. 1282–4 July 1307, Horazdiowitz), Married but line extinct and predeceased his father.
1 CONT    2. Frederick I (1289–13 January 1330, Gutenstein). Married but line extinct.
1 CONT    3. Leopold I (4 August 1290–28 February 1326, Strassburg). Married but line extinct.
1 CONT    4. Albert II (12 December 1298, Vienna–20 July 1358, Vienna).
