0 @N6000000002188625378@ NOTE Charles, Duke of Calabria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles, Duke of Calabria (1298 – November 9, 1328) was the son of King Robert of Naples and Yolanda of Aragon.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in Naples, he became Duke of Calabria in 1309 on his father's accession, and was created Vicar-General of the Kingdom of Sicily (Naples). His father intended him to lead the force sent to aid Florence in 1315, but was constra
1 CONC ined by time to send Robert's brother, Philip I of Taranto, instead. The Florentine-Neapolitan coalition was badly beaten at the ensuing Battle of Montecatini.
1 CONT
1 CONT The victory of Castruccio Castracani at Altopascio in 1325 led the Florentines to elect Charles signore (lord) of the city for ten years in 1326. At the time, he was unsuccessfully attempting to seize Sicily from his first cousin 
1 CONC Frederick III, and sent Walter VI of Brienne as his deputy until he could arrive, where Walter made a (misleadingly) favorable impression. While Charles' arrival checked Castruccio, he exacted onerous taxes from the Florentines, u
1 CONC ntil he was recalled to Naples in December 1327 due to the advance of Emperor Louis IV into Italy. There he died in 1328. He left as heir his eldest surviving daughter, Joan; a posthumous daughter, Marie, was born in 1329.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and ancestry
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1316, he married Catherine of Habsburg (1295–1323), daughter of Albert I of Germany. After her death, he married Marie of Valois (1309–1332), daughter of Charles of Valois. They had five children:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Eloisa (d. December 1325)
1 CONT     * Marie (1326–1328)
1 CONT     * Charles Martel (Florence, April 13, 1327 – April 21, 1327, Florence)
1 CONT     * Joan I of Naples (1328–1382)
1 CONT     * Marie of Calabria (1329–1366), Countess of Alba. Married Charles, Duke of Durazzo.
0 @N6000000002188625501@ NOTE Sancha of Majorca
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancha of Majorca (c. 1285 – 28 July 1345, Naples) was the second wife but only Queen consort of Robert of Naples.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT She was a daughter of James II of Majorca and his Queen consort Esclaramunda of Foix.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her paternal grandparents were James I of Aragon and Violant of Hungary. Her maternal grandparents were Roger IV of Foix and Brunissenda of Cardona.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage
1 CONT
1 CONT In July, 1304, Sancha married Robert of Naples. He was the eldest surviving son of Charles II of Naples and Maria Arpad of Hungary. He was about twenty-seven years old and his first wife Yolanda of Aragon, daughter of Peter III of
1 CONC  Aragon and Constantia of Sicily, had died in August, 1302.
1 CONT
1 CONT Charles II died on 5 May 1309 and Robert succeeded him. Sancha served as his Queen consort for the rest of his reign but remained childless.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 20 January 1343, Robert died and was succeeded by his granddaughter Joan I of Naples. Sancha became Queen Dowager and retired to a local monastery on 20 January 1344. She died as a nun the following year.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Maria Arpad of Hungary Queen Consort of Naples
1 CONT 5 May 1309 –20 January 1343 Succeeded by
1 CONT Andrew, Duke of Calabria
