0 @N6000000002188005479@ NOTE Bianca Lancia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Bianca Lancia d'Agliano (also called Beatrice and Blanche); was an Italian noblewoman, who was the mistress and later wife of emperor Frederick II of Hohenstaufen, although the marriage, conducted while she was on her deathbed, wa
1 CONC s considered non-canonical.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family 
1 CONT 2 Relationship with Frederick II 
1 CONT 3 Marriage and Children 
1 CONT 4 Death 
1 CONT 5 References 
1 CONT 6 Sources 
1 CONT 7 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT Born ca. 1200/1210, Bianca was a member of the Lancia (or Lanza) family of Piedmont, so-called since her grandfather Manfred I had been lancifer of emperor Frederick Barbarossa. There is no source which definitively states who her
1 CONC  parents were, historians have offered two theories:
1 CONT
1 CONT That she was a niece of Manfred II Lancia, Marquess of Busca, as daughter of his sister (maybe named Bianca), by a son (name unknown) of Guglielmo di Moncucco and Belda di Agliano. 
1 CONT That she was a niece of Manfred II Lancia, Marquess of Busca, as daughter of his brother Bonifacio di Agliano possible from a marriage to a lady called Bianca, of the Maletta family.[1] 
1 CONT If she was a daughter of Bonifacio, she had seven siblings: four brothers (Manfred III, Guglielmo, Galvano and Frederick) and three sisters (Beatrice, Agnes and Isabella). Apparently, Bianca would be the third child and first born
1 CONC  daughter[2] [3]. But this is only one of two competing theories.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Relationship with Frederick II
1 CONT Bianca lived most of her life in the Castle of Boro, the ancestral residence of the Lancia family. She met Frederick II, who was then married to Yolande of Jerusalem, in 1225 at Agliano, near Asti. Thenceforth, it is said, the two
1 CONC  maintained a romantic relationship.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to some historians, Bianca was the only true love of Frederick's life; others consider this a romantic exaggeration. It was certainly beneficial to the interests of the Lancia family, who were favoured by the Emperor wit
1 CONC h political posts in Italy (Manfredo III was appointed Imperial Vicar of the Holy Roman Empire's northern Italian territories and Podesta of Alessandria, Milan and Chieri; Galvano became Imperial Vicar of Tuscany, Podesta of Padov
1 CONC a, Prince of Salerno, Count of Fondi and Grand Marshal of Sicily; and Frederick was appointed Count of Squillace and Viceroy of Apulia). Nonetheless, the relationship of Bianca and Frederick was the longest to all the affairs of t
1 CONC he Emperor.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Queen Yolande in 1228, Frederick endowed Bianca with the castle of Monte Sant'Angelo, located in the cities of Vieste and Siponto. By the terms of the will of William II of Sicily, the castle was the traditional
1 CONC  dowry of the Sicilian Queen consorts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Bianca died either in the castle of Paternò or the castle of Gioia del Colle.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and Children
1 CONT The Chronicle of Salimbene di Adam, and also Matthew of Paris claimed that a "confirmatio matrimonii in articulo mortis" ("marriage ceremony in the moments of death") took place between Bianca and Frederick when she was dying. Bia
1 CONC nca, apparently, desired the marriage for the salvation of her soul and the safety of their children's future. [4] [5] This marriage however was not deemed canonical by the Church.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick and Bianca had three children together:
1 CONT
1 CONT Constance (1230-1307), who married the Emperor of Nicea, John Vatatzes, and thereupon her name changed to Anna. 
1 CONT Manfred (1232-1266), who succeeded his father as ruler of Sicily (initially as regent, before usurping the throne for himself). 
1 CONT Violante (1233-1264), who married Richard, Count of Caserta. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT It is not known exactly when she died. There are several conflicting reports. Although the Medlands database gives 1233/1234, Medlands is not reliable for unsourced claims. This date lingers in various reference works but appears 
1 CONC based only on the assumption that she died after the birth of her last child, and before the marriage of her husband to his next wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT There are credible reports that she married Frederick while she was on her deathbed, and that her son was 12 at this time. Bianca may have died a short while before the wedding of her daughter to Ioannes Batatzes in 1244. One sour
1 CONC ce states that she died 20 years after the start of her relationship with Frederick, however the same source states that she died 20 years before events that were occurring in 1256. All of these cannot be simultaneously true.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Yolande of Jerusalem Queen consort of Sicily?
1 CONT c. 1233 Succeeded by
1 CONT Isabella of England 
1 CONT Holy Roman Empress?
1 CONT c. 1233 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT ^ "The mother of Manfred, King of Sicily, was a noblewoman named Blanche, of the family of the Marquis of Lancia and Loreto, in southern Lombardy. She was the daughter of Boniface, Count of Agliano (near Asti), one of the principa
1 CONC l fiefs of Lancia. Even contemporaries were unsure of the exact degree of her relationship with the Marquis of Lancia, who had accompanied Emperor Frederick II to the East. They could not tell if she were his niece or his granddau
1 CONC ghter." [Reference: Source: Paul Crawford, The 'Templar of Tyre': Part III of the 'Deeds of the Cypriots' (Crusade Texts in Translation 6) (2003), pg. 18, footnote 4]. Reference provided by Douglas Richardson on soc.genealogy.medi
1 CONC eval Jan 22, 2008 
1 CONT ^ Monferrato 
1 CONT ^ Lancia family 
1 CONT ^ Matthew of Paris, Mon. Germ. Hist. Scriptores XXVIII pp. 360-361 
1 CONT ^ Cronica Fratris Salimbene di Adam, Ordinis Minorem, MGH SS XXXII p.349 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Natale Ferro, "Chi fu Bianca Lancia di Agliano", in _Bianca Lancia d'Agliano, fra il Piemonte e il regno di Sicilia: Atti del convegno (Asti-Agliano, 28-29 aprile 1990)_, edited by Renato Bordone, Ricerche di storia locale 4 (Ales
1 CONC sandria, 1992) pp 55-80. (in Italian) (Reference provided by Peter Stewart on soc.genealogy.medieval) 
1 CONT "Monumenta Germaniae Historica", using Search Term : "Salimbene, Bianca" will display : "Cronica fratris Salimbene de Adam ordinis Minorum" i.e. "The Chronicle of brother Salimbene de Adam, order of Minors" 
