0 @N6000000002188014572@ NOTE John de Warenne, 7th Earl of Surrey
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT John de Warenne (1231? – September 27, 1304), 7th Earl of Surrey or Warenne, was prominent during the reigns of Henry III and Edward I. During his long life he fought in the Second Barons' War and in Edward I's wars in Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of William de Warenne, Earl of Surrey or Warenne, and Maud (or Matilda) Marshal. His mother was the daughter of William Marshal and widow of Hugh Bigod, 3rd Earl of Norfolk. Thus Roger Bigod, 4th Earl of Norfolk, wa
1 CONC s his elder half-brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT Warenne was a boy when his father died, and for the rest of his minority Peter of Savoy was the guardian of his estates. In 1247 he married Henry III's half-sister Alice de Lusignan. This marriage was to create resentment amongst 
1 CONC the English nobility, who did not like seeing a wealthy English nobleman marrying a penniless outsider.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the following years Warenne was closely associated with the court faction centering on his in-laws. In 1254 he accompanied the king's son Edward (the future Edward I) on Edward's journey to Spain to marry Eleanor of Castile
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT During the conflicts between Henry III and his barons Warenne started as a strong supporter of the king, switched to support for Simon de Montfort, and then returned to the royalist party. He opposed the initial baronial reform pl
1 CONC an of May 1258, but along with other opponents capitulated and took the oath of the Provisions of Oxford.
1 CONT
1 CONT By 1260 Warenne had joined the party of Simon de Montfort, but switched back to the king's side in 1263. After the Battle of Lewes, which was fought near his castle at Lewes, he fled to the Continent, where he remained for about a
1 CONC  year. He returned to fight in the campaign which culminated in the Battle of Evesham and the siege of Kenilworth Castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Warenne served in Edward I's Welsh campaigns in 1277, 1282, and 1283. In 1282 he received the lordships of Bromfield and Yale in Wales. A good part of the following years were spent in Scotland. He was one of the negotiators for t
1 CONC he 1289 treaty of Salisbury and for the 1290 treaty of Birgham, and accompanied the king on Edward's 1296 invasion of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT On August 22, 1296 the king appointed him "warden of the kingdom and land of Scotland". However he returned to England a few months later claiming that the Scottish climate was bad for his health. The following spring saw the rebe
1 CONC llion of William Wallace, and after much delay Warenne led an army northward, where they were defeated at the Battle of Stirling Bridge.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nevertheless the king appointed Warenne captain of the next campaign against the Scots in early 1298. He raised the siege of Roxburgh and re-took the castle at Berwick. The king himself took the field later that year, and Warenne 
1 CONC was one of the commanders at the Battle of Falkirk.
1 CONT
1 CONT Warenne and Alice de Lusignan had three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Alice, who married Henry Percy and was the mother of Henry Percy, 1st Baron Percy of Alnwick; 
1 CONT Isabella, who married John Balliol and was the mother of Edward Balliol; 
1 CONT William, who married Joanna, daughter of Robert de Vere, earl of Oxford, and was accidentally killed at a tournament on December 15, 1286; his son John succeeded his grandfather as earl of Surrey. 
0 @N6000000002176622221@ NOTE Reigned 1199-1216. Signed Magna Carta in 1215 at Runnymede.
1 CONT      His reign saw renewal of war with Phillip II Augustus of France to
1 CONT whom he has
1 CONT      lost several continental possesions including Normandy by 1205. He
1 CONT came into
1 CONT      conflict with his Barons and was forced to Sign the Magna Carta.
1 CONT His later
1 CONT      repudiation of the charter led to the first barons war 1215-17
1 CONT during which
1 CONT      John died. Burke says he was born in 1160.
1 CONT      King of Ireland 1177, Count of Mortain 1189, Earl of Gloucester.
1 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1 CONT JOHN OF ENGLAND  (1167-1216). Vicious, shameless, and ungrateful, King
1 CONT John has been called the worst king ever to rule England. Yet the very
1 CONT excesses of his reign proved positive in that they provoked such a
1 CONT violent reaction that his subjects revolted and forced him to put his
1 CONT seal on the Magna Carta. This document became the safeguard of English
1 CONT liberty. John's nickname was Lackland because at first he owned no
1 CONT land. Later his father, King Henry II, gave him castles, lands, and
1 CONT revenues in both England and France. John plotted against his father,
1 CONT however, and the discovery of this conspiracy was a factor in the
1 CONT king's death. John's brother, Richard the Lion-Hearted, became king and
1 CONT added to John's possessions (see Richard, Kings of England). While
1 CONT Richard was absent from England on the Third Crusade, John conspired
1 CONT against him also.
1 CONT    When Richard died in 1199, the barons selected John to be their
1 CONT king. This denied the royal claim of Arthur, son of another brother,
1 CONT Geoffrey. Two French provinces fought for young Arthur, but the boy
1 CONT fell into the hands of John and died soon after. During the war John
1 CONT lost all his French possessions except Aquitaine. John then quarreled
1 CONT with Pope Innocent III about the appointment of Stephen Langton as
1 CONT archbishop of Canterbury. John was excommunicated, and England was
1 CONT forbidden all religious services except baptism and extreme unction.
1 CONT The growing discontent of his subjects finally forced John to recognize
1 CONT the new archbishop.
1 CONT    When John went to France seeking to regain his lands in Normandy,
1 CONT the barons marched against the king and demanded a charter of
1 CONT liberties. All but a handful of followers deserted John. He was forced
1 CONT to meet the barons at Runnymede on June 15, 1215, and to sign the Great
1 CONT Charter (see Magna Carta).
1 CONT    John had no intention of supporting the charter, however. He
1 CONT recruited a new army and destroyed the estates of the barons. The
1 CONT barons then offered the English crown to Louis, a French prince. In the
1 CONT midst of a war for the throne, John died of a fever. The task of
1 CONT restoring the torn kingdom fell to his nine-year-old son, Henry III
1 CONT (see Henry, Kings of England).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Acceded: 27 MAY 1199, Westminster Abbey, London, England
0 @N6000000002176663253@ NOTE Best known for signing the Magna Carta, at the insistence of the English
1 CONT Barons; the youngest son of Henry II, along with Richard I, the Lion Hearted.
1 CONT John was from the house of Anjou, or Plantagenet.
