0 @N6000000005597849644@ NOTE The administrative and bureaucratic innovations John brought to
1 CONT the Angevin government have not been enough to save his
1 CONT reputation throughout the years. Chronicled as England's most
1 CONT cruel and untrustworthy monarch, John may long be the victim of
1 CONT his early bad press. As a monarch, John displayed no love lost
1 CONT for the Church of Rome and as a result, the historians of the
1 CONT day--almost without exception clerics, took every opportunity
1 CONT to malign and discredit him. However, while not evil incarnate,
1 CONT John certainly demonstrated on many an occasion those qualities
1 CONT that led generations of school children to remember him as Bad
1 CONT King John.
1 CONT
1 CONT The last child born to Henry II and Eleanor of Aquitaine earned
1 CONT his first nickname, Lackland, because as the fourth legitimate
1 CONT son born to the King of England and Duke of Anjou, it was hard
1 CONT to provide him inherited titles and land. John's main rivals
1 CONT were his brothers, the Young King Henry, Geoffrey and Richard,
1 CONT and his nephew Arthur, son of King Henry's brother Geoffrey of
1 CONT Brittany. Yet, John had one advantage over his aspiring
1 CONT siblings: his father's favor. In 1184 Henry asked Richard to
1 CONT hand over to John the rule of Aquitaine, the ancestral land of
1 CONT their monther, Eleanor. Richard's refusal led to the first
1 CONT armed clash between the brothers. Next, Henry devised to let
1 CONT John rule Ireland, but John's rule was a disaster. In 1185, on
1 CONT his first expedition he and his companion laughed at the beards
1 CONT of the Irish chieftains who came to pay homage. John's callous
1 CONT alienation continued until John left six months later, blaming
1 CONT his failure on others.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's generosity bore no loyalty in John. When Richard and
1 CONT Philip II of France forced Henry into a humiliating defeat in
1 CONT 1188, the old king was given a list of those who fought against
1 CONT him, and was shocked to find John's name on it. Henry had
1 CONT forsaken Richard to the point of armed rebellion for the sake
1 CONT of John, but when John saw who would emerge the victor he
1 CONT cynically abandoned his father. As King of England, Richard,
1 CONT however, was not blind to John's raw ambition. He bestowed John
1 CONT with vast estates in Normandy and England, and betrothed him to
1 CONT Isabella of Gloucester, in an effort to bribe him, but it was
1 CONT not enough to quell John. While Richard was away on various
1 CONT Crusades, and now quarrelling from Philip II, John plotted a
1 CONT number of unsuccessful rebellions, at one point joining forces
1 CONT with Philip. When Richard was later kidnapped and held for
1 CONT ransom by the Leopold of Austria, John and Philip were almost
1 CONT successful in having Richard turned over to them for their own
1 CONT devices.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Richard was released from captivity by English barons John
1 CONT was forced to beg for his pardon. Richard, seemingly unaware of
1 CONT the depth of his brother's treachery, pardoned him with the
1 CONT words, "Don't be afraid, John. You are a child. You have fallen
1 CONT into bad company and it is those who have led you astray who
1 CONT will be punished." At this point John was twenty-seven years
1 CONT old. John behaved himself for the next five years and in 1199
1 CONT Richard nominated him as heir and John was invested the Duke of
1 CONT Normandy. It was at this ceremony that John giggled and
1 CONT gossiped so much that he dropped the ceremonial lance. While
1 CONT Richard's right to declare his successor was respected in
1 CONT England, that was not the case in Anjou, Maine and Touraine.
1 CONT There the local barons acknowledged Arthur, son Geoffrey of
1 CONT Brittany as their lord. John persuaded Philip II to oust the
1 CONT twelve-year old Arthur, and by 1200 John was Lord of the
1 CONT Angevin Empire. He had annulled his marriage to Isabella of
1 CONT Gloucester and took as his wife Isabella of Angouleme, in an
1 CONT effort to unite the north and south of the empire. However, his
1 CONT twleve-year old bride had of late been the fiance of Hugh of
1 CONT Lusignan, and when he got no satisfaction from John, Hugh
1 CONT appealed to Philip II. Philip sided with Hugh and declared all
1 CONT of John's recent acquisitions forfeit. John put up a stiff
1 CONT resistance, and succeeded in capturing Arthur of Brittany, but
1 CONT by spring of 1205 John fled to England, the last of his
1 CONT strongholds fell and he lost ancestral Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was while Arthur was in the hands of John that he
1 CONT disappeared, never to be seen again. A contemporary historian
1 CONT wrote:
1 CONT After King John had captured Arthur and kept him alive in
1 CONT prison for some time, at length, in the castle of Rouen, after
1 CONT dinner on the Thursday before Easter, when he was drunk and
1 CONT possessed by the devil he slew him with his own hand, and tying
1 CONT a heavy stone to the body cast it into the Seine. It was
1 CONT discovered by a fisherman in his net, and being dragged to the
1 CONT bank and recognized, was taken for a secret burial, in fear of
1 CONT the tyrant, to the priory of Bec called Notre Dame de Pres.
1 CONT
1 CONT The murder of Arthur has never been proven.
1 CONT
1 CONT John's goal was now to win back his lost territories, but he
1 CONT realized that a confrontation with the King of France would
1 CONT take resources and planning. For eight years he stayed in
1 CONT England and made preparations, but these preparations meant a
1 CONT huge burdon for the English. Huges taxes were levied and forest
1 CONT laws were tightened and enforced. This power, never before seen
1 CONT wielded by an English king, was coinciding with a time of
1 CONT economic recession. At the same time John came to loggerheads
1 CONT with Pope Innocent III over the election of the Archbishop of
1 CONT Canterbury. In 1208 Innocent punished England and Wales by
1 CONT banishing all church services for six years and further
1 CONT punished John by excommunicating him the following year. John's
1 CONT response was to confiscate all church lands--a response that
1 CONT also helped ease his financial difficulties. By 1212, however,
1 CONT Philip was planning an invasion and John decided an
1 CONT excommunicated monarch was particularly vulnerable. John agreed
1 CONT to hold England as a papal fief and with Innocent's support led
1 CONT an offensive against Philip.
1 CONT
1 CONT The beginning months of the struggle looked promising for the
1 CONT English forces, but in July, 1214, Philip won the Battle of
1 CONT Bovines. This bitter defeat only fueled English discontent. The
1 CONT hardships of the previous eight years were only justified with
1 CONT victory in battle and the defeat soon turned to rebellion. In
1 CONT May of 1215 the English rebels captured London and force John
1 CONT to make peace. John met with the lords at Runnymead and the
1 CONT terms laid out and agreed upon became known as Magna Carta.
1 CONT However, the attempt to implement the terms of the treaty only
1 CONT led to more war, for John seemed to agree to the document as a
1 CONT way of buying time. In frustration and contempt for John, the
1 CONT English lords elected Louis of France, Philip's son, as their
1 CONT leader. In May of 1216 Louis invaded England and made an
1 CONT unopposed entry into London. The death of John in October, with
1 CONT his son, Henry, only nine years old, plunged the county into
1 CONT civil war.
1 CONT
1 CONT John's reign now over, its interpretation began, and while none
1 CONT would be impartial, some would be remembered through the years
1 CONT as is this anonymous chronicle:
1 CONT
1 CONT He was munificent and liberal to outsiders but a plunderer of
1 CONT his own people, trusting strangers rather than his subjects,
1 CONT wherefore he was eventually deserted by his own men and, in the
1 CONT end, little mourned.
1 CONT
1 CONT Biographical information from The Lives of the Kings and Queens
1 CONT of England, ed. Antonia Frasier, The Oxford Illustrated History
1 CONT of the British Monarchy, ed. John Cannon and Ralph Griffiths,
1 CONT and The Oxford Book of Royal Anecdotes, ed. Elizabeth Longford.
1 CONT
1 CONT MAGNA CARTA
1 CONT THE GREAT CHARTER OF ENGLISH LIBERTY DECREED BY KING JOHN AT
1 CONT RUNNYMEDE JUNE 15, A.D. 1215[:BOLD]
1 CONT
1 CONT JOHN, by the grace of God, King of England, lord of Ireland,
1 CONT Duke of Normandy and Aquitaine, and Count of Anjuo: To the
1 CONT archbishops, bishops, abbots, earls, barons, justices,
1 CONT foresters, sheriffs, prevosts, serving men, and to all his
1 CONT bailiffs and faithful subjects, Greeting. Know that we, by the
1 CONT will of God and for the safety of our soul, and of the souls of
1 CONT all our predecessors and our heirs, to the honor of God and for
1 CONT the exaltation of the holy Church, and the bettering of our
1 CONT realm: by the counsel of our venerable fathers Stephen
1 CONT archbishop of Canterbury, primate of all England and cardinal
1 CONT of the holy Roman church; of Henry archbishop of Dublin; of the
1 CONT bishops William of London, Peter of Winchester, Jocelin of Bath
1 CONT and Glastonbury, Hugo of Lincoln, Walter of Worcester, William
1 CONT of Coventry and Benedict of Rochester; of master Pandulf,
1 CONT subdeacon and of the household of the lord pope; of brother
1 CONT Aymeric, master of the knights of the Temple in England; and of
1 CONT the nobel men, William Marshall earl of Pembroke, William earl
1 CONT of Salisbury, William earl of Warren, William earl of Arundel,
1 CONT Alan de Galway constable of Scotland, Warin son of Gerold,
1 CONT Peter son of Herbert, Hubert de Burgh seneschal of Poictiers,
1 CONT Hugo de Neville, Matthew son of Herbert, Thomas Basset, Alan
1 CONT Basset, Philip d'Aubigni, Robert de Roppelay, John Marshall,
1 CONT John son of Hugo, and others of our faithful subjects:
1 CONT 1. First of all have granted to God, and, for us and for our
1 CONT heirs forever, have confirmed, by this our present charter,
1 CONT that the English church shall be free and shall have its rights
1 CONT intact and its liberties uninfringed upon. And thus we will
1 CONT that it be observed. As is apparent from the fact that we,
1 CONT spontaneously and of our own free will, before discord broke
1 CONT out between ourselves and our barons, did grant and by our
1 CONT charter confirm--and did cause the lord pope Innocent III, to
1 CONT confirm-- freedom of elections, which is considered most
1 CONT important and most necessary to the church of England. Which
1 CONT charter both we ourselves shall observe, and we will that it be
1 CONT observed with good faith by our heirs forever. We have also
1 CONT granted to all free men of our realm, on the part of ourselves
1 CONT and our heirs forever, all the subjoined liberties, to have and
1 CONT to hold, to them and to their heirs, from us and from our
1 CONT heirs:
1 CONT
1 CONT 2. If any one of our earls or barons, or of others holding from
1 CONT us in chief through military service, shall die; and if, at the
1 CONT time of his death, his heir be of full age and owe a relief: he
1 CONT shall have his inheritance by paying the old relief;--the heir,
1 CONT namely, or the heirs of an earl, by paying one hundred pounds
1 CONT for the whole barony of an earl; the heir or heirs of a baron,
1 CONT by paying one hundred pounds for the whole barony; the heir or
1 CONT heirs of a knight, by paying one hundred shillings at most for
1 CONT a whole knight's fee; and he who shall owe less shall give
1 CONT less, according to the ancient custom of fees.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3. But if the heir of any of the above persons shall be under
1 CONT age and in wardship,--when he comes of age he shall have his
1 CONT inheritance without relief and without fine.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4. The administrator of the land of such heir who shall be
1 CONT under age shall take none but reasonable issues from the land
1 CONT of the heir, and reasonable customs and services; and this
1 CONT without destruction and waste of men or goods. And if we shall
1 CONT have committed the custody of any such land to the sheriff or
1 CONT to any other man who ought to be responsible to us for the
1 CONT issues of it, and he cause destruction or waste to what is in
1 CONT his charge: we will fine him, and the land shall be handed over
1 CONT to two lawful and discreet men of that fee who shall answer to
1 CONT us, or to him to whom we shall have referred them, regarding
1 CONT those issues. And if we shall have given or sold to any one the
1 CONT custody of any such land, and he shall have caused destruction
1 CONT or waste to it,--he shall lose that custody, and it shall be
1 CONT given to two lawful and discreet men of that fee, who likewise
1 CONT shall answer to us, as has been explained.
1 CONT
1 CONT 5. The administrator, moreover, so long as he may have the
1 CONT custody of the land, shall keep in order, from the issues of
1 CONT that land, the houses, parks, warrens, lakes, mills, and other
1 CONT things pertaining to it. And he shall restore to the heir when
1 CONT he comes to full age, his whole land stocked with ploughs and
1 CONT wainnages, according as the time of the wainnage requires and
1 CONT the issues of the land will reasonably permit.
1 CONT
1 CONT 6. Heirs may marry without disparagement; so, nevertheless,
1 CONT that, before the marriage is contracted, it shall be announced
1 CONT to the relations by blood of the heir himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT 7. A widow, after the death of her husband, shall straightway,
1 CONT and without difficulty, have her marriage portion and her
1 CONT inheritance, nor shall she give any thing in return for her
1 CONT dowry, her marriage portion, or the inheritance which belonged
1 CONT to her, and which she and her husband held on the day of the
1 CONT death of that husband. And she may remain in the house of her
1 CONT husband, after his death, for forty days; within which her
1 CONT dowry shall be paid over to her.
1 CONT
1 CONT 8. No widow shall be forced to marry when she prefers to live
1 CONT without a husband; so, however, that she gives security not to
1 CONT marry without our consent, if she hold from us, or the consent
1 CONT of the lord from whom she holds, if she hold from another.
1 CONT
1 CONT 9. Neither we nor our bailiffs shall seize any revenue for any
1 CONT debt, so long as the chattels of the debtor suffice to pay the
1 CONT debt; nor shall the sponsors of that debtor be distrained so
1 CONT long as the chief debtor has enough to pay the debt. But if the
1 CONT chief debtor fail in paying the debt, not having the
1 CONT wherewithal to pay it, the sponsors shall answer for the debt.
1 CONT And, if they shall wish, they may have the lands and revenues
1 CONT of the debtor until satisfaction shall have been given them for
1 CONT the debt previously paid for him; unless the chief debtor shall
1 CONT show that he is quit in that respect towards those same
1 CONT sponsors.
1 CONT
1 CONT 10. If any one shall have taken any sum, great or small, as a
1 CONT loan from the money-lenders, and shall die before that debt is
1 CONT paid,--that debt shall not bear interest so long as the heir,
1 CONT from whomever he may hold, shall be under age. And if the debt
1 CONT fall into our hands, we shall take nothing save the chattel
1 CONT contained in the deed.
1 CONT
1 CONT 11. And if any one dies owing a debt to the money-lenders, his
1 CONT wife shall have her dowry, and shall restore nothing of that
1 CONT debt. But if there shall remain children of that dead man, and
1 CONT they shall be under age, the necessaries shall be provided for
1 CONT them according to the nature of the dead man's holding; and
1 CONT from the residue, the debt shall be paid, saving the service
1 CONT due to the lords. In like manner shall be done concerning debts
1 CONT that are due to others besides money-lenders.
1 CONT
1 CONT 12. No scutage or aid shall be imposed in our realm unless by
1 CONT the common counsel of our realm; except for redeeming our body,
1 CONT and knighting our eldest son, and marrying once our eldest
1 CONT daughter. And for these purposes there shall only be given a
1 CONT reasonable aid. In like manner shall be done concerning the
1 CONT aids of the city of London.
1 CONT
1 CONT 13. And the city of London shall have all its old liberties and
1 CONT free customs as well by land as by water. Moreover we will and
1 CONT grant that other cities and burroughs, and town and ports,
1 CONT shall have all their liberties and free customs.
1 CONT
1 CONT 14. And, in order to have the common counsel of the realm in
1 CONT the matter of assessing an aid otherwise than in the aforesaid
1 CONT cases, or of assessing a scutage--we shall cause, under seal
1 CONT through our letters, the archbishops, bishops, abbots, earls,
1 CONT and greater barons to be summoned for a fixed day--for a term,
1 CONT namely, at least forty days distant,--and for a fixed place.
1 CONT And, moreover, we shall cause to be summoned in general,
1 CONT through our sheriffs and bailiffs, all those who hold of us in
1 CONT chief. And in all those letters of summons we shall express the
1 CONT cause of the summons. And when a summons has thus been made,
1 CONT the business shall be proceeded with on the day appointed
1 CONT according to the counsel of those who shall be present, even
1 CONT though not all shall come who were summoned.
1 CONT
1 CONT 15. We will not allow any one henceforth to take an aid from
1 CONT his freemen save for the redemption of his body, and the
1 CONT knighting of his eldest son, and the marrying, once, of his
1 CONT eldest daughter; and, for these purposes, there shall only be
1 CONT given a reasonable aid.
1 CONT
1 CONT 16. No one shall be forced to do more service for a knight's
1 CONT fee, or for another free holding, than is due from it.
1 CONT
1 CONT 17. Common pleas shall not follow our court but shall be held
1 CONT in a certain fixed place.
1 CONT
1 CONT 18. Assizes of novel disseisin, of mort d'ancestor, and of
1 CONT darrein presentment shall not be held save in their own
1 CONT counties, and in this way: We, or our chief justice, if we
1 CONT shall be absent from the kingdom, shall send two justices
1 CONT through each county four times a year; they, with four knights
1 CONT from each county, chosen by the county, shall hold the
1 CONT aforesaid assizes in the county, and on the day and at the
1 CONT place of the county court.
1 CONT
1 CONT 19. And if on the day of the county court the aforesaid assizes
1 CONT can not be held, a sufficient number of knights and free
1 CONT tenants, from those who were present at the county court on
1 CONT that day, shall remain, so that through them the judgments may
1 CONT be suitably given, according as the matter may have been great
1 CONT or small.
1 CONT
1 CONT 20. A freeman shall only be amerced for a small offence
1 CONT according to the measure of that offence. And for a great
1 CONT offence he shall be amerced according to the magnitude of the
1 CONT offence, saving his contenement; and a merchant, in the same
1 CONT way, saving his merchandize. And a villein, in the same way, if
1 CONT he fall under our mercy, shall be amerced saving his wainnage.
1 CONT And none of the aforesaid fines shall be imposed save upon oath
1 CONT of upright men from the neighbourhood.
1 CONT
1 CONT 21. Earls and barons shall not be amerced save through their
1 CONT peers, and only according to the measure of the offence.
1 CONT
1 CONT 22. No clerk shall be amerced for his lay tenement except
1 CONT according to the manner of the other persons aforesaid; and not
1 CONT according to the amount of his ecclesiastical benefice.
1 CONT
1 CONT 23. Neither a town nor a man shall be forced to make bridges
1 CONT over the rivers, with the exception of those who, from of old
1 CONT and of right ought to do it.
1 CONT
1 CONT 24. No sheriff, constable, coroners, or other bailiffs of ours
1 CONT shall hold the pleas of our crown. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 25. All counties, hundreds, wapentakes, and trithings--our
1 CONT demensne manors being excepted--shall continue according to the
1 CONT old farms, without any increase at all.
1 CONT
1 CONT 26. If any one holding from us a lay fee shall die, and our
1 CONT sheriff or bailiff can show our letters patent containing our
1 CONT summons for the debt which the dead man owed to us,--our
1 CONT sheriff or bailiff may be allowed to attach and enroll the
1 CONT chattels of the dead man to the value of that debt, through
1 CONT view of lawful men; in such way, however, that nothing shall be
1 CONT removed thence until the debt is paid which was plainly owed to
1 CONT us. And the residue shall be left to the executors that they
1 CONT may carry out the will of the dead man. And if nothing is owed
1 CONT to us by him, all the chattels shall go to the use prescribed
1 CONT by the deceased, saving their reasonable portions to his wife
1 CONT and children.
1 CONT
1 CONT 27. If any freeman shall have died intestate his chattels shall
1 CONT be distributed through the hands of his near relatives and
1 CONT friends, by view of the church; saving to any one the debts
1 CONT which the dead man owed him.
1 CONT
1 CONT 28. No constable or other bailiff of ours shall take the corn
1 CONT or other chattels of any one except he straightway give money
1 CONT for them, or can be allowed a respite in that regard by the
1 CONT will of the seller.
1 CONT
1 CONT 29. No constable shall force any knight to pay money for
1 CONT castleward if he be willing to perform that ward in person,
1 CONT or-- he for a reasonable cause not being able to perform it
1 CONT himself-- through another proper man. And if we shall have led
1 CONT or sent him on a military expedition, he shall be quit of ward
1 CONT according to the amount of time during which, through us, he
1 CONT shall have been in military service.
1 CONT
1 CONT 30. No sheriff nor bailiff of ours, no any one else, shall take
1 CONT the horses or carts of any freeman for transport, unless by the
1 CONT will of that freeman.
1 CONT
1 CONT 31. Neither we nor our bailiffs shall take another's wood for
1 CONT castles or for other private uses, unless by the will of him to
1 CONT whom the wood belongs.
1 CONT
1 CONT 32. We shall not hold the lands of those convicted of felony
1 CONT longer than a year and a day; and then the lands shall be
1 CONT restored to the lords of the fiefs.
1 CONT
1 CONT 33. Henceforth all the weirs in the Thames and Medway, and
1 CONT throughout all England, save on the sea-coasts, shall be done
1 CONT away with entirely.
1 CONT
1 CONT 34. Henceforth the writ which is called Praecipe shall not be
1 CONT served on any one for any holding so as to cause a free man to
1 CONT lose his court. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 35. There shall be one measure of wine throughout our whole
1 CONT realm, and one measure of ale and one measure of corn--namely,
1 CONT the London quart;--and one width of dyed and resset and hauberk
1 CONT cloths--namely, two ells below the selvage. And with weights,
1 CONT moreover, it shall be as with measures.
1 CONT
1 CONT 36. Henceforth nothing shall be given or taken for a writ of
1 CONT inquest in a matter concerning life or limb; but it shall be
1 CONT conceded gratis, and shall not be denied. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 37. If any one hold of us in fee-farm, or in socage, or in
1 CONT burkage, and hold land of another by military service, we shall
1 CONT not, by reason of that fee-farm, or socage, or burkage, have
1 CONT the wardship of his heir or of his land which is held in fee
1 CONT from another. Nor shall we have the wardship of that fee-farm,
1 CONT or socage, or burkage unless that fee-farm owe military
1 CONT service. We shall not, by reason of some petit-serjeanty which
1 CONT some one holds of us through the service of giving us knives or
1 CONT arrows or the like, have the wardship of his heir or of the
1 CONT land which he holds of another by military service.
1 CONT
1 CONT 38. No bailiff, on his own simple assertion, shall henceforth
1 CONT put any one to his law, without producing faithful witnesses in
1 CONT evidence. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 39. No freeman shall be taken, or imprisoned, or disseized, or
1 CONT outlawed, or exiled, or in any way harmed--nor will we go upon
1 CONT or send upon him--save by the lawful judgment of his peers or
1 CONT by the law of the land. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 40. To none will we sell, to none deny or delay, right or
1 CONT justice. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 41. All merchants may safely and securely go out of England,
1 CONT and come into England, and delay and pass through England, as
1 CONT well by land as by water, for the purpose of buying and
1 CONT selling, free from all evil taxes, subject to the ancient and
1 CONT right customs--save in time of war, and if they are of the land
1 CONT at war against us. And if such be found in our land at the
1 CONT beginning of the war, they shall be held, without harm to their
1 CONT bodies and goods, until it shall be known to us or our chief
1 CONT justice how the merchants of our land are to be treated who
1 CONT shall, at that time, be found in the land at war against us.
1 CONT And if ours shall be safe there, the others shall be safe in
1 CONT our land.
1 CONT
1 CONT 42. Henceforth any person, saving fealty to us, may go out of
1 CONT our realm and return to it, safely and securely, by land and by
1 CONT water, except perhaps for a brief period in time of war, for
1 CONT the common good of the realm. But prisoners and outlaws are
1 CONT excepted according to the law of the realm; also people of a
1 CONT land at war against us, and the merchants, with regard to whom
1 CONT shall be done as we have said.
1 CONT
1 CONT 43. If any one hold from any escheat--as from the honour of
1 CONT Wallingford, Nottingham, Boloin, Lancaster, or the other
1 CONT escheats which are in our hands and are baronies--and shall
1 CONT die, his heir shall not give another relief, nor shall he
1 CONT perform for us other service than he would perform for a baron
1 CONT if that barony were in the hand of a baron; and we shall hold
1 CONT it in the same way in which the baron has held it.
1 CONT
1 CONT 44. Persons dwelling without the forest shall not henceforth
1 CONT come before the forest justices, through common summonses,
1 CONT unless they are impleaded or are the sponsors of some person or
1 CONT persons attached for matters concerning the forest.
1 CONT
1 CONT 45. We will not make men justices, constables, sheriffs, or
1 CONT bailiffs, unless they are such as know the law of the realm,
1 CONT and are minded to observe it rightly. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 46. All barons who have founded abbeys for which they have
1 CONT charters of the kings of England, or ancient right of tenure,
1 CONT shall have, as they ought to have, their custody when vacant.
1 CONT
1 CONT 47. All forests constituted as such in our time shall
1 CONT straightway be annulled; and the same shall be done for river
1 CONT banks made into places of defense by us in our time.
1 CONT
1 CONT 48. All evil customs concerning forests and warrens, and
1 CONT concerning foresters and warreners, sheriffs and their
1 CONT servants, river banks and their guardians, shall straightway be
1 CONT inquired into in each county, through twelve sworn knights from
1 CONT that county, and shall be eradicated by them, entirely, so that
1 CONT they shall never be renewed, within forty days after the
1 CONT inquest has been made; in such manner that we shall first know
1 CONT about them, or our justice if we be not in England.
1 CONT
1 CONT 49. We shall straightway return all hostages and charters which
1 CONT were delivered to us by Englishmen as a surety for peace or
1 CONT faithful service.
1 CONT
1 CONT 50. We shall entirely remove from their bailiwicks the
1 CONT relatives of Gerard de Athyes, so that they shall henceforth
1 CONT have no bailwick in England: Engelard de Cygnes, Andrew Peter
1 CONT and Gyon de Chanceles, Gyon de Cygnes, Geoffrey de Martin and
1 CONT his brothers, Philip Mark and his brothers, and Geoffrey his
1 CONT nephew, and the whole following of them.
1 CONT
1 CONT 51. And straightway after peace is restored we shall remove
1 CONT from the realm all the foreign soldiers, crossbowmen, servants,
1 CONT hirelings, who may have come with horses and arms to the harm
1 CONT of the realm.
1 CONT
1 CONT 52. If anyone shall have been disseized by us, or removed,
1 CONT without a legal sentence of his peers, from his lands, castles,
1 CONT liberties or lawful right, we shall straightway restore them to
1 CONT him. And if a dispute shall arise concerning this matter it
1 CONT shall be settled according to the judgment of the twenty-five
1 CONT barons who are mentioned below as sureties for the peace. But
1 CONT with regard to all those things of which any one was, by king
1 CONT Henry our father or king Richard our brother, disseized or
1 CONT dispossessed without legal judgement of his peers, which we
1 CONT have in our hand or which others hold, and for which we ought
1 CONT to give a guarantee: We shall have respite until the common
1 CONT term for crusaders. Except with regard to those concerning
1 CONT which a plea was moved, or an inquest made by our order, before
1 CONT we took the cross. But when we return from our pilgrimage, or
1 CONT if, by chance, we desist from our pilgrimage, we shall
1 CONT straightway then show full justice regarding them. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 53. We shall have the same respite, moreover, and in the same
1 CONT manner, in the matter of showing justice with regard to forests
1 CONT to be annulled and forests to remain, which Henry our father or
1 CONT Richard our brother constituted; and in the matter of wardships
1 CONT of lands which belong to the fee of another--wardships of which
1 CONT kind we have hitherto enjoyed by reason of the fee which some
1 CONT one held from us in military service;--and in the matter of
1 CONT abbeys founded in the fee of another than ourselves--in which
1 CONT the lord of the fee may say that he has jurisdiction. And when
1 CONT we return, or if we desist from our pilgrimage, we shall
1 CONT straightway exhibit full justice to those complaining with
1 CONT regard to these matters.
1 CONT
1 CONT 54. No one shall be taken or imprisoned on account of the
1 CONT appeal of a woman concerning the death of another than her
1 CONT husband.
1 CONT
1 CONT 55. All fines imposed by us unjustly and contrary to the law of
1 CONT the land, and all amerciaments made unjustly and contrary to
1 CONT the law of the land, shall be altogether remitted, or it shall
1 CONT be done with regard to them according to the judgment of the
1 CONT twenty five barons mentioned below as sureties for the peace,
1 CONT or according to the judgment of the majority of them together
1 CONT with the aforesaid Stephen archbishop of Canterbury, if he can
1 CONT be present, and with others whom he may wish to associate with
1 CONT himself for this purpose. And if he can not be present, the
1 CONT affair shall nevertheless proceed without him; in such way
1 CONT that, if one or more of the said twenty five barons shall be
1 CONT concerned in a similar complaint, they shall be removed as to
1 CONT this particular decision, and in their place, for this purpose
1 CONT alone, others shall be substituted who shall be chosen and
1 CONT sworn by the remainder of those twenty five. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 56. If we have disseized or dispossessed Welshmen of their
1 CONT lands or liberties or other things without legal judgment of
1 CONT their peers, in England or in Wales,--they shall straightway be
1 CONT restored to them. And if a dispute shall arise concerning this,
1 CONT then action shall be taken upon it in the March through
1 CONT judgment of their peers--concerning English holdings according
1 CONT to the law of England, concerning Welsh holdings according to
1 CONT the law of Wales, concerning holdings in the March according to
1 CONT the law of the March. The Welsh shall do likewise with regard
1 CONT to us and our subjects.
1 CONT
1 CONT 57. But with regard to all those things of which any one of the
1 CONT Welsh was, by king Henry our father or king Richard our
1 CONT brother, disseized or dispossessed without legal judgment of
1 CONT his peers, which we have in our hand or which others hold, and
1 CONT for which we ought to give a guarantee: we shall have respite
1 CONT until the common term for crusaders. Except with regard to
1 CONT those concerning which a plea was moved, or an inquest made by
1 CONT our order, before we took the cross. But when we return from
1 CONT our pilgrimage, or if, by chance, we desist from our
1 CONT pilgrimage, we shall straightway then show full justice
1 CONT regarding them, according to the laws of Wales and the
1 CONT aforesaid districts.
1 CONT
1 CONT 58. We shall straightway return the son of Llewelin and all the
1 CONT Welsh hostages, and the charters delivered to us as surety for
1 CONT the peace.
1 CONT
1 CONT 59. We shall act towards Alexander king of the Scots regarding
1 CONT the restoration of his sisters, and his hostages, and his
1 CONT liberties and his lawful right, as we shall act towards our
1 CONT other barons of England; unless it ought to be otherwise
1 CONT according to the charters which we hold from William, his
1 CONT father, the former king of the Scots. And this shall be done
1 CONT through judgment of his peers in our court.
1 CONT
1 CONT 60. Moreover all the subjects of our realm, clergy as well as
1 CONT laity, shall, as far as pertains to them, observe, with regard
1 CONT to their vassals, all these aforesaid customs and liberties
1 CONT which we have decreed shall, as far as pertains to us, be
1 CONT observed in our realm with regard to our own.
1 CONT
1 CONT 61. Inasmuch as for the sake of God, and for the bettering of
1 CONT our realm, and for the more ready healing of the discord which
1 CONT has arisen between us and our barons, we have made all these
1 CONT aforesaid concessions,--wishing them to enjoy for ever entire
1 CONT and firm stability, we make and grant to them the following
1 CONT security: that the barons, namely, may elect at their pleasure
1 CONT twenty five barons from the realm, who ought, with all their
1 CONT strength, to observe, maintain and cause to be observed, the
1 CONT peace and privileges which we have granted to them and
1 CONT confirmed by this our present charter. in such wise, namely,
1 CONT that if we, our justice, or our bailiffs, or any one of our
1 CONT servants shall have transgressed against any one in any
1 CONT respect, or shall have broken some one of the articles of peace
1 CONT or security, and our transgression shall have been shown to
1 CONT four barons of the aforesaid twenty five: those four barons
1 CONT shall come to us, or, if we are abroad, to our justice, showing
1 CONT to us our error; and they shall ask us to cause that error to
1 CONT be amended without delay. And if we do not amend that error,
1 CONT or, we being abroad, if our justice do not amend it within a
1 CONT term of forty days from the time when it was shown to us or, we
1 CONT being abroad, to our justice: the aforesaid four barons shall
1 CONT refer the matter to the remainder of the twenty five barons,
1 CONT and those twenty five barons, with the whole land in common,
1 CONT shall distrain and oppress us in every way in their
1 CONT power,--namely, by taking our castles, lands and possessions,
1 CONT and in every other way that they can, until amends shall have
1 CONT been made according to their judgment. Saving the persons of
1 CONT ourselves, our queen and our children. And when amends shall
1 CONT have been made they shall be in accord with us as they had been
1 CONT previously. And whoever of the land wishes to do so, shall
1 CONT swear that in carrying out all the aforesaid measures he will
1 CONT obey the mandates of the aforesaid twenty five barons, and
1 CONT that, with them, he will oppress us to the extent of his power.
1 CONT And, to any one who wishes to do so, we publicly and freely
1 CONT give permission to swear; and we will never prevent any one
1 CONT from swearing. Moreover, all those in the land who shall be
1 CONT unwilling, themselves and of their own accord, to swear to the
1 CONT twenty five barons as to distraining and oppressing us with
1 CONT them: such ones we shall make to swear by our mandate, as has
1 CONT been said. And if any one of the twenty five barons shall die,
1 CONT or leave the country, or in any other way be prevented from
1 CONT carrying out the aforesaid measures,--the remainder of the
1 CONT aforesaid twenty five barons shall choose another in his place,
1 CONT according to their judgment, who shall be sworn in the same way
1 CONT as the others. Moreover, in all things entrusted to those
1 CONT twenty five barons to be carried out, if those twenty five
1 CONT shall be present and chance to disagree among themselves with
1 CONT regard to some matter, or if some of them, having been
1 CONT summoned, shall be unwilling or unable to be present: that
1 CONT which the majority of those present shall decide or decree
1 CONT shall be considered binding and valid, just as if all the
1 CONT twenty five had consented to it. And the aforesaid twenty five
1 CONT shall swear that they will faithfully observe all the
1 CONT foregoing, and will cause them to be observed to the extent of
1 CONT their power. And we shall obtain nothing from any one, either
1 CONT through ourselves or through another, by which any of those
1 CONT concessions and liberties may be revoked or diminished. And if
1 CONT any such thing shall have been obtained, it shall be vain and
1 CONT invalid, and we shall never make use of it either through
1 CONT ourselves or through another. *
1 CONT
1 CONT 62. And we have fully remitted to all, and pardoned, all the
1 CONT ill-will, anger and rancour which have arisen between us and
1 CONT our subjects, clergy and laity, from the time of the struggle.
1 CONT Moreover we have fully remitted to all, clergy and laity,
1 CONT and--as far as pertains to us--have pardoned fully all the
1 CONT transgressions committed, on the occasion of that same
1 CONT struggle, from Easter of the sixteenth year of our reign until
1 CONT the re-establishment of peace. In witness of which moreover, we
1 CONT have caused to be drawn up for them letters patent of lord
1 CONT Stephen, archbishop of Canterbury, lord Henry, archbishop of
1 CONT Dublin, and the aforesaid bishops and master Pandulf, regarding
1 CONT that surety and the aforesaid concessions.
1 CONT
1 CONT 63. Wherefore we will and firmly decree that the English church
1 CONT shall be free, and that the subjects of our realm shall have
1 CONT and hold all the aforesaid liberties, rights and concessions,
1 CONT duly and in peace, freely and quietly, fully and entirely, for
1 CONT themselves and their heirs, from us and our heirs, in all
1 CONT matters and in all places, forever, as has been said. Moreover
1 CONT it has been sworn, on our part as well as on the part of the
1 CONT barons, that all these above mentioned provisions shall be
1 CONT observed with good faith and without evil intent. The witnesses
1 CONT being the above mentioned and many others. Given through our
1 CONT hand, in the plain called Runnimede between Windsor and Stanes,
1 CONT on the fifteenth day of June, in the seventeenth year of our
1 CONT reign.
1 CONT NOTE
1 CONT GEDCOM created by TMG...
0 @N6000000005598912885@ NOTE John, was the youngest son of Henry II, by Eleanor of Aquitaine.  Soo n after his birth, he most likly was given to the care of a wet nurse who would have suckled and weaned him.  In his second year he was plac ed in the care of t
1 CONC he Abbey of Fontevrault with the intention that he would devote his life thereafter to the church, but soon he showed up in the household of his older brother Henry.  He is styled as selfish , cruel, shameless, cynical, lustful, d
1 CONC ishonorable and utterly false .  He was not one of the fair-haired Platagenets.  He developed into a groad and heavy man, almost squat.  He is styled as only 'five foot six, with thick, dark red, curly hair'.  He did have a partic
1 CONC ular trai t that allowed him to make friends quickly and could be highly amusing .  He had a turn for scholarship.  Much of his education was placed i n the hands of Ranul de Glanville.  Some sources have described John a s a 'dan
1 CONC dy, who loved to bedeck himself with rubies, emeralds and sapp hires.  In about 1171, Count Humbert of Maurienne offered the hand of his daughter Alice to young Lord John and Henry II started negotiation s in pursuit of the marria
1 CONC ge, but the negotiations drug on for many mo nths.  As King, John had little wisdom and not a trace of statesmanshi p, but did have a skill for political craft and money matters.  He dev eloped finally into a complete tyrant.
1 CONT
1 CONT Being left without any particular provision he received the name of 'J ohans / Jean Sanz Terre' or 'Lackland' from his father; but later his brother, Richard I, on his accession conferred large possessions and l ands on John.  Up 
1 CONC until the time that John was about five he spent muc h of his time with and in his fathers presence.  In 1171, John's fathe r Henry accepted a proposal of marriage for John to the heiress of Hum bert of Maurienne and the contract 
1 CONC was signed at the end of 1172.  Par t of the property settlement, Loudun and Mirebeau resided in Anjou an d John's older brother Henry refused to allow the settlement of any o f his territory upon his younger brother John and soon
1 CONC  Henry revolted against his father.  Throughout the rebellion of his older brothers ag ainst his father, John remained loyal to his father.  After the settle ment of the revolt, young John was granted an income from the royal de m
1 CONC enses in England, any escheats that the King might choose to grant hi m, the castle and county of Nottingham, the castle and lordship of Mar lborough, two castles and a revenue of 1,000 lbs, Angevin in Normandy and one castle each
1 CONC  in Anjou, Touraine and Maine.  John's brother Henr y was made to promise to keep this agreement "firmly and inviolate". ( Norgate - John Lackland - ref. - cf. Gesta Henricus, Vol. I pp. 77-79) .  In a round of banquets and festiv
1 CONC ities at Limoges between 21 and 2 8 February 1173, the betrothal of John and Alice of Maurienne was fina lized.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1174, John received Nottingham and Marlborough Castles in England a s well as certain castles and rents in France.  In 1175, the great est ates of Earl Reginald of Cornwall reverted to the crown and King Henr y set them aside f
1 CONC or Henry. (Norgate - John Lackland - ref. - R. Torig ni a. 1175).  The project of setting John up as 'Marquis in Italy' wa s dead as Alice of Maurienne was dead and Humbert had remarried.  In S eptember 1176, John's father Henry a
1 CONC nd William of Gloucester arranged for young John to marry one of William's three daughters.  By the marr iage that same month, John gained the Earldom of Gloucester.  It is re ported that John and his first wife, Isabella of Glouc
1 CONC ester did not ha ve any children.  In May 1177, at Oxford, King Henry granted John sove reignty of the English dominions in Ireland.  As a result, John is inc luded in that years Pipe Roll. (Norgate - John Lackland - ref. - Eyton 
1 CONC , Itin. of Henry II, p. 210).
1 CONT
1 CONT In March of 1182, when his father went tof sea, John was left under th e guardianship of one Ranulph (de) Glanville.  In September of 1183, a fter the death of his older brother Henry, John's father King Henry ba de his son Richar
1 CONC d to cede the duch of Aquitaine to his younger brothe r John. (Norgate - John Lackland - ref. - cf. Gesta Henricus, Vol. I p p. 304-305).  Richard had no intention of allowing this, and his fathe r Henry told John that Aquitaine w
1 CONC ould be his, if he could take it.  J ohn and his brother Geoffrey marched on Poitou burning and ravishing a s they marched.  In June 1184, king Henry returned to England and gav e his son John permission to "lead an army into Rich
1 CONC ard's territories (Aquitaine) and win them for himself by force".  (Norgate - John Lackl and - ref. - cf. Gesta Henricus, Vol. I p. 311).  In preparation for t his effort he enlisted the support of his half brother Geoffrey.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1185, John's father knighted him at Windsor and sent him to govern Ireland.  The Pope sent him a golden crown adorned with peacocks to ma rk the occassion.  John hoped to get out of the unappealing task of ta ming Ireland.  Bal
1 CONC dwin IV was dying of leprosy and as a minor offshoo t of the House of Anjou, John saw it as an opportunity to avoid Irelan d and head for the middle east.  His father Henry forbade the option .  John sailed for Ireland from Milfor
1 CONC d with a fleet of sixty ships an d some 3,000 men on 24 April 1185 and landed at Waterford the next day .  Accompanying John was Ranulf de Glanville as administrator and Gera ld of Wales as recorder of events.  John, and those who
1 CONC  accompanied hi m, treated the Irish with insolence.  It is reported that John acted a s if he were a school boy, even pulling the beards of the Irish Kings and chiefs as they bent there knee before him.  He alienated the nativ e 
1 CONC Irish by his actions.  He displayed no ability as an administrator o r military leader.  On source referenced John's time in Ireland as 'hi s criminal folly'.  He did arrange to have the castles at Lismore, Tib raghny and Ardfinna
1 CONC n built to defend the area of Waterford.  John was defeated by Donell O'Brien, an Irish knight at both Ardfinnan and Tibr aghny, and some of John's troops began to desert him.  He was subseque ntly recalled by his father in Septem
1 CONC ber of 1185.  Henry subsequently appointed John de Courcy as Viceroy of Ireland. Upon his arrival at hi s fathers court, he blamed his lack of success on Hugh de Lacy. (Norga te - John Lackland - ref. - 'Four Masters', l.c.).
1 CONT
1 CONT John was sent by his father Henry II to France so as to arrane a confe rence between Henry II and Phillippe of France regarding a solution t o the issue of Alice ( Phillippes sister, then still betrothed to Rich ard).  The confere
1 CONC nce broke down and Phillippe and Henry II were soon at arms against each other.  John was given command of one of the Ange vin divisions under the command of his brother Richard.  They marched to the relief of Chateaurous and in J
1 CONC une 1187, a truce was arranged wh en the Pope called for a peaceful settlement to the issue.  Before Jun e1189, Richard demanded that his brother John accompany him on the cru sade, but their father Henry II refused the idea.  Joh
1 CONC n ultimately sid ed with his older brother Richard against his father, but by 12 June 1 189 he was with his father against Richard.  As Henry II retreated fro m the field, John was sent on a reconnaissance.  But when 20 miles ou t
1 CONC  from LeMans, Henry II decided to double back towards Anjou.  Skirtin g LeMans to the west, Henry II made for Chinon, where on 6 Jul 1189, h e collapsed and died.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Richard acceded to the reign of England, and arrived at Portsmout h on 12 August 1189, John was with Richard.  Richard made John Count o f Mortain in Normandie and granted him the castles and honours of Marl borough, Ludgersh
1 CONC all, Lancaster, Bolsover and the Peak, the town of No ttingham and the honours of Tickhill and Wallingford. (Norgate - John Lackland - ref. - Gesta Ric. p. 78).  At the time of John's marriage t o Isabelle of Gloucester, his broth
1 CONC er Richard granted John Mortaine i n Normandie, and six counties (Nottinghamshire, Derbyshire, Dorset, So merset, Devon and Cornwall) in England, as well as full responsibilit y for Ireland.  At the time that Richard ascended to t
1 CONC he throne of Eng land, Wales was again open to strife.  Richard place John in charge o f an English army and assigned him to bring order back to the area an d obtain the homage of the Welsh princes.  John persuaded Rhys ap Gruf fy
1 CONC dd to return with him to Oxford and pay homage to Richard in person .  Towards the end of 1190, John returned to England.
1 CONT
1 CONT During 1191, men with grudges against Longchamp began to turn to John .  Soon after Richard embarked for Palestine, in March of 1191 John to ok the opportunity to drive William de Longchamp from power in England .  John siezed the
1 CONC  Royal Castles of Tickhill and Nottingham.   John an d his half-brother Geoffrey joined each other at Reading.  On 5 Octobe r 1191 John issued writs for the Great Council of England to assemble .  John now began to consolidate his
1 CONC  position as the future King.  Dra wing on his hugh revenue, he showed himself liberal, affable, magnific ent and lavishly hospitalbe.  By 1192, both John and William de Longch amp began to assemble men at arms and Johns army incl
1 CONC uded many rugged Welshman.  Longchamp laid siege to Lincoln castle whose contable was G erard of Cambille, a liegeman of John.  John took the castles of Notti ngham and Tuckhill which offered no resistance.
1 CONT
1 CONT On learning of Richard's imprisonment in Germany on 11 January 1193, J ohn began to conspire with Phillippe of France to keep Richard in capt ivity and attempted unsuccessfully to seize control of England.  At on e point early in 
1 CONC Richard's captivity, John made the assertion that Ric hard was deceased.  John sailed for the continent, where he met with t he Seneschal and baron of Normandie near Alencon.  According to Roger of Howden, John told the assembled 
1 CONC nobles, "Receive me as your lord, s wear fealty to me," gut the nobles refused.  During Richard's absence , John utilized the power of the bribe to make many of his gains.  He made his way to Paris where he swore feality to Philli
1 CONC ppe for Normandi e and returned to England.  Phillippe invaded Normandie and John assem bled a number of Welshman and attempted to gain the support of the Kin g of Scotland - William 'the Lion' Stewart.  William rejected the offe 
1 CONC rs of John.  William de Coustances, Geoffrey (John's half brother) an d Hugh de Puiset raised forces in an effort to march against John and his castles.  John eventually began to help with the collection of mon ies for Richard's r
1 CONC ansom.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the summer of 1193, John threatened to invade Normandie, but wa s forestalled by the intervention of Hubert Walter and Richards offer of a six month truce.  He was however forced to relinquish his castle s at Peak in Derbys
1 CONC hire and at Windsor and Wallingford to his mother E leanor.  In about January of 1194, it is alleged that John and Phillip pe of France outbid Eleanor d'Aquitaine for the person of his older br other Richard so as to continue his 
1 CONC confinement.  Once Richard was rel eased, John viewed his return to England from Normandie and learned th at an order from the council decreeded that he should be deprived of a ll his English lands.  Hubert Walter invested Marlbor
1 CONC ough,Hugh de Puis et marched on Tuckhill and the earls of Chester (Ranulf de Blondeville ), Huntingdon (William I of Scotland) and Derby (William de Ferrers) s urrounded and invested Nottingham.  The castle surrendered on 26 Marc 
1 CONC h 1194 to Richard, who hung several of John supporters in sight of th e castle.  In February of 1194, John surrendered the whole of Normandi e east of the Seine to Phillippe in return for control of Rouen and a few castles.  Upon 
1 CONC Richard's return in 1194, John's support in Englan d quickly evaporated and he was deprived of his English lands and exco mmunicated.    But by May of 1194, the brothers were reconciled by med iation of their mother, Eleanor and t
1 CONC hey met at Bayeux.  Later in 1194 , John was placed in charge of one of Richard's armies and made a ligh ting attack on Evreux which was then held by Phillippes troops and cap tured the castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1195, Richard reinstated John in his possession of Mortain, Glouces ter and the honour of Ely.  In 1196, John captured the castle at Jumie ges and in the spring os 1197 he led a raid on Beauvais, north of Pari s, where he defea
1 CONC ted Philip of Dreux - Bishop of Beauvais.  John was r ecognized by his brother Richard as his to the realm of England in 119 7, with the support of their mother Eleanor.  John learned of his brot her Richard's death on 14 April 11
1 CONC 99 while he was at Chinon and subseq uently by the hand of Robert de Turnham took control of Richards treas ure located there on the instruction of his mother and later that day made his way to Fontevrault Abbey to pay his respect
1 CONC s.  John was soon faced with an army led by Phillippe of France who marched on Maine an d Anjou.  Arthur of Brittany and his mother Constance marched east fro m Brittany and siezed Angers.  John was forced to flee Normandie for h 
1 CONC is own safety, despite the fact that William FitzRalph and Walter de C outances - Archbishop of Rouen stood squarly behind him.  With this st renght, John Marched south against Lemans where Arthur had been since 20 April 1199.  Ph
1 CONC illippe and Arthur had already evacuated Lemans and John proceeded to destroy the castle, tear down the city walls and bur n the homes of the leading citizens.
