0 @N6000000006296781874@ NOTE John (24 December 1166 - 19 October 1216[1]), King of England </wiki/List_of_English_monarchs>, reigned from 6 April 1199 until his death. He acceded to the throne as the younger brother of King Richard I </wiki/Richard_I_of_Engla
1 CONC nd>, who died without issue. John was the youngest of five sons of King Henry II of England </wiki/Henry_II_of_England> and Eleanor, Duchess of Aquitaine </wiki/Eleanor,_Duchess_of_Aquitaine>, and was their second surviving son to
1 CONC  ascend the throne; thus, he was a Plantagenet </wiki/Plantagenet> or Angevin </wiki/Angevin> king of England. John, Earl of Cornwall and Gloucester </wiki/John,_Earl_of_Cornwall_and_Gloucester> had his peerage merged in the Crown
1 CONC  during his 1199 coronation.
1 CONT During his lifetime John acquired two epithets. One was "Lackland" (French </wiki/French_language>: Sans Terre), because, as his father's youngest son, he did not inherit land out of his family's holdings, and because as king he l
1 CONC ost English territories to France </wiki/France>. The other was "Soft-sword", for his alleged military ineptitude.[2]
1 CONT Apart from entering popular legend as the enemy of Robin Hood </wiki/Robin_Hood>, he is perhaps best-known for having acquiesced -to the barons of English nobility- to seal Magna Carta </wiki/Magna_Carta>, a document which limited
1 CONC  kingly power in England </wiki/Kingdom_of_England> and which is popularly thought as an early first step in the evolution of modern democracy </wiki/Democracy>.
1 CONT While John was his father's favourite son, as the youngest he could expect no inheritance </wiki/Inheritance>. His family life was tumultuous, as his older brothers all became involved in rebellions against Henry </wiki/Revolt_of_
1 CONC 1173-1174>. His mother, Eleanor, was imprisoned by Henry in 1173, when John was about 7.
1 CONT As a child, John was betrothed to Alais (pronounced 'Alice'), daughter and heiress of Humbert III of Savoy </wiki/Humbert_III_of_Savoy>. It was hoped that by this marriage the Angevin dynasty would extend its influence beyond the 
1 CONC Alps </wiki/Alps>, because John was promised the inheritance of Savoy </wiki/Savoy>, the Piemonte </wiki/Piedmont>, Maurienne </wiki/Maurienne>, and the other possessions of Count Humbert. King Henry promised his young son castles
1 CONC  in Normandy </wiki/Normandy> which had been previously promised to his brother Geoffrey, which was for some time a bone of contention between King Henry and his son Geoffrey. Alais made the trip over the Alps and joined Henry's c
1 CONC ourt, but she died before being married.
1 CONT Gerald of Wales </wiki/Gerald_of_Wales> relates that King Henry had a curious painting in a chamber of Winchester Castle </wiki/Winchester_Castle>, depicting an eagle being attacked by three of its chicks, while a fourth chick cro
1 CONC uched, waiting for its chance to strike. When asked the meaning of this picture, King Henry said:
1 CONT "The four young ones of the eagle are my four sons,... who will not cease persecuting me even unto death. And the youngest, whom I now embrace with such tender affection, will someday afflict me more grievously and perilously than
1 CONC  all the others."
1 CONT Before his accession, John had already acquired a reputation for treachery, having conspired ometimes with and sometimes against his elder brothers, Henry, Richard and Geoffrey. In 1184, John and Richard both claimed that they wer
1 CONC e the rightful heir to Aquitaine, one of many unfriendly encounters between the two. In 1185, John became the ruler of Ireland </wiki/John%27s_first_expedition_to_Ireland>, whose people grew to despise him, causing John to leave a
1 CONC fter only eight months.
1 CONT During Richard's absence on the Third Crusade </wiki/Third_Crusade> from 1190 to 1194, John attempted to overthrow William Longchamp </wiki/William_Longchamp>, the Bishop of Ely and Richard's designated justiciar </wiki/Chief_just
1 CONC iciar>. This was one of the events that inspired later writers to cast John as the villain in their reworking of the legend of Robin Hood </wiki/Robin_Hood>.
1 CONT John was more popular than Longchamp in London, and in October 1191 the leading citizens of the city opened the gates to him while Longchamp was confined in the tower. John promised the city the right to govern itself as a commune
1 CONC  in return for recognition as Richard's heir presumptive.[3] While returning from the Crusade, Richard was captured by Leopold V, Duke of Austria </wiki/Leopold_V,_Duke_of_Austria>, imprisoned by Henry VI, Holy Roman Emperor </wik
1 CONC i/Henry_VI,_Holy_Roman_Emperor>, and held for ransom. Meanwhile, John had joined forces with the King of France Philip Augustus </wiki/Philip_Augustus> and they sent a letter to Henry asking him to keep Richard away from England f
1 CONC or as long as possible, offering payment to keep Richard imprisoned. Henry declined their offer, and once Richard's ransom was paid by his mother Eleanor of Aquitaine </wiki/Eleanor_of_Aquitaine> (who had to pawn the Crown Jewels 
1 CONC of England </wiki/Crown_Jewels_of_England> to do so), he was set free. Upon the release, John pled forgiveness from Richard, who granted it and named him heir presumptive.
1 CONT When Richard died, John did not gain immediate universal recognition as king. Some regarded his young nephew </wiki/Cousin>, Arthur of Brittany </wiki/Arthur_I,_Duke_of_Brittany>, the son of John's late brother Geoffrey, as the ri
1 CONC ghtful heir. Arthur fought his uncle for the throne, with the support of King Philip II of France </wiki/Philip_II_of_France>. The conflict between Arthur and King John had fatal consequences. By the May 1200 Treaty of Le Goulet <
1 CONC /wiki/Treaty_of_Le_Goulet>, Philip recognised John over Arthur, and the two came to terms regarding John's vassalage for Normandy and the Angevin territories. However, the peace was ephemeral.
1 CONT The war upset the barons of Poitou </wiki/Poitou> enough for them to seek redress from the King of France, who was King John's feudal </wiki/Feudalism> overlord with respect to certain territories on the Continent. In 1202, John w
1 CONC as summoned to the French court to answer the charges one of which was his marriage to Isobel of Angouleme who was already engaged to Guy de Lusignan. John was called to Phillip's court after the Lusignans </wiki/Lusignan_dynasty>
1 CONC  pleaded for his help. John refused, and, under feudal law, because of his failure of service to his lord, the French King claimed the lands and territories ruled by King John as Count of Poitou, declaring all John's French territ
1 CONC ories except Gascony </wiki/Gascony> in the southwest forfeit. The French promptly invaded Normandy; King Philip II invested Arthur with all fiefs King John once held (except for Normandy) and betrothed him to his daughter Marie.
1 CONT Needing to supply a war across the English Channel </wiki/English_Channel>, in 1203 John ordered all shipyards (including inland places such as Gloucester </wiki/Gloucester>) in England to provide at least one ship, with places su
1 CONC ch as the newly-built Portsmouth </wiki/Portsmouth_Naval_Base> being responsible for several. He made Portsmouth the new home of the navy. (The Anglo-Saxon kings </wiki/Anglo-Saxon_monarchs>, such as Edward the Confessor </wiki/Ed
1 CONC ward_the_Confessor>, had royal harbours constructed on the south coast at Sandwich </wiki/Sandwich,_Kent>, and most importantly, Hastings.) By the end of 1204, he had 45 large galleys </wiki/Galley> available to him, and from then
1 CONC  on an average of four new ones every year. He also created an Admiralty </wiki/Admiralty> of four admirals, responsible for various parts of the new navy. During John's reign, major improvements were made in ship design, includin
1 CONC g the addition of sails and removable forecastles </wiki/Forecastle>. He also created the first big transport ships, called buisses. John is sometimes credited with the founding of the modern Royal Navy </wiki/History_of_the_Royal
1 CONC _Navy>. What is known about this navy comes from the Pipe Rolls </wiki/Pipe_Rolls>, since these achievements are ignored by the chroniclers and early historians.
1 CONT In the hope of avoiding trouble in England and Wales while he was away fighting to recover his French </wiki/France> lands, in 1205, John formed an alliance by marrying off his illegitimate daughter, Joan </wiki/Joan,_Lady_of_Wale
1 CONC s>, to the Welsh </wiki/Wales> prince Llywelyn the Great </wiki/Llywelyn_the_Great>.
1 CONT As part of the war, Arthur attempted to kidnap </wiki/Kidnap> his own grandmother, Eleanor of Aquitaine, at Mirebeau, but was defeated and captured by John's forces. Arthur was imprisoned first at Falaise and then at Rouen. No one
1 CONC  is certain what happened to Arthur after that. According to the annals of Margam Abbey </wiki/Margam_Abbey>, Wales, where John would lodge on his trips between England and Ireland: On 3 April 1203
1 CONT "After King John had captured Arthur and kept him alive in prison for some time in the castle of Rouen... when John was drunk he slew Arthur with his own hand and tying a heavy stone to the body cast it into the Seine </wiki/Seine
1 CONC >."[citation needed </wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed>]
1 CONT However, Hubert de Burgh </wiki/Hubert_de_Burgh>, the officer commanding the Rouen fortress, claimed to have delivered Arthur around Easter </wiki/Easter> 1203 to agents of the King sent to castrate </wiki/Castration> him and that
1 CONC  Arthur had died of shock </wiki/Shock_(medical)>. Hubert later retracted his statement and claimed Arthur still lived, but no one saw Arthur alive again, and the supposition that he was murdered caused Brittany </wiki/Brittany>, 
1 CONC and later Normandy, to rebel against King John.
1 CONT Besides Arthur, John also captured his niece, Eleanor, Fair Maid of Brittany </wiki/Eleanor,_Fair_Maid_of_Brittany>. Eleanor remained a prisoner the rest of her life (which ended in 1241); through deeds such as these, John acquire
1 CONC d a reputation for ruthlessness.
1 CONT In 1203, John exempted the citizens and merchants of Bordeaux </wiki/Bordeaux> from the Grande Coutume </wiki/Grande_Coutume>, which was the principal tax on their exports. In exchange, the regions of Bordeaux, Bayonne </wiki/Bayo
1 CONC nne> and Dax </wiki/Dax> pledged support against the French Crown. The unblocked ports gave Gascon merchants open access to the English wine market for the first time. The following year, John granted the same exemptions to La Roc
1 CONC helle </wiki/La_Rochelle> and Poitou </wiki/Poitou>.[4]
1 CONT When Archbishop of Canterbury </wiki/Archbishop_of_Canterbury> Hubert Walter </wiki/Hubert_Walter> died on 13 July </wiki/July_13> 1205 </wiki/1205>, John became involved in a dispute with Pope Innocent III </wiki/Pope_Innocent_II
1 CONC I>. The Canterbury Cathedral </wiki/Canterbury_Cathedral> chapter claimed the sole right to elect Hubert's successor and favoured Reginald, a candidate out of their midst. However, both the English bishops and the king had an inte
1 CONC rest in the choice of successor to this powerful office. The king wanted John de Gray </wiki/John_de_Gray>, one of his own men, so he could influence the church more.[5] When their dispute could not be settled, the Chapter secretl
1 CONC y elected one of their members as Archbishop. A second election imposed by John resulted in another nominee. When they both appeared in Rome </wiki/Rome>, Innocent disavowed both elections, and his candidate, Stephen Langton </wik
1 CONC i/Stephen_Langton>, was elected over the objections of John's observers. John was supported in his position by the English barons </wiki/Baron> and many of the English bishops and refused to accept Langton.
1 CONT John expelled the Chapter in July 1207, to which the Pope reacted by imposing the interdict </wiki/Interdict_(Roman_Catholic_Church)> on the kingdom. John immediately retaliated by closing down the churches. Although he issued ins
1 CONC tructions for the confiscation of all church possessions, individual institutions were able to negotiate terms for managing their own properties and keeping the produce of their estates.[6] After his excommunication John tightened
1 CONC  these measures and he accrued significant sums from the income of vacant sees and abbeys: for example, it was calculated that the church lost 100,000 marks </wiki/Mark_(money)> to the Crown </wiki/The_Crown> in 1213.[7] The Pope,
1 CONC  realising that too long a period without church services could lead to loss of faith, gave permission for some churches to hold Mass </wiki/Mass_(liturgy)> behind closed doors in 1209. In 1212, they allowed last rites </wiki/Anoi
1 CONC nting_of_the_Sick> to the dying. While the interdict was a burden to many, it did not result in rebellion against John.
1 CONT In November 1209 John was excommunicated </wiki/Excommunication>, and in February 1213, Innocent threatened stronger measures unless John submitted. The papal terms for submission were accepted in the presence of the papal legate 
1 CONC </wiki/Papal_legate> Pandulph </wiki/Pandulph> in May 1213 (according to Matthew Paris </wiki/Matthew_Paris>, at the Templar Church </wiki/Knights_Templar_in_England> at Dover </wiki/Dover>);[8] in addition, John offered to surren
1 CONC der the Kingdom of England to God </wiki/God> and the Saints Peter </wiki/Saint_Peter> and Paul </wiki/Paul_of_Tarsus> for a feudal service of 1,000 marks annually, 700 for England and 300 for Ireland.[7] With this submission, for
1 CONC malised in the Bulla Aurea (Golden Bull </wiki/Papal_Bull>), John gained the valuable support of his papal overlord in his new dispute with the English barons.
1 CONT Coming to terms with Llywelyn I, Prince of Gwynedd </wiki/Llywelyn_the_Great>, following the Welsh Uprising of 1211 </wiki/Gwynedd_in_the_High_Middle_Ages> and settling his dispute with the papacy, John turned his attentions back 
1 CONC to his overseas interests. The European wars culminated in defeat at the Battle of Bouvines </wiki/Battle_of_Bouvines> (1214), which forced the king to accept an unfavourable peace with France.
1 CONT This finally turned the barons against him (some had already rebelled against him after he was excommunicated), and he met their leaders along with their French and Scots allies at Runnymede </wiki/Runnymede>, near London </wiki/L
1 CONC ondon> on 15 June </wiki/June_15> 1215 </wiki/1215> to seal the Great Charter </wiki/Magna_Carta>, called in Latin </wiki/Latin_(language)> Magna Carta. Because he had sealed under duress, however, John received approval from his 
1 CONC overlord the Pope to break his word as soon as hostilities had ceased, provoking the First Barons' War </wiki/First_Barons%27_War> and an invited French invasion by Prince Louis </wiki/Louis_VIII_of_France> of France (whom the maj
1 CONC ority of the English barons had invited to replace John on the throne). John travelled around the country to oppose the rebel forces, including a personal two-month siege of the rebel-held Rochester Castle </wiki/Rochester_Castle>
1 CONC .
1 CONT Retreating from the French invasion, John took a safe route around the marshy area of the Wash </wiki/The_Wash> to avoid the rebel held area of East Anglia </wiki/East_Anglia>. His slow baggage train (including the Crown Jewels </
1 CONC wiki/Crown_Jewels_of_the_United_Kingdom>), however, took a direct route across it and was lost to the unexpected incoming tide. This dealt John a terrible blow, which affected his health and state of mind. Succumbing to dysentery 
1 CONC </wiki/Dysentery> and moving from place to place, he stayed one night at Sleaford Castle </wiki/Sleaford_Castle> before dying on 18 October </wiki/October_18> (or possibly 19 October </wiki/October_19>) 1216, at Newark Castle </wi
1 CONC ki/Newark_Castle,_Nottinghamshire> (then in Lincolnshire </wiki/Lincolnshire>, now on Nottinghamshire </wiki/Nottinghamshire>'s border with that county). Numerous, possibly fictitious, accounts circulated soon after his death that
1 CONC  he had been killed by poisoned ale, poisoned plums or a "surfeit of peaches".[9][10]
1 CONT He was buried in Worcester Cathedral </wiki/Worcester_Cathedral> in the city of Worcester </wiki/Worcester,_England>.
1 CONT His nine-year-old son succeeded him and became King Henry III of England </wiki/Henry_III_of_England> (1216-72), and although Louis continued to claim the English throne, the barons switched their allegiance to the new king, forci
1 CONC ng Louis to give up his claim and sign the Treaty of Lambeth </wiki/Treaty_of_Lambeth> in 1217.
1 CONT King John's reign has been traditionally characterised as one of the most disastrous in English history, earning him the nickname "Bad King John": it began with defeats-he lost Normandy </wiki/Normandy> to Philip Augustus </wiki/P
1 CONC hilip_II_of_France> of France in his first five years on the throne-and ended with England torn by civil war </wiki/Civil_war> and himself on the verge of being forced out of power. In 1213, he made England a papal </wiki/Papacy> 
1 CONC fief </wiki/Fief> to resolve a conflict with the Roman Catholic Church </wiki/Roman_Catholic_Church>, and his rebellious barons forced him to seal Magna Carta </wiki/Magna_Carta> in 1215, the act for which he is best remembered.
1 CONT As far as the administration of his kingdom went, John functioned as an efficient ruler, but he lost approval of the English barons by taxing them in ways that were outside those traditionally allowed by feudal overlords. The tax 
1 CONC known as scutage </wiki/Scutage>, payment made instead of providing knights (as required by feudal law), became particularly unpopular. John was a very fair-minded and well informed king, however, often acting as a judge in the Ro
1 CONC yal Courts, and his justice was much sought after. Also, John's employment of an able Chancellor and certain clerks resulted in the first proper set of records-the Pipe Rolls </wiki/Pipe_Rolls>. Tudor historiography was particular
1 CONC ly interested in him, for his independence from the papacy (or lack of it) - this atmosphere produced not only Shakespeare's own King John </wiki/The_Life_and_Death_of_King_John> but also its model The Troublesome Reign of King Jo
1 CONC hn </wiki/The_Troublesome_Reign_of_King_John> and John Bale's Kynge Johan </wiki/John_Bale>.
1 CONT Winston Churchill </wiki/Winston_Churchill> summarised the legacy of John's reign: "When the long tally is added, it will be seen that the British nation and the English-speaking world owe far more to the vices of John than to the
1 CONC  labours of virtuous sovereigns".[11] Medieval historian C. Warren Hollister called John an "enigmatic figure":
1 CONT ...talented in some respects, good at administrative detail, but suspicious, unscrupulous, and mistrusted. He was compared in a recent scholarly article, perhaps unfairly, with Richard Nixon </wiki/Richard_Nixon>. His crisis-prone
1 CONC  career was sabotaged repeatedly by the halfheartedness with which his vassals supported him-and the energy with which some of them opposed him.
1 CONT In 2006, he was selected by the BBC History Magazine </wiki/BBC_History_(magazine)> as the 13th century's worst Briton </wiki/Worst_Britons_(BBC_History_poll)>.[12]
0 @N6000000003006365397@ NOTE [F560.ftw]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruled 1189-1216
