0 @N6000000005076803159@ NOTE Umberto II, Conte di Savoia is the son of Amadeo II, Conte di Savoia.1 He married Gisela de Bourgogne, daughter of Guillaume I, Comte de Bourgogne, circa 1090.2 He died on 18 September 1103.
1 CONT      Umberto II, Conte di Savoia also went by the nick-name of Umberto 'the Fat' (?).1 He gained the title of Comte de Maurienne. He succeeded to the title of Conte di Savoia in 1078.1
1 CONT Child of Umberto II, Conte di Savoia and Gisela de Bourgogne
1 CONT Adelaide di Savoia+ d. 18 Nov 11543
1 CONT Child of Umberto II, Conte di Savoia
1 CONT Amadeo III, Conte di Savoia+ b. c 1092, d. 30 Aug 11481
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S38] John Morby, Dynasties of the World: a chronological and genealogical handbook (Oxford, Oxfordshire, U.K.: Oxford University Press, 1989), page 110. Hereinafter cited as Dynasties of the World.
1 CONT [S45] Marcellus Donald R. von Redlich, Pedigrees of Some of the Emperor Charlemagne's Descendants, volume I (1941; reprint, Baltimore, Maryland, U.S.A.: Genealogical Publishing Company, 2002), page 64. Hereinafter cited as Pedigre
1 CONC es of Emperor Charlemagne, I.
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 52. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
0 @N6000000005598888052@ NOTE Humbert is also styled as 'Umberto ', as 'the fat' de Maurienne and a s Umberto II "il Rinforzato" of Savoy, Aosta and Moriana.  At least on e source indicates that Humbert's mother may have been 'Matilda of Vie nne'. Humbert II, 
1 CONC was Count of Savoy from 1080 until his death in 1103 .  He was married to Gisela of Burgundy, daughter of William I, Counto f Burgundy and Stephany de Longwy, and had 4 children: Amedeo; William , Bishop of Liège; Adelaide, (d. 11
1 CONC 54), married to Louis VI of France ; Agnes, (d. 1127), married to Arcimboldo VI (Archibald VI in English? ) of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time of his fathers death, Humbert's sister Adelaide succeededt o the throne, but after her death, the supporters of Humbert claimedth e throne for him in 1198.  At an early age he took the crusading vow, but affairs at hom
1 CONC e did not permit him to fulfill it.  He devoted muc h of his time to the territorial aggrandizement of this house.  Befor e he died he had extended his control over one episcopal see and adde d one county to the dominions of Savoy
1 CONC .  Because of his military powe r he was styled 'le Renforce', meaning the Strong.  Tradition relatest hat despite his forceful policies, he remained on good terms with the church.
