1 CONT
1 CONT !Second Earl of Lincoln.  He is called the second Earl, although the title was never attribut ed to him in any charter, by reason that he died before his mother, through whom the dignit y came.  Dugdale states that he married, in 
1 CONC 1247, "an outlandish lady from the parts of Savoy , brought over purposely for him by Peter de Savoy, uncle to the queen, which occasioned muc h discontent amongst the nobles of England." This lady was Alice, daughter of the Marqu
1 CONC ess o f Saluces, in Italy, and a cousin of the queen. [Magna Charta Barons, p. 102-3]
0 @N6000000000337150888@ NOTE (Research):Pedro III (Peter III) Peter the Great, 1239-1285, king of Arag≡n and count of Barcelona (1276-85) and king of Sicily (1282-85): son and successor of Jaime (James) I.  In 1280 he established Aragonese influence on the no
1 CONC rthern shores of Africa. From his marriage (1262) to Constance daughter and heir of Manfred of Sicily,were derived the claims of the house of Arag≡n to Sicily and S Italy.  After the insurrection of the Sicilian Vespers against Ch
1 CONC arles I of Anjou, Peter was offered the crown of Sicily and took possesion of the island (1282). Pope Martin IV excommunicated him and declared him deprived of his states on the basis of Peter II's declaration of vassalage to the 
1 CONC Holy See. A crusade against Arag≡n was organized by the Pope and the French, who invaded Catalonia but were repulsed by Peter and defeated at sea by Roger of Loria. Peter's Sicilian ventyre was unpopular with the Aragonese nobilit
1 CONC y and towns, and he was compelled to grant them with wide privileges to quell their opposition. He founded the first university in Arag≡n at Huesca. Peter was succeeded in Arag≡n by his son Alfonso III, and in Sicily by his second
1 CONC  son Jaime (James), (later James II of Arag≡n.
1 CONT
1 CONT Peter III CPedro III "The Great" ) Encyclopædia Britannica Article   born 1239 died Nov. 11, 1285, Villafranca del Panades, Catalonia   byname Peter The Great,  Spanish Pedro El Grande king of Aragon from July 1276, on the death o
1 CONC f his father, James I, and king of Sicily (as Peter I) from 1282.   In 1262 he had married Constance, heiress of Manfred, the Hohenstaufen king of Sicily; and after the revolt of the Sicilians in 1282 he invaded the island and was
1 CONC  proclaimed king at Palermo, despite strong Guelph and papal opposition (see Sicilian Vespers). His Sicilian enterprise was unpopular in Aragon, where an association of nobles and some municipalities, the Unión Aragonesa, forced h
1 CONC im to grant a privilege not only confirming the Aragonese fueros (legal rights) but diminishing some of the crown's rights. In 1285 Philip III of France invaded Aragon to dethrone Peter but was disastrously defeated. Peter, howeve
1 CONC r, soon died. His great stature and physical strength were famous. Among his children were Alfonso III of Aragon, James I of Sicily (II of Aragon), and Frederick III of Sicily.
