0 @N6000000000437174829@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> Iv, Of France "The Fair"
0 @N6000000003051295730@ NOTE [from Wikipedia]
1 CONT Philip IV the Fair (French: Philippe IV le Bel) (1268 - November 29, 1314) was King of France from 1285 until his death in 1314.
1 CONT
1 CONT Life
1 CONT A member of the Capetian dynasty, Philip was born at the Palace of Fontainebleau at Seine-et-Marne, the son of King Philip III and Isabella of Aragon. Philip was nicknamed the Fair (le Bel) because of his handsome appearance.
1 CONT Philippe IV, recumbent statue on his tomb, Royal Necropolis, Saint Denis Basilica
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Philippe IV, recumbent statue on his tomb, Royal Necropolis, Saint Denis Basilica
1 CONT
1 CONT As prince, just before his father's death, he negotiated the safe passage of the royal family out of Aragon after the unsuccessful Aragonese Crusade. As a king, Philip was determined to strengthen the monarchy at any cost. He reli
1 CONC ed, more than any of his predecessors, on a professional bureaucracy of legalists. Because to the public he kept aloof and left specific policies, especially unpopular ones, to his ministers, he was called a "useless owl" by his c
1 CONC ontemporaries. His reign marks the French transition from a charismatic monarchy ? which could all but collapse in an incompetent reign ? to a bureaucratic kingdom, a move towards modernity.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip married queen Jeanne of Navarre (1271?1305) on August 16, 1284. The primary administrative benefit of this was the inheritance of Jeanne in Champagne and Brie, which were adjacent to the royal demesne in Ile-de-France and b
1 CONC ecame thus effectively united to the king's own lands, forming an expansive area. During the reigns of Jeanne herself, and her three sons (1284-1329), these lands belonged to the person of the king; but by 1329 they had become so 
1 CONC entrenched in the royal domain that king Philip VI of France (who was not an heir of Jeanne) switched lands with the then rightful heiress, Joan II of Navarre, with the effect that Champagne and Brie remained part of the royal dem
1 CONC esne and Jeannne received compensation with lands in western Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Kingdom of Navarre in the Pyrenees was not so important to contemporary interests of the French crown. It remained in personal union 1284-1329, after which it went its separate way.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip arrested Jews so he could seize their assets to accommodate the inflated costs of modern warfare; he was condemned by his enemies in the Catholic Church for his spendthrift lifestyle. When he also levied taxes on the French
1 CONC  clergy of one half their annual income, he caused an uproar within the Roman Catholic Church and the papacy, prompting Pope Boniface VIII to issue the Bull "Clericis Laicos" forbidding the transferance of any church property to t
1 CONC he French Crown. Still, Philip emerged victorious when the French archbishop Bertrand de Goth was elected pope as Clement V and the official seat of the papacy moved to Avignon, an enclave surrounded by French territories.
1 CONT
1 CONT He suffered a major embarrassment when an army of 2,500 noble men-at-arms (Knights and Squires) and 4,000 infantry he sent to suppress an uprising in Flanders was defeated in the Battle of the Golden Spurs near Kortrijk on 11 July
1 CONC  1302. Philip reacted with energy to the humiliation and personally defeated the Flemings at Mons-en-Pévèle two years later. Finally, in 1305, Philip forced the Flemish to accept a harsh peace treaty that exacted heavy reparations
1 CONC  and humiliating penalties.
1 CONT
1 CONT On October 13, 1307, hundreds of Knights Templar in France were simultaneously arrested by agents of Philip the Fair, to be later tortured into admitting heresy in the Order. The Knights Templar were a 200-year-old military order,
1 CONC  supposedly answerable only to the Pope. But Philip used his influence over Clement V, who was largely his pawn, to disband the order and remove its ecclesiastical status and protection in order to plunder it.
1 CONT
1 CONT What became of the Templar treasures in France has long been a mystery that has led to many theories and speculations. There are a number of stories regarding Templars who escaped from Philip's agents, such as the tale that a numb
1 CONC er of ships sailed from France to Scotland possibly containing some of the Templar treasure, and that some of the Knights who sailed to Scotland later fought in the Battle of Bannockburn with Robert the Bruce when the Scots gained
1 CONC  their independence from England.
1 CONT
1 CONT A modern historical view is that Philip, who seized the considerable Templar treasury and broke up the Templar monastic banking system, simply sought to control it for himself. In 1314, he had the last Grand Master of the Templars
1 CONC , Jacques de Molay, burnt at the stake in Paris. According to legend, de Molay cursed both Philip and Clement V from the flames, saying that he would summon them before God's Tribunal within a year; as it turned out, both King and
1 CONC  Pope died within the next year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip IV's rule signaled the decline of the papacy's power from its near complete authority. He died during a hunt and is buried in Saint Denis Basilica. He was succeeded by his son Louis X.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children
1 CONT The children of Philippe IV and Jeanne of Navarre were:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1. Marguerite (1288-1300)
1 CONT 2. Louis X - (October 4, 1289 - June 5, 1316)
1 CONT 3. Philippe V - (1291 - January 3, 1322)
1 CONT 4. Isabelle - (c. 1292 - August 23, 1358)
1 CONT 5. Charles IV - (1294 - February 1, 1328)
1 CONT 6. Robert (1297-1308)
1 CONT
1 CONT All three of his sons reaching adulthood would become kings of France, and his daughter, as consort of Edward II, was queen of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT * Goyau, Georges. "Philip IV (the Fair)." The Catholic Encyclopedia, Volume XII. 1911. [1]
1 CONT * Knights Templar History and Mythology [2]
0 @N6000000002187755488@ NOTE Philip IV of France
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip IV the Fair (French: Philippe IV le Bel) (1268 – November 29, 1314) was King of France from 1285 until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT A member of the Capetian dynasty, Philip was born at the Palace of Fontainebleau at Seine-et-Marne, the son of King Philip III and Isabella of Aragon. Philip was nicknamed the Fair (le Bel) because of his handsome appearance.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a king, Philip was determined to strengthen the monarchy at any cost. He relied, more than any of his predecessors, on a professional bureaucracy of legalists. His reign marks the French transition from a charismatic monarchy–w
1 CONC hich could all but collapse in an incompetent reign–to a bureaucratic kingdom, a move towards modernity.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip married queen Jeanne of Navarre (1271–1305) on August 16, 1284. The primary administrative benefit of this was the inheritance of Jeanne in Champagne and Brie, which were adjacent to the royal demesne in Ile-de-France and b
1 CONC ecame thus effectively united to the king's own lands, forming an expansive area. During the reigns of Jeanne herself, and her three sons (1284-1329), these lands belonged to the person of the king; but by 1329 they had become so 
1 CONC entrenched in the royal domain that king Philip VI of France (who was not an heir of Jeanne) switched lands with the then rightful heiress, Joan II of Navarre, with the effect that Champagne and Brie remained part of the royal dem
1 CONC esne and Jeannne received compensation with lands in western Normandy.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Kingdom of Navarre in the Pyrenees was not so important to contemporary interests of the French crown. It remained in personal union 1284-1329, after which it went its separate way.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip arrested Jews so he could seize their assets to accommodate the inflated costs of modern warfare; he was condemned by his enemies in the Catholic Church for his spendthrift lifestyle. When he also levied taxes on the French
1 CONC  clergy of one half their annual income, he caused an uproar within the Roman Catholic Church and the papacy. Still, Philip emerged victorious when the French archbishop Bertrand de Goth was elected pope as Clement V and the offic
1 CONC ial seat of the papacy moved to Avignon, an enclave surrounded by French territories.
1 CONT
1 CONT He suffered a major embarrassment when an army of 2,500 noble men-at-arms (Knights and Squires) and 4,000 infantry he sent to suppress an uprising in Flanders was defeated in the Battle of the Golden Spurs near Kortrijk on 11 July
1 CONC  1302. Philip reacted with energy to the humiliation and personally defeated the Flemings at Mons-en-Pévèle two years later. Finally, in 1305, Philip forced the Flemish to accept a harsh peace treaty that exacted heavy reparations
1 CONC  and humiliating penalties.
1 CONT
1 CONT French Monarchy-
1 CONT Capetian Dynasty
1 CONT (direct Capetians branch)  
1 CONT
1 CONT Hugh Capet 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Robert II 
1 CONT Robert II 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Henry I 
1 CONT    Robert I, Duke of Burgundy 
1 CONT Henry I 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Philip I 
1 CONT    Hugh, Count of Vermandois 
1 CONT Philip I 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Louis VI 
1 CONT Louis VI 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Louis VII 
1 CONT    Robert I of Dreux 
1 CONT Louis VII 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Mary, Countess of Champagne 
1 CONT    Alix 
1 CONT    Marguerite 
1 CONT    Alys, Countess of the Vexin 
1 CONT    Philip II 
1 CONT    Agnes of France 
1 CONT Philip II
1 CONT (Philip Augustus) 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Louis VIII 
1 CONT Louis VIII 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Louis IX 
1 CONT    Robert I, Count of Artois 
1 CONT    Alphonse, Count of Poitou and Toulouse 
1 CONT    Isabel of France 
1 CONT    Charles I of Anjou and Sicily 
1 CONT Louis IX 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Phillip III 
1 CONT    Robert, Count of Clermont 
1 CONT    Agnes of France 
1 CONT Philip III 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Philip IV 
1 CONT    Charles III, Count of Valois 
1 CONT    Louis d'Evreux 
1 CONT    Margaret of France 
1 CONT Philip IV 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Louis X 
1 CONT    Philip V 
1 CONT    Isabella of France 
1 CONT    Charles IV 
1 CONT Louis X 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    Joan II of Navarre 
1 CONT    John I 
1 CONT John I 
1 CONT Philip V 
1 CONT Charles IV 
1 CONT
1 CONT On October 13, 1307, what may have been all the Knights Templar in France were simultaneously arrested by agents of Philip the Fair, to be later tortured into admitting heresy in the Order. A modern historical view is that Philip,
1 CONC  who seized the considerable Templar treasury and broke up the Templar monastic banking system, simply sought to control it for himself.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Knights Templar were a military order answerable only to the pope. But Philip used his influence over Clement V, who was largely his pawn, to disband the order and remove its ecclesiastical status and protection in order to pl
1 CONC under it.
1 CONT
1 CONT What became of the Templar treasures in France has long been a mystery that has led to many theories and speculations. There are a number of stories (legends?) regarding Templars who escaped from Philip's agents, such as the tale 
1 CONC that a number of ships sailed from France to Scotland possibly containing some of the Templar treasure, and that some of the Knights who sailed to Scotland later fought in the Battle of Bannockburn with Robert the Bruce when the S
1 CONC cots gained their independence from England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip tried and tortured a number of the Templars that he had captured, and in 1314 he had Jacques de Molay, the Templar Grand Master, and Geoffrey de Charney, the Preceptor of Normandy, burned at the stake. It is said that de Mo
1 CONC lay cursed both Philip and Clement V from the flames; both king and pope died within the next year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip IV's rule signaled the decline of the papacy's power from its near complete authority. He died during a hunt and is buried in Saint Denis Basilica.
1 CONT
1 CONT Image:King Philip the Fair in War dress on the Occasion of his entering Paris in 1304 after having conquered the Communes of Flanders Equestrian Statue placed in Notre Dame Paris and destroyed in 1772.png 
1 CONT King Philip le Bel in War-dress, on the Occasion of his entering Paris in 1304, after having conquered the Communes of Flanders.--Equestrian Statue placed in Notre Dame, Paris, and destroyed in 1772.--Facsimile of a Woodcut from T
1 CONC hevet's "Cosmographie Universelle," 1575.Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Children 
