0 @N6000000000708930576@ NOTE Wikipedia article: Joan of Acre
1 CONT
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&diff=220468880&oldid=199675777
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan of Acre (April 1272 - April 7 </wiki/April_7>, 1307 </wiki/1307>) was a daughter of King Edward I of England </wiki/Edward_I_of_England> and his first wife, Eleanor of Castile (1241-1290) </wiki/Eleanor_of_Castile>.
1 CONT Contents [hide <javascript:toggleToc()>]  1 Born on the Ninth Crusade  2 French Childhood  3 Marriage & Issue  4 Secret 2nd Marriage  5 Issue  6 Death in Childbirth  7 Fiction  8 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=1>] Born on the Ninth Crusade
1 CONT Joan got her name from her birthplace, Acre </wiki/Akko>, in Kingdom of Acre </wiki/Kingdom_of_Acre>. It differentiates her from an earlier Joan born to the couple, who died in infancy. Joan of Acre was born while her Royal parent
1 CONC s were traveling to the Middle East </wiki/Middle_East> on the Ninth Crusade </wiki/Ninth_Crusade>.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=2>] French Childhood
1 CONT At least part of her childhood she spent in France with her maternal grandmother, Jeanne de Dammartin, Countess of Ponthieu </wiki/Jeanne_de_Dammartin%2C_Countess_of_Ponthieu>. She was betrothed as a child to Hartman, son of King 
1 CONC Rudolph I of Germany </wiki/Rudolph_I_of_Germany>, but he died in 1282 after drowning in the Rhine </wiki/Rhine>.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=3>] Marriage & Issue
1 CONT On 30 April </wiki/April_30> 1290 </wiki/1290>, at Westminster Abbey </wiki/Westminster_Abbey>, Joan married Gilbert de Clare, 7th Earl of Hertford </wiki/Gilbert_de_Clare%2C_7th_Earl_of_Hertford>. He was nearly thirty years her s
1 CONC enior. Their four children were:
1 CONT Gilbert de Clare, 8th Earl of Hertford </wiki/Gilbert_de_Clare%2C_8th_Earl_of_Hertford>
1 CONT Eleanor de Clare </wiki/Eleanor_de_Clare>
1 CONT Margaret de Clare </wiki/Margaret_de_Clare>
1 CONT Elizabeth de Clare </wiki/Elizabeth_de_Clare>
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=4>] Secret 2nd Marriage
1 CONT Following her husband's death in 1295, Joan clandestinely married Ralph de Monthermer, 1st Baron Monthermer </wiki/Ralph_de_Monthermer%2C_1st_Baron_Monthermer>, a knight in her household, in January 1297. Her father, King Edward I
1 CONC  </wiki/Edward_I_of_England>, was enraged by this lowly second marriage, especially since he was arranging a marriage for her to Amadeus V, Count of Savoy </wiki/Amadeus_V%2C_Count_of_Savoy>.
1 CONT He had Monthermer thrown in prison, and Joan had to plead for the release of her husband. According to the St. Albans chronicler, she told her father, "No one sees anything wrong if a great Earl marries a poor and lowly woman. Why
1 CONC  should there be anything wrong if a countess marries a young and promising man?" At last her father relented, released Monthermer from prison in August 1297, and allowed him to hold the title of Earl of Gloucester </wiki/Earl_of_
1 CONC Gloucester> and Earl of Hereford </wiki/Earl_of_Hereford> during Joan's lifetime.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=5>] Issue
1 CONT Monthermer and Joan had four children:
1 CONT Mary de Monthermer </w/index.php?title=Mary_de_Monthermer&action=edit&redlink=1>, born October 1297. In 1306 her grandfather King Edward I arranged for her to wed Duncan Macduff, 8th Earl of Fife </wiki/Duncan_Macduff%2C_8th_Earl_
1 CONC of_Fife>.
1 CONT Joan de Monthermer </w/index.php?title=Joan_de_Monthermer&action=edit&redlink=1>, born 1299, became a nun at Amesbury </wiki/Amesbury>.
1 CONT Thomas de Monthermer, 2nd Baron Monthermer </w/index.php?title=Thomas_de_Monthermer%2C_2nd_Baron_Monthermer&action=edit&redlink=1>, born 1301.
1 CONT Edward de Monthermer </w/index.php?title=Edward_de_Monthermer&action=edit&redlink=1>, born 1304 and died 1339. He fought in the Scottish campaign in 1335 and spent much of his life in service to his half-sister Elizabeth </wiki/El
1 CONC izabeth_de_Clare>, who provided for him during his last illness and buried him next to their mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=6>] Death in Childbirth
1 CONT Joan died in childbirth on 7 April 1307 at the manor of Clare in Suffolk </wiki/Suffolk>, England </wiki/England>, a Clare family possession, and was buried with her stillborn child, 23 April </wiki/April_23> 1307 </wiki/1307>, at
1 CONC  the Augustinian </wiki/Augustinian> priory there. Miracles were said to occur at her grave, especially the healing of toothache, back pain, and fever. A fifteenth-century English chronicle reports that when her tomb was opened a 
1 CONC century and more after her death, her body was found incorrupt, which was seen in the medieval period as a strong indication of sanctity. So far as is known, however, no process for her sanctification was ever undertaken.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=7>] Fiction
1 CONT Joan of Acre makes an appearance in Virginia Henley's latest historical romance, entitled "Infamous." In the book, Joan, known as Joanna, is described as a promiscuous young princess, vain, shallow and spoiled to the core. Her one
1 CONC  constant friend and loyal servant is Marjorie de Warrenne. Princess Joanna is then contracted to marry the Earl of Gloucester, who is 30 plus years her senior. There are rumours that her first child with the Earl was fathered by 
1 CONC Henry de Bohun.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=8>] Sources
1 CONT Costain, Thomas. A History of the Plantagenets, Vol III </w/index.php?title=A_History_of_the_Plantagenets%2C_Vol_III&action=edit&redlink=1>.
1 CONT Underhill, Frances A. For Her Good Estate, 1999.
1 CONT Retrieved from "<http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_of_Acre>"
0 @N6000000000337389254@ NOTE Source #1:  Frederick Lewis Weis, "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700" - Seventh Edition, with additions and corrections by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., assisted by Davis Faris (Baltimore, M
1 CONC D:  Genealogical Publishing Co, 1995), p. 102
1 CONT
1 CONT Married 30 APR 1290, Westminster Abbey, London, England to de Clare, Gilbert "the Red", Earl Gloucester 3rd
1 CONT
1 CONT Child 1: de Clare, Gilbert, Earl of Gloucester, b. 10 MAY 1291
1 CONT Child 2: de Clare, Eleanor, b. OCT 1292
1 CONT Child 3: de Clare, Margaret, b. OCT 1292
1 CONT Child 4: de Clare, Elizabeth, b. 16 SEP 1295
1 CONT
1 CONT Countess of Gloucester and Hertford. Her father had arranged for her to be
1 CONT married to Amadeus of Savoy, but she had already secretly married to Ralph,
1 CONT a member of the Kings household.
1 CONT
1 CONT Married JAN 1297 to de Monthermer, Ralph, Baron, Earl of Hertford
1 CONT
1 CONT Child 5: de Monthermer, Mary, b. 1298
1 CONT Child 6: de Monthermer, Thomas, Baron Monthermer 2nd, b. 1301
1 CONT Child 7: de Monthermer, Edward, Baron Monthermer 3rd, b. 1304
1 CONT Child 8: de Monthermer, Joan, Nun at Amesbury
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2:  Marcellus Donald Alexander R. von Redlich, "Pedigrees of Some of the Emperor Charlemagne's Descendants" (Baltimore:  Genealogical Publishing Co, Inc, 1988 reprint of 1941 edition), pp. 257-258; p. 126
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #3: Gary Boyd Roberts, "The Royal Descents of 500 Immigrants to the American Colonies or the United States, Who Were Themselves Notable or Left Descendants Notable in American History" (Baltimore:  Genealogical Publishing C
1 CONC o, Inc., 2001) p. 277.
0 @N6000000000437728020@ NOTE REFERENCE: 2404
0 @N6000000000664510493@ NOTE Wikipedia article: Joan of Acre
1 CONT
1 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&diff=220468880&oldid=199675777
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan of Acre (April 1272 - April 7 </wiki/April_7>, 1307 </wiki/1307>) was a daughter of King Edward I of England </wiki/Edward_I_of_England> and his first wife, Eleanor of Castile (1241-1290) </wiki/Eleanor_of_Castile>.
1 CONT Contents [hide <javascript:toggleToc()>]  1 Born on the Ninth Crusade  2 French Childhood  3 Marriage & Issue  4 Secret 2nd Marriage  5 Issue  6 Death in Childbirth  7 Fiction  8 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=1>] Born on the Ninth Crusade
1 CONT Joan got her name from her birthplace, Acre </wiki/Akko>, in Kingdom of Acre </wiki/Kingdom_of_Acre>. It differentiates her from an earlier Joan born to the couple, who died in infancy. Joan of Acre was born while her Royal parent
1 CONC s were traveling to the Middle East </wiki/Middle_East> on the Ninth Crusade </wiki/Ninth_Crusade>.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=2>] French Childhood
1 CONT At least part of her childhood she spent in France with her maternal grandmother, Jeanne de Dammartin, Countess of Ponthieu </wiki/Jeanne_de_Dammartin%2C_Countess_of_Ponthieu>. She was betrothed as a child to Hartman, son of King 
1 CONC Rudolph I of Germany </wiki/Rudolph_I_of_Germany>, but he died in 1282 after drowning in the Rhine </wiki/Rhine>.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=3>] Marriage & Issue
1 CONT On 30 April </wiki/April_30> 1290 </wiki/1290>, at Westminster Abbey </wiki/Westminster_Abbey>, Joan married Gilbert de Clare, 7th Earl of Hertford </wiki/Gilbert_de_Clare%2C_7th_Earl_of_Hertford>. He was nearly thirty years her s
1 CONC enior. Their four children were:
1 CONT Gilbert de Clare, 8th Earl of Hertford </wiki/Gilbert_de_Clare%2C_8th_Earl_of_Hertford>
1 CONT Eleanor de Clare </wiki/Eleanor_de_Clare>
1 CONT Margaret de Clare </wiki/Margaret_de_Clare>
1 CONT Elizabeth de Clare </wiki/Elizabeth_de_Clare>
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=4>] Secret 2nd Marriage
1 CONT Following her husband's death in 1295, Joan clandestinely married Ralph de Monthermer, 1st Baron Monthermer </wiki/Ralph_de_Monthermer%2C_1st_Baron_Monthermer>, a knight in her household, in January 1297. Her father, King Edward I
1 CONC  </wiki/Edward_I_of_England>, was enraged by this lowly second marriage, especially since he was arranging a marriage for her to Amadeus V, Count of Savoy </wiki/Amadeus_V%2C_Count_of_Savoy>.
1 CONT He had Monthermer thrown in prison, and Joan had to plead for the release of her husband. According to the St. Albans chronicler, she told her father, "No one sees anything wrong if a great Earl marries a poor and lowly woman. Why
1 CONC  should there be anything wrong if a countess marries a young and promising man?" At last her father relented, released Monthermer from prison in August 1297, and allowed him to hold the title of Earl of Gloucester </wiki/Earl_of_
1 CONC Gloucester> and Earl of Hereford </wiki/Earl_of_Hereford> during Joan's lifetime.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=5>] Issue
1 CONT Monthermer and Joan had four children:
1 CONT Mary de Monthermer </w/index.php?title=Mary_de_Monthermer&action=edit&redlink=1>, born October 1297. In 1306 her grandfather King Edward I arranged for her to wed Duncan Macduff, 8th Earl of Fife </wiki/Duncan_Macduff%2C_8th_Earl_
1 CONC of_Fife>.
1 CONT Joan de Monthermer </w/index.php?title=Joan_de_Monthermer&action=edit&redlink=1>, born 1299, became a nun at Amesbury </wiki/Amesbury>.
1 CONT Thomas de Monthermer, 2nd Baron Monthermer </w/index.php?title=Thomas_de_Monthermer%2C_2nd_Baron_Monthermer&action=edit&redlink=1>, born 1301.
1 CONT Edward de Monthermer </w/index.php?title=Edward_de_Monthermer&action=edit&redlink=1>, born 1304 and died 1339. He fought in the Scottish campaign in 1335 and spent much of his life in service to his half-sister Elizabeth </wiki/El
1 CONC izabeth_de_Clare>, who provided for him during his last illness and buried him next to their mother.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=6>] Death in Childbirth
1 CONT Joan died in childbirth on 7 April 1307 at the manor of Clare in Suffolk </wiki/Suffolk>, England </wiki/England>, a Clare family possession, and was buried with her stillborn child, 23 April </wiki/April_23> 1307 </wiki/1307>, at
1 CONC  the Augustinian </wiki/Augustinian> priory there. Miracles were said to occur at her grave, especially the healing of toothache, back pain, and fever. A fifteenth-century English chronicle reports that when her tomb was opened a 
1 CONC century and more after her death, her body was found incorrupt, which was seen in the medieval period as a strong indication of sanctity. So far as is known, however, no process for her sanctification was ever undertaken.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=7>] Fiction
1 CONT Joan of Acre makes an appearance in Virginia Henley's latest historical romance, entitled "Infamous." In the book, Joan, known as Joanna, is described as a promiscuous young princess, vain, shallow and spoiled to the core. Her one
1 CONC  constant friend and loyal servant is Marjorie de Warrenne. Princess Joanna is then contracted to marry the Earl of Gloucester, who is 30 plus years her senior. There are rumours that her first child with the Earl was fathered by 
1 CONC Henry de Bohun.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit </w/index.php?title=Joan_of_Acre&action=edit&section=8>] Sources
1 CONT Costain, Thomas. A History of the Plantagenets, Vol III </w/index.php?title=A_History_of_the_Plantagenets%2C_Vol_III&action=edit&redlink=1>.
1 CONT Underhill, Frances A. For Her Good Estate, 1999.
1 CONT Retrieved from "<http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_of_Acre>"
0 @N6000000001563228601@ NOTE Princes Joan was a child of the marriage of King Edward the First (Longshanks) to Eleanor/Leonor, Princess of Castile and Leon, Spain. Joan was born in Israel while her father was on the Seventh Crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan Plantagenet's mother died in 1290, the same year she married Gilbert De Clare. She was 18 and he was 47. According to author Thomas B. Constain in "The Three Edwards" Gilbert was the most powerful man in England after Joan's 
1 CONC father, while young Joan was "Fiery-spirited, sloe-eyed." According to records of the day, Joan left her home for the Red Earl's country retreat with the following: "One sumpter-horse carried her chapel apparatus, another her bed,
1 CONC  a third her jewels, a fourth her shamber furniture, a fifth her candles, a sixth her pantry-stores and table linen, and a secenth, her kitchen furniture." Joan was quite active after marrying Gilbert; she controlled her entire in
1 CONC heritance and did it well; historians regard her as one of the two best examples of 13th century women who operated large estates and managed complex business affairs.
1 CONT
1 CONT She was only 23 when Gilbert died, and a year later, she married an obscure family squire, Ralph De Monthermer in Acre, Palestine, near the place of her birth. It was a happy marriage and, although Ralph was opposed at first by Lo
1 CONC ngshanks, the two men developed a friendship. The couple had a son, Sir Thomas de Monthermer, born 1301; he was killed 1340 at the Battle of Sluys. Their daughter Mary married Duncan, the son of Gilbert de Clare's daughter Joan an
1 CONC d Duncan of Fife: after his death, she married Scot rebel Gervase Avenel and forfeited her lands, which later were granted to Hugh Despenser.
1 CONT Married first Gilbert de Clare, Earl of Gloucester; second Ralph de Monthermer.
