1 CONC d Duncan of Fife: after his death, she married Scot rebel Gervase Avenel and forfeited her lands, which later were granted to Hugh Despenser.
1 CONT Married first Gilbert de Clare, Earl of Gloucester; second Ralph de Monthermer.
0 @N6000000002187747774@ NOTE Joan of Acre
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan of Acre (May 1271 – April 7, 1307) was a daughter of King Edward I of England and his first wife, Eleanor of Castile (1241-1290). Se is not to be confused with Joan of Arc.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan got her name from her birthplace, Akko (Acre), Hazofan, Palestine. It differentiates her from an earlier Joan born to the couple, who died in infancy. Joan of Acre was born while her parents were traveling to the Middle East 
1 CONC on the Ninth Crusade. At least part of her childhood she spent in France with her maternal grandmother, Jeanne de Dammartin, Countess of Ponthieu. She was betrothed as a child to Hartman, son of King Rudolph I of Germany, but he d
1 CONC ied in 1282 after drowning in the Rhine.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1290, at Westminster Abbey, Joan married Gilbert de Clare, 7th Earl of Hertford. He was nearly thirty years her senior. Their four children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Gilbert de Clare, 8th Earl of Hertford 
1 CONT Eleanor de Clare 
1 CONT Margaret de Clare 
1 CONT Elizabeth de Clare 
1 CONT Following her husband's death in 1295, Joan clandestinely married Ralph de Monthermer, 1st Baron Monthermer, a knight in her household, in 1297. Her father, King Edward I, was enraged by this lowly second marriage, especially sinc
1 CONC e he was arranging a marriage for her to an Italian nobleman. He had Monthermer thrown in prison, and Joan had to plead for the release of her husband. According to the St. Albans chronicler, she told her father, "No one sees anyt
1 CONC hing wrong if a great earl marries a poor and lowly woman. Why should there be anything wrong if a countess marries a young and promising man?" At last her father relented, released Monthermer from prison in August 1297, and allow
1 CONC ed him to hold the title of Earl of Gloucester and Hereford during Joan's lifetime. Monthermer and Joan had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary de Monthermer, born October 1297. In 1306 her grandfather King Edward I arranged for her to wed Duncan Macduff, 8th Earl of Fife. 
1 CONT Joan de Monthermer, born 1299, became a nun at Amesbury. 
1 CONT Thomas de Monthermer, 2nd Baron Monthermer, born 1301. 
1 CONT Edward de Monthermer, born 1304 and died 1339. He fought in the Scottish campaign in 1335, but spent much of his life in service to his half-sister Elizabeth, who provided for him during his last illness and buried him next to the
1 CONC ir mother. 
1 CONT Joan died in childbirth in 1307 at the manor of Clare in Suffolk, England, a family possession, and was buried at the Augustinian priory there. Her child was stillborn. Miracles were said to occur at her grave, especially the heal
1 CONC ing of toothache, back pain, and fever.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Costain, Thomas. A History of the Plantagenets, Vol III. 
1 CONT Underhill, Frances A. For Her Good Estate, 1999. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Fiction
1 CONT Joan of Acre makes an appearance in Virginia Henley's latest historical romance, entitled "Infamous." In the book, Joan, known as Joanna, is described as a promiscuous young princess, vain, shallow and spoiled to the core. Her one
1 CONC  constant friend and loyal servant is Marjorie de Warrenne. Princess Joanna is then contracted to marry the Earl of Gloucester, who is 30 plus years her sneiour. There are rumours that her first child with the Earl was fathered by
1 CONC  Henry de Bohun.
