0 @N6000000003658760386@ NOTE GILBERT DE CLARE, Knt., 6th Earl of Gloucester, 6th Earl of Hertford, Baron of Clare, Suffolk, Steward of St. Edmund's Abbey, son and heir, born at Christchurch, Hampshire 2 Sept. 1243.  He married (1st) in the spring of 1253 ALIC
1 CONC E DE LUSIGNAN (or DE LA MARCHE), daughter of Hugh XI le Brun, Count of La Marche and Angoulême, seigneur of Lusignan in Poitou (uterine brother of Henry III, King of England), by Yolande, daughter of Pierre de Braine styled Maucle
1 CONC rc, Knt., Duke of Brittany, Earl of Richmond.  They had two daughters, Isabel and Joan.  Earl Gilbert played an important role in the constitutional crisis and the Barons' War, 1258-1267.  Initially he supported the Baronial cause
1 CONC .  IN 1264 he led the massacre of the Jews at Canterbury, Kent.  He and Simon de Montfort were denounced as traitors by the king 12 May 1264.  He commanded the 2nd line at the Battle of Lewes in May 1264, and took the king prisone
1 CONC r.  In May 1264 Prince Edward managed to ecape from Montford and met Gilbert as Ludlow, where he swore to observe the "good old laws" and to remove aliens from the royal councils.  The following month, Prince Edward and Gilbert we
1 CONC re denounced as rebels by Montford.  He shared Prince Edward's victory at Kenilworth 176 July 1264, and, at the Battle of Evesham, 4 August 1265, he commanded the 2nd division and contributed largely to the victory.  He and his wi
1 CONC fe, Alice, were legally separated at Norwich, Norfolk 18 July 1271.  In 1271 he had license to enditch his new castle of Caerphilly in Wales.  At the death of King Henry III 16 Nov. 1272, the Earl took the lead in swearing fealty 
1 CONC to Edward I, who was then in Sicily returning from the Crusade.  He was Joint Guardian of England during the King's absence.  In 1275 he went to France to negotiate for peace.  He was summoned to serve against the Weslh in 1277 an
1 CONC d 1282.  In 1278 he was among those selected to escort King Alexander III of Scotland to the king.  In May 1283 he was contracted to marry the king's daughter, Joan, provided he be formally divorced from his wife, Alice, and be fr
1 CONC ee to marry where he will, and also that he obtain a dispensation from the Pope to marry Joan, a first cousin once removed of Alice.  In 1285 Gilbert was "wholly absolved" from the contract of marriage between him and his former w
1 CONC ife, Alice, he granting her various manors including Thaxted, Essex, and Wells and Warham, Norfolk for her support.  In 1286 and 1287 he was beyond seas with the King, and, in June 1287, he was again in Wales on the King's service
1 CONC .  He married (2nd) at Winchester Abbey 23 April 1290 (by dispensation dated 16 Nov. 1289, they being related in the 2nd & 3rd degrees of affinity) JOAN OF ENGLAND [sometimes styled JOAN OF ACRE], daughter of Edward I, King of Eng
1 CONC land, by his 1st wife, Eleanor of Castile.  She was born at Acre in Palestine in Spring 1272.  They had one son ... and three daughters. ... In 1291 his quarrels with the Earl of Hereford about Brecknock culminated in a private wa
1 CONC r between them.  Both earls were imprisoned by the King, Gilbert being fined 10,000 marks as the aggressor.  In 1291 his wife, Joan, received a papal indult to enter Cisterian monasteries accompanied by eight honest matrons. In 12
1 CONC 93 he was appointed Captain of the forces in Ireland, and resided tehre until some time in 1294.  SIR GILBERT DE CLARE, Earl of Gloucester and Hertford, died at Monmouth Castle 7 Dec. 1295, and was buried at Tewkesbury Abbey, Glou
1 CONC cestershire.  His widow, Joan, married (2nd) early in 1297 RALPH DE MONTHERMER, Knt., in right of his wife, of Tonbridge, Kent, Keeper of Cardiff Castle, Guardian and Lieutenant in Scotland, Warden of the Royal Forests south of Tr
1 CONC ent.  They had two sons ... and one daughter. ... Their marriage enraged her father, the King, and he committed Ralph to prison in Bristol Castle, and all of Joan's lands were seised into the King's hand.  By the mediation of Anth
1 CONC ony Bec, Bishop of Durham, peace was made between the King and his daughter, and her lands were restored to her 31 July 1297.  The King afterwards became much attached to his new son-in-law, who was summoned to Parliament as Earl 
1 CONC of Gloucester and Hertford during the minority of his step-son, Gilbert de Clare.  In 1297 he was summoned to serve with the King's overseas expedition.  He was summoned to serve against the Scots, 1299, 1300, and frequently there
1 CONC after.  He fought at the Battle of Falkirk 22 July 1298, and was present at the Siege of Caerlaverock Castle in 1300.  He signed the Barons' letter to Pope Boniface VIII in 1301 as Rad'us de Monte H'meri Com' Glouc' & Herf'.  He w
1 CONC as briefly Earl of Atholl, 1306-1307, and was defeated by Robert de Brus in 1307.  JOAN OF ENGLAND died 7 April 1307, and was buried in the Austin Friars at Clare, Suffolk.  Ralph was summoned to Parliament from 4 March 1308/9 to 
1 CONC 30 Oct. 1324, by writs directed Raulpho de Monte Hermerii.  In 1309 he was granted the barony of Erlestoke, Wiltshire, including the manors of Stokenham, Oakford, Pyworthy, and Sterte, Devon and Hunton, Hampshire.  He fought at Ba
1 CONC nnockburn 24 June 1314, where he was taken prisoner.  In 1315 he had permission to go on pilgrimage to Santiago of Compostella in Spain.  He married (2nd0 before 20 Nov. 1318 Isabel le Despenser, widow successively of Gilbert de C
1 CONC lare, Knt. (died shortly before 16 Nov. 1307) ... and John de Hastings, Knt., 1st Lord Hastings (died 10 Feb. 1312/3) ..., and daughter of Hugh le Despenser, Knt., Earl of Winchester, 1st Lord Despenser, by Isabel, daughter of Wil
1 CONC liam de Beauchamp, 9th Earl of Warwick. ... SIR RALPH DE MONTHERMER, 1st Lord Monthermer, died testate 10 May (or 5 April) 1325, and was buried at Grey Friars, Salisbury, Wiltshire.  His widow, Isabel, died testate 4 Dec. 1334, an
1 CONC d was buried at Grey Friars, Salisbury.
0 @N6000000003949783829@ NOTE Daughter of Edward I, King of England, by Eleanor of Castile; widow of Gilbert de Clare, Earl of Gloucester and Hertford; m. (2) 1297 Sir Ralph de Monthermer, styled Earl of Gloucester and Hertford during the life of his wife, cre
1 CONC ated Lord Monthermer 1308, d. 5 Apr 1325. [Magna Charta Sureties]
1 CONT Gilbert de Clare was not young when he married the fiery-spirited, sloe-eyed Joanna and took her to live at his country retreat in Clerkenwell not far from the Tower, where the king and queen were again in residence. She left for 
1 CONC her new home with great fanfare, laden with royal gifts. After being a widow a year, she secretly married a completely unknown squire in her husbands retinue, Ralph de Monthermer. Through this marriage he became possessed in his o
1 CONC wn right of the earldoms of Gloucester & Hertford. The fact that a royal princess had dared to marry this obscure fellow became a cause celebré which for a time separated her from the affection of her father. It proved to be a mar
1 CONC riage, however, leading ultimately to a firm friendship between the new son-in-law and Edward.
0 @N6000000004286739891@ NOTE !Tite is;  Joan "of Acre", Princess of /ENGLAND/
0 @N6000000004533823224@ NOTE From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Joan of Acre (May 1271 - April 7, 1307) was a daughter of King EdwardI of England and his first wife, Eleanor of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan got her name from her birthplace, Akko (Acre), Hazofan,Palestine. It differentiates her from an earlier Joan born to thecouple, who died in infancy. Joan of Acre was born while her parentswere traveling to the Middle East on 
1 CONC the Seventh Crusade. She wasbetrothed as a child to Hartman, son of the King of Germany, but hedied in 1282 after drowning in the Rhine.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1290, at Westminster Abbey, Joan married Gilbert de Clare, 3rd Earlof Gloucester. He was nearly thirty years her senior. Their fourchildren were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Gilbert de Clare, 8th Earl of Hertford, 4th Earl of Gloucester
1 CONT Eleanor de Clare
1 CONT Margaret de Clare
1 CONT Elizabeth de Clare
1 CONT Following her husband's death in 1295, Joan clandestinely marriedRalph, 1st Baron Monthermer, a knight in her household, in 1297. Herfather, King Edward I, was enraged by this lowly second marriage,especially since he was arrangin
1 CONC g a marriage for her to an Italiannobleman. He had Monthermer thrown in prison, and Joan had to pleadfor the release of her husband. According to the St. Albanschronicler, she told her father, "No one sees anything wrong if agreat
1 CONC  earl marries a poor and lowly woman. Why should there beanything wrong if a countess marries a young and promising man?" Atlast her father relented, released Monthermer from prison, and allowedhim to hold the title of Earl of Glo
1 CONC ucester and Hereford during Joan'slifetime. Ralph and Joan had four children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary de Monthermer, born October 1297. In 1306 her grandfather KingEdward I arranged for her to wed Duncan Macduff, 8th Earl of Fife.
1 CONT Joan de Monthermer, born 1299, became a nun at Amesbury.
1 CONT Thomas de Monthermer, 2nd Baron Monthermer, born 1301.
1 CONT Edward de Monthermer, born 1304.
1 CONT She died in childbirth in 1307 at the manor of Clare in Suffolk,England, a family possession, and was buried there. Her child wasstillborn.
1 CONT
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_of_Acre"
