0 @N6000000001744969310@ NOTE Ruled 1307-1327. Best says, "Few kings have been sillier or less suited to the throne than Edward. Born in 1284, he always preferred the company of workmen to courtiers and was not in the least interested in statecraft or the resp
1 CONC onsibilities of kingship. It was a disaster for the country when he succeeded to the throne in 1307. Although married to Isabella, Edward spent far more time with his handsome friend Piers Gaveston, a French adventurer who milked 
1 CONC the royal connection for all it was worth. But rival barons executed Gaveston and his successor as royal favorite, Hugh Despenser. Matters eventually came to such a pass that Isabella and her lover Roger Mortimer imprisoned Edward
1 CONC  in Berkeley Castle. He was murdered there in 1327 and his young son was declared king in his place (though Mortimer actually ruled for a number of years).
1 CONT
1 CONT From Nicholas Best, "The Kings and Queens of England" (Boston: Bullfinch, 1995).
1 CONT
1 CONT Was Edward's mother Marguerite of France or Eleanor of Castile??
