0 @N6000000003300512850@ NOTE Edmund held the castle of Arundel, after the execution and forfeiture o f the 7th Earl, from 1327 until his own execution in 1330, but does not h imself appear to have been styled Earl of Arundel.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund was beheaded for conspiring to rescue King Edward II from prison . He had to wait five hours for an executioner, because nobody wanted t o do it.
1 CONT
1 CONT Portrayed by Jerome Flynn in the 1991 film, "Edward II."
0 @N6000000002188008454@ NOTE Edmund of Woodstock, 1st Earl of Kent
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund Plantagenet, or Edmund of Woodstock (August 5, 1301 – March 19, 1330) was Earl of Kent from July 28, 1321 (fourth creation).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was born at Woodstock, Oxfordshire, the son of King Edward I of England by his second Queen consort Marguerite of France. As the youngest of the six princes he enjoyed his father's favour. Woodstock was married to Margaret Wake
1 CONC , the daughter of Baron John Wake by Joan, sometime between October and December in 1325 at Blisworth in Northamptonshire.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was from 1327 'after the execution and forfeiture of John FitzAlan, 7th Earl of Arundel' for the three remaining years of his life to hold the castle and honour of Arundel, although he was never formally invested with the title
1 CONC s appropriate to his barony. He was the father of Joan of Kent, through whom the earldom eventually passed into the Holland family.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund was executed for treason, having supported his half-brother, the deposed King Edward II, by order of the 'Regents Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March and Queen Isabella of France', before the outer walls of Winchester Castle.
1 CONC  It was said that he had conspired to rescue King Edward from prison. Such was public hostility to the execution that "he had to wait five hours for an executioner, because nobody wanted to do it".
1 CONT
1 CONT Woodstock was buried on March 31 at the Church of the Dominican Friars in Winchester.
1 CONT
1 CONT Woodstock's execution would appear a retaliation for Edward I's crushing defeat against Simon de Montfort at the Battle of Evesham in 1265, and because the king had treated his rebellious cousins with such great savagery, pursuing
1 CONC  the surviving members of the de Montfort family relentlessly.
1 CONT
1 CONT The children of Edmund Plantagenet by Margaret Wake, Baroness Wake of Lydell, were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Edmund Plantagenet, 2nd Earl of Kent About 1326 - Before 5 October 1331 
1 CONT Margaret Plantagenet (b bfr 1327-d 1352). Married Arnaud-Amanieu VIII d'Albret, viscount of Tartas. 
1 CONT Joan Plantagenet ("The Fair Maid of Kent") b 28 September 1328 - d 7 August 1385. Married Thomas Holland, 1st Earl of Kent and Edward, the Black Prince son of Edward III 
1 CONT John Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Kent 7 Apr 1330 - About 27 December 1352 
