0 @N6000000002188011254@ NOTE Thomas Plantagenet, 2nd Earl of Lancaster
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas, Earl of Lancaster (c. 1278 – March 22, 1322) was one of the leaders of the baronial opposition to Edward II of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas was the eldest son of Edmund Crouchback, 1st Earl of Lancaster and Blanche of Artois. His paternal grandparents were Henry III of England and Eleanor of Provence. His maternal grandparents were Robert I of Artois and Matild
1 CONC a of Brabant. Matilda was a daughter of Henry II, Duke of Brabant.
1 CONT
1 CONT From his father Thomas inherited the Earldoms of Lancaster, Leicester, and Derby. By his marriage to Alice de Lacy, Countess of Lincoln, daughter of Henry de Lacy, 3rd Earl of Lincoln, he became Earl of Lincoln and Salisbury upon 
1 CONC the death of his father-in-law in 1311. Master of five earldoms, he was one of the wealthiest and most powerful men in England.
1 CONT
1 CONT He served in the coronation of his cousin, King Edward II of England, on February 25, 1308, carrying Curtana, the sword of St Edward the Confessor. At the beginning of the king's reign, Lancaster openly supported Edward, but as th
1 CONC e conflict between the king and the nobles wore on, Lancaster's allegiances changed. He despised the royal favorite, Piers Gaveston, who mocked him as "the Fiddler", and swore revenge when Gaveston demanded that the king dismiss o
1 CONC ne of Lancaster's retainers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Lancaster was one of the Lords Ordainers who demanded the banishment of Gaveston and the establishment of a baronial oligarchy. His private army helped separate the king and Gaveston, and Lancaster was one of the "judges" who conv
1 CONC icted Gaveston and saw him executed.
1 CONT
1 CONT His marriage to Alice de Lacy was not successful. They had no children, though he had two illegitimate sons. In 1317 she was abducted from her manor at Canford, Dorset by Richard de St Martin, a knight in the service of John de Wa
1 CONC renne, 8th Earl of Surrey. This incident caused a feud between Lancaster and Surrey; Lancaster divorced his wife and seized two of Surrey's castles in retaliation. King Edward then intervened, and the two earls came to an uneasy t
1 CONC ruce. Although divorced from his wife he continued to hold the powerful earldoms Lincoln & Salisbury. This was due to the marriage contract the two families had agreed, in effect upon the death of his father in law, Earl Thomas he
1 CONC ld these earldoms in his own right, not in right of his wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the disaster at Bannockburn in 1314, Edward submitted to Lancaster, who in effect became ruler of England. He attempted to govern for the next four years, but was unable to keep order or prevent the Scots from raiding and re
1 CONC taking territory in the North. In 1318 a new faction of barons arose, and Lancaster was deposed from office.
1 CONT
1 CONT The new leadership, eventually headed by Hugh le Despenser, 1st Earl of Winchester and his son Hugh the younger Despenser, proved no more popular with the baronage, and in 1321 Lancaster was again at the head of a rebellion. This 
1 CONC time, however, he was defeated at the Battle of Boroughbridge, and taken prisoner. He was tried by a tribunal consisting of, among others, the two Despensers, Edmund FitzAlan, 9th Earl of Arundel, and King Edward. Lancaster was no
1 CONC t allowed to speak in his own defense, nor was he allowed to have anyone to speak for him. Because of their kinship and Lancaster's royal blood, the king commuted the sentence to mere beheading (as opposed to being drawn, quartere
1 CONC d, and beheaded) and Lancaster was convicted of treason and executed near the Pontefract Castle.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Pontefract Castle, Lancaster's favourite residence.Upon his death his titles and estates were forfeited, but in 1323 his younger brother Henry successfully petitioned to take possession of the Earldom of Leicester. In 1326 or 1327
1 CONC  Parliament posthumously reversed Thomas's conviction, and Henry was further permitted to take possession of the Earldoms of Lancaster, Derby, Salisbury and Lincoln.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas became venerated as a martyr and saint within a few months of his death. Hagiographies were written about him, and Edward III wrote three times to the Pope requesting his canonization. He was never canonized, though rumors 
1 CONC to that effect arose in the 1390s, when his cult experienced something of a revival.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT The Earl of Leicester and Lancaster Lord High Steward
1 CONT 1296–1322 Succeeded by:
1 CONT The Earl of Leicester and Lancaster 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Edmund Crouchback Earl of Leicester and Lancaster Succeeded by:
1 CONT Henry of Lancaster 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT J. R. Maddicott, Thomas of Lancaster, 1307-1322: A study in the reign of Edward II, (Oxford, 1970) 
1 CONT C. Given-Wilson, "Richard II, Edward II, and the Lancastrian Inheritance", English Historical Review, 109 (1994), 553-571 
1 CONT Ian Mortimer, The Greatest Traitor, (2003). 
