0 @N6000000003177897358@ NOTE 3rd Earl of Lancaster
0 @N6000000003949733132@ NOTE Following is a brief summary of Henry's entry from the "Dictionary of National Biography" :
1 CONT He served with EDWARD I in Flanders in 1297 and 1298. He fought in Scotland several times between 1298 and 1305. In 1315 he, in common with the other lords of the Welsh marches, joined the Earl of Hereford in putting down the rebe
1 CONC llion of Llewelyn Bren, and in 1318 he was ordered to bring his Welsh retainers to Newcastle to serve against the Scots. He was opposed to the Despensers, for the greediness of the younger threatened the lords marchers generally; 
1 CONC but he does not seem to have had any violent feelings against the king, and was not involved in his brother's [Thomas, Earl of Lancaster] treason. In 1324 he was created Earl of Lancaster, Earl of Leicester, and Steward of England
1 CONC , dignities which had been held by his brother. It is evident that he was indignant at his brother's fate, and was resolved to avenge it, and was not appeased by these honors. In 1324 the King unsuccessfully had him tried for trea
1 CONC son. He was, at this time, regarded as the foremost man in the kingdom. Henry supported Queen Isabel upon her return to England and he was instrumental in the downfall of the Despensers and had custody of King Edward II for a time
1 CONC  during his imprisonment. He is said to have treated the King very humanely. Henry was the guardian of King Edward III during his minority and was the chief member of the council of government. In 1326 he took the lead in oppositi
1 CONC on to the rule of Queen Isabel and her paramour, ROGER MORTIMER, EARL OF MARCH (RIN 684). Early in the year 1330, following a slow degeneration of his vision, Henry became totally blind. Still, he persuaded the king of the necessi
1 CONC ty of getting rid of MORTIMER and was the mastermind of the plot which led to MORTIMER'S capture, trial, and execution late in the year. The Earl's blindness, which he bore with patience, forced him to retire from active life; he 
1 CONC gave himself wholly to devotion. He was courteous and kindhearted, of sound judgement, religious, and apparently of high
0 @N6000000005076784978@ NOTE 3rd Earl of Lancaster
1 CONT
1 CONT from thePeerage.com:
1 CONT
1 CONT  Henry Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Lancaster was born circa 1281 at Grosmont Castle, Monmouthshire, Wales.2 He was the son of Edmund 'Crouchback' Plantagenet, Earl of Leicester and Blanche d'Artois. He married Matilda de Chaworth, da
1 CONC ughter of Sir Patrick de Chaworth and Isabella de Beauchamp, before 2 March 1297.2 He married Alice de Joinville, daughter of Jean de Joinville, Seneschal de Champagne and Alice de Risnal, after 1322.3 He died on 22 September 1345
1 CONC  at Leicester, Leicestershire, England.2 He died in 1345. He was buried at Newark Abbey, Leicester, Leicestershire, England.2
1 CONT      Henry Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Lancaster gained the title of Earl of Leicester on 29 March 1324.2 He gained the title of 3rd Earl of Lancaster on 26 October 1326.2 He gained the title of Lord of Beaufort and Nogent circa 1336
1 CONC .2
1 CONT Children of Henry Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Lancaster and Matilda de Chaworth
1 CONT Henry Grosmont of Derby Plantagenet, 1st Duke of Lancaster+ b. bt 1299 - 1314, d. 24 Mar 1360/61
1 CONT Lady Blanche Plantagenet b. c 1305, d. 10 Jul 13804
1 CONT Lady Matilda of Lancaster+ b. c 1310, d. b 5 May 13774
1 CONT Lady Joan Plantagenet+ b. c 1312, d. 7 Jul 13455
1 CONT Isabella Plantagenet b. c 1317, d. a 1 Feb 13476
1 CONT Lady Eleanor Plantagenet+ b. c 1318, d. 11 Jan 13726
1 CONT Lady Mary Plantagenet+ b. c 1320, d. 1 Sep 13623
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S106] Royal Genealogies Website (ROYAL92.GED), online ftp://ftp.cac.psu.edu/genealogy/public_html/royal/index.html. Hereinafter cited as Royal Genealogies Website.
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family: A Complete Genealogy (London, U.K.: The Bodley Head, 1999), page 75. Hereinafter cited as Britain's Royal Family.
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family, page 79.
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family, page 76.
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family, page 77.
1 CONT [S11] Alison Weir, Britain's Royal Family, page 78.
0 @N6000000006296781855@ NOTE Henry Plantagenet, 3rd Earl of Lancaster (1281 - 25 March 1345) was an English </wiki/England> nobleman, one of the principals behind the deposition of Edward II </wiki/Edward_II_of_England>.
1 CONT He was the younger son of Blanche of Artois </wiki/Blanche_of_Artois> and Edmund Crouchback, 1st Earl of Lancaster </wiki/Edmund_Crouchback,_1st_Earl_of_Lancaster>, Earl of Leicester </wiki/Earl_of_Leicester>, who was a son of Hen
1 CONC ry III of England </wiki/Henry_III_of_England> and Eleanor of Provence </wiki/Eleanor_of_Provence>.
1 CONT Henry's elder brother Thomas, 2nd Earl of Lancaster </wiki/Thomas,_2nd_Earl_of_Lancaster> succeeded their father in 1296, but Henry was summoned to Parliament </wiki/Parliament_of_England> on 6 February 1298/99 by writ directed He
1 CONC nrico de Lancastre nepoti Regis, by which he is held to have become Lord Lancaster. He took part in the siege of Carlaverock in July 1300.
1 CONT After a period of longstanding opposition to Edward II and his advisors, including joining two open rebellions, Thomas, in 1322, was convicted of treason, executed and his lands and titles forfeited. Henry did not participate in h
1 CONC is brother's rebellions; he later petitioned for his brother's lands and titles, and on 29 March </wiki/March_29> 1324 </wiki/1324> he was invested as Earl of Leicester </wiki/Leicester>, and a few years later the earldom of Lanca
1 CONC ster was also restored to him.
1 CONT On the Queen’s return to England with Roger Mortimer, 1st Earl of March </wiki/Roger_Mortimer,_1st_Earl_of_March> in September 1326, Henry joined her party against King Edward II, which led to a general desertion of the King’s cau
1 CONC se and overturned the power of Hugh le Despenser, 1st Earl of Winchester </wiki/Hugh_le_Despenser,_1st_Earl_of_Winchester> and his namesake son Hugh the younger Despenser </wiki/Hugh_the_younger_Despenser>.
1 CONT He was sent in pursuit and captured the king at Neath in South Wales. He was appointed to take charge of the King, and was responsible for his custody at Kenilworth Castle </wiki/Kenilworth_Castle>.
1 CONT After Edward II's death Henry was appointed guardian of the new king Edward III of England </wiki/Edward_III_of_England>, and was also appointed captain-general of all the King's forces in the Scottish Marches </wiki/Scottish_Marc
1 CONC hes>.
1 CONT In about the year 1330, he became blind </wiki/Blindness>.
1 CONT He was succeeded as Earl of Lancaster and Leicester by his eldest son, Henry of Grosmont </wiki/Henry_of_Grosmont>, who subsequently became Duke of Lancaster.
1 CONT Prior to his restoration to his earldoms, Henry bore the arms of the kingdom, differenced by a bend azure. Upon his restoration, his difference changed, to a label France of three points (that is to say azure three fleur-de-lys or
1 CONC , each).[1]
1 CONT He married Maud Chaworth </wiki/Maud_Chaworth>, before 2 March </wiki/March_2> 1296 </wiki/1296>/1297.
1 CONT Henry and Maud had seven children:
1 CONT Henry of Grosmont, 1st Duke of Lancaster </wiki/Henry_of_Grosmont,_1st_Duke_of_Lancaster>, (about 1300-1360/61)
1 CONT Blanche of Lancaster, (about 1305 - 1380) married Thomas Wake, 2nd Baron Wake of Liddell </wiki/Thomas_Wake,_2nd_Baron_Wake_of_Liddell>
1 CONT Maud of Lancaster, (about 1310-1377); married William de Burgh, 3rd Earl of Ulster </wiki/William_de_Burgh,_3rd_Earl_of_Ulster>
1 CONT Joan of Lancaster </wiki/Joan_of_Lancaster>, (about 1312-1345); married John de Mowbray, 3rd Baron Mowbray </wiki/John_de_Mowbray,_3rd_Baron_Mowbray>
1 CONT Isabel of Lancaster, Abbess of Ambresbury, (about 1317-after 1347)
1 CONT Eleanor of Lancaster </wiki/Eleanor_of_Lancaster>, (about 1318-1371/72) married (1) John De Beaumont and (2) 5 Feb. 1344/5, Richard FitzAlan, 10th Earl of Arundel </wiki/Richard_FitzAlan,_10th_Earl_of_Arundel>;
1 CONT Mary of Lancaster, (about 1320-1362), who married Henry de Percy, 3rd Baron Percy </w/index.php?title=Henry_de_Percy,_3rd_Baron_Percy&action=edit&redlink=1>, and was the mother of Henry Percy, 1st Earl of Northumberland </wiki/Hen
1 CONC ry_Percy,_1st_Earl_of_Northumberland>.
