0 @N6000000002187757300@ NOTE Philip of Courtenay
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip I of Courtenay (1243, Constantinople – 1283, Viterbo) was titular Emperor of Constantinople 1273–1283. He was the son of Baldwin II of Constantinople and Marie of Brienne.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his youth, his father was forced to mortgage him to Venetian merchants to raise money for the support of his empire, which was lost to the Empire of Nicaea in 1261.
1 CONT
1 CONT By the Treaty of Viterbo in 1267, his father agreed to marry him to Beatrice, daughter of Charles I of Sicily and Beatrice of Provence. Her maternal grandparents were Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence and Beatrice of Savoy.
1 CONT
1 CONT The marriage was performed in October 1273 at Foggia; shortly thereafter, Baldwin died, and Philip inherited his claims on Constantinople. Although Philip was recognized as emperor by the Latin possessions in Greece, much of the a
1 CONC ctual authority devolved on the Angevin kings of Naples and Sicily. Philip and Beatrice had a daughter, Catherine (1274 – January 2, 1307 or 1308, Paris). Philip died in 1283.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Baldwin II titular Emperor of Constantinople
1 CONT 1273–1283 Succeeded by
1 CONT Catherine I of Courtenay 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT Peter Lock, The Franks in the Aegean 1204–1500, New York, 1995. 
0 @N6000000002187845125@ NOTE Catherine I of Courtenay
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Catherine I of Courtenay (1274–1307/8) was Titular Empress of Constantinople from 1283 to her death in 1307/8. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT She was the daughter of the Titular Emperor of Constantinople Philip I of Courtenay by Beatrice of Anjou. Her maternal grandparents were King Charles I of Sicily and Countess Beatrice of Provence. The senior Beatrice was the fourt
1 CONC h daughter of Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence and Beatrice of Savoy.
1 CONT
1 CONT On her father's death in 1283, Catherine inherited his claims to the throne of Constaninople and was recognized as empress by the Latin states in Greece. On February 8, 1301, she married Count Charles of Valois, son of King Philip
1 CONC  III of France and Isabella of Aragon. Charles became titular emperor with Catherine until her death in January 1307 or 1308.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT By Charles of Valois, Catherine I had three children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Catherine, who died young. 
1 CONT John, count of Chartres, who died young. 
1 CONT Catherine II of Valois, Princess of Achaea and titular empress of Constantinople. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Philip I Titular Latin Empress
1 CONT 1283–1308
1 CONT with Charles of Valois 1301–1308 Succeeded by
1 CONT Catherine II 
