0 @N6000000003949863069@ NOTE John Yohalem names Stephen as III of Axonne, while Ed Mann & Robert O'Connor name him Stephen or Etienne III of Auxerre (I believe following Stewart's Royalty for Commoners). I don't know whether he was count of both Auxonne and A
1 CONC uxerre, but I will treat this line as if they were counts of both. John Yohalem (Jean Ceour de Lapin) seems to know an enormous amount about French genealogy and history, and I would probably take his side against Stewart's RFC.
0 @N6000000002187814769@ NOTE Otto I, Count of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto I (between 1167 and 1171 – January 13, 1200) was the Count of Burgundy from 1190 to his death and briefly Count of Luxembourg from 1196 to 1197. He was the fourth son of the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick Barbarossa and Beatric
1 CONC e I, Countess of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Henry IV of Luxembourg died in 1196, that county escheated to the Emperor and Henry VI enfeoffed his brother Otto. Theobald I of Bar, who had married Ermesinda, heiress of Henry IV, negotiated the renunciation of Luxembourg w
1 CONC ith Otto the next year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto married Margaret, daughter of Theobald V of Blois, in 1190 and it was his eldest daughter by her who succeeded him. His second daughter, Beatrice, was also later countess.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was assassinated at Besançon. His daughter Joanna inherited the rule of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Frederick Count of Burgundy
1 CONT 1190–1200 Succeeded by
1 CONT Joanna I 
