0 @N6000000002187814781@ NOTE Hedwig of Andechs
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Saint Hedwig of Andechs (1174 - October 1243; Polish: Swieta Jadwiga Slaska) was born at Castle Andechs, Bavaria, the daughter of Berthold III, Count of Tirol and Duke of Carinthia and Istria (Andechs-Meran), and his wife Agnes.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of Hedwig's sisters married Andrew, King of Hungary. Their daughter was Saint Elisabeth of Hungary, also known as Elizabeth of Thuringia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another of Hedwig's sisters was abbess at the Benedictine convent of Lutzingen in Franconia, where Hedwig received her education.
1 CONT
1 CONT At age 18, Hedwig married Henry I the Bearded of Glogów. In 1233 Henryk also became Duke of Greater Poland. He died in battle with the Mongols in 1238. Upon his death, Hedwig entered the Cistercian convent of Trzebnica.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedwig and Henry I the Bearded had a son, Henry II the Pious. In 1241 he was also killed in battle with the Mongols, who had attacked Eastern Europe and come all the way to Silesia and Brandenburg. The Mongols' aim was to reach th
1 CONC e Baltic Sea, subjugating all the peoples in their path. The Mongol army was turned back by German and Polish forces under Gotthart Brandis.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedwig and Henryk had lived a very pious life, and Hedwig had great zeal for religion. She always helped the poor, went barefoot even in winter, and donated all her fortune to the Church and the poor.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT 18th century wooden church of Saint Hedwig of Andechs, near Kepno, Wielkopolska Voivodship.She died in October 1243 and was buried at Trzebnica, while relics of her are preserved at Andechs Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedwig was canonized in 1267.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedwig is the patron saint of Brandenburg, Berlin, Silesia and its capital Wroclaw, of Trzebnica, the Diocese of Goerlitz, of Andechs and of Kraków. St. Hedwig's Cathedral is the mother church of the Archdiocese of Berlin.
0 @N6000000002187639671@ NOTE Hedwig of Andechs
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT This article is about the 13th century saint. For the 14th century queen and saint of the same name, St. Hedwig the Queen of Poland (Sw. Jadwiga Królowa Polski), see Jadwiga of Poland. 
1 CONT Saint Hedwig of Andechs (1174 - October 1243) (Polish: Sw. Jadwiga Slaska) was born in Castle Andechs, Bavaria, the daughter of Berthold III, count of Tirol and prince of Carinthia and Istria (Andechs-Meran), and his wife Agnes. O
1 CONC ne of her sisters married Andrew, king of Hungary. Their daughter was Saint Elisabeth of Hungary, also known as Elizabeth of Thuringia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Another sister was abbess at Lutzingen in Franconia. Hedwig received her education at Lutzingen Benedictine convent. At the age of 18, she was married to Henry I the Bearded of Glogów. In 1233 Henry also became duke of Greater Pol
1 CONC and. Henry I died in battle with the Mongols in 1238. Hedwig and Henry had a son named Henry II. Hedwig entered the Cistercian convent of Trzebnica upon her husband's death. In 1241 her son Henry II was also killed in battle with 
1 CONC the Mongols, who attacked Eastern Europe and had come all the way to Silesia and Brandenburg. Their aim was to reach the Baltic Sea and subjugate all people in their path. The German and Polish troops under Gotthart Brandis manage
1 CONC d to turn back the Mongol army.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedwig and Henry lived a very pious life and Hedwig had great zeal for religion. She always helped the poor, went barefoot even in winter and she donated all her fortune to the church and the poor.
1 CONT
1 CONT She died in October 1243 and was buried at Trzebnica, while her relics are kept at Kloster Andechs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Hedwig was canonized in 1267.
1 CONT
1 CONT Saint Hedwig is the patron saint of Berlin, Silesia and the capital Wroclaw, of Trzebnica, Diocese of Goerlitz, of Andechs and of Kraków. St. Hedwig's Cathedral is the mother church of the Archdiocese of Berlin.
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Hedwig (Jadwiga, Avoice), OSB Cist. Queen Religious (RM) 
1 CONT Born in Bavaria c. 1174; died in Silesia, 1243; canonized 1267.
1 CONT Hedwig was one of the eight children born to Berthold IV, the count of Andechs, who ruled over the Tyrol and Istria (Croatia and Dalmatia). Two of her brothers became bishops and two of her sisters became queens. One of them, Gert
1 CONC rude, who married Andrew II of Hungary, was the mother of Saint Elizabeth (of Hungary, my fav!).
1 CONT
1 CONT As a child she was placed in the Benedictine monastery of Kitzingen in Franconia (see Saint Thecla).
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1186, when she was 12 years old, Hedwig was married to 18-year- old Henry the Bearded, prince of Poland and future duke of Silesia. She bore him 6 (some say 7) children and the family was closely knit. But from 1209 onwards she
1 CONC  and her husband agreed to live in perpetual continence. Hedwig was then 35 and Duke Henry was barely past 40, but he submitted to the austere disciple without complaint or resistance. 
1 CONT
1 CONT After succeeding to his father's dukedom in 1202, and under Hedwig's influence, Henry founded the monastery of Cistercian nuns at Trebnitz (near Breslau, now Wroclaw), the first convent of women in Silesia. The convent was built w
1 CONC ith the labor of those convicted of crimes. It was the first of a large number of such establishments founded by the couple, including houses of Augustinian canons, Cistercian monks, and Dominican and Franciscan friars, by which r
1 CONC eligion and German culture were spread over their territories.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry also founded the Hospital of the Holy Ghost in Breslau, and Hedwig founded a hospital for female lepers.
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the example of his wife, he was sustained by a great and ennobling piety. He let his beard grow in the manner of Cistercian converts (when his name Henry the Bearded) and greatly reduced his household expenses, devoting 
1 CONC the money that he saved to charitable purposes. After their separation Henry never again wore gold, silver, or purple. 
1 CONT
1 CONT There have been few duchesses like her. She was humble, serving the poor and the lepers, pardoning offenses, helping her enemies, and bringing aid to even the most insolent and hardened sinners. She kept barely a hundredth part of
1 CONC  her income, giving the rest away with an open hand. Beneath her tattered cloak she wore a hair shirt. She went about with naked feet in all weather, and when, in obedience to her confessor, she bought a pair of new shoes, she car
1 CONC ried them under her arms. She scourged herself and subjected her soul and her body to countless mortifications. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Towards the end of her life she had the gift of working cures and making predictions. Several miracles are recorded of her--she fell asleep, it was said, one night while reading the Bible by candlelight; the book caught fire and b
1 CONC urned, but was undamaged. A blind man's sight was restored because of her blessing.
1 CONT
1 CONT As for Henry I, her good and faithful husband, she outlived him by five years. In 1227, Henry engaged in fighting Conrad of Masovia for the land of Ladislaus of Sandomir who had been killed in battle. Henry triumphed and establish
1 CONC ed himself at Cracow, but he was kidnapped during Mass and taken by Conrad to Plock. Hedwig followed and helped bring the two to a peaceful agreement, which included the marriage of her two granddaughters to Conrad's sons. Upon Du
1 CONC ke Henry's death in 1238 Hedwig moved into the monastery at Trebnitz. Hedwig did not cry at her husband's death; she consoled the sorrowing nuns instead.
1 CONT
1 CONT God treats harshly those whom he loves. All her children died before she did, except for one daughter, Gertrude, who was the abbess of the convent of Trebnitz. Two of her sons dishonored the family name by engaging in fratricidal 
1 CONC wars, and another son, Henry the Pious who succeeded his father, was killed in 1241 by the Tartars at the battle of Liegnitz. Again, Hedwig comforted the others.
1 CONT
1 CONT She took the habit of the nuns but not the vows, wishing to administer her property as she wished to help the needy. She predicted her own death, insisting on being anointed before anyone else would acknowledge she was in danger. 
1 CONC Worn out by the hardships she had endured, she died in 1243, in her seventieth year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Riches have never been able to buy entrance into heaven. Hedwig, the duchess with the naked feet and workworn hands, had no need to knock on the gates which, at her approach, swung open of themselves. And someone was on the thresh
1 CONC old to greet with open arms the woman who had freely given of her heart, her wealth, and her light, and who had been a supreme example of the life of poverty in the example of God (Attwater, Bentley, Delaney, Encyclopedia, White).
1 CONT
1 CONT The policies and foundations of Duke Henry and Saint Hedwig were important in Silesian history through the increase of German influence they brought to the country (Attwater).
1 CONT
1 CONT She is the patroness of Silesia, and venerated in Franconia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Depicted in art with the church and a statue of the Virgin Mary in her hands; or washing the feet of the poor; or barefoot with her shoes in her hands; or in a religious habit with the robes and crown of a princess near her (White
1 CONC ). Sometimes she is seen holding a picture of the Virgin and Child in her hand or Christ blessing her from the Cross (Roeder).
1 CONT
1 CONT
0 @N6000000002187814556@ NOTE Agnes of Meran
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes Maria of Andechs-Meran (d. 1201), queen of France, was the daughter of Bertold IV (d. 1251), who was independent Count of Andechs, a castle and territory near Ammersee, Bavaria and from 1183 duke of Meran in Tirol, which has
1 CONC  derived its name from his castle Tyrol, above the valley of Meran. The count held his fiefs directly from the Emperor, so he was independent of the great territorial dukes of Germany. Bertold IV was made Archbishop of Kalocsa (in
1 CONC  Hungary) and in 1218 he was made Patriarch of Aquileia.
1 CONT
1 CONT She is called Marie by some of the French chroniclers. In June 1196 she married Philip Augustus (Philip II), king of France, who had repudiated his second wife Ingeborg of Denmark in 1193. Pope Innocent III espoused the cause of I
1 CONC ngeborg; but Philip did not submit until 1200, when, nine months after interdict had been added to excommunication, he consented to a separation from Agnes. She died broken-hearted in July of the next year, at the castle of Poissy
1 CONC , and was buried in the church of St. Corentin, near Nantes. Her two children by Philip II, Philip, count of Clermont (d. 1234), and Mary, who married Philip, count of Namur, were legitimized by the pope in 1201 at the request of 
1 CONC the king. Little is known of the personality of Agnes, beyond the remarkable influence which she seems to have exercised over Philip II. She has been made the heroine of a tragedy by François Ponsard, Agnès de Méranie.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her sister Hedwig of Andechs married Henry I, duke of Silesia and was canonized as Saint Hedwig in 1267. Another sister, Gertrude was Queen of Hungary.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT External link
1 CONT Jožko Šavli, "Andechs (A Bavarian Family with Carantanian Roots)" 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, a publication in the public domain. 
