0 @N6000000004533233910@ NOTE Notes for Philip V Roi of France:
1 CONT
1 CONT "The Tall" ; King of Navarre
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip V (Philip the Tall), c. 1294-1322, king of France (1317-22),son of King Philip IV. He became regent in 1316 on the death of hisbrother Louis X, who was survived by his pregnant wife and infantdaughter. On the death of John 
1 CONC I (1316), the posthumous son of Louis,Philip took the crown for himself in the absence of a direct male heirand was crowned (1317) king. This helped to establish the Salic law inFrance, which excluded females from the royal succes
1 CONC sion. Philip'sreign was notable for his frequent consultations of nationalassemblies and for his administrative, judiciary, and militaryreforms. He was succeeded by his brother, Charles IV.
0 @N6000000005598936350@ NOTE Historians have styled Philip as striking handsome and he continued th e work of his father and older brother.  He received the town of Poiti ers and its banlieu as an apanage.  At the time of his older brother L ouis' death in 13
1 CONC 16, Philip was in the provinces and made all haste t o return to the court.  He initially declared himself as regent for hi s yet to be born nephew Jean, King of France at the time of the death of his brother Louis, and after the 
1 CONC death of Jean proclaimed himself K ing.  His ascendancy to the throne was not disputed by Charles of Valo is, his fathers brother.  Upon becoming King, Philip called a gatherin g of nobles and clergy for the purpose of making his 
1 CONC position tight an d secure.  He also tried to regain the ground lost to the various lord s and clergy during the short reign of his older brother Louis.  He gr anted the burghers the right to bear arms in certain situations that s
1 CONC  upported his goals to regain the lost properties.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1320, at the conclusion of the war against Flanders begun during th e reign of Philip IV, France acquired some of the former Flemish terri tory.  Philip frequently convoked the Estates-General to discuss monet ary problems. He 
1 CONC made administrative reforms & attempted to unify coin age, weights, and measures but encountered strong opposition in the Es tates-General.  He imposed heavy fines on the Jewish population.  He i s critized for his effort to destr
1 CONC oy and elimanate those with the gene rally feared disease of leprosy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip died without a male heir.  In 1307 he was married to Jeanne de Bourgogne (daughter and heiress of Otto IV, count of Burgundy) and the y had three daughters: 1.) Jeanne, 2.) Marguerite and 3.) Isabelle.
1 CONT
1 CONT Philip V the Tall was King of France from 1317 to 1322, a member of th e Capetian dynasty.  The 1319/1320 Oridnance of Philip extended the de velopment of the French 'Parlement' as the judicial section of the Fre nch 'curis Regis,
1 CONC  originally initiated by Philip III in 1278.  He was the second son of King Philippe IV and Jeanne of Navarre. Philippe bec ame regent for his infant nephew King Jean I, and when Jean lived onl y a few days, he proclaimed himself 
1 CONC King. There was much speculation t hat Philippe was responsible for the infant king's demise.  In proclai ming himself King, he displaced the legitimate heiress (his niece Joan ) and had himself crowed at Rheims on 9 january 1317.
1 CONT
1 CONT They also had a son whose name was either Philip or Louis and who die d in 1317.  In 1320, Philippe V managed to expand his territory at th e expense of Flanders as a result of war. On domestic matters, King Ph ilippe attempted to
1 CONC  institute government reforms and worked to standar dize weights and measures.  King Philippe V died at Longchamp, Paris a nd is interred in Saint Denis Basilica.  Without a male heir, he was s ucceeded by his younger brother, Cha
1 CONC rles IV.
0 @N6000000003265463527@ NOTE
1 CONT Philippe was knighted in 1313 in the presence of his father.  He took up the cross and was bound to carry out the project.  The pope John had given him a new for-year grant of annates: this was followed in 1318 by another two-year
1 CONC  tenth grant from the church.
0 @N6000000002187845483@ NOTE Margaret I, Countess of Artois
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite de France (1310 - 9 May 1382) was a medieval ruler, reigning countess of Artois and the Palatine Burgundy (Franche-Comté) as well as countess-consort of Flanders, Nevers and Rethel. Her father was king of France 1316-24
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT She was born as the second daughter of Jeanne de Chalon, Countess of Burgundy and her husband Philippe, count of Poitiers. Her mother was Countess Palatine of Burgundy (daughter and heiress of Otto IV, Count of Burgundy) and her f
1 CONC ather was the second son of king Philip IV of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1316 her father became king Philip V of France, after her infant cousin John. Margaret was married to Louis I, Count of Flanders (1304-1346) who reigned Flanders, Nevers and Rethel from 1322. Her husband was dependent on her fa
1 CONC ther in suppressing the rebellion of Nikolaas Zannekin. Her father died in 1324, leaving France to her uncle Charles IV.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite's mother, Dowager Queen Joanna, succeeded the grandmother Mahaut, Countess of Artois as ruler of Artois in 1329. Her elder sister Jeanne, duchess of Burgundy (1308-49) inherited their mother in 1330, becoming Countess o
1 CONC f Artois and Palatine Countess of Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her husband Louis was killed in the Battle of Crécy 26 August 1346. He and Marguerite had one son, Louis II, Count of Flanders (1330-84) who in 1346 succeeded in Flanders, Nevers and Rethel. In 1355, the younger Louis claimed the 
1 CONC duchy of Brabant in right of his wife, but Joanna, Duchess of Brabant managed to keep her possessions.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1357, her granddaughter Margaret (1345-1405), then all seven years old, was married (betrothed) to her great-nephew Philip I, Duke of Burgundy (1346-1361), who thus was the young bride's second cousin. The girl thus became duch
1 CONC ess-consort of Burgundy, as well as countess of Artois, Boulogne, Auvergne and County Palatine of Burgundy (=Franche-Comté).
1 CONT
1 CONT Coastal Low Countries was a restless region in the latter half of 14th century, due to civil strife. Margaret succeeded in 1361 her great-nephew Philip I, Duke of Burgundy as ruler of Artois and Franche-Comté (County Palatine of B
1 CONC urgundy), since her elder sister Jeanne's issue went extinct by his death. This was the event why Margaret, already Dowager Countess of Flanders, received fiefs to her own possession in her own right (and not in joint right of mar
1 CONC riage).
1 CONT
1 CONT Her granddaughter lost her first husband at that. The duchy of Burgundy, having hitherto been united to elder Margaret's new counties of Palatine Burgundy and Artois 1347-61, went to a relative, king John II of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1369 her only granddaughter, the Margaret aforementioned, now 19 years old, was again married, to Philip the Bold (1342-1404), king John's youngest son, who had been since 1363 Duke of Burgundy (thus her granddaughter became ag
1 CONC ain the duchess of Burgundy). Philip had already held the duchy of Touraine in 1359-63 but given it in exchange for the bigger Burgundy.
1 CONT
1 CONT The unrest in coastal Low Countries escalated to open rebellions in Margaret's last years. A revolt in Ghent was put down by joint operation of Margaret's son Louis II of Flanders and grandson-in-law Philip II of Burgundy. However
1 CONC , after the Battle of Beverhoutsveld Margaret's son was expelled from Flanders by the Flemings under Philip van Artevelde. A French army (and Duke Philip) came to help them to regain Flanders, and the revolting Flemings were decis
1 CONC ively defeated at the Battle of Roosebeke the same year when Margaret died. However, the citizens of Ghent continued to resist (with English aid) and it was left to her granddaughter and her consort to subdue the town.
1 CONT
1 CONT Countess Margaret died at quite an advanced age, in 1382. Her counties of Artois and Burgundy were inherited by her only son Louis, Count of Flanders etc; who survived her just for two years. In 1384, all her possessions, together
1 CONC  with Louis' inheritance (Flanders etc), went to her only surviving grandchild, Margaret, the then duchess-consort of Burgundy, who thus became possessor of Palatine County of Burgundy and county of Artois, countess in her own rig
1 CONC ht, where she had been countess-consort almost thirty years earlier, as they then were held by her first husband.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Philip III Countess of Artois
1 CONT 1361–1382 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Louis III 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Philip III Palatine Countess of Burgundy
1 CONT 1361–1382 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Louis I 
0 @N6000000005076720622@ NOTE Comtesse d'Artois
1 CONT
1 CONT Marguerite d' Artois, Comtesse d'Artois was born in 1310.1 She was the daughter of Philippe V, Roi de France and Jeanne de Bourgogne, Comtesse d'Artois. She married Louis I de Flandre, Comte de Flandre, son of Louis de Flandre, Co
1 CONC mte de Rethel and Joan de Rethel, in 1320.1 She died in 1382.1
1 CONT      Marguerite d' Artois, Comtesse d'Artois gained the title of Comtesse d'Artois.
1 CONT Child of Marguerite d' Artois, Comtesse d'Artois and Louis I de Flandre, Comte de Flandre
1 CONT Louis II de Mâle, Comte de Flandre+ b. 25 Nov 1330, d. 13842
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 65. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
1 CONT [S16] Louda and MacLagan, Lines of Succession, table 74.
