0 @N6000000002187825699@ NOTE Rudolph I of Germany
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolph I (German: Rudolf von Habsburg, Latin Rudolfus; May 1, 1218 – July 15, 1291) was a king of the Holy Roman Empire, who played a vital role in raising the Habsburg family to a leading position among the royal dynasties of Ge
1 CONC rmany.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT Rudolph was the son of Albert IV, Count of Habsburg, and Hedwig, daughter of Ulrich, Count of Kyburg, and was born at Limburg an der Lahn. At his father's death in 1239, Rudolph inherited the family estates in Alsace, and in 1245 
1 CONC he married Gertrude, daughter of Burkhard III, Count of Hohenberg. He paid frequent visits to the court of his godfather the Emperor Frederick II, and his loyalty to Frederick and to his son Conrad IV of Germany was richly rewarde
1 CONC d by grants of land, but in 1254 he was excommunicated by Pope Innocent IV. The disorder in Germany after the fall of the Hohenstaufen afforded an opportunity for Rudolph to increase his possessions. His wife was an heiress; and o
1 CONC n the death of his childless uncle, Hartmann VI, Count of Kyburg, in 1264, he seized his valuable estates. Successful feuds with the bishops of Strassburg and Basel further augmented his wealth and his reputation; rights over vari
1 CONC ous tracts of land were purchased from abbots and others; and he was also the possessor of large estates in the regions now known as Switzerland and Alsace.
1 CONT
1 CONT These various sources of wealth and influence had rendered Rudolph the most powerful prince in south-western Germany when, in the autumn of 1273, the princes met to elect a king. His election at Frankfurt on the 29th of September 
1 CONC 1273 was largely due to the efforts of his brother-in-law, Frederick III of Hohenzollern, Burgrave of Nuremberg. The support of Albert, Duke of Saxe-Lauenburg, and of Louis II, Count Palatine of the Rhine and Duke of Upper Bavaria
1 CONC , had been purchased by betrothing them to two of Rudolph's daughters; so that Otakar II, King of Bohemia, a candidate for the throne, was almost alone in his opposition. Rudolph was crowned at Aachen on the 24th of October 1273, 
1 CONC and the feast which followed has been described by Friedrich Schiller in Der Graf von Habsburg ("The Count of Habsburg"). To win the approbation of the pope, Rudolph renounced all imperial rights in Rome, the papal territory and S
1 CONC icily, and promised to lead a new crusade; and Pope Gregory X, in spite of Otakar's protests, not only recognized Rudolph himself, but persuaded Alfonso X, King of Castile, who had been chosen German king in 1257, to do the same. 
1 CONC In November 1274 it was decided by the diet at Nuremberg that all crown estates seized since the death of the emperor Frederick II must be restored, and that Otakar of Bohemia must answer to the diet for not recognizing the new ki
1 CONC ng. Otakar refused to appear or to restore the provinces of Austria, Styria, Carinthia and Carniola which he had seized. He was placed under the ban; and in June 1276 war was declared against him. Having detached Henry I, Duke of 
1 CONC Lower Bavaria, from his side, Rudolph compelled the Bohemian king to cede the four provinces in November 1276. Otakar was then invested with Bohemia by Rudolph, and his son Wenceslaus was betrothed to a daughter of the German king
1 CONC , who made a triumphal entry into Vienna. Otakar, however, raised questions about the execution of the treaty, made an alliance with some Polish chiefs and procured the support of several German princes, including his former ally,
1 CONC  Henry of Lower Bavaria. To meet this combination Rudolph entered into alliance with Ladislaus IV, King of Hungary, and gave additional privileges to the citizens of Vienna. On the 26th of August 1278 the rival armies met on the b
1 CONC anks of the River March in the Battle of Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen, and Otakar was defeated and killed. Moravia was subdued and its government entrusted to Rudolph's representatives, while Wenceslaus was again betrothed to one of 
1 CONC his daughters.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolph's attention was next turned to his new possessions in Austria and the adjacent countries. He spent several years in establishing his authority there, but found some difficulty in making these provinces hereditary in his fa
1 CONC mily. At length the hostility of the princes was overcome, and in December 1282 Rudolph invested his sons Albert and Rudolph with the duchies of Austria and Styria at Augsburg, and so laid the foundation of the importance of the h
1 CONC ouse of Habsburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Turning to the west he compelled Philip, Count Palatine of Burgundy, to cede some districts to him in 1281, forced the citizens of Bern to pay the tribute which they had previously refused, and in 1289 marched against Philip's suc
1 CONC cessor, Otto IV, and compelled him to do homage. In 1281 his first wife died, and on the 5th of February 1284 he married Isabella, daughter of Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy. Rudolph was not very successful in restoring internal peace 
1 CONC to Germany. Orders were indeed issued for the establishment of landpeaces in Bavaria, Franconia and Swabia, and afterwards for the whole of Germany; but the king lacked the power, or the determination, to enforce them, although in
1 CONC  December 1289 he led an expedition into Thuringia where he destroyed a number of robber-castles. In 1291 he attempted to secure the election of his son Albert as German king; but the princes refused on the pretext of their inabil
1 CONC ity to support two kings, but perhaps because they feared the increasing power of the Habsburgs. Rudolph died at Speyer on July 15, 1291 and was buried in the cathedral of that city. He had a large family, but only one of his sons
1 CONC , Albert, afterwards the German king Albert I, survived him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolph was a tall man with pale face and prominent nose. He possessed many excellent qualities, bravery, piety and generosity; but his reign is memorable rather in the history of the house of Habsburg than in that of the kingdom 
1 CONC of Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Divine Comedy, Dante finds Rudolph sitting outside the gates of Purgatory with his contemporaries, and berates him as "he who neglected that which he ought to have done".
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He was married two times. First, 1245 with Gertrude of Hohenberg and second, 1284 with Agnes/Isabelle of Burgundy, daughter of Hugh IV, Duke of Burgundy and Beatrice of Champagne. All children were from first marriage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Albert I of Germany (July 1255 – 1 May 1308). 
1 CONT Hartmann (1263, Rheinfelden–21 December 1281), drowned at Rheinau. 
1 CONT Rudolph II, Duke of Austria (1270–10 May 1290, Prague). 
1 CONT Matilde (ca. 1251/53, Rheinfelden–23 December 1304, Munich), married in Aachen 1273 to Louis II, Duke of Bavaria. 
1 CONT Katharina (1256–4 April 1282, Landshut), married 1279 in Vienna to Otto III, Duke of Bavaria. 
1 CONT Agnes (ca. 1257–11 October 1322, Wittenberg), married 1273 to Albert III, Duke of Saxe-Wittenberg. 
1 CONT Hedwig (d. 1285/86), married 1270 in Vienna to Otto VI, Margrave of Brandenburg. 
1 CONT Klementia (ca. 1262–after 7 February 1293), married 1281 in Vienna to Charles Martel d'Anjou. 
1 CONT Jutte/Bona (13 March 1271–18 June 1297, Prague), married 24 January 1285 to King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia. 
1 CONT Also he had illegitimate son, Albrecht I of Schenkenberg, Count of Löwenstein.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, a publication in the public domain. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Richard of Cornwall
1 CONT and
1 CONT Alfonso of Castile King of Germany Succeeded by:
1 CONT Adolf of Nassau-Weilburg 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Otakar II of Bohemia Duke of Austria
1 CONT Duke of Styria Succeeded by:
1 CONT Albert I
1 CONT Rudolph II (co-ruler until 1282) 
1 CONT Duke of Carinthia
1 CONT Duke of Carniola Succeeded by:
1 CONT Meinhard II
1 CONT (given as pawn in 1276, as a fief in 1286) 
