0 @N6000000002187825710@ NOTE Gertrude of Hohenburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Gertrude of Hohenburg (c. 1225 – 16 February 1281, Vienna) was the first Queen consort of Rudolph I of Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT
1 CONT She was born to Burchard V, Count of Hohenberg (d. 1253) and his wife Mechtild of Tübingen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Burchard IV, Count of Hohenberg and his unnamed wife. Her maternal grandparents were Rudolph II, Count palatine and his wife, a daughter of Henry, Margrave of Ronsberg and Udilhild of Gammertingen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Burchard IV was a son of Burchard III, Count of Hohenberg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Burchard III was one of two sons of Burchard II, Count of Hohenberg. He was co-ruler with his brother Frederick, Count of Hohenberg. His brother had no known descendants and the two brothers consequently had a single successor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Burchard II was one of five known sons of Frederick I, Count of Zollern and his wife Udachild of Urach.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederich I was the son of Burchard I, Count of Zollern. He was the founder of the so-called Burchardinger family line, male-line ancestors of the House of Hohenzollern.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriage and children
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1245, Gertrude married Rudolph IV, Count of Habsburg. They had nine children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Albert I of Germany (July 1255 – 1 May 1308), Duke of Austria and also of Styria.
1 CONT    2. Hartmann (1263, Rheinfelden–21 December 1281), drowned in Rheinau.
1 CONT    3. Rudolph II, Duke of Austria and Styria (1270–10 May 1290, Prague), titular Duke of Swabia, father of John the Patricide of Austria.
1 CONT    4. Matilda (ca. 1251/53, Rheinfelden–23 December 1304, Munich), married 1273 in Aachen to Louis II, Duke of Bavaria and became mother of Rudolf I, Count Palatine of the Rhine and Louis IV, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT    5. Katharina (1256–4 April 1282, Landshut), married 1279 in Vienna to Otto III, Duke of Bavaria who later (after her death) became the disputed King Bela V of Hungary and left no surviving issue.
1 CONT    6. Agnes (ca. 1257–11 October 1322, Wittenberg), married 1273 to Albert II, Duke of Saxe-Wittenberg and became the mother of Rudolf I, Elector of Saxony.
1 CONT    7. Hedwig (d. 1285/86), married 1270 in Vienna to Otto VI, Margrave of Brandenburg and left no issue.
1 CONT    8. Klementia (ca. 1262–after 7 February 1293), married 1281 in Vienna to Charles Martel of Anjou, the Papal claimant to the throne of Hungary and mother of king Charles I of Hungary, as well as of queen Clementia of France, her
1 CONC self the mother of the baby king John I of France.
1 CONT    9. Jutte/Bona (13 March 1271–18 June 1297, Prague), married 24 January 1285 to King Wenceslaus II of Bohemia and became the mother of king Wenceslaus III of Bohemia, Poland and Hungary, of queen Anna I of Bohemia, duchess of Ca
1 CONC rinthia, and of queen Elisabeth I of Bohemia, countess of Luxembourg.
1 CONT
1 CONT Grave in Basel
1 CONT Grave in Basel
1 CONT
1 CONT Her husband was elected King of Germany in Frankfurt on 29 September 1273, largely due to the efforts of her cousin Frederick III, Burgrave of Nuremberg. Rudolph was crowned in Aachen on 24 October 1273. She served as his Queen co
1 CONC nsort for the following eight years.
1 CONT
1 CONT She died early in 1281. Rudolph remained a widower for three years and proceeded to marry Isabelle of Burgundy.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Elisabeth of Bavaria German Queen
1 CONT 29 September 1273 –16 February 1281 Succeeded by
1 CONT Isabelle of Burgundy
0 @N6000000006127668484@ NOTE Gräfin Gertrud Anna von Hohenberg (* um 1225, † 16. Februar 1281) war durch Heirat Gräfin von Habsburg und ab 1273 römisch-deutsche Königin. Sie gilt als Stammmutter der Dynastie der Habsburger in Österreich.Herkunft Älteste Tocht
1 CONC er von Graf Burkhard III. von Hohenberg und seiner Ehefrau Pfalzgräfin Mechthild von Tübingen, Tochter von Pfalzgraf Rudolf II. von Tübingen.Heirat und Nachkommen  Um 1253 heiratete sie im Elsass den Grafen Rudolf von Habsburg, So
1 CONC hn des Grafen Albrecht IV. von Habsburg und seiner Gemahlin Gräfin Heilwig von Kyburg.Aus dieser Ehe gingen unter anderem die folgenden Kinder hervor:Mathilde (1253-1304) 8 1273 in Heidelberg mit Ludwig II., dem Strengen, Pfalzgra
1 CONC f bei Rhein und Herzog in Bayern Albrecht I. (1255-1308) 8 1276 in Wien mit Elisabeth von Kärnten, Görz und Tirol Katharina (1256-1282) 8 1279 in Wien mit Otto III., Herzog von Niederbayern Agnes Gertrud (1257-1322) 8 1273 in Witt
1 CONC enberg mit Albrecht II. von Anhalt, Herzog von Sachsen-Wittenberg Hedwig (1259 - 1303) 8 1279 in Lehnin mit Otto IV., Markgraf von Brandenburg Klementia (1262-1293) 8 1281 in Neapel mit Karl I. Martell, König von Ungarn. Sohn von 
1 CONC Karl II. von Neapel aus dem Haus Anjou. Hartmann (1263-1281 ertrunken) verlobt mit Prinzessin Johanna, Tochter König Eduard I. von England Rudolf II. (1270-1290) 8 1289 in Prag mit Agnes von Böhmen, Tochter König Ottokar II. Premy
1 CONC sl Guta (Jutta) (1271-1297) 8 1285 in Prag mit Wenzel II., König von Böhmen Karl (*/† 1276) Zwanzig Jahre lang war Gertrud von Hohenberg eine brave Burggräfin und hielt das Hauswesen zusammen. Am 1. Oktober 1273 wählten die Kurfür
1 CONC sten ihren Mann, den Grafen Rudolf IV. von Habsburg, in Frankfurt am Main einstimmig zum deutschen König.Nach der Krönung in Aachen nannte sie sich "Königin Anna".Grabmal in BaselIm Chorgang des Basler Münsters befindet sich ihr S
1 CONC arkophag und der ihres jüngsten Sohns Karl. Ihre Gebeine wurden 1770 ins Kloster St. Blasien verlegt; heute ruhen sie im Stift St. Paul im Lavanttal in Kärnten.
0 @N6000000002187825777@ NOTE Agnes of Habsburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes of Habsburg (ca. 1257 – 11 October 1322) was a daughter of Emperor Rudolph I of Germany and his first wife Countess Gertrude of Hohenberg.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1273 she was married to Elector Albert II of Saxony. They had six children:
1 CONT
1 CONT    1. Duke Rudolf I of Saxe-Wittenberg (d. 11 March 1356) he was an Elector of Saxony
1 CONT    2. Otto (d. after 29 August 1311)
1 CONT    3. Albrecht (d. 19 May 1342), Bishop of Passau in 1322-1342
1 CONT    4. Wenzel (d. 17 March 1327), a canon in Halberstadt
1 CONT    5. Anna (d. 22 November 1327), married:
1 CONT          1. on 8 August 1308 to Margrave Friedrich der Lahme, son of Frederick I, Margrave of Meissen;
1 CONT          2. on 6 Jule 1315 Duke Henry II of Mecklenburg
1 CONT    6. Elisabeth (d. 1341)
0 @N6000000002187825751@ NOTE Rudolf II, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Rudolph II of Austria, titular Duke of Swabia (1270–May 10, 1290) was born as the younger son of Rudolph of Habsburg, from 1273 King of the Romans.
1 CONT
1 CONT In December 1282 he became Duke of Austria and Styria jointly with his brother Albert I. However, in the Treaty of Rheinfelden (June 1, 1283) he had to waive his share. He was married 1289 with Agnes of Bohemia(1269-96), daughter 
1 CONC of Otakar II of Bohemia and had an only son John. He died in the same year as his son was born at the age of 20.
1 CONT
1 CONT His brother's failure to ensure that he would be adequately compensated was the reason for strife in the Habsburg family, which led to the assassination of Albert by Rudolph's son John Parricida, in 1308.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Rudolf created him also Duke of Swabia, a province whose last duke, the underage Conradin, had been killed in 1268 and which was in long-term disarray, Habsburgs themselves being the most powerful provincial lords there.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Rudolph I Duke of Austria and Styria
1 CONT co-ruler to
1 CONT Albert I until 1283 Succeeded by
1 CONT Albert I
