0 @N6000000002188307745@ NOTE Andrew III of Hungary
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Andrew III
1 CONT King of Hungary
1 CONT Reign 1290 – 1301
1 CONT Consort Agnes of Austria
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew III the Venetian (Hungarian: III. (Velencei) András/Endre, Croatian: Andrija II, Slovak: Ondrej III) (c. 1265, Venice, Italy – 14 January 1301, Buda, Hungary), king of Hungary and Croatia (1290-1301).
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early years
1 CONT     * 2 King of Hungary
1 CONT     * 3 Marriages and children
1 CONT     * 4 Ancestors
1 CONT     * 5 Legacy
1 CONT     * 6 See also
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Early years
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a grandson of Andrew II of Hungary (reigned 1205-35), being the only son of Andrew II's youngest and posthumous son (possibly illegitimate), Stephen who was born of the old king's third marriage with Beatrice d'Este. His mo
1 CONC ther was Tomasina Morosini, descendant of a patrician family of Venice. After the death of his father (1272), he was educated with his Venetian relatives.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1278, Ivan Koszegi, an aristocrat who held several strongholds in the Western part of the kingdom of Hungary, invited him. Having arrived to the kingdom, Andrew claimed the government of the duchy of Slavonia, but king Ladislau
1 CONC s IV of Hungary refused him. After this failure, Andrew returned to Venice.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the beginning of 1290 Ivan Koszegi and Archbishop Lodomer of Esztergom, who had excommunicated king Ladislaus IV of Hungary, invited Andrew to Hungary and offered him the crown. Andrew accepted the offer, but he was arrested by
1 CONC  a Hungarian noble, Arnold de genere Hahót who handed him over to Duke Albert I of Austria.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] King of Hungary
1 CONT
1 CONT However, king Ladislaus IV of Hungary was assassinated by his own Cuman followers on July 10, 1290; therefore the main branch of the Árpád dynasty became extinct. Andrew, having been informed on the king's death, escaped from Vien
1 CONC na and went to Esztergom, where Archbishop Lodomer crowned him with the Holy Crown on July 23, 1290. After his coronation an assembly of the 'prelates, barons and nobles' of the kingdom of Hungary in Óbuda authorized the new king 
1 CONC to review his predecessor's donations. Andrew was married hastily with a Polish princess, Fenenna of Kujavia.
1 CONT
1 CONT The legitimacy of Andrew's rule was questioned soon, because his father had been declared bastard by his brothers; therefore the new king had to face several pretenders during his reign. On August 31, 1290 King Rudolph I of German
1 CONC y, who understood that Hungary belongs to the Holy Roman Empire, invested the kingdom to his son, Duke Albert I of Austria. Moreover, an adventurer from Poland pretended as he were Prince Andrew of Slavonia, the younger brother of
1 CONC  king Ladislaus IV of Hungary, but his troops were defeated by Andrew's followers. In April 1291 Maria of Hungary, queen of Charles II of Naples, the assassinated king's sister also announced her claim to the kingdom. She later tr
1 CONC ansferred her claim to her son, Prince Charles Martel of Salerno, and after his death (1295) to her grandson Charles Robert.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the beginning of 1291 Andrew III visited the Eastern part of his kingdom, where the assemblies of the local nobility held in Oradea (Nagyvárad) and Alba Iulia (Gyulafehérvár) accepted his rule. Afterwards, he lead his armies ag
1 CONC ainst Austria and defeated the Austrian troops. Duke Albert I of Austria, in the peace, concluded on 26 August, 1291 in Hainburg, renounced his claim to Hungary. In compensation Andrew III promised to demolish several smaller fort
1 CONC resses at the border of the two countries which were held by the powerful Koszegi family; consequently, Miklós Koszegi rebelled against Andrew and made an allince with the Babonic (Babonics) and Frankopan (Frangepán) families, fol
1 CONC lowers of the queen of Naples. The king tried to pacify the rebellion, but he was captured by Miklós Koszegi and had to pay ransom to retrieve his liberty.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1293 Andrew III invited his mother to Hungary, who carried out successful negotiations with several rebellious barons (Henrik Koszegi, Stefan Dragutin), who accepted her son's rule. During 1294 and 1295 Andrew III and his mothe
1 CONC r lead several campaigns against the followers of Charles Martel.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of his first wife, on February 6, 1296 Andrew III married Agnes of Austria, the daughter of Duke Albert I of Austria. Afterwards, with his father-in-law's support, he managed to defeat the revolt of Miklós Koszegi 
1 CONC and Matthew Csák and occupy the castles of Koszeg and Bratislava. In 1298 Andrew supported with troops his father-in-law's revolt against King Adolf of Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Andrew III, however, did never manage to strengthen his position in Hungary, because major parts of the kingdom were held by powerful barons like Miklós Koszegi, Matthew Csák, László Kán. Moreover, the new Archbishop of Esztergom,
1 CONC  Gergely Bicskei, appointed by pope Benedict VIII in 1298, supported the claims of the Neapolitan pretenders. Although, the assembly of the 'prelates, nobles, Saxons and Cumans' re-confirmed Andrew's reign in August, 1298 in Pest,
1 CONC  but the Archbishop began soon to organise the party of the Neapolitan prince, Charles Robert among the prelates. When, in the next year, the Archbishop openly denied to appear in the assembly held by the 'prelates and nobles', An
1 CONC drew occupied the estates of the Archbishopric.
1 CONT
1 CONT In August 1300, Charles Robert landed in Split, and he managed to come through Zagreb with the support of his Croatian followers. Andrew was hindered from any counter-attack by the sudden death of his mother and later by his own m
1 CONC ortal disease. He was buried in the Greyfriars Church in Buda.
1 CONT
1 CONT The death of Andrew III on January 14, 1301, ended the male line of the Árpáds. One of his contemporaries called him 'the last golden twig of the Árpáds'.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and children
1 CONT
1 CONT #1. 19 August/24 September 1290: Fennena of Kujavia (c. 1276 – c. 1295), daughter of prince Ziemomysl of Kujavia and his wife, Salome of Pommerellen
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Elizabeth (1292 – 6 May 1338, Töss, Switzerland), nun in the Dominican monastery in Töss
1 CONT
1 CONT #2. 13 February 1296: Agnes of Austria (18 May, 1281 – 10 Juny 1364, Königsfelden), daughter of duke Albert I of Austria (later king Albert I of Germany) and his wife, Elisabeth of Tirol.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT An interregnum followed Andrew's death. Otto III, Duke of Bavaria (Bela V), the future Venceslas III of Bohemia and Charles Robert of Anjou (Charles I) were proclaimed kings by different factions.
1 CONT
1 CONT After a short interregnum the Angevin dynasty seized power and Charles Robert (grandson of Maria of Hungary, sister of Ladislaus IV of Hungary, and son and heir to Charles Martel) became the recognized king.
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ladislaus IV of Hungary King of Hungary
1 CONT 1290–1301 Succeeded by
1 CONT Wenceslas III of Bohemia
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Ladislaus IV of Hungary King of Croatia
1 CONT 1290–1301 Succeeded by
1 CONT Charles I of Hungary
0 @N6000000002176663400@ NOTE When Ladislas IV died, he left no heirs. Then, when Andrew III died with
1 CONT no heirs, the Arpad dynasty, whose power had already dimished
1 CONT significantly, became extinct. Thereafter, the Hungarian throne became an
1 CONT object of contention among several foreign royal houses related by marriage
1 CONT to the house of Arpad.
0 @N6000000002244407171@ NOTE When Ladislas IV died, he left no heirs. Then, when Andrew III died with
1 CONT no heirs, the Arpad dynasty, whose power had already dimished
1 CONT significantly, became extinct. Thereafter, the Hungarian throne became an
1 CONT object of contention among several foreign royal houses related by marriage
1 CONT to the house of Arpad.
0 @N6000000002188378891@ NOTE Rudolf IV, Duke of Austria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolf IV of Austria. This portrait is the first half frontal portrait of the Occident. It had been on display above his grave in the Stephansdom of Vienna for several decades after his death, but can now be seen in the Museum of 
1 CONC the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Vienna. Apart from the (invented) archdukal crown, the foreshortening of which the artist did not completely master, the portrait is completely realistic. Even the duke's incipient facioplegia is 
1 CONC shown.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolf IV der Stifter (the Founder) (November 1, 1339 – July 27, 1365) was a member of the House of Habsburg and Duke (self-proclaimed Archduke) of Austria from 1358 to 1365.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in Vienna, Rudolf was the eldest son of Albert II and Johanna von Pfirt. One of the third generation of Habsburg dukes in Austria, he was the first to be born in Austria. Therefore, he considered Austria his home, a sentiment
1 CONC  that no doubt communicated itself to his subjects and contributed to his popularity. He was one of the most energetic and active rulers of Austria in the late Middle Ages, and it was said of him that as a young man he already had
1 CONC  the air of a king.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was married to Katharine of Bohemia the daughter of Emperor Charles IV. Eager to compete with his father-in-law, who had made Prague a radiant center of culture, Rudolf desired to raise the importance of Vienna to a comparable 
1 CONC or greater height.
1 CONT
1 CONT For more than a century, the Habsburg dukes had chafed at the Popes' failure to make Vienna the seat of its own diocese, a status that they considered appropriate for the seat of a duchy. The Bishops of Passau, the diocese that in
1 CONC cluded Vienna, had excellent connections to the Pope, apparently dooming Vienna's prospects in this regard. Rudolf, however, resorted to something which could be considered imposture: He initiated the creation in the Cathedral of 
1 CONC Saint Stephan (Stephansdom) of a Metropolitan Chapter (which, according to the name, should be assigned to a bishop), whose members wore red garments as cardinals do. The provost of the chapter received the title of Arch-Chancello
1 CONC r of Austria.
1 CONT The decipherment of the epitaph accompanying the cenotaph, or symbolic tomb, of Duke Rudolph IV in the Stephansdom in Vienna. The translation of the secret writing into English is "This is the sepulchre of Rudolph, by the Grace of
1 CONC  God, Duke and Founder" and "Almighty God and great lord Jesus Christ, a shepherd." Rudolf was, in fact, never buried within the almost-solid stone structure, but in the Ducal Crypt of the Stephansdom in Vienna. The text is writte
1 CONC n using the Alphabetum Kaldeorum, a code he probably invented.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rodolf extended Stephansdom, with the construction of its gothic nave being started under Rudolf's rule. Rudolf had himself be depicted on the cathedral's entrance. The construction efforts can be seen as an attempt to compete wit
1 CONC h St. Vitus Cathedral in Prague.
1 CONT
1 CONT Similarly, by founding the University of Vienna in 1365, Rudolf sought to match Charles IV's founding of the Charles University of Prague in 1348. Still known as Alma Mater Rudolphina today, the University of Vienna is the oldest 
1 CONC continuously operating university in the German-speaking world. However, a faculty of theology, which was considered crucial for a university at that time, was not established until 1385, twenty years after Rudolf's death.
1 CONT
1 CONT To improve the economy of Vienna Rudolf introduced many other measures, including the supervision by the mayor of sales of real property, instituted to prevent sales to the dead hand, i.e., to prevent economically unproductive own
1 CONC ership by the Church. Rudolf also managed to establish a relatively stable currency, the so-called Wiener Pfennig (Vienna Penny).
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolf is best known for another bluff, the forgery of the Privilegium Maius, which de facto put him on par with the Electors of the Holy Roman Empire, compensating for Austria's failure to receive an electoral vote in the Golden 
1 CONC Bull. The title of Archduke, invented by Rudolf, became an honorific title of all males of the House of Habsburg in the 16th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1363, Rudolf entered into a contract of inheritance with Countess Margarete Maultasch of the Tyrol, which brought the Tyrol under Austrian rule only after her death in 1369 since her brother-in-law Stephen II, Duke of Bavaria h
1 CONC ad invaded the country. The next year, he concluded another Contract of Inheritance with his father-in-law Emperor Charles, providing for mutual inheritance between the Habsburgs and Luxemburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT In spite of the high-flying (and maybe sometimes megalomaniac) character of his plans, he managed to modernize his territories and his city, the prominence of which considerably increased. His untimely death halted further progres
1 CONC s, however. His brothers Albert III and Leopold III, who were to rule jointly under the Rudolfinische Hausordnung (Rudolfinian House Rules), began to quarrel ceaselessly and ultimately agreed to divide the Habsburg territories bet
1 CONC ween them in 1379.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolf is also remembered for founding Novo Mesto in Slovenia whose German name, Rudolfswert, was given in his honor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Rudolf died in Milan in 1365. He and his wife are buried in the Ducal Crypt in the Stephansdom in Vienna.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Baum, Wilhelm (1996). Rudolf IV. der Stifter. Seine Welt und seine Zeit. Graz-Wien-Köln. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Wikimedia Commons has media related to:
1 CONT Rudolf IV, Duke of Austria
1 CONT
