0 @N6000000002187825623@ NOTE Otto II Wittelsbach, Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto II of Bavaria (German: Otto II der Erlauchte , Herzog von Bayern, Pfalzgraf bei Rhein) (7 April 1206, Kehlheim–29 November 1253, Landshut). He was the Duke of Bavaria and Count Palatine of the Rhine (see Palatinate). He was a
1 CONC  son of Louis I and Ludmila of Bohemia.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT At the age of six, Otto was married with Agnes of Palatinate, and with this marriage, he inherited Palatinate. Her grandfathers were Duke Henry the Lion and the Hohenstaufen Conrad. Otto's descendents kept it as a Wittelsbach poss
1 CONC esion until 1918.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otto acquired the rich regions of Bogen in 1240, and Andechs and Ortenburg in 1248. After a dispute with emperor Frederick II was ended, he joined the Hohenstaufen party in 1241. His daughter, Elizabeth, was married to Frederick's
1 CONC  son Conrad IV. Because of this, Otto was banned by the pope.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT Otto married Agnes, daughter of Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine (a son of Henry the Lion) and Agnes von Staufen, in Worms in 1222. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis II, Duke of Bavaria (13 April 1229, Heidelberg–2 February 1294, Heidelberg). 
1 CONT Henry XIII, Duke of Bavaria (19 November 1235, Landshut–3 February 1290, Burghausen). 
1 CONT Elisabeth (ca. 1227, Landshut–9 October 1273), married to: 
1 CONT 1246 in Vohburg to Conrad IV of Germany; 
1 CONT 1259 in Munich to Count Meinhard II of Gorizia-Tyrol, Duke of Carinthia. 
1 CONT Sophie (1236, Landshut–9 August 1289, Castle Hirschberg), married 1258 to Count Gerhard IV of Sulzbach and Hirschberg. 
1 CONT Agnes (ca. 1240–ca. 1306). 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Louis I Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT Count Palatine of the Rhine
1 CONT 1231–1253 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Louis II and Henry XIII 
0 @N6000000006127668607@ NOTE Otto II. der Erlauchte (* 7. April 1206 in Kelheim; † 29. November 1253 in Landshut) aus dem Geschlecht der Wittelsbacher war von 1231 bis 1253 Herzog von Bayern und von 1214 bis 1253 Pfalzgraf bei Rhein.Leben Otto war der Sohn He
1 CONC rzog Ludwigs I. von Bayern und Ludmillas von Böhmen, der Witwe des Grafen Albert III. von Bogen. Otto wurde im Alter von sechs Jahren mit Agnes von der Pfalz († 1267), der Erbin der Pfalzgrafschaft bei Rhein (der späterenKurpfalz)
1 CONC  verlobt. Ottos Vater Ludwig hatte die Pfalz im Jahre 1214 als Lehen erhalten. Unter seiner Herrschaft kamen Gebiete der aussterbenden Adelsgeschlechter der Grafen von Bogen, der Grafen von Andechs und der Ortenburger zu Bayern. M
1 CONC it denBesitztümern der Grafen von Bogen kam 1242 auch deren weiß-blaues Rautenwappen zu Bayern, das bis heute Bestandteil des Bayerischen Staatswappens ist. Otto II. starb 1253 und wurde im Benediktinerkloster Scheyern begraben.Fa
1 CONC milie  Herzog Otto II. heiratete im Mai 1222 die Prinzessin Agnes (1201–1267), eine Tochter des Welfen Heinrich von Braunschweig und seiner Gattin Pfalzgräfin Agnes bei Rhein. Aus der Ehe gingen fünf Kinder hervor:Elisabeth (1227–
1 CONC 1273) 1) 1246 in Vohburg König Konrad IV. von Hohenstaufen (1228–1254), 2) 1258 in München Graf Meinhard IV. von Görz und Tirol, später Herzog von Kärnten (1235–1295); Ludwig II. (1229–1294) 1) 1254 in Landshut Herzogin Maria von 
1 CONC Brabant (1226–1256), 2) 1260 in Heidelberg Herzogin Anna von Schlesien-Glogau (1240–1271), 3) 1273 in Aachen Gräfin Mechthild Mathilde von Habsburg (1253–1304); Heinrich XIII. (1235–1290) 8 1244 Prinzessin Elisabeth von Ungarn (12
1 CONC 36–1271); Sophie (1236–1289) 8 1258 in München Graf Gebhard VI. von Sulzbach und Hirschberg (1220–1275); Agnes (1240–1306), Nonne in Seligenthal. 
0 @N6000000000269772276@ NOTE Duke Louis II of Bavaria (German: Ludwig II der Strenge, Herzog von Bayern, Pfalzgraf bei Rhein; 13 April 1229 – 2 February 1294), was Duke of Bavaria and Count Palatine of the Rhine from 1253. Born in Heidelberg, he was a son of 
1 CONC duke Otto II and Agnes of Palatinate. She was a daughter of the Welf Henry V, Count Palatine of the Rhine, her grandfathers were Henry XII the Lion and Conrad of Hohenstaufen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide] 1 Biography 2 Family and children 2.1 The execution of Marie of Brabant 2.2 Later marriages 3 Links
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography In 1246 Louis supported his brother-in-law king Conrad IV of Germany against Heinrich Raspe and in 1251 Louis was in war again against the bishop of Regensburg. In 1253 Louis succeeded his father as duke of Bavari
1 CONC a.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the Wittelsbach country was divided in 1255 among Otto's sons, Louis received the Palatinate and Upper Bavaria, while his brother duke Henry XIII of Bavaria received Lower Bavaria. This partition was against the law and there
1 CONC fore caused the anger of the bishops in Bavaria who allied themselves with king Otakar II of Bohemia in 1257. In August 1257 Ottokar invaded Bavaria, but Louis and Henry managed to repulse the attack. It was one of the rare harmon
1 CONC ious actions of the two brothers, who often argued.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the German interregnum after king William's death in 1256 Louis supported Richard of Cornwall. Together with his brother Louis also aided his young Hohenstaufen nephew Conradin in his duchy of Swabia, but it was not possibl
1 CONC e to enforce Conradin's election as German king. As a result for his support Louis was banned by the pope in 1266. In 1267 Louis accompanied Conradin only to Verona. After the young prince's execution in Naples in 1268, Louis inhe
1 CONC rited some of Conradin's possessions in Swabia and supported the election of the Habsburg Rudolph I against Ottokar II in 1273. On 26 August 1278 the armies of Rudolph and Louis met Otakar's forces on the banks of the River March 
1 CONC in the Battle of Dürnkrut and Jedenspeigen where Otakar was defeated and killed. In 1289 the electoral dignity of Bavaria passed to Bohemia again, but Louis remained an elector as Count Palatine of the Rhine. After Rudolph's death
1 CONC  in 1291 Louis could not enforce the election of his Habsburg brother-in-law Albert I against Adolf of Nassau.
1 CONT
1 CONT Louis died at Heidelberg. His son Rudolf succeded him, with Adolf of Nassau becoming his father-in-law a few months later. Louis was buried in the crypt of Fürstenfeld Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family and children Louis II was married three times.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] The execution of Marie of Brabant He had his first wife Marie of Brabant —a daughter of duke Heinrich II. (Henry II.) of Brabant and Lothringen (Lorraine), and Maria, daughter of king Philipp of Schwaben (Swabia)— executed 
1 CONC in Donauwörth in 1256 due to mistaken suspicion of adultery; back in those days the punishment for an adulterous wife was beheading. Any actual guilt on her part could never be validated. As expiation Louis founded the Cistercian 
1 CONC friary Fürstenfeld Abbey (Fürstenfeldbruck) near Munich.
1 CONT
1 CONT Different sources tell varying tales about how this terrible mistake could happen in the first place: In 1256 Louis had been away from home for an extended time, due to his responsibilities as a sovereign in the area of the Rhine.
1 CONC  His wife wrote two letters, one to her husband, and another to the earl of Kyburg at Hunsrück, a vassal of Louis. Details about the actual content of the second letter vary, but according to the chroniclers the messenger who carr
1 CONC ied the letter to Louis had been given the wrong one, and Ludwig came to the conclusion that his wife had a secret love affair.
1 CONT
1 CONT Over time a great many tales of folklore sprang up around Louis' bloody deed, most of them written long after Louis' death: Ballad-mongers embellished the tale into a murderous frenzy, during which Louis allegedly not only killed 
1 CONC his wife after having ridden home for five days and nights, but also stabbed the messenger who brought him the wrong letter, then upon entering his castle stabbed his own castellan and a court lady and threw his wife's maid from t
1 CONC he battlements, before he massacred his wife either by stabbing her (why change a winning formula?) or cutting off her head.
1 CONT
1 CONT Several more restrained chronicles support the account of Marie's execution in January 18, 1256 in Donauwörth at castle Mangoldstein by ducal decree for alleged adultery, but nothing beyond that.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Later marriages In 1260 Louis married his second wife Anna of Glogau. They had the following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria (b. 1261), a nun in Marienburg abbey. Ludwig (13 September 1267–23 November 1290, killed at a tournament at Nuremberg. He married his third wife Mechthild, one of king Rudolph's daughters, on 27 October 1273. Their children 
1 CONC were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes (ca. 1267/77–1345), married to: 1290 in Donauwörth Landgrave Henry II of Hesse; 1298/1303 Heinrich I "Ohneland" ("Lackland"), Margrave of Brandenburg. Rudolf I (4 October 1274, Basle–12 August 1319). Mechthild (1275–28 March
1 CONC  1319, Lüneburg), married 1288 to Duke Otto II of Braunschweig-Lüneburg. Louis IV (1 April 1282, Munich–11 October 1347, Puch bei Fürstenfeldbruck). Louis II was succeeded by his oldest son Rudolf.
0 @N6000000002187825568@ NOTE Louis II, Duke of Bavaria
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Duke Louis II of Bavaria (13 April 1229, Heidelberg–2 February 1294, Heidelberg) (German: Ludwig II der Strenge , Herzog von Bayern, Pfalzgraf bei Rhein), from 1253 Duke of Bavaria and Count Palatine of the Rhine (see Palatinate).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Life
1 CONT He was a son of duke Otto II and Agnes. Her grandfathers were duke Henry the Lion and the Hohenstaufen Conrad. When the Wittelsbach country was divided in 1255 among Otto's sons, Louis received the Palatinate and Upper Bavaria; hi
1 CONC s brother Henry XIII received Lower Bavaria.
1 CONT
1 CONT Together with his brother he supported his nephew Conradin. After the young man's execution in Naples in 1268, Louis inherited some of Conradin's possesions in Swabia and supported the election of Rudolph I against Otakar II.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family and children
1 CONT He was married three times. Louis stabbed his first wife Mary of Brabant due to wrong accusations of adultery in Donauwörth 1256. As expiation he founded Fürstenfeldbruck monastery. Second, he married 1260 Anna of Glogau and had t
1 CONC he following children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Maria (b. 1261), nun in cloister Marienburg. 
1 CONT Ludwig (13 September 1267–23 November 1290, killed at a tournament at Nuremberg. 
1 CONT Third, he married 27 October 1273 Mechthild, one of king Rudolph's daughters. They children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT Agnes (ca. 1267/77–1345), married to: 
1 CONT 1290 in Donauwörth Landgrave Henry II of Hesse; 
1 CONT 1298/1303 Heinrich I "Ohneland", Margrave of Brandenburg. 
1 CONT Rudolf I (4 October 1274, Basel–12 August 1319). 
1 CONT Mechtild (1275–28 March 1319, Lüneburg), married 1288 to Duke Otto II of Braunschweig-Lüneburg. 
1 CONT Louis IV (1 April 1282, Munich–11 October 1347, Puch bei Fürstenfeldbruck). 
1 CONT Louis II was succeeded by his oldest son Rudolf.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Otto II Duke of Upper Bavaria
1 CONT Count Palatine of the Rhine
1 CONT 1253–1294 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Rudolf I 
0 @N6000000006127668463@ NOTE Ludwig II., Herzog von Bayern (1253-1294), genannt der Strenge (* 13. April 1229 in Heidelberg; † 2. Februar 1294 in Heidelberg), ab 1255 Herzog von Oberbayern und Kurfürst von der Pfalz, aus der Dynastie der Wittelsbacher.Leben  
1 CONC Der älteste Sohn Herzog Ottos II. sammelte schon in jungen Jahren Kriegserfahrungen, so 1246 mit König Konrad IV. gegen den Landgrafen von Thüringen und 1251 gegen den Bischof von Regensburg. Ludwig II. trat 1253 die Regierung als
1 CONC  Herzog vonBayern an. Am 28. März 1255 teilte er die Regierung mit seinem Bruder Heinrich XIII.. Heinrich erhielt Niederbayern, Ludwig Oberbayern – wo er München zur Residenz machte – und die Pfalz. Bei den angeblichen Konflikten 
1 CONC zwischen beiden handelte essich eher um Streitigkeiten zwischen Gefolgsleuten beider Seiten.Sein Beiname der Strenge rührt daher, dass er seine erste Ehefrau Maria von Brabant (* 1226) 1256 hinrichten ließ, weil er sie fälschliche
1 CONC rweise des Ehebruchs verdächtigte. Als Sühne für diese Tat stiftete Ludwig das Kloster Fürstenfeld(Fürstenfeldbruck). In zweiter Ehe war er mit Anna von Schlesien-Glogau (um 1240-1271) verheiratet.Der Herzog war Vormund seines Nef
1 CONC fen Konradin von Hohenstaufen, verschaffte diesem das Herzogtum Schwaben und begleitete ihn bei seinem Italienzug 1267 bis Verona. Er zog sich rechtzeitig zurück und wurde dadurch nicht in den Untergang des 1268 inNeapel hingerich
1 CONC teten Konradin verwickelt. Materiell profitierte er von dessen Tod, da Konradin ihn als Erben einsetzte und in der so genannten Konradinischen Schenkung Besitzungen in der Oberpfalz, um Sulzbach, in Südwestbayern und bayerischSchw
1 CONC aben abtrat. Eine Bestätigung dieser Erwerbungen erhielt Ludwig von Rudolf von Habsburg als Preis für seine Unterstützung bei der Königswahl. Darüber hinaus erhielt er die Hand von Rudolfs Tochter Mathilde. Aufgrund dieser Verbind
1 CONC ung wurdeLudwig ein Parteigänger der Habsburger, unterstützte seinen Schwager gegen König Ottokar II. von Böhmen, erhielt 1276 dessen Kurwürde und nahm 1278 an der Schlacht auf dem Marchfeld teil, in der Ottokar getötet wurde.Er k
1 CONC onnte nach dem Tod seines Schwiegervaters 1291 die Wahl seines Schwagers Albrecht von Habsburg zum König nicht durchsetzen. Die Kurwürde der Pfalz konnte Ludwig zwar behaupten, die bayerische fiel jedoch 1289 an Böhmen zurück. Lud
1 CONC wig II.gewann erhebliche neue Besitzungen für sein Herzogtum hinzu – auch in der Pfalz – und baute die herzogliche Macht stark aus. Er starb am 2. Februar 1294 in seiner pfälzischen Residenz in Heidelberg. Nachfolger wurde sein So
1 CONC hn Rudolf I. aus seinerdritten Ehe mit Mathilde von Habsburg.Nachkommen  Herzog Ludwig II. heiratete am 2. August 1254 in Landshut die Herzogin Maria (1226-1256, ermordet), Tochter des Herzog Heinrich II. von Brabant-Lothringen un
