0 @N6000000002187814219@ NOTE Henry IV of Limburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry IV (1195–25 February 1247) was the duke of Limburg and count of Berg from 1226 to his death. He was the son of Waleran III, count of Luxembourg and duke of Limburg, and Cunigunda, daughter of Frederick I, Duke of Lorraine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Originally lord of Montjoie, he married the heiress of the county of Berg, a daughter of the count Adolph III, who died at the Siege of Damietta in 1218. Ermengard and Henry could not immediately inherit the county, as it was held
1 CONC  by Engelbert I, Archbishop of Cologne. Engelbert being the principal adviser of Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor, he was confirmed in the county for life, paying a rent to Henry and Ermengard.
1 CONT
1 CONT Engelbert was assassinated on November 7, 1225 and Henry inherited Berg, inheriting Limburg a little while later. He then entrusted Montjoie to his brother Waleran, who already held Fauquemont. He then fought the count of Isambert
1 CONC , his brother-in-law, who was suspected of the assassination of Engelbert. In 1228, took part in the Sixth Crusade to the Holy Land. Upon returning to Germany, he made war on the archbishop of Cologne, Konrad von Hochstaden betwee
1 CONC n 1238 and 1241.. He was counted among the partisans of the Hohenstaufen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Waleran III Duke of Limburg
1 CONT 1226–1247 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Waleran IV 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Engelbert II Count of Berg
1 CONT 1225–1247 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Adolph IV 
0 @N6000000002187814251@ NOTE Henry III of Limburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry III (c.1140 – 21 June 1221) was the duke of Limburg and count of Arlon from 1165 to his death. He was the son and successor of Henry II and Matilda of Saffenberg.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1172, he fought against the count of Luxembourg, Henry IV the Blind, and the his ally, the count of Hainault, Baldwin V. The environs of Arlon were devastated and the duke, overcome, had to recompense the count of Luxembourg fo
1 CONC r the wrongs he had done him. In 1183, he supported the election of Fulmar as archbishop of Trier. This was opposed by the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry had to fight his nephew, Henry I of Brabant in connection with the advocacy of the abbey of Saint-Trond. The Brabantine duke claimed the advocacy as part of his mother's dowry. An arrangement put an end to the war in 1191 an
1 CONC d the duke of Limburg became a vassal of the duke of Brabant. The two dukes collaborated in the internal affairs of the region (Belgia).
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry later supported Otto of Brunswick over Philip of Swabia as German king and imperial claimant. He fought at the Battle of Bouvines in 1214. It was a disaster for Otto.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry II Duke of Limburg
1 CONT 1165–1221 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Waleran III 
1 CONT Count of Arlon
1 CONT 1165–1221 
