0 @N6000000001956363376@ NOTE
1 CONT During the late Roman period (8th/9th century), the south of France encountered several partitions of its lands under Charlemagne (Charles I, a.k.a. Charles the Great). One partition occurred about 50 miles NNE of Toulouse (25 mil
1 CONC es SE of Cahors, Lot: where the New Orleans Valette's originated), and where there was located upon a hill a monastery of Cistercian monks. The control of this particular area was given over to the Roman commander Castellum Parisi
1 CONC us; and the village that subsequently developed became known as Parisius.At sometime before the 11th century, the surname Parisius evolved into the French language as Parisot. During the 13th century, a branch of the La Valettes c
1 CONC ame to power as knight-vassal of the local area, though under the suzerainty of the Comte de Toulouse and 
1 CONT the Duke d'Aquitaine. Their home at Parisot was the Chateau Labro.
