0 @N6000000005598912327@ NOTE Henry is styled as pallid, intellectual, coldly inhuman, with immensel y large ambitions and possessed an unusual fund of energy and imaginat ion.  He is know to have been a brillant chess player.  He had learne d how to write son
1 CONC gs when he was in his early twenties while in Italy .  By 1191, having become Emperor, his main residence became the palac e at Hagenau.  Due to his father Frederick, Henry VI enjoyed prestige and political control in northern Ita
1 CONC ly.  While Henry was involved in attacks upon the papal lands in Italy, and Phllippe of France was retu rning from the crusade, they met and one of the subjects was Richard a nd what to do about him once he returned from the crusa
1 CONC de.  On 28 Dece mber 1192, Henry was informed by Leopold V of Austria, his cousin, tha t Richard I of England had been captured near Vienna and was being hel d at the fortress of Durnstein above the Danube river.  At the time, H e
1 CONC nry was at Hordhausen near Nuremburg.  Within ten days of Richards ca pture, Henry VI ordered that Richard be brought to the imperial court at Regensburg.  Henry subsequently notified Phillippe of France of th e capture.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time, Henry's armies were invading the papal lands of Pope Cele stine III, but his march into Italy had proved to be a miserable and e xpensive failure.  At the time of Richard's capture, Henry was facing a powerful challen
1 CONC ge to his power from the princes of the Lower Rhinel and and the powerful Bishops of Cologne and Mainz.  On 14 Feb 1193, a t Wurzburg, Henry VI and Leopold V met to discuss Richard's release. It was agreed that Richard should pay 
1 CONC 100,000 marks ransom, of which L eopold would received half, that Isaac Comnenus would be released alon g with his daughter and that Richard would supply Henry with 50 fully armed galleys, 100 knights and 50 crossbow-men.  It was 
1 CONC also agreed th at Richard was to provide 200 hostages to be held until such time tha t Richard had provided the ransom.  Also they agreed that a trial of R ichard would be held at Henry's court at Speyer on Palm Sunday of 1193 .  
1 CONC One of the conditions for this release by Leopold, was that Richar d would be forced to persuade the Pope to absolve Leopold and Henry V from the sin of capturing a returning crusader.  While Leopold did exp erience excommunicatio
1 CONC n, Henry was never excommunicated.
1 CONT
1 CONT On about 20 March 1193, Henry met with Richard at Speyer, where it is said that Richard refused the terms of the release agreement, and the trial began the next day.  With a 'Kiss of peace', Henry dismissed th e charges against Ri
1 CONC chard.  The next day, Leopold turned Richard over to Henry.  Henry moved Richard to Trifels castle west of the city of S peyer.  As the spring turned into the summer of 1193, Henry's difficul ties with his Rhineland princes contin
1 CONC ued to grow.  Richard, by then h oused at Hagenau, became a great asset to Henry in his negotiations wi th his princes.  In June of 1193, Henry and Richard I agreed to the be trothal of Richards niece Eleanor of Brittany to the so
1 CONC n of Leopold V of Austria.  On 24 January 1194, Henry VI announced that he intended t o crown Richard I as the King of Provence, an honor which Richard I ev entually turned down.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the son of Tancred, William, became King of Sicily, Henry VI init iated a campaing in southern Italy with the aim of establishing himsel f in southern Italy during which he invaded and took Palermo.  He and Constance were cro
1 CONC wned as monarchs of Sicily at Palermo in November 11 94, despite local opposition.  He negotiated a settlement with Tancred 's son William that allowed the child King to hold Toranto.  But upon learning of a plot on his life in th
1 CONC e Spring of 1197, Henry had Willia m, his mother and sister, blindfolded and removed to confinement in Ge rmany.  He then opened the tombs of Tancred and his predecessors to re move any jewells and regalia which was then removed t
1 CONC o his castle at T rifels.  It is said that he had one of the conspirators skinned alive and another was crowned with iron nails through the temple.  At the ti me of his death, Henry was still under the edict of excommunication an 
1 CONC d thus was not able to receive a Christian burial.
